window.NARRATION = {"videos":[{"id":"d01-es","day":1,"lang":"es","title":"LLMs en QA: el mapa 2026, dónde sirven y dónde no","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d01-es.mp4","duration":382.419,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":13.926,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 1 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.405,"terms":[{"start":29,"end":31,"say":"cu a"},{"start":34,"end":36,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"LLMs en QA: el mapa 2026, dónde sirven y dónde no.","start":3.955,"end":7.986,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":4,"say":"ele ele emes"},{"start":8,"end":10,"say":"cu a"}]}],[{"text":"Seis usos de LLM en QA que hoy funcionan, tres que no, y una regla simple para decidir cuándo delegar.","start":8.536,"end":13.926,"terms":[{"start":20,"end":22,"say":"cu a"}]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":14.726,"end":31.913,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"En 2026 casi todos los equipos de QA usan algún LLM, pero pocos pueden decir en qué tareas les ahorra tiempo de verdad y en cuáles les suma riesgo.","start":15.226,"end":22.973,"terms":[{"start":34,"end":36,"say":"cu a"}]},{"text":"El resultado típico es usarlo para todo durante dos semanas, encontrarse con casos de prueba inventados y un par de tests frágiles, y volver a trabajar como antes.","start":23.273,"end":31.913,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":32.713,"end":48.74,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Esta guía ordena ese terreno.","start":33.213,"end":34.992,"terms":[]},{"text":"No parte de la herramienta sino de tus tareas: cuáles son de texto a texto, cuáles tienen una fuente de verdad contra la que comparar y cuáles se pueden deshacer si salen mal.","start":35.292,"end":43.143,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con eso alcanza para armar un mapa propio y una regla de decisión que vas a usar durante toda la serie.","start":43.443,"end":48.74,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":49.54,"end":60.176,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":50.04,"end":51.97,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Una user story real de tu backlog.","start":52.52,"end":54.612,"terms":[{"start":4,"end":14,"say":"iúser stori"}]}],[{"text":"Un LLM a mano: Claude, ChatGPT o un modelo local con Ollama.","start":55.162,"end":58.497,"terms":[{"start":15,"end":21,"say":"clod"},{"start":23,"end":30,"say":"chat ye pe te"},{"start":53,"end":59,"say":"olama"}]}],[{"text":"20 minutos.","start":59.047,"end":60.176,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":60.976,"end":97.672,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":61.476,"end":62.372,"terms":[]},{"text":"Inventario de tareas reales.","start":62.672,"end":64.428,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Listá tus tareas de QA de la última semana y marcá las que son texto a texto: analizar, redactar, clasificar, resumir.","start":64.978,"end":71.784,"terms":[{"start":20,"end":22,"say":"cu a"}]}],[{"text":"Abrí tu calendario y tu tablero de la semana pasada y anotá cada tarea con un verbo: revisar un requisito, escribir casos, preparar datos, automatizar un flujo, analizar un pipeline rojo, reportar un bug.","start":72.334,"end":82.96,"terms":[{"start":173,"end":181,"say":"páiplain"},{"start":200,"end":203,"say":"bag"}]},{"text":"No anotes lo que te gustaría hacer, sino lo que hiciste.","start":83.26,"end":86.165,"terms":[]},{"text":"Al lado de cada una poné cuánto tiempo te llevó.","start":86.465,"end":88.975,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las tareas de texto a texto que además te consumen horas son las primeras candidatas, porque ahí el modelo produce un borrador en segundos y vos pasás a revisar en lugar de redactar.","start":89.275,"end":97.672,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":98.472,"end":142.509,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":98.972,"end":99.868,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los seis usos que rinden.","start":100.168,"end":101.762,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ubicalas en los seis usos que funcionan: análisis de requisitos y ambigüedades, diseño de casos desde user stories, datos de prueba, escritura y refactor de automation con agentes, triage de fallos sobre logs y traces, y evals de features que usan IA.","start":102.312,"end":115.271,"terms":[{"start":102,"end":114,"say":"iúser storis"},{"start":157,"end":167,"say":"otoméishon"},{"start":181,"end":187,"say":"triásh"},{"start":211,"end":217,"say":"tréises"},{"start":230,"end":238,"say":"fíchers"},{"start":248,"end":250,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Cada uno funciona por la misma razón: hay algo contra qué comparar.","start":115.821,"end":119.434,"terms":[]},{"text":"En análisis de requisitos, comparás contra el product owner.","start":119.734,"end":122.872,"terms":[{"start":46,"end":59,"say":"próduct óuner"}]},{"text":"En diseño de casos, contra las reglas de negocio.","start":123.172,"end":125.891,"terms":[]},{"text":"En datos de prueba, contra un schema.","start":126.191,"end":128.121,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":36,"say":"skima"}]},{"text":"En automation, contra la ejecución del test.","start":128.421,"end":130.838,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":13,"say":"otoméishon"}]},{"text":"En triage, contra el trace.","start":131.138,"end":132.825,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":9,"say":"triásh"},{"start":21,"end":26,"say":"treis"}]},{"text":"En evals, contra un dataset etiquetado.","start":133.125,"end":135.298,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si para una tarea no podés nombrar ese punto de comparación, todavía no pertenece a esta lista, por más que el resultado se vea prolijo.","start":135.598,"end":142.509,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":143.309,"end":179.332,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":143.809,"end":144.705,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los tres usos que fallan.","start":145.005,"end":146.61,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tachá los tres que no funcionan: decidir un release sin una persona, usar el modelo como oráculo sin fuente de verdad, y mergear tests de UI generados sin revisar.","start":147.16,"end":155.336,"terms":[{"start":44,"end":51,"say":"rilís"},{"start":138,"end":140,"say":"u i"}]}],[{"text":"Decidir un release es un juicio de riesgo de negocio y el modelo no conoce tu contexto ni responde por la decisión.","start":155.886,"end":161.311,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":18,"say":"rilís"}]},{"text":"Usarlo de oráculo sin fuente de verdad produce respuestas seguras y a veces falsas, que es la peor combinación para QA.","start":161.611,"end":167.43,"terms":[{"start":116,"end":118,"say":"cu a"}]},{"text":"Y los tests de UI generados en lote suelen pasar una vez y fallar a la semana, porque dependen de selectores adivinados y esperas fijas.","start":167.73,"end":174.269,"terms":[{"start":15,"end":17,"say":"u i"}]},{"text":"No están prohibidos: necesitan una persona que mire el resultado antes de darlo por bueno.","start":174.569,"end":179.332,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":180.132,"end":214.982,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":180.632,"end":181.633,"terms":[]},{"text":"La regla de tres preguntas.","start":181.933,"end":183.642,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Aplicá la regla: delego solo si el resultado es verificable, barato de revisar y reversible.","start":184.192,"end":189.245,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Verificable significa que existe un chequeo objetivo: un test que corre, un schema, una regla escrita.","start":189.795,"end":195.069,"terms":[{"start":76,"end":82,"say":"skima"}]},{"text":"Barato de revisar significa que leerlo te cuesta bastante menos que hacerlo vos; si revisar cuarenta casos generados te lleva más que escribir quince buenos, no ganaste nada.","start":195.369,"end":204.044,"terms":[]},{"text":"Reversible significa que un error no deja consecuencias: un borrador se descarta, un merge a producción no.","start":204.344,"end":209.919,"terms":[{"start":85,"end":90,"say":"merch"}]},{"text":"Si falla una de las tres, la IA puede asistir, pero la decisión y la firma son tuyas.","start":210.219,"end":214.982,"terms":[{"start":29,"end":31,"say":"i a"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":215.782,"end":248.171,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":216.282,"end":217.283,"terms":[]},{"text":"Primera prueba con una user story.","start":217.583,"end":219.618,"terms":[{"start":23,"end":33,"say":"iúser stori"}]}],[{"text":"Probalo: pegá tu user story y pedí \"antes de escribir casos, listá ambigüedades y preguntas para el PO\".","start":220.168,"end":225.522,"terms":[{"start":17,"end":27,"say":"iúser stori"},{"start":100,"end":102,"say":"pe o"}]}],[{"text":"Elegí una story que ya esté en desarrollo, así podés comparar con lo que el equipo descubrió por las malas.","start":226.072,"end":231.218,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pegá el texto completo con sus criterios de aceptación y pedí solo ambigüedades y preguntas, no casos.","start":231.518,"end":236.676,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tomá nota de cuántas preguntas son reales, cuántas ya estaban respondidas en otro lado y cuántas no aplican.","start":236.976,"end":242.226,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esa proporción te dice si para tu dominio conviene usarlo en refinamiento o si primero necesita más contexto escrito.","start":242.526,"end":248.171,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":248.971,"end":280.548,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":249.471,"end":250.437,"terms":[]},{"text":"El registro de aciertos y errores.","start":250.737,"end":252.841,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Anotá qué acertó y qué inventó.","start":253.391,"end":255.252,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ese registro es tu primer eval.","start":255.552,"end":257.505,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Armá una tabla simple con cuatro columnas: tarea, qué pediste, qué acertó y qué inventó.","start":258.055,"end":262.759,"terms":[]},{"text":"Completala durante dos semanas cada vez que uses un modelo para algo de QA.","start":263.059,"end":266.94,"terms":[{"start":72,"end":74,"say":"cu a"}]},{"text":"Sin ese registro tu opinión sobre la herramienta va a depender del último resultado que viste.","start":267.24,"end":271.747,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con el registro tenés evidencia para decidir dónde seguir usándola, y además el primer borrador de un dataset de evaluación, que es justo lo que vamos a construir más adelante.","start":272.047,"end":280.548,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":281.348,"end":318.005,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":281.848,"end":282.768,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Una analista toma la story \"aplicar cupón de descuento en el checkout\".","start":283.318,"end":286.571,"terms":[{"start":61,"end":69,"say":"chécaut"}]},{"text":"Le pide al modelo ambigüedades y recibe siete preguntas.","start":286.871,"end":289.893,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tres son reales y nadie las había hecho: qué pasa si el cupón vence durante la compra, si se combina con envío gratis y cómo se redondea.","start":290.193,"end":297.034,"terms":[]},{"text":"Dos ya estaban respondidas en el documento de reglas.","start":297.334,"end":300.332,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las otras dos asumen una regla de \"un cupón por cliente\" que no existe.","start":300.632,"end":304.14,"terms":[]},{"text":"Resultado en la tabla: tres aciertos, dos sobras y dos inventos.","start":304.44,"end":308.216,"terms":[]},{"text":"Diez minutos de trabajo y tres preguntas que evitaron un bug en producción.","start":308.516,"end":312.176,"terms":[{"start":57,"end":60,"say":"bag"}]},{"text":"Ese es un uso que pasa la regla: verificable con el PO, barato de leer y sin consecuencias si se descarta.","start":312.476,"end":318.005,"terms":[{"start":52,"end":54,"say":"pe o"}]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":318.805,"end":340.535,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":319.305,"end":320.829,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Evaluar la herramienta por una demo o por el último resultado, sin registro de aciertos y errores.","start":321.379,"end":326.281,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pedir el entregable final (casos, tests) antes de pedir preguntas y ambigüedades.","start":326.831,"end":330.92,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Dar por bueno un resultado porque está bien redactado, sin compararlo contra una fuente de verdad.","start":331.47,"end":336.209,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Medir el ahorro solo en tiempo de escritura y olvidar el tiempo de revisión.","start":336.759,"end":340.535,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":341.335,"end":358.816,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":341.835,"end":344.102,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tenés diez tareas reales clasificadas en delegables y no delegables.","start":344.652,"end":348.138,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cada tarea delegable tiene nombrado contra qué se verifica.","start":348.688,"end":351.732,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Corriste el prompt de ambigüedades sobre una story real.","start":352.282,"end":355.117,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tu tabla tiene al menos tres aciertos y un error anotados.","start":355.667,"end":358.816,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":359.616,"end":367.975,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":360.116,"end":361.686,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Inventa reglas de negocio con mucha seguridad.","start":362.236,"end":364.793,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sin una fuente de verdad al lado, no sirve de oráculo.","start":365.093,"end":367.975,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":368.775,"end":380.919,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 1.","start":369.275,"end":370.555,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":370.855,"end":376.094,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 2: Cómo se está reorientando el rol SDET en el mundo de la IA.","start":376.644,"end":380.919,"terms":[{"start":53,"end":57,"say":"ese de e te"},{"start":76,"end":78,"say":"i a"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d01-en","day":1,"lang":"en","title":"LLMs in QA: the 2026 map, where they help and where they don't","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d01-en.mp4","duration":422.485,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":16.248,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 1 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":3.87,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"LLMs in QA: the 2026 map, where they help and where they don't.","start":4.42,"end":9.426,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Six LLM uses in QA that work today, three that don't, and a simple rule to decide when to delegate.","start":9.976,"end":16.248,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":17.048,"end":35.094,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"In 2026 almost every QA team uses some LLM, but few can say which tasks it really speeds up and which ones it makes riskier.","start":17.548,"end":26.142,"terms":[]},{"text":"The usual outcome is to use it for everything for two weeks, run into invented test cases and a couple of brittle tests, and go back to working as before.","start":26.442,"end":35.094,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":35.894,"end":52.884,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This guide sorts that ground out.","start":36.394,"end":38.684,"terms":[]},{"text":"It doesn't start from the tool but from your tasks: which are text to text, which have a source of truth to compare against, and which can be undone if they go wrong.","start":38.984,"end":47.868,"terms":[]},{"text":"That is enough to build your own map and a decision rule you will use for the whole series.","start":48.168,"end":52.884,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":53.684,"end":66.085,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":54.184,"end":55.882,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A real user story from your backlog.","start":56.432,"end":59.024,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Any LLM: Claude, ChatGPT or a local model with Ollama.","start":59.574,"end":64.197,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"20 minutes.","start":64.747,"end":66.085,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":66.885,"end":105.346,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":67.385,"end":68.421,"terms":[]},{"text":"Inventory of real tasks.","start":68.721,"end":70.744,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"List last week's QA tasks and flag the text-to-text ones: analyze, draft, classify, summarize.","start":71.294,"end":78.135,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Open last week's calendar and board and write each task down with a verb: review a requirement, write cases, prepare data, automate a flow, analyze a red pipeline, report a bug.","start":78.685,"end":90.262,"terms":[]},{"text":"Don't list what you'd like to do, list what you did.","start":90.562,"end":93.618,"terms":[]},{"text":"Next to each one, note how long it took.","start":93.918,"end":96.824,"terms":[]},{"text":"Text-to-text tasks that also eat hours are the first candidates, because the model produces a draft in seconds and you move from writing to reviewing.","start":97.124,"end":105.346,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":106.146,"end":156.034,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":106.646,"end":107.845,"terms":[]},{"text":"The six uses that pay off.","start":108.145,"end":110.249,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Place them in the six uses that work: requirements and ambiguity analysis, test design from user stories, test data, writing and refactoring automation with agents, failure triage over logs and traces, and evals for AI-powered features.","start":110.799,"end":126.068,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each one works for the same reason: there is something to compare against.","start":126.618,"end":130.742,"terms":[]},{"text":"In requirements analysis you compare against the product owner.","start":131.042,"end":134.598,"terms":[]},{"text":"In test design, against the business rules.","start":134.898,"end":137.873,"terms":[]},{"text":"In test data, against a schema.","start":138.173,"end":140.404,"terms":[]},{"text":"In automation, against the test run.","start":140.704,"end":143.482,"terms":[]},{"text":"In triage, against the trace.","start":143.782,"end":146.141,"terms":[]},{"text":"In evals, against a labeled dataset.","start":146.441,"end":148.998,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you can't name that point of comparison for a task, it doesn't belong on this list yet, however neat the output looks.","start":149.298,"end":156.034,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":156.834,"end":197.211,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":157.334,"end":158.417,"terms":[]},{"text":"The three uses that fail.","start":158.717,"end":160.461,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cross out the three that don't: release decisions with no human, the model as an oracle with no source of truth, and merging generated UI tests unreviewed.","start":161.011,"end":170.267,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A release decision is a business risk judgment, and the model neither knows your context nor answers for the decision.","start":170.817,"end":176.961,"terms":[]},{"text":"Using it as an oracle with no source of truth produces confident and sometimes false answers, the worst combination for QA.","start":177.261,"end":184.555,"terms":[]},{"text":"And UI tests generated in bulk tend to pass once and fail a week later, because they rely on guessed selectors and fixed waits.","start":184.855,"end":192.531,"terms":[]},{"text":"They aren't forbidden: they need a person to look at the result before calling it good.","start":192.831,"end":197.211,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":198.011,"end":236.101,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":198.511,"end":199.524,"terms":[]},{"text":"The three-question rule.","start":199.824,"end":201.336,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Apply the rule: delegate only if the result is verifiable, cheap to review and reversible.","start":201.886,"end":207.496,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Verifiable means an objective check exists: a test that runs, a schema, a written rule.","start":208.046,"end":214.039,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cheap to review means reading it costs you much less than doing it yourself; if reviewing forty generated cases takes longer than writing fifteen good ones, you gained nothing.","start":214.339,"end":223.897,"terms":[]},{"text":"Reversible means a mistake leaves no consequences: a draft gets discarded, a merge to production doesn't.","start":224.197,"end":230.492,"terms":[]},{"text":"If one of the three fails, AI can assist, but the decision and the signature are yours.","start":230.792,"end":236.101,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":236.901,"end":275.199,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":237.401,"end":238.529,"terms":[]},{"text":"First trial with a user story.","start":238.829,"end":240.991,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Try it: paste your user story and ask \"before writing cases, list ambiguities and questions for the PO\".","start":241.541,"end":248.684,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pick a story already in development, so you can compare with what the team found out the hard way.","start":249.234,"end":254.531,"terms":[]},{"text":"Paste the full text with its acceptance criteria and ask only for ambiguities and questions, not cases.","start":254.831,"end":261.463,"terms":[]},{"text":"Note how many questions are real, how many were already answered elsewhere and how many don't apply.","start":261.763,"end":267.733,"terms":[]},{"text":"That ratio tells you whether, in your domain, it's worth using in refinement or whether it first needs more written context.","start":268.033,"end":275.199,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":275.999,"end":309.77,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":276.499,"end":277.686,"terms":[]},{"text":"The log of hits and misses.","start":277.986,"end":279.835,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Write down what it got right and what it made up.","start":280.385,"end":283.127,"terms":[]},{"text":"That log is your first eval.","start":283.427,"end":285.253,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Build a simple table with four columns: task, what you asked, what it got right and what it made up.","start":285.803,"end":292.075,"terms":[]},{"text":"Fill it in for two weeks every time you use a model for something in QA.","start":292.375,"end":296.15,"terms":[]},{"text":"Without that log, your opinion of the tool will depend on the last result you saw.","start":296.45,"end":301.155,"terms":[]},{"text":"With it you have evidence to decide where to keep using it, plus the first draft of an evaluation dataset, which is exactly what we build later on.","start":301.455,"end":309.77,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":310.57,"end":352.545,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":311.07,"end":312.176,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"An analyst takes the story \"apply a discount coupon at checkout\".","start":312.726,"end":316.351,"terms":[]},{"text":"She asks the model for ambiguities and gets seven questions.","start":316.651,"end":320.392,"terms":[]},{"text":"Three are real and nobody had asked them: what happens if the coupon expires mid-purchase, whether it combines with free shipping and how rounding works.","start":320.692,"end":329.46,"terms":[]},{"text":"Two were already answered in the rules document.","start":329.76,"end":332.317,"terms":[]},{"text":"The other two assume a \"one coupon per customer\" rule that doesn't exist.","start":332.617,"end":336.59,"terms":[]},{"text":"Result in the table: three hits, two leftovers and two inventions.","start":336.89,"end":341.154,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ten minutes of work and three questions that prevented a production bug.","start":341.454,"end":345.23,"terms":[]},{"text":"That use passes the rule: verifiable with the PO, cheap to read and with no consequences if discarded.","start":345.53,"end":352.545,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":353.345,"end":377.293,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":353.845,"end":355.508,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Judging the tool by a demo or the last result, with no log of hits and misses.","start":356.058,"end":361.169,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Asking for the final deliverable (cases, tests) before asking for questions and ambiguities.","start":361.719,"end":367.306,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Accepting a result because it reads well, without comparing it against a source of truth.","start":367.856,"end":372.943,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Measuring savings only in writing time and forgetting review time.","start":373.493,"end":377.293,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":378.093,"end":396.537,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":378.593,"end":380.836,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"You have ten real tasks classified as delegable or not.","start":381.386,"end":385.057,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each delegable task names what it gets verified against.","start":385.607,"end":389.267,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"You ran the ambiguity prompt on a real story.","start":389.817,"end":392.815,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Your table has at least three hits and one error logged.","start":393.365,"end":396.537,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":397.337,"end":406.892,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":397.837,"end":399.732,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"It makes up business rules with full confidence.","start":400.282,"end":403.176,"terms":[]},{"text":"Without a source of truth next to it, it is not an oracle.","start":403.476,"end":406.892,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":407.692,"end":420.985,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 1.","start":408.192,"end":409.634,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":409.934,"end":415.916,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 2: How the SDET role is being reshaped by AI.","start":416.466,"end":420.985,"terms":[{"start":29,"end":33,"say":"ess det"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d02-es","day":2,"lang":"es","title":"Cómo se está reorientando el rol SDET en el mundo de la IA","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d02-es.mp4","duration":366.19,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":12.777,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 2 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.347,"terms":[{"start":29,"end":31,"say":"cu a"},{"start":34,"end":36,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Cómo se está reorientando el rol SDET en el mundo de la IA.","start":3.897,"end":7.081,"terms":[{"start":33,"end":37,"say":"ese de e te"},{"start":56,"end":58,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Qué cambia y qué no en el rol SDET, y cómo armar tu matriz de habilidades para los próximos 30 días.","start":7.631,"end":12.777,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":34,"say":"ese de e te"}]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":13.577,"end":33.993,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"La pregunta que más se repite en la comunidad de testing es si la IA va a reemplazar al SDET.","start":14.077,"end":18.688,"terms":[{"start":66,"end":68,"say":"i a"},{"start":88,"end":92,"say":"ese de e te"}]},{"text":"Es una pregunta mal planteada: el trabajo no desaparece, cambia de lugar.","start":18.988,"end":22.846,"terms":[]},{"text":"Escribir un test dejó de ser la parte escasa, porque un agente lo hace en segundos.","start":23.146,"end":27.165,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lo escaso ahora es saber qué vale la pena probar, darle contexto a quien lo escribe y poder afirmar que el resultado es confiable.","start":27.465,"end":33.993,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":34.793,"end":47.092,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Esta guía describe ese corrimiento con cuatro competencias nuevas y tres que no cambian, y termina con una matriz para medirte sin autoengaño.","start":35.293,"end":41.843,"terms":[]},{"text":"No es una lista de herramientas de moda: es un mapa de habilidades con evidencia verificable para cada una.","start":42.143,"end":47.092,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":47.892,"end":57.796,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":48.392,"end":50.322,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Una hoja de cálculo o una página de notas.","start":50.872,"end":53.301,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tu descripción de puesto actual.","start":53.851,"end":55.537,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"30 minutos de honestidad.","start":56.087,"end":57.796,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":58.596,"end":95.19,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":59.096,"end":59.993,"terms":[]},{"text":"Antes y ahora, por escrito.","start":60.293,"end":61.967,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Escribí qué hacías hace dos años y qué hacés hoy.","start":62.517,"end":65.248,"terms":[]},{"text":"El cambio de fondo: de escribir cada test a diseñar, guiar, revisar y validar sistemas que testean.","start":65.548,"end":70.787,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tomá tu descripción de puesto y tus últimas diez tareas.","start":71.337,"end":74.207,"terms":[]},{"text":"Para cada una preguntate qué parte fue producir (escribir código de test, datos, reportes) y qué parte fue decidir (qué cubrir, qué riesgo aceptar, qué resultado creer).","start":74.507,"end":82.985,"terms":[]},{"text":"Hace dos años la mayor parte del tiempo se iba en producir.","start":83.285,"end":86.55,"terms":[]},{"text":"Hoy esa parte se abarata y la de decidir pesa más.","start":86.85,"end":89.616,"terms":[]},{"text":"Verlo escrito con tus propias tareas evita discutir en abstracto y te muestra cuánto de tu semana ya cambió.","start":89.916,"end":95.19,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":95.99,"end":137.772,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":96.49,"end":97.386,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las cuatro competencias nuevas.","start":97.686,"end":99.593,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Sumá las cuatro competencias nuevas: contexto para agentes (reglas, skills, specs), evals para salidas no deterministas, revisión de tests generados por IA, y costo e infraestructura (tokens, CI, runners).","start":100.143,"end":111.024,"terms":[{"start":153,"end":155,"say":"i a"},{"start":192,"end":194,"say":"ci ai"},{"start":196,"end":203,"say":"ráners"}]}],[{"text":"Contexto para agentes es escribir reglas, convenciones y especificaciones que un agente pueda seguir sin vos al lado.","start":111.574,"end":117.254,"terms":[]},{"text":"Evals es medir salidas que no son iguales dos veces, con datasets y umbrales.","start":117.554,"end":121.574,"terms":[]},{"text":"Revisión de tests generados es detectar relleno: aserciones débiles, casos duplicados, mocks de lo que se debería probar.","start":121.874,"end":127.89,"terms":[]},{"text":"Costo e infraestructura es saber cuánto cuesta cada corrida en tokens y minutos de CI, y dónde corre cada cosa.","start":128.19,"end":133.998,"terms":[{"start":83,"end":85,"say":"ci ai"}]},{"text":"Ninguna reemplaza a la automatización clásica: se apoyan en ella.","start":134.298,"end":137.772,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":138.572,"end":174.62,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":139.072,"end":139.969,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lo que no cambia.","start":140.269,"end":141.374,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Marcá lo que no cambia: pensar en riesgo, diseñar buenos casos y conocer el sistema.","start":141.924,"end":146.28,"terms":[]},{"text":"Eso es lo que la IA no trae.","start":146.58,"end":148.29,"terms":[{"start":17,"end":19,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Pensar en riesgo es decidir dónde un fallo le duele al negocio y concentrar ahí el esfuerzo.","start":148.84,"end":153.498,"terms":[]},{"text":"Diseñar buenos casos es conocer técnicas como particiones, bordes y tablas de decisión, y saber cuándo aplicar cada una.","start":153.798,"end":160.302,"terms":[]},{"text":"Conocer el sistema es entender sus dependencias, sus datos y sus puntos frágiles.","start":160.602,"end":164.831,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un agente sin estas tres cosas genera cobertura numérica, no calidad.","start":165.131,"end":168.895,"terms":[]},{"text":"Son además las habilidades que te permiten revisar lo que el agente produce, así que su valor sube, no baja.","start":169.195,"end":174.62,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":175.42,"end":208.506,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":175.92,"end":176.921,"terms":[]},{"text":"La matriz de habilidades.","start":177.221,"end":178.686,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Armá la matriz: una fila por competencia, columnas Nivel hoy (0 a 3), Evidencia y Próximo paso.","start":179.236,"end":184.823,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Usá una fila por competencia: las cuatro nuevas y las tres que no cambian.","start":185.373,"end":189.184,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la columna de nivel poné un número de cero a tres, donde cero es no lo hice nunca, uno es lo probé, dos es lo uso en un proyecto real y tres es puedo enseñarlo.","start":189.484,"end":197.637,"terms":[]},{"text":"En evidencia, un enlace o un artefacto concreto: un repo, un pipeline, un reporte.","start":197.937,"end":202.467,"terms":[{"start":61,"end":69,"say":"páiplain"}]},{"text":"Si no hay evidencia, el nivel no puede ser mayor que uno.","start":202.767,"end":205.858,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esa regla es la que vuelve honesta a la matriz.","start":206.158,"end":208.506,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":209.306,"end":239.164,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":209.806,"end":210.807,"terms":[]},{"text":"Dos focos, no siete.","start":211.107,"end":212.654,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Elegí dos competencias con nivel 0 o 1 y atales un día de esta serie.","start":213.204,"end":217.235,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Mejorar todo a la vez no funciona.","start":217.785,"end":219.854,"terms":[]},{"text":"Elegí las dos filas con nivel más bajo que además importen para tu trabajo actual o para el que buscás.","start":220.154,"end":225.045,"terms":[]},{"text":"A cada una asignale un día de esta serie y una fecha: por ejemplo, evals con el día ocho y costo e infraestructura con el día veintinueve.","start":225.345,"end":232.406,"terms":[]},{"text":"El próximo paso tiene que ser un entregable chico y visible, como un eval harness con diez casos o un job que publique tokens por corrida.","start":232.706,"end":239.164,"terms":[{"start":74,"end":81,"say":"járnes"},{"start":102,"end":105,"say":"yob"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":239.964,"end":271.68,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":240.464,"end":241.43,"terms":[]},{"text":"Evidencia en tu perfil.","start":241.73,"end":243.115,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Actualizá tu perfil con evidencia concreta: un repo con evals vale más que \"experiencia con IA\".","start":243.665,"end":248.346,"terms":[{"start":92,"end":94,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Un perfil que dice \"experiencia con IA aplicada a testing\" no se distingue de otros mil.","start":248.896,"end":253.125,"terms":[{"start":36,"end":38,"say":"i a"}]},{"text":"Uno que enlaza un repositorio con un eval harness, un pipeline con un quality gate y un caso escrito de triage sí.","start":253.425,"end":258.93,"terms":[{"start":42,"end":49,"say":"járnes"},{"start":54,"end":62,"say":"páiplain"},{"start":78,"end":82,"say":"gueit"},{"start":104,"end":110,"say":"triásh"}]},{"text":"Cuando termines cada entregable, sumalo con una frase que diga qué mide y qué decisión permitió tomar.","start":259.23,"end":264.144,"terms":[]},{"text":"En entrevistas, esa evidencia te deja hablar de experiencia propia en lugar de repetir teoría, y eso es lo que un entrevistador técnico busca.","start":264.444,"end":271.68,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":272.48,"end":307.419,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":272.98,"end":273.9,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un SDET con seis años de experiencia completa la matriz.","start":274.45,"end":277.668,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":7,"say":"ese de e te"}]},{"text":"En diseño de casos y conocimiento del sistema se pone tres, con evidencia de sobra.","start":277.968,"end":282.313,"terms":[]},{"text":"En contexto para agentes se pone uno: usó un asistente, pero nunca escribió reglas para el repo.","start":282.613,"end":287.422,"terms":[]},{"text":"En evals, cero.","start":287.722,"end":288.944,"terms":[]},{"text":"En revisión de tests generados, uno.","start":289.244,"end":291.371,"terms":[]},{"text":"En costo, cero.","start":291.671,"end":292.858,"terms":[]},{"text":"Elige evals y contexto para agentes como focos.","start":293.158,"end":295.9,"terms":[]},{"text":"En tres semanas arma un AGENTS.md para su repo de automation y un eval de diez casos para el clasificador de bugs del equipo.","start":296.2,"end":302.565,"terms":[{"start":24,"end":30,"say":"éiyents"},{"start":50,"end":60,"say":"otoméishon"},{"start":109,"end":113,"say":"bags"}]},{"text":"Su perfil pasa de una frase genérica a dos enlaces con una línea de explicación cada uno.","start":302.865,"end":307.419,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":308.219,"end":325.363,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":308.719,"end":310.243,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ponerte nivel alto sin un artefacto que lo respalde.","start":310.793,"end":313.489,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Elegir como foco la herramienta de moda en lugar de la competencia que te falta.","start":314.039,"end":317.861,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Abandonar diseño de casos y riesgo porque \"eso lo hace la IA\".","start":318.411,"end":321.583,"terms":[{"start":58,"end":60,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Planear siete mejoras en paralelo y no terminar ninguna.","start":322.133,"end":325.363,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":326.163,"end":342.843,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":326.663,"end":328.93,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"La matriz tiene siete filas con nivel, evidencia y próximo paso.","start":329.48,"end":333.082,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ningún nivel mayor a uno carece de evidencia enlazada.","start":333.632,"end":336.653,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Elegiste dos focos con fecha y entregable.","start":337.203,"end":339.516,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tu perfil enlaza al menos un artefacto verificable.","start":340.066,"end":342.843,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":343.643,"end":352.199,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":344.143,"end":345.713,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Le pedí a un modelo que evaluara mi nivel y me puso todo en \"avanzado\".","start":346.263,"end":349.633,"terms":[]},{"text":"No sirve para autoevaluarte: te halaga.","start":349.933,"end":352.199,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":352.999,"end":364.69,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 2.","start":353.499,"end":354.768,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":355.068,"end":360.306,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 3: Las nuevas formas de probar: de asserts a evals.","start":360.856,"end":364.69,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d02-en","day":2,"lang":"en","title":"How the SDET role is being reshaped by AI","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d02-en.mp4","duration":411.121,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":14.541,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 2 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.009,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"How the SDET role is being reshaped by AI.","start":4.559,"end":7.824,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":12,"say":"ess det"}]}],[{"text":"What changes and what doesn't in the SDET role, and how to build your skills matrix for the next 30 days.","start":8.374,"end":14.541,"terms":[{"start":37,"end":41,"say":"ess det"}]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":15.341,"end":38.288,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most repeated question in the testing community is whether AI will replace the SDET.","start":15.841,"end":21.127,"terms":[{"start":83,"end":87,"say":"ess det"}]},{"text":"It's the wrong question: the work doesn't disappear, it moves.","start":21.427,"end":25.319,"terms":[]},{"text":"Writing a test stopped being the scarce part, because an agent does it in seconds.","start":25.619,"end":30.37,"terms":[]},{"text":"What's scarce now is knowing what is worth testing, giving context to whoever writes it and being able to state that the result is trustworthy.","start":30.67,"end":38.288,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":39.088,"end":53.21,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This guide describes that shift with four new competencies and three that don't change, and ends with a matrix to measure yourself without self-deception.","start":39.588,"end":47.881,"terms":[]},{"text":"It isn't a list of trendy tools: it's a skills map with verifiable evidence for each one.","start":48.181,"end":53.21,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":54.01,"end":63.45,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":54.51,"end":56.208,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A spreadsheet or a notes page.","start":56.758,"end":58.862,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Your current job description.","start":59.412,"end":61.319,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"30 honest minutes.","start":61.869,"end":63.45,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":64.25,"end":105.975,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":64.75,"end":65.786,"terms":[]},{"text":"Before and now, in writing.","start":66.086,"end":68.167,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Write what you did two years ago and what you do today.","start":68.717,"end":72.121,"terms":[]},{"text":"The core shift: from writing every test to designing, guiding, reviewing and validating systems that test.","start":72.421,"end":78.392,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Take your job description and your last ten tasks.","start":78.942,"end":82.021,"terms":[]},{"text":"For each one, ask which part was producing (writing test code, data, reports) and which part was deciding (what to cover, what risk to accept, which result to believe).","start":82.321,"end":92.622,"terms":[]},{"text":"Two years ago most of the time went into producing.","start":92.922,"end":95.815,"terms":[]},{"text":"Today that part gets cheaper and deciding weighs more.","start":96.115,"end":99.125,"terms":[]},{"text":"Seeing it written with your own tasks avoids arguing in the abstract and shows how much of your week has already changed.","start":99.425,"end":105.975,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":106.775,"end":154.27,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":107.275,"end":108.474,"terms":[]},{"text":"The four new competencies.","start":108.774,"end":110.622,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Add the four new competencies: context for agents (rules, skills, specs), evals for non-deterministic outputs, reviewing AI-generated tests, and cost and infrastructure (tokens, CI, runners).","start":111.172,"end":124.109,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Context for agents is writing rules, conventions and specifications an agent can follow without you beside it.","start":124.659,"end":131.047,"terms":[]},{"text":"Evals is measuring outputs that are never the same twice, with datasets and thresholds.","start":131.347,"end":136.562,"terms":[]},{"text":"Reviewing generated tests is spotting filler: weak assertions, duplicated cases, mocks of what should be tested.","start":136.862,"end":144.051,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cost and infrastructure is knowing what each run costs in tokens and CI minutes, and where each thing runs.","start":144.351,"end":150.647,"terms":[]},{"text":"None replaces classic automation: they build on it.","start":150.947,"end":154.27,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":155.07,"end":194.96,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":155.57,"end":156.652,"terms":[]},{"text":"What doesn't change.","start":156.952,"end":158.452,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Flag what doesn't change: risk thinking, good test design and knowing the system.","start":159.002,"end":163.893,"terms":[]},{"text":"That is what AI doesn't bring.","start":164.193,"end":166.413,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Risk thinking is deciding where a failure hurts the business and focusing effort there.","start":166.963,"end":171.621,"terms":[]},{"text":"Good test design is knowing techniques such as partitions, boundaries and decision tables, and when to apply each.","start":171.921,"end":178.321,"terms":[]},{"text":"Knowing the system is understanding its dependencies, its data and its fragile points.","start":178.621,"end":183.697,"terms":[]},{"text":"An agent without these three produces numeric coverage, not quality.","start":183.997,"end":188.284,"terms":[]},{"text":"They are also the skills that let you review what the agent produces, so their value goes up, not down.","start":188.584,"end":194.96,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":195.76,"end":231.9,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":196.26,"end":197.273,"terms":[]},{"text":"The skills matrix.","start":197.573,"end":199.062,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Build the matrix: one row per competency, columns Level today (0 to 3), Evidence and Next step.","start":199.612,"end":206.046,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Use one row per competency: the four new ones and the three unchanged.","start":206.596,"end":210.825,"terms":[]},{"text":"In the level column put a number from zero to three, where zero is never done it, one is tried it, two is use it on a real project and three is can teach it.","start":211.125,"end":220.032,"terms":[]},{"text":"Under evidence, a link or a concrete artifact: a repo, a pipeline, a report.","start":220.332,"end":225.641,"terms":[]},{"text":"With no evidence, the level can't be above one.","start":225.941,"end":228.834,"terms":[]},{"text":"That rule is what makes the matrix honest.","start":229.134,"end":231.9,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":232.7,"end":266.308,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":233.2,"end":234.329,"terms":[]},{"text":"Two focus areas, not seven.","start":234.629,"end":236.884,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pick two competencies at level 0 or 1 and tie each to a day of this series.","start":237.434,"end":242.475,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Improving everything at once doesn't work.","start":243.025,"end":245.42,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pick the two lowest rows that also matter for your current job or the one you're after.","start":245.72,"end":250.204,"terms":[]},{"text":"Assign each a day from this series and a date: for example, evals with day eight and cost and infrastructure with day twenty-nine.","start":250.504,"end":258.006,"terms":[]},{"text":"The next step must be a small, visible deliverable, such as an eval harness with ten cases or a job that publishes tokens per run.","start":258.306,"end":266.308,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":267.108,"end":304.966,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":267.608,"end":268.795,"terms":[]},{"text":"Evidence on your profile.","start":269.095,"end":270.793,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Update your profile with concrete evidence: a repo with evals beats \"experience with AI\".","start":271.343,"end":276.918,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A profile saying \"experience with AI applied to testing\" is indistinguishable from a thousand others.","start":277.468,"end":283.531,"terms":[]},{"text":"One that links a repository with an eval harness, a pipeline with a quality gate and a written triage case is not.","start":283.831,"end":290.034,"terms":[]},{"text":"When you finish each deliverable, add it with one sentence saying what it measures and which decision it enabled.","start":290.334,"end":296.582,"terms":[]},{"text":"In interviews, that evidence lets you speak from your own experience instead of repeating theory, which is what a technical interviewer looks for.","start":296.882,"end":304.966,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":305.766,"end":346.185,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":306.266,"end":307.371,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"An SDET with six years of experience fills in the matrix.","start":307.921,"end":311.442,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":7,"say":"ess det"}]},{"text":"For test design and system knowledge he gives himself a three, with plenty of evidence.","start":311.742,"end":316.435,"terms":[]},{"text":"For context for agents, a one: he has used an assistant but never wrote rules for the repo.","start":316.735,"end":322.635,"terms":[]},{"text":"For evals, zero.","start":322.935,"end":324.575,"terms":[]},{"text":"For reviewing generated tests, one.","start":324.875,"end":327.385,"terms":[]},{"text":"For cost, zero.","start":327.685,"end":329.336,"terms":[]},{"text":"He picks evals and context for agents as focus areas.","start":329.636,"end":333.215,"terms":[]},{"text":"In three weeks he builds an AGENTS.md for his automation repo and a ten-case eval for the team's bug classifier.","start":333.515,"end":340.762,"terms":[]},{"text":"His profile goes from one generic sentence to two links with one line of explanation each.","start":341.062,"end":346.185,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":346.985,"end":365.197,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":347.485,"end":349.148,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Giving yourself a high level with no artifact to back it.","start":349.698,"end":352.696,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Picking the trendy tool as a focus instead of the competency you lack.","start":353.246,"end":356.778,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Dropping test design and risk because \"AI does that\".","start":357.328,"end":361.138,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Planning seven improvements in parallel and finishing none.","start":361.688,"end":365.197,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":365.997,"end":384.012,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":366.497,"end":368.741,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The matrix has seven rows with level, evidence and next step.","start":369.291,"end":373.125,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"No level above one lacks linked evidence.","start":373.675,"end":376.208,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"You picked two focus areas with a date and a deliverable.","start":376.758,"end":380.104,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Your profile links at least one verifiable artifact.","start":380.654,"end":384.012,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":384.812,"end":395.435,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":385.312,"end":387.207,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"I asked a model to rate my level and it marked everything \"advanced\".","start":387.757,"end":391.673,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's useless for self-assessment: it flatters you.","start":391.973,"end":395.435,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":396.235,"end":409.621,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 2.","start":396.735,"end":398.177,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":398.477,"end":404.459,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 3: The new ways of testing: from asserts to evals.","start":405.009,"end":409.621,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d03-es","day":3,"lang":"es","title":"Las nuevas formas de probar: de asserts a evals","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d03-es.mp4","duration":361.657,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":12.15,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 3 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.452,"terms":[{"start":29,"end":31,"say":"cu a"},{"start":34,"end":36,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Las nuevas formas de probar: de asserts a evals.","start":4.002,"end":6.628,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cómo convertir un test con assert exacto sobre una salida de IA en un eval de tres capas que no sea frágil.","start":7.178,"end":12.15,"terms":[{"start":61,"end":63,"say":"i a"}]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":12.95,"end":31.334,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Cuando una funcionalidad incluye un modelo, el test clásico deja de servir tal como está.","start":13.45,"end":17.922,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un assert que compara contra un texto exacto falla aunque la respuesta sea correcta, porque el modelo la redacta distinto cada vez.","start":18.222,"end":24.192,"terms":[]},{"text":"La reacción habitual es marcar el test como flaky, agregarle reintentos y seguir.","start":24.492,"end":28.651,"terms":[{"start":44,"end":49,"say":"fléiki"}]},{"text":"Eso tapa el problema: el test dejó de medir.","start":28.951,"end":31.334,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":32.134,"end":49.479,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"La salida no es abandonar la automatización sino cambiar la pregunta.","start":32.634,"end":36.247,"terms":[]},{"text":"En lugar de \"¿es igual a esto?\", preguntamos \"¿cumple estos criterios, la mayoría de las veces?\".","start":36.547,"end":41.757,"terms":[]},{"text":"Eso es un eval.","start":42.057,"end":43.232,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta guía muestra cómo armarlo en tres capas, de la más barata y determinista a la más cara, y cómo decidir un umbral.","start":43.532,"end":49.479,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":50.279,"end":60.636,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":50.779,"end":52.709,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Node.js 22 o superior.","start":53.259,"end":55.2,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":7,"say":"noud yei es"}]}],[{"text":"Un test tuyo que compare texto exacto.","start":55.75,"end":58.04,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Opcional: Ollama con un modelo local.","start":58.59,"end":60.636,"terms":[{"start":10,"end":16,"say":"olama"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":61.436,"end":66.757,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":61.936,"end":63.111,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":63.411,"end":66.757,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":67.557,"end":104.474,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":68.057,"end":68.954,"terms":[]},{"text":"Assert contra eval.","start":69.254,"end":70.65,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Entendé la diferencia: un assert compara contra un valor exacto; un eval mide si la salida cumple criterios, varias veces, y da un porcentaje.","start":71.2,"end":78.761,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un assert tiene una entrada, una salida esperada y un resultado binario.","start":79.311,"end":83.028,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un eval tiene un conjunto de entradas, criterios en lugar de una salida única, varias corridas por entrada y un resultado en porcentaje que se compara contra un umbral.","start":83.328,"end":91.214,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cambia también cómo se lee un fallo: en un assert, rojo significa bug; en un eval, una corrida mala puede ser ruido y cinco seguidas son una regresión.","start":91.514,"end":99.156,"terms":[{"start":66,"end":69,"say":"bag"}]},{"text":"Por eso hace falta registrar la tasa de aprobación en el tiempo y no solo el último resultado.","start":99.456,"end":104.474,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":105.274,"end":137.733,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":105.774,"end":106.671,"terms":[]},{"text":"Capa uno: formato.","start":106.971,"end":108.286,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Capa 1, formato: validá estructura con checks deterministas (es JSON válido, tiene los campos, respeta el largo).","start":108.836,"end":115.061,"terms":[{"start":64,"end":68,"say":"yeison"}]}],[{"text":"Es la capa más barata y la que más atrapa.","start":115.611,"end":117.854,"terms":[]},{"text":"Verificá que la salida sea JSON válido, que tenga los campos obligatorios con el tipo correcto, que respete largos máximos y que no traiga texto fuera de la estructura.","start":118.154,"end":126.621,"terms":[{"start":27,"end":31,"say":"yeison"}]},{"text":"Todo eso se resuelve con un schema y sin llamar a ningún modelo.","start":126.921,"end":130.105,"terms":[{"start":28,"end":34,"say":"skima"}]},{"text":"Si esta capa falla, no tiene sentido evaluar las siguientes: una respuesta que tu aplicación no puede parsear ya es un defecto, diga lo que diga.","start":130.405,"end":137.733,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":138.533,"end":171.921,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":139.033,"end":139.93,"terms":[]},{"text":"Capa dos: contenido.","start":140.23,"end":141.637,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Capa 2, contenido: verificá hechos clave con contains o regex, sin exigir la frase exacta.","start":142.187,"end":147.066,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Acá verificás hechos, no redacción.","start":147.616,"end":150.045,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si la respuesta tiene que mencionar un monto, una fecha y un estado, buscá esos tres datos con contains o con una expresión regular.","start":150.345,"end":156.652,"terms":[]},{"text":"También sirve verificar lo contrario: que no aparezca información que no debería estar, como un dato de otro cliente o un paso inexistente.","start":156.952,"end":164.002,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta capa sigue siendo determinista y barata.","start":164.302,"end":166.859,"terms":[]},{"text":"La regla práctica es que todo dato duro se valida acá, nunca con la opinión de otro modelo.","start":167.159,"end":171.921,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":172.721,"end":205.633,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":173.221,"end":174.222,"terms":[]},{"text":"Capa tres: criterio.","start":174.522,"end":175.93,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Capa 3, criterio: usá una rúbrica evaluada por otro modelo para lo que no se puede expresar con reglas (tono, completitud).","start":176.48,"end":182.647,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Queda lo que no se puede expresar con reglas: si el tono es el adecuado, si la explicación está completa, si la respuesta realmente contesta lo que se preguntó.","start":183.197,"end":190.746,"terms":[]},{"text":"Para eso se usa una rúbrica que evalúa otro modelo, con criterios observables y resultado pasa o falla.","start":191.046,"end":196.61,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es la capa más cara y la menos confiable, así que conviene mantenerla chica.","start":196.91,"end":200.709,"terms":[]},{"text":"El día dieciséis muestra cómo calibrar ese juez contra tu propio criterio antes de creerle.","start":201.009,"end":205.633,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":206.433,"end":237.232,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":206.933,"end":207.934,"terms":[]},{"text":"Corridas y umbral.","start":208.234,"end":209.421,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Corré N veces y fijá un umbral: por ejemplo, pasa si 9 de 10 corridas cumplen las tres capas.","start":209.971,"end":214.826,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Corré cada caso varias veces: tres para trabajo diario, cinco o diez cuando vas a tomar una decisión.","start":215.376,"end":220.499,"terms":[]},{"text":"El umbral depende del riesgo.","start":220.799,"end":222.45,"terms":[]},{"text":"Para una capa de formato conviene exigir cien por ciento, porque un JSON roto rompe la aplicación.","start":222.75,"end":227.884,"terms":[{"start":68,"end":72,"say":"yeison"}]},{"text":"Para criterio, un noventa por ciento puede ser razonable.","start":228.184,"end":231.136,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lo importante es fijar el número antes de mirar los resultados y escribir por qué, para no acomodarlo después a lo que dio.","start":231.436,"end":237.232,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":238.032,"end":269.608,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":238.532,"end":239.498,"terms":[]},{"text":"El lugar en la pirámide.","start":239.798,"end":241.217,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ubicalo en tu pirámide: unit y contract siguen siendo deterministas; los evals son una capa más para todo lo que toca un modelo.","start":241.767,"end":248.063,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Los evals no reemplazan nada de lo que ya tenés.","start":248.613,"end":251.239,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los tests unitarios y de contrato siguen siendo deterministas y siguen corriendo en cada commit.","start":251.539,"end":256,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los evals son una capa adicional que cubre solo lo que toca un modelo, y suelen correr con otra frecuencia porque cuestan más.","start":256.3,"end":262.224,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un error común es intentar cubrir con evals lógica que no depende del modelo: eso se prueba mejor, más rápido y más barato con un test clásico.","start":262.524,"end":269.608,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":270.408,"end":303.756,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":270.908,"end":271.828,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un equipo tiene un test que compara el resumen de un pedido contra un texto fijo y falla una de cada tres corridas.","start":272.378,"end":277.675,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lo reescriben así.","start":277.975,"end":279.289,"terms":[]},{"text":"Capa uno: la salida es JSON con los campos resumen y estado, y el resumen no supera las 280 letras.","start":279.589,"end":285.327,"terms":[{"start":23,"end":27,"say":"yeison"}]},{"text":"Capa dos: el resumen contiene el número de pedido y el monto total.","start":285.627,"end":289.067,"terms":[]},{"text":"Capa tres: una rúbrica verifica que no agregue pasos que el pedido no tiene.","start":289.367,"end":293.27,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lo corren diez veces.","start":293.57,"end":295.059,"terms":[]},{"text":"La capa uno pasa diez de diez, la dos también, la tres nueve de diez.","start":295.359,"end":299.146,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con umbral de noventa por ciento, pasa, y la corrida fallida queda guardada para revisar.","start":299.446,"end":303.756,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":304.556,"end":320.377,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":305.056,"end":306.58,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tapar un assert exacto con reintentos en lugar de cambiar lo que se mide.","start":307.13,"end":310.708,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Usar un modelo juez para validar datos duros como montos o fechas.","start":311.258,"end":314.709,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Fijar el umbral después de ver los resultados.","start":315.259,"end":317.584,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Sacar conclusiones de una sola corrida.","start":318.134,"end":320.377,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":321.177,"end":337.195,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":321.677,"end":323.944,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El test original quedó reescrito en tres capas separadas.","start":324.494,"end":327.399,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Formato y contenido se validan sin llamar a un modelo.","start":327.949,"end":330.9,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Hay un umbral escrito con su justificación.","start":331.45,"end":333.949,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Guardaste y revisaste al menos una corrida fallida.","start":334.499,"end":337.195,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":337.995,"end":346.668,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":338.495,"end":340.065,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"El modelo juez aprobó una respuesta con un dato incorrecto.","start":340.615,"end":343.567,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los hechos se validan con reglas, no con otro modelo.","start":343.867,"end":346.668,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":347.468,"end":360.157,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 3.","start":347.968,"end":349.305,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":349.605,"end":354.844,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 4: Tu laboratorio de QA con IA: setup completo en 30 minutos.","start":355.394,"end":360.157,"terms":[{"start":38,"end":40,"say":"cu a"},{"start":45,"end":47,"say":"i a"},{"start":49,"end":54,"say":"sétap"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d03-en","day":3,"lang":"en","title":"The new ways of testing: from asserts to evals","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d03-en.mp4","duration":405.775,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":14.251,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 3 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.02,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The new ways of testing: from asserts to evals.","start":4.57,"end":7.859,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"How to turn a test with an exact assert on an AI output into a three-layer eval that isn't brittle.","start":8.409,"end":14.251,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":15.051,"end":35.189,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"When a feature includes a model, the classic test stops working as is.","start":15.551,"end":19.64,"terms":[]},{"text":"An assert comparing against exact text fails even when the answer is right, because the model words it differently every time.","start":19.94,"end":26.863,"terms":[]},{"text":"The usual reaction is to mark the test as flaky, add retries and move on.","start":27.163,"end":31.67,"terms":[]},{"text":"That hides the problem: the test stopped measuring.","start":31.97,"end":35.189,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":35.989,"end":55.446,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The way out isn't abandoning automation but changing the question.","start":36.489,"end":40.264,"terms":[]},{"text":"Instead of \"is it equal to this?\", we ask \"does it meet these criteria, most of the time?\".","start":40.564,"end":45.971,"terms":[]},{"text":"That is an eval.","start":46.271,"end":47.423,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide shows how to build one in three layers, from the cheapest and most deterministic to the most expensive, and how to choose a threshold.","start":47.723,"end":55.446,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":56.246,"end":67.648,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":56.746,"end":58.444,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Node.js 22 or later.","start":58.994,"end":61.098,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"One of your tests that compares exact text.","start":61.648,"end":64.611,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Optional: Ollama with a local model.","start":65.161,"end":67.648,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":68.448,"end":74.745,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":68.948,"end":70.228,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":70.528,"end":74.745,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":75.545,"end":117.036,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":76.045,"end":77.081,"terms":[]},{"text":"Assert versus eval.","start":77.381,"end":78.997,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Understand the difference: an assert compares against an exact value; an eval measures whether the output meets criteria, several times, and yields a percentage.","start":79.547,"end":89.233,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"An assert has one input, one expected output and a binary result.","start":89.783,"end":94.186,"terms":[]},{"text":"An eval has a set of inputs, criteria instead of a single output, several runs per input and a percentage result compared against a threshold.","start":94.486,"end":102.824,"terms":[]},{"text":"How you read a failure changes too: in an assert, red means bug; in an eval, one bad run may be noise and five in a row are a regression.","start":103.124,"end":111.718,"terms":[]},{"text":"That is why you need to record the pass rate over time and not just the latest result.","start":112.018,"end":117.036,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":117.836,"end":154.951,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":118.336,"end":119.535,"terms":[]},{"text":"Layer one: format.","start":119.835,"end":121.8,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Layer 1, format: validate structure with deterministic checks (valid JSON, required fields, length).","start":122.35,"end":129.33,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"It's the cheapest layer and the one that catches the most.","start":129.88,"end":133.052,"terms":[]},{"text":"Check that the output is valid JSON, that required fields exist with the right type, that maximum lengths hold and that no text sits outside the structure.","start":133.352,"end":142.515,"terms":[]},{"text":"All of that is solved with a schema and no model call.","start":142.815,"end":145.987,"terms":[]},{"text":"If this layer fails, evaluating the next ones is pointless: an answer your application can't parse is already a defect, whatever it says.","start":146.287,"end":154.951,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":155.751,"end":193.725,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":156.251,"end":157.333,"terms":[]},{"text":"Layer two: content.","start":157.633,"end":159.342,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Layer 2, content: verify key facts with contains or regex, without demanding the exact sentence.","start":159.892,"end":166.594,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Here you verify facts, not wording.","start":167.144,"end":169.666,"terms":[]},{"text":"If the answer must mention an amount, a date and a status, look for those three with contains or a regular expression.","start":169.966,"end":176.784,"terms":[]},{"text":"Checking the opposite also helps: that information which shouldn't be there doesn't appear, such as another customer's data or a nonexistent step.","start":177.084,"end":185.051,"terms":[]},{"text":"This layer is still deterministic and cheap.","start":185.351,"end":187.815,"terms":[]},{"text":"The practical rule is that every hard fact gets validated here, never by another model's opinion.","start":188.115,"end":193.725,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":194.525,"end":230.617,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":195.025,"end":196.038,"terms":[]},{"text":"Layer three: judgment.","start":196.338,"end":198.14,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Layer 3, judgment: use a rubric graded by another model for what rules can't express (tone, completeness).","start":198.69,"end":205.623,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"What remains is what rules can't express: whether the tone fits, whether the explanation is complete, whether the answer really addresses the question.","start":206.173,"end":214.884,"terms":[]},{"text":"For that you use a rubric graded by another model, with observable criteria and a pass or fail result.","start":215.184,"end":221.223,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's the most expensive and least reliable layer, so keep it small.","start":221.523,"end":225.659,"terms":[]},{"text":"Day sixteen shows how to calibrate that judge against your own judgment before trusting it.","start":225.959,"end":230.617,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":231.417,"end":266.512,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":231.917,"end":233.046,"terms":[]},{"text":"Runs and threshold.","start":233.346,"end":234.916,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Run N times and set a threshold: for example, pass if 9 of 10 runs satisfy all three layers.","start":235.466,"end":241.634,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Run each case several times: three for daily work, five or ten when you're about to make a decision.","start":242.184,"end":247.782,"terms":[]},{"text":"The threshold depends on risk.","start":248.082,"end":250.012,"terms":[]},{"text":"For the format layer, demand one hundred percent, because broken JSON breaks the application.","start":250.312,"end":256.039,"terms":[]},{"text":"For judgment, ninety percent can be reasonable.","start":256.339,"end":259.221,"terms":[]},{"text":"What matters is setting the number before looking at results and writing down why, so you don't adjust it afterwards to whatever came out.","start":259.521,"end":266.512,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":267.312,"end":303.393,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":267.812,"end":268.999,"terms":[]},{"text":"Its place in the pyramid.","start":269.299,"end":271.032,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Place it in your pyramid: unit and contract stay deterministic; evals are an extra layer for anything that touches a model.","start":271.582,"end":279.026,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Evals replace nothing you already have.","start":279.576,"end":282.122,"terms":[]},{"text":"Unit and contract tests stay deterministic and keep running on every commit.","start":282.422,"end":287.08,"terms":[]},{"text":"Evals are an extra layer covering only what touches a model, and they usually run at a different frequency because they cost more.","start":287.38,"end":294.906,"terms":[]},{"text":"A common mistake is trying to cover logic that doesn't depend on the model with evals: that is tested better, faster and cheaper with a classic test.","start":295.206,"end":303.393,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":304.193,"end":342.429,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":304.693,"end":305.799,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A team has a test comparing an order summary against fixed text, failing one run in three.","start":306.349,"end":311.901,"terms":[]},{"text":"They rewrite it like this.","start":312.201,"end":313.876,"terms":[]},{"text":"Layer one: the output is JSON with summary and status fields, and the summary stays within 280 characters.","start":314.176,"end":321.225,"terms":[]},{"text":"Layer two: the summary contains the order number and the total amount.","start":321.525,"end":325.394,"terms":[]},{"text":"Layer three: a rubric checks that it adds no steps the order doesn't have.","start":325.694,"end":330.283,"terms":[]},{"text":"They run it ten times.","start":330.583,"end":332.222,"terms":[]},{"text":"Layer one passes ten out of ten, so does layer two, layer three nine out of ten.","start":332.522,"end":337.262,"terms":[]},{"text":"With a ninety percent threshold it passes, and the failed run is saved for review.","start":337.562,"end":342.429,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":343.229,"end":360.966,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":343.729,"end":345.392,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Covering an exact assert with retries instead of changing what is measured.","start":345.942,"end":350.113,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Using a judge model to validate hard facts such as amounts or dates.","start":350.663,"end":354.996,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Setting the threshold after seeing the results.","start":355.546,"end":357.963,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Drawing conclusions from a single run.","start":358.513,"end":360.966,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":361.766,"end":379.525,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":362.266,"end":364.509,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The original test is rewritten into three separate layers.","start":365.059,"end":368.452,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Format and content are validated without calling a model.","start":369.002,"end":372.441,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"There is a written threshold with its justification.","start":372.991,"end":375.931,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"You saved and reviewed at least one failed run.","start":376.481,"end":379.525,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":380.325,"end":389.787,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":380.825,"end":382.72,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The judge model approved an answer with a wrong fact.","start":383.27,"end":386.106,"terms":[]},{"text":"Facts get validated with rules, not with another model.","start":386.406,"end":389.787,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":390.587,"end":404.275,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 3.","start":391.087,"end":392.541,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":392.841,"end":398.823,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 4: Your AI QA lab: full setup in 30 minutes.","start":399.373,"end":404.275,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d04-es","day":4,"lang":"es","title":"Tu laboratorio de QA con IA: setup completo en 30 minutos","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d04-es.mp4","duration":370.312,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":14.425,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 4 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.568,"terms":[{"start":29,"end":31,"say":"cu a"},{"start":34,"end":36,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Tu laboratorio de QA con IA: setup completo en 30 minutos.","start":4.118,"end":7.696,"terms":[{"start":18,"end":20,"say":"cu a"},{"start":25,"end":27,"say":"i a"},{"start":29,"end":34,"say":"sétap"}]}],[{"text":"El repo de laboratorio que se usa el resto de la serie: modelo local, herramienta de evals, Playwright y manejo de secretos.","start":8.246,"end":14.425,"terms":[{"start":92,"end":102,"say":"pléirait"}]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":15.225,"end":34.12,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"La mayoría de los tutoriales de IA aplicada a testing asumen que ya tenés un entorno funcionando, una API key con crédito y tiempo para pelear con versiones.","start":15.725,"end":23.553,"terms":[{"start":32,"end":34,"say":"i a"},{"start":106,"end":109,"say":"ki"}]},{"text":"En la práctica, ese arranque es donde mucha gente abandona.","start":23.853,"end":26.944,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta guía resuelve el punto de partida: un repositorio chico, reproducible y gratuito, que vas a reutilizar en todos los días que siguen.","start":27.244,"end":34.12,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":34.92,"end":52.56,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"La decisión central es trabajar con un modelo local.","start":35.42,"end":38.197,"terms":[]},{"text":"No es el más capaz, pero tiene tres ventajas para aprender: no cuesta nada, ningún dato sale de tu máquina y sus errores son frecuentes, lo cual es ideal para practicar cómo medirlos.","start":38.497,"end":47.776,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cuando necesites más calidad, cambiar a un modelo en la nube es una línea de configuración.","start":48.076,"end":52.56,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":53.36,"end":66.035,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":53.86,"end":55.79,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Node.js 22+ y Python 3.10+.","start":56.34,"end":59.292,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":7,"say":"noud yei es"},{"start":14,"end":20,"say":"páiton"}]}],[{"text":"Git.","start":59.842,"end":60.413,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":3,"say":"guit"}]}],[{"text":"Ollama, para correr un modelo local sin costo.","start":60.963,"end":63.427,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":6,"say":"olama"}]}],[{"text":"Opcional: API key de un proveedor.","start":63.977,"end":66.035,"terms":[{"start":14,"end":17,"say":"ki"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":66.835,"end":72.156,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":67.335,"end":68.51,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":68.81,"end":72.156,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":72.956,"end":75.595,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":73.456,"end":75.595,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":76.395,"end":79.034,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":76.895,"end":79.034,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":79.834,"end":112.665,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":80.334,"end":81.231,"terms":[]},{"text":"Versiones primero.","start":81.531,"end":82.799,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Verificá versiones de Node, Python y Git.","start":83.349,"end":85.592,"terms":[{"start":22,"end":26,"say":"noud"},{"start":28,"end":34,"say":"páiton"},{"start":37,"end":40,"say":"guit"}]},{"text":"Si Node es menor a 22, actualizá primero.","start":85.892,"end":88.681,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":7,"say":"noud"}]}],[{"text":"Corré los tres comandos de versión antes de instalar nada.","start":89.231,"end":91.997,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las herramientas de evals y los agentes de código piden versiones recientes de Node, y buena parte de los errores raros de instalación vienen de un Node viejo.","start":92.297,"end":99.219,"terms":[{"start":79,"end":83,"say":"noud"},{"start":148,"end":152,"say":"noud"}]},{"text":"Si usás un gestor de versiones, fijá la versión en un archivo como .nvmrc para que cualquiera que clone el repo use la misma.","start":99.519,"end":105.942,"terms":[]},{"text":"Python lo vas a necesitar más adelante para evals de RAG y para medir performance, así que conviene dejarlo verificado desde ahora.","start":106.242,"end":112.665,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":6,"say":"páiton"},{"start":70,"end":81,"say":"perfórmans"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":113.465,"end":147.689,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":113.965,"end":114.862,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un modelo local.","start":115.162,"end":116.244,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Instalá Ollama, bajá llama3.2 y probá que responde.","start":116.794,"end":120.187,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":14,"say":"olama"}]},{"text":"Con esto tenés un modelo gratis y sin mandar datos afuera.","start":120.487,"end":123.612,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ollama corre modelos en tu máquina y expone una API compatible con la de OpenAI en el puerto 11434.","start":124.162,"end":130.249,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":6,"say":"olama"},{"start":73,"end":79,"say":"open ei ai"}]},{"text":"Eso significa que casi cualquier herramienta que sepa hablar con un proveedor en la nube puede hablar con tu modelo local cambiando la URL.","start":130.549,"end":136.867,"terms":[{"start":135,"end":138,"say":"u erre ele"}]},{"text":"Un modelo chico alcanza para empezar y corre sin placa de video dedicada, más lento.","start":137.167,"end":141.431,"terms":[]},{"text":"Probá que responda desde la terminal antes de seguir: si ese paso falla, todo lo demás va a fallar con errores menos claros.","start":141.731,"end":147.689,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":148.489,"end":181.217,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":148.989,"end":149.886,"terms":[]},{"text":"Estructura del repo.","start":150.186,"end":151.547,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Creá el repo qa-ai-lab con las carpetas evals, tests, data y prompts.","start":152.097,"end":155.803,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cuatro carpetas con un propósito cada una.","start":156.353,"end":158.666,"terms":[]},{"text":"En evals van las configuraciones de evaluación.","start":158.966,"end":161.43,"terms":[]},{"text":"En tests, los tests de Playwright.","start":161.73,"end":163.706,"terms":[{"start":23,"end":33,"say":"pléirait"}]},{"text":"En data, los datasets y los schemas.","start":164.006,"end":166.226,"terms":[]},{"text":"En prompts, las plantillas de prompt como archivos de texto.","start":166.526,"end":169.687,"terms":[]},{"text":"Separar los prompts del código tiene una razón práctica: se pueden versionar, comparar entre versiones y revisar en un pull request igual que cualquier otro cambio.","start":169.987,"end":177.397,"terms":[{"start":119,"end":131,"say":"pul ricuést"}]},{"text":"Un prompt embebido en un string dentro del código no se revisa nunca.","start":177.697,"end":181.217,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":182.017,"end":215.892,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":182.517,"end":183.518,"terms":[]},{"text":"Dependencias de desarrollo.","start":183.818,"end":185.469,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Instalá promptfoo y Playwright como dependencias de desarrollo, así las versiones viajan con el repo.","start":186.019,"end":190.956,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":17,"say":"promptfú"},{"start":20,"end":30,"say":"pléirait"}]}],[{"text":"Instalá promptfoo y Playwright como dependencias de desarrollo del proyecto, no de forma global.","start":191.506,"end":196.13,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":17,"say":"promptfú"},{"start":20,"end":30,"say":"pléirait"}]},{"text":"Así las versiones quedan registradas en el lockfile y dos personas con el mismo repo obtienen el mismo resultado.","start":196.43,"end":202.005,"terms":[{"start":43,"end":51,"say":"lókfail"}]},{"text":"Con herramientas que cambian cada pocas semanas, una instalación global distinta en cada máquina es una fuente constante de diferencias inexplicables.","start":202.305,"end":209.471,"terms":[]},{"text":"Instalá solo Chromium por ahora: para lo que sigue no hacen falta los otros navegadores y la descarga es bastante más liviana.","start":209.771,"end":215.892,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":216.692,"end":247.7,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":217.192,"end":218.193,"terms":[]},{"text":"Secretos fuera del repo.","start":218.493,"end":219.959,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Secretos: las API keys van en .env, y .env va en .gitignore antes del primer commit.","start":220.509,"end":225.492,"terms":[{"start":18,"end":22,"say":"kis"}]}],[{"text":"Aunque hoy trabajes solo con un modelo local, dejá resuelto el manejo de secretos antes del primer commit.","start":226.042,"end":231.188,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las API keys van en un archivo .env que está en .gitignore.","start":231.488,"end":235.066,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":12,"say":"kis"}]},{"text":"Subí en cambio un .env.example con los nombres de las variables y sin valores, para documentar qué hace falta.","start":235.366,"end":241.07,"terms":[]},{"text":"Una key que llega a un repositorio, aunque sea privado, hay que darla por comprometida y rotarla; prevenirlo cuesta una línea.","start":241.37,"end":247.7,"terms":[{"start":4,"end":7,"say":"ki"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":248.5,"end":280.634,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":249,"end":249.966,"terms":[]},{"text":"El smoke eval.","start":250.266,"end":251.255,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":8,"say":"smouk"}]}],[{"text":"Corré el smoke eval.","start":251.805,"end":252.969,"terms":[{"start":9,"end":14,"say":"smouk"}]},{"text":"Si ves un caso en verde en la tabla y en el visor web, el laboratorio está listo.","start":253.269,"end":257.382,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El archivo de ejemplo tiene un prompt con una variable, un provider local y un test con un assert que no depende de mayúsculas.","start":257.932,"end":264.053,"terms":[{"start":59,"end":67,"say":"prováider"}]},{"text":"Correrlo verifica toda la cadena: la herramienta instalada, el modelo respondiendo y el assert evaluando.","start":264.353,"end":269.708,"terms":[]},{"text":"El visor web muestra cada caso con su entrada, su salida y el resultado.","start":270.008,"end":273.725,"terms":[]},{"text":"Dejalo como primer paso de cualquier sesión de trabajo: si el smoke eval falla, el problema es de entorno y no de lo que estés probando.","start":274.025,"end":280.634,"terms":[{"start":62,"end":67,"say":"smouk"}]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":281.434,"end":311.576,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":281.934,"end":282.854,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Una tester sigue los pasos en una notebook de trabajo sin placa de video.","start":283.404,"end":286.971,"terms":[]},{"text":"Node estaba en la versión 18 y la instalación de promptfoo mostraba advertencias; actualiza a la 22 y desaparecen.","start":287.271,"end":293.241,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":4,"say":"noud"},{"start":49,"end":58,"say":"promptfú"}]},{"text":"Baja el modelo local y la primera respuesta tarda varios segundos, lo cual es esperable en esa máquina.","start":293.541,"end":298.373,"terms":[]},{"text":"Crea el repo, agrega .env al .gitignore y corre el smoke eval: un caso en verde.","start":298.673,"end":303.297,"terms":[{"start":51,"end":56,"say":"smouk"}]},{"text":"Después agrega un segundo test con otro país y lo corre de nuevo.","start":303.597,"end":306.873,"terms":[]},{"text":"En media hora tiene un entorno que puede clonar en otra máquina y obtener el mismo resultado.","start":307.173,"end":311.576,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":312.376,"end":330.356,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":312.876,"end":314.4,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Instalar las herramientas de forma global y perder el control de versiones.","start":314.95,"end":318.575,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Hacer el primer commit antes de agregar .env al .gitignore.","start":319.125,"end":322.738,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Saltear la prueba del modelo en la terminal y depurar después errores confusos.","start":323.288,"end":327.261,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Dejar los prompts como strings dentro del código.","start":327.811,"end":330.356,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":331.156,"end":347,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":331.656,"end":333.923,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Node 22 o superior, Python y Git verificados.","start":334.473,"end":337.506,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":4,"say":"noud"},{"start":20,"end":26,"say":"páiton"},{"start":29,"end":32,"say":"guit"}]}],[{"text":"El modelo local responde desde la terminal.","start":338.056,"end":340.311,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El repo tiene las cuatro carpetas y .env ignorado.","start":340.861,"end":343.743,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El smoke eval muestra dos casos en verde en el visor.","start":344.293,"end":347,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":8,"say":"smouk"}]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":347.8,"end":355.555,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":348.3,"end":349.87,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Los asistentes sugieren flags y versiones viejas.","start":350.42,"end":353.128,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un comando no corrido es una hipótesis.","start":353.428,"end":355.555,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":356.355,"end":368.812,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 4.","start":356.855,"end":358.286,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":358.586,"end":363.825,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 5: Casos de prueba desde user stories: el prompt que sí funciona.","start":364.375,"end":368.812,"terms":[{"start":42,"end":54,"say":"iúser storis"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d04-en","day":4,"lang":"en","title":"Your AI QA lab: full setup in 30 minutes","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d04-en.mp4","duration":407.418,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":15.157,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 4 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.009,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Your AI QA lab: full setup in 30 minutes.","start":4.559,"end":7.882,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The lab repo used for the rest of the series: local model, eval tool, Playwright and secrets handling.","start":8.432,"end":15.157,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":15.957,"end":35.675,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Most tutorials on AI applied to testing assume you already have a working environment, an API key with credit and time to wrestle with versions.","start":16.457,"end":24.54,"terms":[]},{"text":"In practice, that start is where many people give up.","start":24.84,"end":28.047,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide solves the starting point: a small, reproducible, free repository you will reuse on every day that follows.","start":28.347,"end":35.675,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":36.475,"end":55.706,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The central decision is to work with a local model.","start":36.975,"end":39.753,"terms":[]},{"text":"It isn't the most capable, but it has three advantages for learning: it costs nothing, no data leaves your machine and its errors are frequent, which is ideal for practicing how to measure them.","start":40.053,"end":50.481,"terms":[]},{"text":"When you need more quality, switching to a cloud model is one configuration line.","start":50.781,"end":55.706,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":56.506,"end":71.352,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":57.006,"end":58.704,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Node.js 22+ and Python 3.10+.","start":59.254,"end":63.552,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Git.","start":64.102,"end":64.779,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ollama, to run a local model at no cost.","start":65.329,"end":68.292,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Optional: a provider API key.","start":68.842,"end":71.352,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":72.152,"end":78.449,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":72.652,"end":73.932,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":74.232,"end":78.449,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":79.249,"end":82.015,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":79.749,"end":82.015,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":82.815,"end":85.582,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":83.315,"end":85.582,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":86.382,"end":121.558,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":86.882,"end":87.918,"terms":[]},{"text":"Versions first.","start":88.218,"end":89.8,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Check Node, Python and Git versions.","start":90.35,"end":92.918,"terms":[]},{"text":"If Node is below 22, upgrade first.","start":93.218,"end":96.332,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Run the three version commands before installing anything.","start":96.882,"end":100.031,"terms":[]},{"text":"Eval tools and coding agents ask for recent Node versions, and a good share of odd install errors come from an old Node.","start":100.331,"end":107.323,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you use a version manager, pin the version in a file such as .nvmrc so anyone cloning the repo uses the same one.","start":107.623,"end":115.265,"terms":[]},{"text":"You will need Python later for RAG evals and for measuring performance, so it's worth verifying now.","start":115.565,"end":121.558,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":122.358,"end":163.432,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":122.858,"end":124.057,"terms":[]},{"text":"A local model.","start":124.357,"end":125.52,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Install Ollama, pull llama3.2 and confirm it replies.","start":126.07,"end":130.763,"terms":[]},{"text":"You now have a free model with no data leaving your machine.","start":131.063,"end":134.514,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ollama runs models on your machine and exposes an OpenAI-compatible API on port 11434.","start":135.064,"end":142.648,"terms":[]},{"text":"That means almost any tool that can talk to a cloud provider can talk to your local model by changing the URL.","start":142.948,"end":149.58,"terms":[]},{"text":"A small model is enough to start and runs without a dedicated graphics card, only slower.","start":149.88,"end":155.421,"terms":[]},{"text":"Confirm it replies from the terminal before moving on: if that step fails, everything else will fail with less clear errors.","start":155.721,"end":163.432,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":164.232,"end":200.501,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":164.732,"end":165.815,"terms":[]},{"text":"Repo structure.","start":166.115,"end":167.452,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Create the qa-ai-lab repo with evals, tests, data and prompts folders.","start":168.002,"end":173.16,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Four folders with one purpose each.","start":173.71,"end":175.837,"terms":[]},{"text":"evals holds evaluation configs.","start":176.137,"end":178.659,"terms":[]},{"text":"tests, the Playwright tests.","start":178.959,"end":181.04,"terms":[]},{"text":"data, datasets and schemas.","start":181.34,"end":183.699,"terms":[]},{"text":"prompts, prompt templates as text files.","start":183.999,"end":187.183,"terms":[]},{"text":"Keeping prompts apart from code has a practical reason: they can be versioned, compared across versions and reviewed in a pull request like any other change.","start":187.483,"end":196.472,"terms":[]},{"text":"A prompt embedded in a string inside code never gets reviewed.","start":196.772,"end":200.501,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":201.301,"end":236.117,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":201.801,"end":202.814,"terms":[]},{"text":"Development dependencies.","start":203.114,"end":204.951,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Install promptfoo and Playwright as dev dependencies so versions travel with the repo.","start":205.501,"end":211.065,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":17,"say":"prompt foo"}]}],[{"text":"Install promptfoo and Playwright as project dev dependencies, not globally.","start":211.615,"end":216.737,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":17,"say":"prompt foo"}]},{"text":"That way versions are recorded in the lockfile and two people with the same repo get the same result.","start":217.037,"end":222.462,"terms":[]},{"text":"With tools that change every few weeks, a different global install on each machine is a constant source of unexplained differences.","start":222.762,"end":230.137,"terms":[]},{"text":"Install only Chromium for now: what follows doesn't need the other browsers and the download is much lighter.","start":230.437,"end":236.117,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":236.917,"end":271.918,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":237.417,"end":238.546,"terms":[]},{"text":"Secrets out of the repo.","start":238.846,"end":240.416,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Secrets: API keys go in .env, and .env goes in .gitignore before the first commit.","start":240.966,"end":246.843,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Even if today you only work with a local model, settle secrets handling before the first commit.","start":247.393,"end":252.841,"terms":[]},{"text":"API keys go in a .env file listed in .gitignore.","start":253.141,"end":257.114,"terms":[]},{"text":"Commit a .env.example instead, with variable names and no values, to document what is needed.","start":257.414,"end":263.79,"terms":[]},{"text":"A key that reaches a repository, even a private one, must be considered compromised and rotated; preventing it costs one line.","start":264.09,"end":271.918,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":272.718,"end":307.337,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":273.218,"end":274.405,"terms":[]},{"text":"The smoke eval.","start":274.705,"end":275.869,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Run the smoke eval.","start":276.419,"end":277.745,"terms":[]},{"text":"One green case in the table and in the web viewer means the lab is ready.","start":278.045,"end":282.088,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The sample file has a prompt with one variable, a local provider and a test with a case-insensitive assert.","start":282.638,"end":288.91,"terms":[]},{"text":"Running it verifies the whole chain: the tool installed, the model answering and the assert evaluating.","start":289.21,"end":295.064,"terms":[]},{"text":"The web viewer shows each case with its input, output and result.","start":295.364,"end":299.453,"terms":[]},{"text":"Keep it as the first step of any work session: if the smoke eval fails, the problem is the environment and not whatever you are testing.","start":299.753,"end":307.337,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":308.137,"end":343.875,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":308.637,"end":309.743,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A tester follows the steps on a work laptop with no graphics card.","start":310.293,"end":314.347,"terms":[]},{"text":"Node was on version 18 and the promptfoo install showed warnings; she upgrades to 22 and they disappear.","start":314.647,"end":321.581,"terms":[{"start":31,"end":40,"say":"prompt foo"}]},{"text":"She pulls the local model and the first reply takes several seconds, which is expected on that machine.","start":321.881,"end":327.468,"terms":[]},{"text":"She creates the repo, adds .env to .gitignore and runs the smoke eval: one green case.","start":327.768,"end":334.353,"terms":[]},{"text":"Then she adds a second test with another country and runs it again.","start":334.653,"end":338.348,"terms":[]},{"text":"In half an hour she has an environment she can clone on another machine and get the same result.","start":338.648,"end":343.875,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":344.675,"end":363.039,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":345.175,"end":346.838,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Installing tools globally and losing control of versions.","start":347.388,"end":350.92,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Making the first commit before adding .env to .gitignore.","start":351.47,"end":355.258,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Skipping the terminal check of the model and debugging confusing errors later.","start":355.808,"end":359.816,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Leaving prompts as strings inside code.","start":360.366,"end":363.039,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":363.839,"end":381.018,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":364.339,"end":366.582,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Node 22 or later, Python and Git verified.","start":367.132,"end":370.478,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The local model replies from the terminal.","start":371.028,"end":373.539,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The repo has the four folders and .env ignored.","start":374.089,"end":377.342,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The smoke eval shows two green cases in the viewer.","start":377.892,"end":381.018,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":381.818,"end":391.024,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":382.318,"end":384.213,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Assistants suggest outdated flags and versions.","start":384.763,"end":388.028,"terms":[]},{"text":"A command you haven't run is a hypothesis.","start":388.328,"end":391.024,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":391.824,"end":405.918,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 4.","start":392.324,"end":393.65,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":393.95,"end":399.932,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 5: Test cases from user stories: the prompt that actually works.","start":400.482,"end":405.918,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d05-es","day":5,"lang":"es","title":"Casos de prueba desde user stories: el prompt que sí funciona","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d05-es.mp4","duration":368.103,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":12.823,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 5 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.544,"terms":[{"start":29,"end":31,"say":"cu a"},{"start":34,"end":36,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Casos de prueba desde user stories: el prompt que sí funciona.","start":4.094,"end":7.464,"terms":[{"start":22,"end":34,"say":"iúser storis"}]}],[{"text":"Una plantilla de prompt versionada que genera casos por técnica de diseño y te dice qué no cubrió.","start":8.014,"end":12.823,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":13.623,"end":29.928,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Pedirle a un modelo \"generame casos de prueba para esta historia\" devuelve siempre lo mismo: una lista larga, pareja, con casos repetidos y dos o tres reglas que nadie definió.","start":14.123,"end":22.497,"terms":[]},{"text":"No es un problema de capacidad del modelo.","start":22.797,"end":25.075,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es que el pedido no le dice contra qué reglas trabajar, qué técnica usar ni cuándo detenerse.","start":25.375,"end":29.928,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":30.728,"end":46.859,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Esta guía reemplaza ese pedido por un flujo de tres pasos que imita cómo trabaja un buen analista: primero pregunta, después diseña con técnica y al final declara qué dejó afuera.","start":31.228,"end":40.008,"terms":[]},{"text":"El resultado no es una lista más larga sino una más corta y defendible, donde cada caso tiene un motivo y vos sabés qué huecos quedan.","start":40.308,"end":46.859,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":47.659,"end":59.676,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":48.159,"end":50.089,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El repo qa-ai-lab del día 4.","start":50.639,"end":52.592,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Una user story con criterios de aceptación.","start":53.142,"end":55.78,"terms":[{"start":4,"end":14,"say":"iúser stori"}]}],[{"text":"Las reglas de negocio escritas, aunque sean cinco líneas.","start":56.33,"end":59.676,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":60.476,"end":65.798,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":60.976,"end":62.152,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":62.452,"end":65.798,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":66.598,"end":100.091,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":67.098,"end":67.994,"terms":[]},{"text":"Reglas de negocio como fuente de verdad.","start":68.294,"end":70.491,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Escribí las reglas de negocio primero.","start":71.041,"end":73.134,"terms":[]},{"text":"Son la fuente de verdad; sin eso el modelo las inventa.","start":73.434,"end":76.269,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Antes de abrir el chat, escribí las reglas que gobiernan la funcionalidad, aunque sean cinco líneas: límites, estados posibles, quién puede hacer qué.","start":76.819,"end":84.728,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si no existen por escrito, ese es tu primer hallazgo y vale más que cualquier caso.","start":85.028,"end":89.21,"terms":[]},{"text":"En el prompt, decí de forma explícita que esas reglas son la única fuente de verdad y que no debe inventar otras.","start":89.51,"end":95.086,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sin esa frase el modelo completa los huecos con lo que es habitual en otros sistemas, no en el tuyo.","start":95.386,"end":100.091,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":100.891,"end":133.338,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":101.391,"end":102.287,"terms":[]},{"text":"La plantilla en el repo.","start":102.587,"end":104.111,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Guardá la plantilla en prompts/test-design.md.","start":104.661,"end":107.833,"terms":[]},{"text":"Funciona igual si la traducís al español.","start":108.133,"end":110.492,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Guardá el prompt como archivo con variables para las reglas y la historia.","start":111.042,"end":114.54,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tenerlo versionado permite mejorarlo de a poco: cada vez que el resultado falla de una forma nueva, agregás una instrucción y queda para todo el equipo.","start":114.84,"end":122.087,"terms":[]},{"text":"También vuelve comparable el trabajo entre personas, porque todos parten del mismo pedido.","start":122.387,"end":126.883,"terms":[]},{"text":"Funciona igual en español; lo que importa es que las tres etapas estén separadas y que la primera termine con una orden de esperar.","start":127.183,"end":133.338,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":134.138,"end":167.597,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":134.638,"end":135.535,"terms":[]},{"text":"Paso uno: preguntas.","start":135.835,"end":137.335,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Paso 1: pedí solo ambigüedades y preguntas.","start":137.885,"end":140.489,"terms":[]},{"text":"Respondelas vos o el PO antes de seguir.","start":140.789,"end":142.962,"terms":[{"start":21,"end":23,"say":"pe o"}]}],[{"text":"Esta etapa es la que más valor entrega y la que casi todos saltean.","start":143.512,"end":146.951,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pedí ambigüedades, contradicciones entre criterios y datos faltantes, y nada más.","start":147.251,"end":151.631,"terms":[]},{"text":"Llevá las preguntas reales al product owner antes de seguir.","start":151.931,"end":154.894,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":43,"say":"próduct óuner"}]},{"text":"Cada ambigüedad resuelta acá es un defecto que no llega a escribirse.","start":155.194,"end":158.738,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si el modelo no encuentra ninguna pregunta en una historia que vos sabés incompleta, es una señal de que le falta contexto y de que los casos que genere después van a ser superficiales.","start":159.038,"end":167.597,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":168.397,"end":200.589,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":168.897,"end":169.898,"terms":[]},{"text":"Paso dos: casos por técnica.","start":170.198,"end":172.035,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Paso 2: pedí casos por técnica (particiones, bordes, tabla de decisión, estados de error) en tabla con riesgo.","start":172.585,"end":178.66,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Con las respuestas en mano, pedí casos nombrando las técnicas: particiones de equivalencia, valores de borde, tabla de decisión y estados de error.","start":179.21,"end":186.016,"terms":[]},{"text":"Exigí una tabla con la técnica de cada caso.","start":186.316,"end":188.838,"terms":[]},{"text":"Eso te permite ver de un vistazo si falta alguna: una tabla sin bordes o sin estados de error está incompleta.","start":189.138,"end":194.586,"terms":[]},{"text":"También reduce los duplicados, porque dos casos de la misma partición saltan a la vista cuando están etiquetados.","start":194.886,"end":200.589,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":201.389,"end":235.022,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":201.889,"end":202.89,"terms":[]},{"text":"Paso tres: lo que no cubrió.","start":203.19,"end":204.98,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Paso 3: pedí la lista de lo no cubierto.","start":205.53,"end":208.018,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es la parte más útil para tu revisión.","start":208.318,"end":210.456,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pedí una lista de lo que quedó afuera y por qué.","start":211.006,"end":213.436,"terms":[]},{"text":"Suelen aparecer concurrencia, accesibilidad, performance, permisos y migración de datos existentes.","start":213.736,"end":219.288,"terms":[{"start":45,"end":56,"say":"perfórmans"}]},{"text":"No es un defecto del resultado: es información para decidir.","start":219.588,"end":223.12,"terms":[]},{"text":"Algunas cosas las vas a cubrir con otro tipo de prueba y otras las vas a aceptar como riesgo, pero la decisión queda explícita.","start":223.42,"end":229.82,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta lista es además el mejor insumo para la conversación con el equipo sobre qué se prueba y qué no.","start":230.12,"end":235.022,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":235.822,"end":274.43,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":236.322,"end":237.288,"terms":[]},{"text":"La revisión con tres preguntas.","start":237.588,"end":239.483,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Revisá con tres preguntas: ¿hay casos duplicados?, ¿alguna regla inventada?, ¿faltan negativos?","start":240.033,"end":245.486,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lo que sobreviva va al gestor de casos.","start":245.786,"end":247.96,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Revisá la tabla buscando tres cosas.","start":248.51,"end":250.695,"terms":[]},{"text":"Duplicados: casos que ejercitan la misma partición con otro valor.","start":250.995,"end":254.527,"terms":[]},{"text":"Reglas inventadas: cualquier resultado esperado que no puedas rastrear a una regla escrita.","start":254.827,"end":259.207,"terms":[]},{"text":"Negativos faltantes: entradas inválidas, permisos insuficientes, estados imposibles.","start":259.507,"end":264.362,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es normal descartar un tercio.","start":264.662,"end":266.488,"terms":[]},{"text":"La prioridad también la ponés vos, porque el modelo tiende a marcar todo como riesgo alto.","start":266.788,"end":271.202,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lo que sobrevive pasa a tu gestor de casos con tu nombre.","start":271.502,"end":274.43,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":275.23,"end":310.354,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":275.73,"end":276.65,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"La historia dice: \"como cliente quiero transferir dinero a otra cuenta\".","start":277.2,"end":280.686,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las reglas escritas son cuatro: monto mínimo, tope diario, cuenta destino activa y saldo suficiente.","start":280.986,"end":286.19,"terms":[]},{"text":"En el paso uno el modelo pregunta qué pasa si el tope diario se alcanza a mitad de una transferencia programada y si el tope es por cliente o por cuenta; nadie lo había definido.","start":286.49,"end":294.689,"terms":[]},{"text":"En el paso dos devuelve veintidós casos etiquetados.","start":294.989,"end":297.848,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la revisión se descartan cinco duplicados de la partición \"monto válido\" y dos que asumían una comisión inexistente.","start":298.148,"end":303.549,"terms":[]},{"text":"Quedan quince.","start":303.849,"end":304.838,"terms":[]},{"text":"El paso tres declara que no cubrió concurrencia entre dos sesiones, y el equipo decide probarlo aparte.","start":305.138,"end":310.354,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":311.154,"end":327.323,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":311.654,"end":313.177,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pedir casos sin pasar las reglas de negocio.","start":313.727,"end":316.029,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Juntar los tres pasos en un solo pedido y perder la etapa de preguntas.","start":316.579,"end":320.239,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cargar los casos al gestor sin revisar duplicados ni reglas inventadas.","start":320.789,"end":324.53,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Aceptar la prioridad que asigna el modelo.","start":325.08,"end":327.323,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":328.123,"end":343.735,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":328.623,"end":330.89,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"La plantilla está versionada en prompts/.","start":331.44,"end":333.602,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Al menos una ambigüedad real llegó al product owner.","start":334.152,"end":336.732,"terms":[{"start":38,"end":51,"say":"próduct óuner"}]}],[{"text":"Cada caso final tiene técnica y se rastrea a una regla escrita.","start":337.282,"end":340.465,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Hay una lista de descartes con el motivo de cada uno.","start":341.015,"end":343.735,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":344.535,"end":352.592,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":345.035,"end":346.605,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Asigna riesgo alto a casi todo.","start":347.155,"end":349.12,"terms":[]},{"text":"Priorizar es una decisión de negocio, no de redacción.","start":349.42,"end":352.592,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":353.392,"end":366.603,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 5.","start":353.892,"end":355.264,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":355.564,"end":360.803,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 6: Datos de prueba sintéticos con LLM: realistas, válidos y sin PII.","start":361.353,"end":366.603,"terms":[{"start":81,"end":84,"say":"pe i i"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d05-en","day":5,"lang":"en","title":"Test cases from user stories: the prompt that actually works","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d05-en.mp4","duration":413.277,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":14.739,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 5 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.16,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Test cases from user stories: the prompt that actually works.","start":4.71,"end":8.59,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A versioned prompt template that generates cases by design technique and tells you what it didn't cover.","start":9.14,"end":14.739,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":15.539,"end":33.98,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Asking a model to \"generate test cases for this story\" always returns the same thing: a long, even list with repeated cases and two or three rules nobody defined.","start":16.039,"end":25.713,"terms":[]},{"text":"It isn't a model capability problem.","start":26.013,"end":28.395,"terms":[]},{"text":"The request doesn't say which rules to work against, which technique to use or when to stop.","start":28.695,"end":33.98,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":34.78,"end":51.84,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This guide replaces that request with a three-step flow that mirrors how a good analyst works: ask first, then design with technique and finally declare what was left out.","start":35.28,"end":44.652,"terms":[]},{"text":"The result isn't a longer list but a shorter, defensible one, where each case has a reason and you know which gaps remain.","start":44.952,"end":51.84,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":52.64,"end":64.75,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":53.14,"end":54.837,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The qa-ai-lab repo from day 4.","start":55.387,"end":58.014,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A user story with acceptance criteria.","start":58.564,"end":61.248,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Business rules in writing, even five lines.","start":61.798,"end":64.75,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":65.55,"end":71.847,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":66.05,"end":67.33,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":67.63,"end":71.847,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":72.647,"end":109.727,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":73.147,"end":74.183,"terms":[]},{"text":"Business rules as the source of truth.","start":74.483,"end":76.958,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Write the business rules first.","start":77.508,"end":79.542,"terms":[]},{"text":"They are the source of truth; without them the model invents some.","start":79.842,"end":83.595,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Before opening the chat, write the rules governing the feature, even if it's five lines: limits, possible states, who can do what.","start":84.145,"end":92.077,"terms":[]},{"text":"If they don't exist in writing, that's your first finding and it's worth more than any case.","start":92.377,"end":97.57,"terms":[]},{"text":"In the prompt, say explicitly that those rules are the only source of truth and that it must not invent others.","start":97.87,"end":104.2,"terms":[]},{"text":"Without that sentence the model fills gaps with what's common in other systems, not yours.","start":104.5,"end":109.727,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":110.527,"end":143.949,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":111.027,"end":112.225,"terms":[]},{"text":"The template in the repo.","start":112.525,"end":114.072,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Save the template in prompts/test-design.md.","start":114.622,"end":118.375,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Save the prompt as a file with variables for the rules and the story.","start":118.925,"end":122.863,"terms":[]},{"text":"Having it versioned lets you improve it gradually: every time the result fails in a new way, you add an instruction and the whole team gets it.","start":123.163,"end":131.061,"terms":[]},{"text":"It also makes work comparable across people, because everyone starts from the same request.","start":131.361,"end":136.727,"terms":[]},{"text":"It works the same in Spanish; what matters is that the three stages are separate and the first one ends with an order to wait.","start":137.027,"end":143.949,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":144.749,"end":184.373,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":145.249,"end":146.332,"terms":[]},{"text":"Step one: questions.","start":146.632,"end":148.55,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Step 1: ask only for ambiguities and questions.","start":149.1,"end":153.178,"terms":[]},{"text":"You or the PO answer them before moving on.","start":153.478,"end":156.092,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"This stage delivers the most value and almost everyone skips it.","start":156.642,"end":160.581,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ask for ambiguities, contradictions between criteria and missing data, and nothing else.","start":160.881,"end":166.874,"terms":[]},{"text":"Take the real questions to the product owner before continuing.","start":167.174,"end":170.59,"terms":[]},{"text":"Every ambiguity resolved here is a defect that never gets written.","start":170.89,"end":175.363,"terms":[]},{"text":"If the model finds no questions in a story you know is incomplete, that signals missing context, and the cases it generates afterwards will be shallow.","start":175.663,"end":184.373,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":185.173,"end":222.299,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":185.673,"end":186.685,"terms":[]},{"text":"Step two: cases by technique.","start":186.985,"end":189.333,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Step 2: ask for cases by technique (partitions, boundaries, decision table, error states) in a table with risk.","start":189.883,"end":197.653,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"With answers in hand, ask for cases naming the techniques: equivalence partitioning, boundary values, decision table and error states.","start":198.203,"end":206.367,"terms":[]},{"text":"Require a table with the technique for each case.","start":206.667,"end":209.874,"terms":[]},{"text":"That lets you see at a glance whether one is missing: a table with no boundaries or no error states is incomplete.","start":210.174,"end":216.156,"terms":[]},{"text":"It also reduces duplicates, because two cases from the same partition stand out when labeled.","start":216.456,"end":222.299,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":223.099,"end":260.749,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":223.599,"end":224.727,"terms":[]},{"text":"Step three: what it didn't cover.","start":225.027,"end":227.41,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Step 3: ask for the not-covered list.","start":227.96,"end":230.83,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's the most useful part for your review.","start":231.13,"end":233.769,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ask for a list of what was left out and why.","start":234.319,"end":237.409,"terms":[]},{"text":"Concurrency, accessibility, performance, permissions and migration of existing data usually show up.","start":237.709,"end":244.098,"terms":[]},{"text":"That isn't a flaw in the result: it's information for deciding.","start":244.398,"end":248.208,"terms":[]},{"text":"Some things you'll cover with another kind of test and others you'll accept as risk, but the decision becomes explicit.","start":248.508,"end":254.896,"terms":[]},{"text":"This list is also the best input for the team conversation about what gets tested and what doesn't.","start":255.196,"end":260.749,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":261.549,"end":305.068,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":262.049,"end":263.235,"terms":[]},{"text":"The three-question review.","start":263.535,"end":265.21,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Review with three questions: any duplicates? any invented rule? any missing negatives?","start":265.76,"end":271.526,"terms":[]},{"text":"Whatever survives goes to your test management tool.","start":271.826,"end":275.033,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Review the table looking for three things.","start":275.583,"end":277.966,"terms":[]},{"text":"Duplicates: cases exercising the same partition with another value.","start":278.266,"end":282.843,"terms":[]},{"text":"Invented rules: any expected result you can't trace to a written rule.","start":283.143,"end":287.615,"terms":[]},{"text":"Missing negatives: invalid inputs, insufficient permissions, impossible states.","start":287.915,"end":293.015,"terms":[]},{"text":"Discarding a third is normal.","start":293.315,"end":295.221,"terms":[]},{"text":"Priority is yours to set as well, because the model tends to mark everything as high risk.","start":295.521,"end":300.888,"terms":[]},{"text":"What survives goes into your test management tool under your name.","start":301.188,"end":305.068,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":305.868,"end":347.47,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":306.368,"end":307.474,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The story says: \"as a customer I want to transfer money to another account\".","start":308.024,"end":312.264,"terms":[]},{"text":"There are four written rules: minimum amount, daily cap, active destination account and sufficient balance.","start":312.564,"end":319.417,"terms":[]},{"text":"In step one the model asks what happens if the daily cap is reached in the middle of a scheduled transfer and whether the cap is per customer or per account; nobody had defined it.","start":319.717,"end":329.193,"terms":[]},{"text":"In step two it returns twenty-two labeled cases.","start":329.493,"end":332.77,"terms":[]},{"text":"In review, five duplicates from the \"valid amount\" partition and two assuming a nonexistent fee are discarded.","start":333.07,"end":339.121,"terms":[]},{"text":"Fifteen remain.","start":339.421,"end":340.654,"terms":[]},{"text":"Step three declares it didn't cover concurrency between two sessions, and the team decides to test that separately.","start":340.954,"end":347.47,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":348.27,"end":366.935,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":348.77,"end":350.433,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Asking for cases without passing the business rules.","start":350.983,"end":354.167,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Merging the three steps into one request and losing the questions stage.","start":354.717,"end":358.818,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Loading cases into the tool without checking duplicates or invented rules.","start":359.368,"end":363.643,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Accepting the priority the model assigns.","start":364.193,"end":366.935,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":367.735,"end":385.042,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":368.235,"end":370.479,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The template is versioned under prompts/.","start":371.029,"end":373.214,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"At least one real ambiguity reached the product owner.","start":373.764,"end":377.319,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each final case has a technique and traces to a written rule.","start":377.869,"end":381.506,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"There is a discard list with the reason for each.","start":382.056,"end":385.042,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":385.842,"end":395.327,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":386.342,"end":388.237,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"It assigns high risk to almost everything.","start":388.787,"end":391.472,"terms":[]},{"text":"Prioritizing is a business decision, not a writing task.","start":391.772,"end":395.327,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":396.127,"end":411.777,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 5.","start":396.627,"end":398.197,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":398.497,"end":404.479,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 6: Synthetic test data with LLMs: realistic, valid and PII-free.","start":405.029,"end":411.777,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d06-es","day":6,"lang":"es","title":"Datos de prueba sintéticos con LLM: realistas, válidos y sin PII","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d06-es.mp4","duration":390.745,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":15.482,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 6 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.463,"terms":[{"start":29,"end":31,"say":"cu a"},{"start":34,"end":36,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Datos de prueba sintéticos con LLM: realistas, válidos y sin PII.","start":4.013,"end":8.068,"terms":[{"start":61,"end":64,"say":"pe i i"}]}],[{"text":"Un dataset de prueba que combina Faker para volumen y un LLM para casos borde, validado contra un schema y sin datos de producción.","start":8.618,"end":15.482,"terms":[{"start":33,"end":38,"say":"féiker"},{"start":98,"end":104,"say":"skima"}]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":16.282,"end":36.384,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los datos de prueba son uno de los cuellos de botella más viejos de QA.","start":16.782,"end":20.221,"terms":[{"start":68,"end":70,"say":"cu a"}]},{"text":"O usás una copia de producción, con todo el riesgo legal que implica, o usás datos tan prolijos que nunca disparan un bug: nombres sin acentos, direcciones perfectas, montos redondos.","start":20.521,"end":29.881,"terms":[{"start":118,"end":121,"say":"bag"}]},{"text":"Los defectos reales aparecen con el apellido que tiene apóstrofo, el mail con subdominio o la nota que alguien completó con un emoji.","start":30.181,"end":36.384,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":37.184,"end":54.987,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un LLM es bueno justamente para eso: producir variedad realista.","start":37.684,"end":41.529,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pero también produce filas inválidas con mucha seguridad.","start":41.829,"end":44.885,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta guía combina tres piezas para quedarse con lo mejor de cada una: un schema que define qué es válido, Faker para el volumen y el modelo solo para los casos raros, con validación automática de cada fila.","start":45.185,"end":54.987,"terms":[{"start":73,"end":79,"say":"skima"},{"start":106,"end":111,"say":"féiker"}]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":55.787,"end":62.073,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":56.287,"end":58.217,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El repo qa-ai-lab.","start":58.767,"end":60.035,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un modelo local con Ollama.","start":60.585,"end":62.073,"terms":[{"start":20,"end":26,"say":"olama"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":62.873,"end":68.195,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":63.373,"end":64.549,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":64.849,"end":68.195,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":68.995,"end":71.634,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":69.495,"end":71.634,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":72.434,"end":75.073,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":72.934,"end":75.073,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":75.873,"end":110.724,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":76.373,"end":77.269,"terms":[]},{"text":"El schema manda.","start":77.569,"end":78.71,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":9,"say":"skima"}]}],[{"text":"Definí el schema primero.","start":79.26,"end":80.83,"terms":[{"start":10,"end":16,"say":"skima"}]},{"text":"Es la fuente de verdad de qué es un dato válido.","start":81.13,"end":83.663,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Escribí el schema antes de generar una sola fila.","start":84.213,"end":86.735,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":17,"say":"skima"}]},{"text":"Es el contrato de tus datos: qué campos existen, de qué tipo son, qué rangos aceptan y cuáles son obligatorios.","start":87.035,"end":93.04,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con Zod o JSON Schema lo expresás una vez y lo usás en tres lugares: para validar lo que produce Faker, para validar lo que produce el modelo y como documentación para el equipo.","start":93.34,"end":102.132,"terms":[{"start":10,"end":14,"say":"yeison"},{"start":15,"end":21,"say":"skima"},{"start":97,"end":102,"say":"féiker"}]},{"text":"Si el schema está flojo, por ejemplo acepta cualquier string como fecha, los datos inválidos pasan de largo y los vas a descubrir en un test que falla por la razón equivocada.","start":102.432,"end":110.724,"terms":[{"start":6,"end":12,"say":"skima"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":111.524,"end":144.97,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":112.024,"end":112.92,"terms":[]},{"text":"Faker para el volumen.","start":113.22,"end":114.616,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":5,"say":"féiker"}]}],[{"text":"Usá Faker con seed fija para el volumen: es rápido, gratis y reproducible.","start":115.166,"end":119.325,"terms":[{"start":4,"end":9,"say":"féiker"},{"start":14,"end":18,"say":"sid"}]}],[{"text":"Para las miles de filas \"normales\" no hace falta un modelo.","start":119.875,"end":122.745,"terms":[]},{"text":"Faker las genera en milisegundos, sin costo y sin red.","start":123.045,"end":125.835,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":5,"say":"féiker"}]},{"text":"Lo importante es fijar la semilla: con la misma semilla obtenés exactamente los mismos datos en tu máquina, en la de tu compañero y en CI.","start":126.135,"end":133.184,"terms":[{"start":135,"end":137,"say":"ci ai"}]},{"text":"Eso convierte un fallo intermitente en uno reproducible.","start":133.484,"end":136.54,"terms":[]},{"text":"Elegí además el locale que corresponde a tus usuarios, porque cambia el formato de nombres, teléfonos y direcciones, y esos formatos son parte de lo que querés probar.","start":136.84,"end":144.97,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":145.77,"end":186.322,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":146.27,"end":147.167,"terms":[]},{"text":"El modelo para los casos raros.","start":147.467,"end":149.257,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Usá el LLM para lo que Faker no da: casos borde realistas (apellidos con apóstrofo, nombres con ñ y tildes, emails con subdominios, notas con emojis o comillas).","start":149.807,"end":158.564,"terms":[{"start":23,"end":28,"say":"féiker"},{"start":108,"end":114,"say":"iméils"}]}],[{"text":"Pedile al modelo lo que una librería no sabe inventar: variantes creíbles que rompen supuestos.","start":159.114,"end":163.83,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sé específico en el pedido.","start":164.13,"end":165.723,"terms":[]},{"text":"En lugar de \"generá datos raros\", listá las categorías que te interesan: apellidos compuestos y con apóstrofo, nombres de una sola letra, mails con subdominios y signos más, textos libres con comillas, saltos de línea o emojis, fechas en el límite de un rango.","start":166.023,"end":179.203,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pedí pocas filas por categoría.","start":179.503,"end":181.294,"terms":[]},{"text":"Diez casos bien pensados valen más que quinientos parecidos, y son más baratos de revisar.","start":181.594,"end":186.322,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":187.122,"end":222.554,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":187.622,"end":188.623,"terms":[]},{"text":"Validación fila por fila.","start":188.923,"end":190.597,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pedile al modelo salida en JSON y validá cada fila contra el schema.","start":191.147,"end":194.668,"terms":[{"start":27,"end":31,"say":"yeison"},{"start":61,"end":67,"say":"skima"}]},{"text":"Lo que no valida se descarta y se cuenta.","start":194.968,"end":197.095,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pedí la salida en JSON y pasá cada fila por el schema antes de aceptarla.","start":197.645,"end":201.398,"terms":[{"start":18,"end":22,"say":"yeison"},{"start":47,"end":53,"say":"skima"}]},{"text":"Lo que no valida se descarta y se cuenta.","start":201.698,"end":203.825,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ese contador es una métrica útil: si el modelo falla una de cada cuatro filas, el prompt necesita un ejemplo de formato o el schema tiene una regla que no explicaste.","start":204.125,"end":212.138,"terms":[{"start":125,"end":131,"say":"skima"}]},{"text":"No corrijas a mano las filas inválidas, porque rompe la reproducibilidad.","start":212.438,"end":216.203,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tampoco confíes en una revisión visual: una fecha como 2026-02-30 se ve perfecta y no existe.","start":216.503,"end":222.554,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":223.354,"end":259.471,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":223.854,"end":224.855,"terms":[]},{"text":"Producción no entra al prompt.","start":225.155,"end":226.853,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Nunca pegues datos de producción en el prompt.","start":227.403,"end":229.739,"terms":[]},{"text":"Describí la forma del dato, no el dato.","start":230.039,"end":232.41,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"La regla es simple y no tiene excepciones: en el prompt va la forma de los datos, nunca los datos.","start":232.96,"end":238.303,"terms":[]},{"text":"Describí campos, formatos y rarezas que querés ver, sin pegar registros reales ni siquiera \"anonimizados a mano\", porque esa anonimización casi siempre deja algo identificable.","start":238.603,"end":247.279,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si necesitás que los datos sintéticos se parezcan estadísticamente a los reales, describí las distribuciones con palabras.","start":247.579,"end":253.259,"terms":[]},{"text":"Y verificá el resultado: revisá que no aparezcan nombres o documentos que coincidan con personas reales de tu base.","start":253.559,"end":259.471,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":260.271,"end":294.704,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":260.771,"end":261.737,"terms":[]},{"text":"Semilla, prompt y dataset versionados.","start":262.037,"end":264.466,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Versioná en data/ el seed, el prompt y el dataset final.","start":265.016,"end":267.886,"terms":[{"start":21,"end":25,"say":"sid"}]},{"text":"Un dataset que no se puede regenerar es deuda.","start":268.186,"end":270.697,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Guardá en data/ las tres cosas que permiten regenerar el conjunto: la semilla de Faker, el prompt usado con el modelo y el archivo final ya validado.","start":271.247,"end":278.877,"terms":[{"start":81,"end":86,"say":"féiker"}]},{"text":"La parte generada por el modelo no es reproducible bit a bit, por eso se versiona el resultado y no solo la receta.","start":279.177,"end":285.089,"terms":[]},{"text":"Anotá también el modelo y la fecha.","start":285.389,"end":287.261,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cuando un test falle dentro de seis meses, vas a poder saber con qué datos corrió, y si hay que ampliar el conjunto vas a partir de algo conocido.","start":287.561,"end":294.704,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":295.504,"end":328.607,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":296.004,"end":296.924,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un equipo prueba un formulario de alta de clientes.","start":297.474,"end":300.286,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con Faker y semilla fija generan mil clientes válidos en menos de un segundo.","start":300.586,"end":304.327,"terms":[{"start":4,"end":9,"say":"féiker"}]},{"text":"Después le piden al modelo veinte casos raros en cinco categorías.","start":304.627,"end":308.031,"terms":[]},{"text":"De las veinte filas, el schema rechaza cuatro: dos fechas de nacimiento imposibles, un teléfono con letras y un mail sin dominio.","start":308.331,"end":314.905,"terms":[{"start":24,"end":30,"say":"skima"}]},{"text":"Quedan dieciséis.","start":315.205,"end":316.415,"terms":[]},{"text":"Al correr la suite con esos dieciséis, dos tests fallan: el apellido O'Connor rompe una consulta mal escapada y un nombre con emoji se guarda truncado.","start":316.715,"end":324.322,"terms":[{"start":13,"end":18,"say":"suit"}]},{"text":"Ninguno de los mil registros prolijos había encontrado esos dos defectos.","start":324.622,"end":328.607,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":329.407,"end":346.54,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":329.907,"end":331.431,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Generar todo con el modelo, incluso el volumen que Faker resuelve gratis.","start":331.981,"end":335.884,"terms":[{"start":51,"end":56,"say":"féiker"}]}],[{"text":"Aceptar las filas por cómo se ven, sin validarlas contra el schema.","start":336.434,"end":339.839,"terms":[{"start":60,"end":66,"say":"skima"}]}],[{"text":"Pegar un registro real \"como ejemplo de formato\".","start":340.389,"end":342.934,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"No guardar el dataset final y no poder repetir una corrida.","start":343.484,"end":346.54,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":347.34,"end":365.39,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":347.84,"end":350.107,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El schema rechaza fechas, mails y rangos inválidos.","start":350.657,"end":353.492,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":9,"say":"skima"}]}],[{"text":"Faker corre con semilla fija y da el mismo resultado dos veces.","start":354.042,"end":357.597,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":5,"say":"féiker"}]}],[{"text":"Cada fila del modelo pasó por el schema y hay un conteo de descartes.","start":358.147,"end":361.679,"terms":[{"start":33,"end":39,"say":"skima"}]}],[{"text":"Semilla, prompt, modelo, fecha y dataset están en data/.","start":362.229,"end":365.39,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":366.19,"end":375.849,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":366.69,"end":368.26,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Devuelve JSON que parece válido y no lo es (fechas, formatos).","start":368.81,"end":372.226,"terms":[{"start":9,"end":13,"say":"yeison"}]},{"text":"Sin validación automática, esos errores llegan a tus tests.","start":372.526,"end":375.849,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":376.649,"end":389.245,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 6.","start":377.149,"end":378.51,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":378.81,"end":384.049,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 7: Arneses 101: test harness, eval harness y agent harness.","start":384.599,"end":389.245,"terms":[{"start":38,"end":45,"say":"járnes"},{"start":52,"end":59,"say":"járnes"},{"start":62,"end":67,"say":"éiyent"},{"start":68,"end":75,"say":"járnes"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d06-en","day":6,"lang":"en","title":"Synthetic test data with LLMs: realistic, valid and PII-free","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d06-en.mp4","duration":443.002,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":17.978,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 6 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.102,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Synthetic test data with LLMs: realistic, valid and PII-free.","start":4.652,"end":9.763,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A test dataset combining Faker for volume and an LLM for edge cases, validated against a schema and with no production data.","start":10.313,"end":17.978,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":18.778,"end":42.038,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Test data is one of the oldest bottlenecks in QA.","start":19.278,"end":22.38,"terms":[]},{"text":"Either you use a copy of production, with all the legal risk that implies, or you use data so tidy it never triggers a bug: names without accents, perfect addresses, round amounts.","start":22.68,"end":34.131,"terms":[]},{"text":"Real defects show up with the surname that has an apostrophe, the email with a subdomain or the note someone filled in with an emoji.","start":34.431,"end":42.038,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":42.838,"end":62.313,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An LLM is good at exactly that: producing realistic variety.","start":43.338,"end":47.729,"terms":[]},{"text":"But it also produces invalid rows with great confidence.","start":48.029,"end":51.468,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide combines three pieces to keep the best of each: a schema that defines what is valid, Faker for volume and the model only for the odd cases, with automatic validation of every row.","start":51.768,"end":62.313,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":63.113,"end":70.038,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":63.613,"end":65.31,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The qa-ai-lab repo.","start":65.86,"end":67.662,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A local model with Ollama.","start":68.212,"end":70.038,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":70.838,"end":77.135,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":71.338,"end":72.618,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":72.918,"end":77.135,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":77.935,"end":80.701,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":78.435,"end":80.701,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":81.501,"end":84.268,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":82.001,"end":84.268,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":85.068,"end":124.458,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":85.568,"end":86.604,"terms":[]},{"text":"The schema rules.","start":86.904,"end":88.381,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Define the schema first.","start":88.931,"end":90.605,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's the source of truth for what valid data is.","start":90.905,"end":93.88,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Write the schema before generating a single row.","start":94.43,"end":97.382,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's the contract for your data: which fields exist, what type they are, which ranges they accept and which are required.","start":97.682,"end":105.115,"terms":[]},{"text":"With Zod or JSON Schema you express it once and use it in three places: to validate what Faker produces, to validate what the model produces and as documentation for the team.","start":105.415,"end":115.495,"terms":[]},{"text":"If the schema is loose, for example it accepts any string as a date, invalid data slips through and you'll discover it in a test failing for the wrong reason.","start":115.795,"end":124.458,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":125.258,"end":163.476,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":125.758,"end":126.957,"terms":[]},{"text":"Faker for volume.","start":127.257,"end":128.618,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Use Faker with a fixed seed for volume: fast, free and reproducible.","start":129.168,"end":133.838,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"For the thousands of \"normal\" rows you don't need a model.","start":134.388,"end":137.13,"terms":[]},{"text":"Faker generates them in milliseconds, at no cost and with no network.","start":137.43,"end":141.682,"terms":[]},{"text":"What matters is pinning the seed: with the same seed you get exactly the same data on your machine, on your teammate's and in CI.","start":141.982,"end":149.636,"terms":[]},{"text":"That turns an intermittent failure into a reproducible one.","start":149.936,"end":153.584,"terms":[]},{"text":"Also choose the locale matching your users, because it changes the format of names, phone numbers and addresses, and those formats are part of what you want to test.","start":153.884,"end":163.476,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":164.276,"end":210.436,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":164.776,"end":165.859,"terms":[]},{"text":"The model for the odd cases.","start":166.159,"end":168.042,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Use the LLM for what Faker can't give: realistic edge cases (apostrophes in surnames, accented names, emails with subdomains, notes with emojis or quotes).","start":168.592,"end":178.568,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ask the model for what a library can't invent: believable variants that break assumptions.","start":179.118,"end":184.31,"terms":[]},{"text":"Be specific in the request.","start":184.61,"end":186.517,"terms":[]},{"text":"Instead of \"generate weird data\", list the categories you care about: compound surnames and ones with apostrophes, single-letter names, emails with subdomains and plus signs, free text with quotes, line breaks or emojis, dates at the edge of a range.","start":186.817,"end":202.017,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ask for a few rows per category.","start":202.317,"end":204.688,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ten well-thought-out cases are worth more than five hundred similar ones, and they are cheaper to review.","start":204.988,"end":210.436,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":211.236,"end":252.868,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":211.736,"end":212.748,"terms":[]},{"text":"Row-by-row validation.","start":213.048,"end":214.711,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ask the model for JSON output and validate every row against the schema.","start":215.261,"end":219.78,"terms":[]},{"text":"Whatever fails gets discarded and counted.","start":220.08,"end":222.788,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ask for JSON output and pass every row through the schema before accepting it.","start":223.338,"end":228.135,"terms":[]},{"text":"Whatever doesn't validate gets discarded and counted.","start":228.435,"end":231.561,"terms":[]},{"text":"That counter is a useful metric: if the model fails one row in four, the prompt needs a format example or the schema has a rule you didn't explain.","start":231.861,"end":240.235,"terms":[]},{"text":"Don't fix invalid rows by hand, because that breaks reproducibility.","start":240.535,"end":245.042,"terms":[]},{"text":"Don't trust a visual review either: a date such as 2026-02-30 looks perfect and doesn't exist.","start":245.342,"end":252.868,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":253.668,"end":294.417,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":254.168,"end":255.297,"terms":[]},{"text":"Production stays out of the prompt.","start":255.597,"end":257.561,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Never paste production data into the prompt.","start":258.111,"end":260.656,"terms":[]},{"text":"Describe the shape of the data, not the data.","start":260.956,"end":263.757,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The rule is simple and has no exceptions: the prompt carries the shape of the data, never the data.","start":264.307,"end":269.964,"terms":[]},{"text":"Describe fields, formats and oddities you want to see, without pasting real records, not even \"hand-anonymized\" ones, because that anonymization almost always leaves something identifiable.","start":270.264,"end":281.749,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you need synthetic data to resemble the real data statistically, describe the distributions in words.","start":282.049,"end":288.286,"terms":[]},{"text":"And verify the result: check that no names or ID numbers match real people in your database.","start":288.586,"end":294.417,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":295.217,"end":334.201,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":295.717,"end":296.904,"terms":[]},{"text":"Seed, prompt and dataset versioned.","start":297.204,"end":299.714,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Version the seed, the prompt and the final dataset in data/.","start":300.264,"end":304.237,"terms":[]},{"text":"A dataset you can't regenerate is debt.","start":304.537,"end":307.071,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Store in data/ the three things that let you regenerate the set: the Faker seed, the prompt used with the model and the final, already validated file.","start":307.621,"end":316.041,"terms":[]},{"text":"The part generated by the model isn't reproducible bit for bit, which is why you version the result and not just the recipe.","start":316.341,"end":323.205,"terms":[]},{"text":"Also note the model and the date.","start":323.505,"end":325.597,"terms":[]},{"text":"When a test fails six months from now, you'll be able to tell which data it ran with, and if the set needs extending you'll start from something known.","start":325.897,"end":334.201,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":335.001,"end":371.471,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":335.501,"end":336.607,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A team tests a customer sign-up form.","start":337.157,"end":339.702,"terms":[]},{"text":"With Faker and a fixed seed they generate a thousand valid customers in under a second.","start":340.002,"end":344.567,"terms":[]},{"text":"Then they ask the model for twenty odd cases across five categories.","start":344.867,"end":349.026,"terms":[]},{"text":"Of the twenty rows, the schema rejects four: two impossible birth dates, a phone number with letters and an email with no domain.","start":349.326,"end":357.258,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sixteen remain.","start":357.558,"end":358.826,"terms":[]},{"text":"Running the suite with those sixteen, two tests fail: the surname O'Connor breaks a badly escaped query and a name with an emoji gets stored truncated.","start":359.126,"end":367.732,"terms":[]},{"text":"None of the thousand tidy records had found those two defects.","start":368.032,"end":371.471,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":372.271,"end":392.457,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":372.771,"end":374.434,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Generating everything with the model, even the volume Faker handles for free.","start":374.984,"end":379.573,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Accepting rows by how they look, without validating them against the schema.","start":380.123,"end":384.618,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pasting a real record \"as a format example\".","start":385.168,"end":388.132,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Not saving the final dataset and being unable to repeat a run.","start":388.682,"end":392.457,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":393.257,"end":413.525,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":393.757,"end":396.001,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The schema rejects invalid dates, emails and ranges.","start":396.551,"end":400.199,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Faker runs with a fixed seed and gives the same result twice.","start":400.749,"end":404.467,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Every model row went through the schema and there is a discard count.","start":405.017,"end":408.851,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seed, prompt, model, date and dataset are in data/.","start":409.401,"end":413.525,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":414.325,"end":425.389,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":414.825,"end":416.72,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"It returns JSON that looks valid and isn't (dates, formats).","start":417.27,"end":421.475,"terms":[]},{"text":"Without automatic validation, those errors reach your tests.","start":421.775,"end":425.389,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":426.189,"end":441.502,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 6.","start":426.689,"end":428.177,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":428.477,"end":434.459,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 7: Harnesses 101: test harness, eval harness and agent harness.","start":435.009,"end":441.502,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d07-es","day":7,"lang":"es","title":"Arneses 101: test harness, eval harness y agent harness","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d07-es.mp4","duration":401.579,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":12.719,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 7 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.405,"terms":[{"start":29,"end":31,"say":"cu a"},{"start":34,"end":36,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Arneses 101: test harness, eval harness y agent harness.","start":3.955,"end":7.371,"terms":[{"start":18,"end":25,"say":"járnes"},{"start":32,"end":39,"say":"járnes"},{"start":42,"end":47,"say":"éiyent"},{"start":48,"end":55,"say":"járnes"}]}],[{"text":"Los tres arneses que hoy conviven en QA, qué envuelve cada uno y cómo ubicar qué le falta a tu stack.","start":7.921,"end":12.719,"terms":[{"start":37,"end":39,"say":"cu a"}]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":13.519,"end":37.616,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"La palabra \"harness\" aparece en casi todas las conversaciones sobre IA y testing, y casi siempre con un significado distinto.","start":14.019,"end":20.279,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":19,"say":"járnes"},{"start":68,"end":70,"say":"i a"}]},{"text":"Para algunos es el framework de automatización de siempre.","start":20.579,"end":23.658,"terms":[{"start":19,"end":28,"say":"fréimuork"}]},{"text":"Para otros, la herramienta que evalúa un prompt.","start":23.958,"end":26.631,"terms":[]},{"text":"Para otros, el programa que convierte un modelo en un agente capaz de editar código.","start":26.931,"end":31.23,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las tres cosas existen y las tres se llaman igual, y esa confusión hace que los equipos compren o construyan lo que no necesitan.","start":31.53,"end":37.616,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":38.416,"end":51.713,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Esta guía separa los tres significados con una sola definición común y muestra la relación entre ellos.","start":38.916,"end":44.143,"terms":[]},{"text":"No hay código hoy: el objetivo es que puedas mirar tu propio stack y decir qué harness tenés, cuál te falta y, sobre todo, cuál verifica a cuál.","start":44.443,"end":51.713,"terms":[{"start":79,"end":86,"say":"járnes"}]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":52.513,"end":60.971,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":53.013,"end":54.943,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Conocer tu stack actual de automation.","start":55.493,"end":57.644,"terms":[{"start":27,"end":37,"say":"otoméishon"}]}],[{"text":"La lista de abajo para completar con tus herramientas.","start":58.194,"end":60.971,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Los tres arneses","start":61.771,"end":89.084,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los tres arneses.","start":62.271,"end":63.574,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Test harness: envuelve tu aplicación.","start":64.124,"end":66.368,"terms":[{"start":5,"end":12,"say":"járnes"}]},{"text":"Runner, fixtures, mocks y reporters.","start":66.668,"end":68.899,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":6,"say":"ráner"},{"start":8,"end":16,"say":"fícschers"}]},{"text":"Ejemplos: Playwright Test, JUnit, pytest.","start":69.199,"end":71.733,"terms":[{"start":10,"end":20,"say":"pléirait"},{"start":27,"end":32,"say":"yei iúnit"}]}],[{"text":"Eval harness: envuelve un modelo o un prompt.","start":72.283,"end":74.654,"terms":[{"start":5,"end":12,"say":"járnes"}]},{"text":"Dataset, runner, scorers y reporte.","start":74.954,"end":77.406,"terms":[{"start":9,"end":15,"say":"ráner"}]},{"text":"Ejemplos: promptfoo, DeepEval, Inspect AI.","start":77.706,"end":80.391,"terms":[{"start":10,"end":19,"say":"promptfú"}]}],[{"text":"Agent harness: envuelve un modelo que actúa.","start":80.941,"end":83.428,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":5,"say":"éiyent"},{"start":6,"end":13,"say":"járnes"}]},{"text":"Loop, tools, contexto, permisos y memoria.","start":83.728,"end":86.145,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":4,"say":"lup"},{"start":6,"end":11,"say":"tuls"}]},{"text":"Ejemplos: Claude Code, Copilot agent, Cursor.","start":86.445,"end":89.084,"terms":[{"start":10,"end":16,"say":"clod"},{"start":17,"end":21,"say":"coud"},{"start":31,"end":36,"say":"éiyent"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":89.884,"end":125.339,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":90.384,"end":91.28,"terms":[]},{"text":"Una sola definición.","start":91.58,"end":93.046,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Quedate con la definición: un arnés es todo lo que rodea a lo que probás para poder ejecutarlo, controlarlo y medirlo.","start":93.596,"end":99.252,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un harness es todo lo que rodea a la cosa bajo prueba para poder ejecutarla, controlarla y medirla.","start":99.802,"end":104.6,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":10,"say":"járnes"}]},{"text":"Ejecutarla significa ponerla en marcha de forma repetible.","start":104.9,"end":107.979,"terms":[]},{"text":"Controlarla, fijar sus entradas y su entorno.","start":108.279,"end":110.94,"terms":[]},{"text":"Medirla, obtener un resultado comparable.","start":111.24,"end":113.797,"terms":[]},{"text":"La definición sirve para los tres tipos; lo único que cambia es qué hay en el centro: tu aplicación, un modelo o un agente.","start":114.097,"end":120.462,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cuando alguien use la palabra, preguntá qué está en el centro y la conversación se ordena sola.","start":120.762,"end":125.339,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":126.139,"end":161.246,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":126.639,"end":127.536,"terms":[]},{"text":"Test harness: el que ya tenés.","start":127.836,"end":129.638,"terms":[{"start":5,"end":12,"say":"járnes"}]}],[{"text":"Test harness: ya lo tenés.","start":130.188,"end":131.967,"terms":[{"start":5,"end":12,"say":"járnes"}]},{"text":"Runner, fixtures, mocks y reportes alrededor de tu aplicación.","start":132.267,"end":135.764,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":6,"say":"ráner"},{"start":8,"end":16,"say":"fícschers"}]}],[{"text":"Si corrés tests automatizados, ya tenés uno.","start":136.314,"end":139.022,"terms":[]},{"text":"El runner ejecuta, las fixtures preparan el estado, los mocks aíslan dependencias y los reportes muestran el resultado.","start":139.322,"end":145.501,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":9,"say":"ráner"},{"start":23,"end":31,"say":"fícschers"}]},{"text":"Es determinista: la misma entrada da la misma salida y un fallo significa algo.","start":145.801,"end":150.123,"terms":[]},{"text":"Vale la pena reconocerlo como harness porque es la base de los otros dos.","start":150.423,"end":154.001,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":37,"say":"járnes"}]},{"text":"Un equipo con un test harness lento, frágil o que nadie mira va a trasladar esos mismos problemas a todo lo que construya con IA encima.","start":154.301,"end":161.246,"terms":[{"start":22,"end":29,"say":"járnes"},{"start":126,"end":128,"say":"i a"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":162.046,"end":197.536,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":162.546,"end":163.443,"terms":[]},{"text":"Eval harness: lo mismo alrededor de un modelo.","start":163.743,"end":166.393,"terms":[{"start":5,"end":12,"say":"járnes"}]}],[{"text":"Eval harness: lo mismo, pero alrededor de un modelo o un prompt.","start":166.943,"end":170.428,"terms":[{"start":5,"end":12,"say":"járnes"}]},{"text":"Cambia el assert por scorers y el caso por un dataset.","start":170.728,"end":173.517,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Acá la cosa bajo prueba es un modelo o un prompt.","start":174.067,"end":176.624,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las piezas tienen equivalentes directos: los casos de prueba pasan a ser un dataset, los asserts pasan a ser scorers que puntúan una salida, y el reporte muestra tasas de aprobación en lugar de verde o rojo.","start":176.924,"end":187.167,"terms":[]},{"text":"Se agregan dos cosas que el test harness no necesitaba: repetir cada caso varias veces y registrar costo y latencia.","start":187.467,"end":193.24,"terms":[{"start":33,"end":40,"say":"járnes"}]},{"text":"Lo vas a necesitar el día que una funcionalidad de tu producto dependa de un modelo.","start":193.54,"end":197.536,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":198.336,"end":236.508,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":198.836,"end":199.837,"terms":[]},{"text":"Agent harness: el que trabaja.","start":200.137,"end":201.997,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":5,"say":"éiyent"},{"start":6,"end":13,"say":"járnes"}]}],[{"text":"Agent harness: el loop que convierte un modelo en un agente, con herramientas, instrucciones, permisos y memoria.","start":202.547,"end":208.134,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":5,"say":"éiyent"},{"start":6,"end":13,"say":"járnes"},{"start":18,"end":22,"say":"lup"}]},{"text":"Acá el agente hace trabajo de QA.","start":208.434,"end":210.283,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"}]}],[{"text":"Es el bucle que convierte un modelo en un agente: recibe una tarea, decide qué herramienta usar, lee el resultado y vuelve a decidir, hasta terminar.","start":210.833,"end":218.44,"terms":[]},{"text":"A su alrededor hay instrucciones del repositorio, herramientas disponibles, permisos y memoria.","start":218.74,"end":223.538,"terms":[]},{"text":"La diferencia clave con los otros dos es de rol: este no mide, produce.","start":223.838,"end":227.753,"terms":[]},{"text":"Escribe tests, modifica código, investiga un fallo.","start":228.053,"end":231.167,"terms":[]},{"text":"Por eso su configuración se parece más a incorporar a un compañero nuevo que a configurar una herramienta.","start":231.467,"end":236.508,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":237.308,"end":272.914,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":237.808,"end":238.81,"terms":[]},{"text":"Quién verifica a quién.","start":239.11,"end":240.482,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Relación clave: el agent harness produce tests y cambios; el test harness y el eval harness verifican lo que produjo.","start":241.032,"end":246.724,"terms":[{"start":19,"end":24,"say":"éiyent"},{"start":25,"end":32,"say":"járnes"},{"start":66,"end":73,"say":"járnes"},{"start":84,"end":91,"say":"járnes"}]},{"text":"Nunca al revés.","start":247.024,"end":248.199,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"La relación es de una sola dirección.","start":248.749,"end":250.969,"terms":[]},{"text":"El agent harness produce tests y cambios; el test harness y el eval harness verifican lo producido.","start":251.269,"end":256.241,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":8,"say":"éiyent"},{"start":9,"end":16,"say":"járnes"},{"start":50,"end":57,"say":"járnes"},{"start":68,"end":75,"say":"járnes"}]},{"text":"Si invertís la flecha y dejás que el agente juzgue su propio trabajo, perdés la verificación independiente, que es la razón de ser de QA.","start":256.541,"end":263.173,"terms":[{"start":134,"end":136,"say":"cu a"}]},{"text":"En la práctica esto se traduce en una regla concreta: lo que cuenta como terminado es el resultado de un comando que corre fuera del agente, con su código de salida, y no el resumen que el agente escribe.","start":263.473,"end":272.914,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":273.714,"end":308.428,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":274.214,"end":275.18,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tu stack en la lista.","start":275.48,"end":276.725,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Completá la lista con tu stack y marcá el arnés que no tenés.","start":277.275,"end":280.424,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ese es tu próximo paso.","start":280.724,"end":282.318,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Completá la lista de los tres harnesses con nombres propios.","start":282.868,"end":285.854,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":39,"say":"járneses"}]},{"text":"Qué runner, qué fixtures y qué reportes usás.","start":286.154,"end":288.711,"terms":[{"start":4,"end":10,"say":"ráner"},{"start":16,"end":24,"say":"fícschers"}]},{"text":"Si evaluás algún modelo, con qué dataset y qué scorers.","start":289.011,"end":291.893,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si usás un agente, qué reglas, herramientas y permisos tiene.","start":292.193,"end":295.307,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las celdas vacías son información.","start":295.607,"end":297.722,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si te falta el eval harness y tu producto ya usa un modelo, ese es el próximo paso, y es el tema de mañana.","start":298.022,"end":303.702,"terms":[{"start":20,"end":27,"say":"járnes"}]},{"text":"Si te falta el de agentes, puede esperar: es productividad, no verificación.","start":304.002,"end":308.428,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":309.228,"end":343.922,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":309.728,"end":310.648,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un equipo de producto completa la lista.","start":311.198,"end":313.488,"terms":[]},{"text":"Test harness: Playwright, fixtures de login, reporte HTML en CI; completo.","start":313.788,"end":318.237,"terms":[{"start":5,"end":12,"say":"járnes"},{"start":14,"end":24,"say":"pléirait"},{"start":26,"end":34,"say":"fícschers"},{"start":61,"end":63,"say":"ci ai"}]},{"text":"Eval harness: vacío, aunque el producto tiene un asistente que responde consultas de clientes desde hace tres meses.","start":318.537,"end":324.368,"terms":[{"start":5,"end":12,"say":"járnes"}]},{"text":"Agent harness: dos personas usan un agente de código sin reglas compartidas.","start":324.668,"end":328.978,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":5,"say":"éiyent"},{"start":6,"end":13,"say":"járnes"}]},{"text":"El diagnóstico es claro.","start":329.278,"end":330.848,"terms":[]},{"text":"La funcionalidad con más riesgo, el asistente, no tiene ninguna medición, mientras el equipo discutía qué agente adoptar.","start":331.148,"end":337.478,"terms":[]},{"text":"Deciden armar primero un eval de veinte casos para el asistente y después escribir un archivo de reglas común para los agentes.","start":337.778,"end":343.922,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":344.722,"end":360.624,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":345.222,"end":346.746,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Usar \"harness\" sin aclarar qué hay en el centro.","start":347.296,"end":349.586,"terms":[{"start":6,"end":13,"say":"járnes"}]}],[{"text":"Dejar que el agente declare terminado su propio trabajo.","start":350.136,"end":353.029,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Invertir en un agent harness teniendo un test harness frágil.","start":353.579,"end":356.635,"terms":[{"start":15,"end":20,"say":"éiyent"},{"start":21,"end":28,"say":"járnes"},{"start":46,"end":53,"say":"járnes"}]}],[{"text":"Evaluar un modelo con asserts exactos en lugar de un eval harness.","start":357.185,"end":360.624,"terms":[{"start":58,"end":65,"say":"járnes"}]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":361.424,"end":378.522,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":361.924,"end":364.191,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Podés explicar los tres harnesses con la misma definición.","start":364.741,"end":367.751,"terms":[{"start":24,"end":33,"say":"járneses"}]}],[{"text":"Tu lista tiene nombres concretos en cada celda o un vacío explícito.","start":368.301,"end":371.821,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Identificaste cuál te falta y por qué importa.","start":372.371,"end":375.032,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tenés escrita la regla de que el gate corre fuera del agente.","start":375.582,"end":378.522,"terms":[{"start":33,"end":37,"say":"gueit"}]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":379.322,"end":388.331,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":379.822,"end":381.392,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Sin un gate externo, el agente reporta éxito sin haber ejecutado.","start":381.942,"end":385.532,"terms":[{"start":7,"end":11,"say":"gueit"}]},{"text":"Verificá con el exit code, no con el resumen.","start":385.832,"end":388.331,"terms":[{"start":21,"end":25,"say":"coud"}]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":389.131,"end":400.079,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 7.","start":389.631,"end":390.981,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":391.281,"end":396.519,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 8: Tu primer eval harness con promptfoo.","start":397.069,"end":400.079,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":42,"say":"járnes"},{"start":47,"end":56,"say":"promptfú"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d07-en","day":7,"lang":"en","title":"Harnesses 101: test harness, eval harness and agent harness","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d07-en.mp4","duration":447.729,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":16.004,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 7 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.102,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Harnesses 101: test harness, eval harness and agent harness.","start":4.652,"end":9.472,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The three harnesses that coexist in QA today, what each one wraps and how to spot what your stack lacks.","start":10.022,"end":16.004,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":16.804,"end":41.668,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The word \"harness\" shows up in almost every conversation about AI and testing, and almost always with a different meaning.","start":17.304,"end":24.006,"terms":[]},{"text":"For some it's the usual automation framework.","start":24.306,"end":26.99,"terms":[]},{"text":"For others, the tool that evaluates a prompt.","start":27.29,"end":30.033,"terms":[]},{"text":"For others, the program that turns a model into an agent able to edit code.","start":30.333,"end":35.189,"terms":[]},{"text":"All three exist and all three share the name, and that confusion makes teams buy or build what they don't need.","start":35.489,"end":41.668,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":42.468,"end":57.507,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This guide separates the three meanings with a single common definition and shows how they relate.","start":42.968,"end":47.974,"terms":[]},{"text":"There is no code today: the goal is for you to look at your own stack and say which harness you have, which one you lack and, above all, which one verifies which.","start":48.274,"end":57.507,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":58.307,"end":66.347,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":58.807,"end":60.504,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Know your current automation stack.","start":61.054,"end":63.1,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The list below, to fill in with your tools.","start":63.65,"end":66.347,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The three harnesses","start":67.147,"end":101.506,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The three harnesses.","start":67.647,"end":69.042,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Test harness: wraps your app.","start":69.592,"end":71.65,"terms":[]},{"text":"Runner, fixtures, mocks and reporters.","start":71.95,"end":74.762,"terms":[]},{"text":"Examples: Playwright Test, JUnit, pytest.","start":75.062,"end":79.082,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Eval harness: wraps a model or prompt.","start":79.632,"end":82.491,"terms":[]},{"text":"Dataset, runner, scorers and report.","start":82.791,"end":85.568,"terms":[]},{"text":"Examples: promptfoo, DeepEval, Inspect AI.","start":85.868,"end":90.015,"terms":[{"start":10,"end":19,"say":"prompt foo"}]}],[{"text":"Agent harness: wraps a model that acts.","start":90.565,"end":93.645,"terms":[]},{"text":"Loop, tools, context, permissions and memory.","start":93.945,"end":97.442,"terms":[]},{"text":"Examples: Claude Code, Copilot agent, Cursor.","start":97.742,"end":101.506,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":102.306,"end":141.303,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":102.806,"end":103.842,"terms":[]},{"text":"A single definition.","start":104.142,"end":105.608,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Keep the definition: a harness is everything around the thing under test that lets you run, control and measure it.","start":106.158,"end":112.279,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A harness is everything around the thing under test that lets you run, control and measure it.","start":112.829,"end":117.731,"terms":[]},{"text":"Running it means starting it in a repeatable way.","start":118.031,"end":120.982,"terms":[]},{"text":"Controlling it, fixing its inputs and its environment.","start":121.282,"end":124.547,"terms":[]},{"text":"Measuring it, getting a comparable result.","start":124.847,"end":127.729,"terms":[]},{"text":"The definition works for all three types; the only thing that changes is what sits in the middle: your application, a model or an agent.","start":128.029,"end":135.706,"terms":[]},{"text":"When someone uses the word, ask what's in the middle and the conversation sorts itself out.","start":136.006,"end":141.303,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":142.103,"end":181.634,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":142.603,"end":143.801,"terms":[]},{"text":"Test harness: the one you already have.","start":144.101,"end":146.774,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Test harness: you already have it.","start":147.324,"end":149.626,"terms":[]},{"text":"Runner, fixtures, mocks and reports around your app.","start":149.926,"end":153.585,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"If you run automated tests, you already have one.","start":154.135,"end":157.215,"terms":[]},{"text":"The runner executes, fixtures prepare state, mocks isolate dependencies and reports show the result.","start":157.515,"end":163.822,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's deterministic: the same input gives the same output and a failure means something.","start":164.122,"end":169.163,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's worth recognizing as a harness because it's the base for the other two.","start":169.463,"end":173.819,"terms":[]},{"text":"A team with a slow, brittle test harness that nobody looks at will carry those same problems into everything it builds with AI on top.","start":174.119,"end":181.634,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":182.434,"end":219.282,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":182.934,"end":184.016,"terms":[]},{"text":"Eval harness: the same around a model.","start":184.316,"end":186.908,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Eval harness: the same idea around a model or prompt.","start":187.458,"end":191.233,"terms":[]},{"text":"Asserts become scorers and cases become a dataset.","start":191.533,"end":194.845,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Here the thing under test is a model or a prompt.","start":195.395,"end":198.149,"terms":[]},{"text":"The pieces have direct equivalents: test cases become a dataset, asserts become scorers that grade an output, and the report shows pass rates instead of green or red.","start":198.449,"end":208.274,"terms":[]},{"text":"Two things are added that the test harness didn't need: repeating each case several times and recording cost and latency.","start":208.574,"end":215.45,"terms":[]},{"text":"You'll need it the day a feature of your product depends on a model.","start":215.75,"end":219.282,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":220.082,"end":263.792,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":220.582,"end":221.594,"terms":[]},{"text":"Agent harness: the one that works.","start":221.894,"end":224.347,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Agent harness: the loop that turns a model into an agent, with tools, instructions, permissions and memory.","start":224.897,"end":231.9,"terms":[]},{"text":"Here the agent does QA work.","start":232.2,"end":234.153,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"It's the loop that turns a model into an agent: it receives a task, decides which tool to use, reads the result and decides again, until done.","start":234.703,"end":243.622,"terms":[]},{"text":"Around it are repository instructions, available tools, permissions and memory.","start":243.922,"end":249.126,"terms":[]},{"text":"The key difference from the other two is the role: this one doesn't measure, it produces.","start":249.426,"end":254.479,"terms":[]},{"text":"It writes tests, modifies code, investigates a failure.","start":254.779,"end":258.787,"terms":[]},{"text":"That's why configuring it feels more like onboarding a new teammate than setting up a tool.","start":259.087,"end":263.792,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":264.592,"end":305.77,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":265.092,"end":266.221,"terms":[]},{"text":"Who verifies whom.","start":266.521,"end":267.905,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Key relationship: the agent harness produces tests and changes; the test harness and eval harness verify what it produced.","start":268.455,"end":276.05,"terms":[]},{"text":"Never the other way round.","start":276.35,"end":277.77,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The relationship runs in one direction only.","start":278.32,"end":281.109,"terms":[]},{"text":"The agent harness produces tests and changes; the test harness and the eval harness verify what was produced.","start":281.409,"end":288.122,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you reverse the arrow and let the agent judge its own work, you lose independent verification, which is the whole point of QA.","start":288.422,"end":295.843,"terms":[]},{"text":"In practice this becomes a concrete rule: what counts as done is the result of a command running outside the agent, with its exit code, and not the summary the agent writes.","start":296.143,"end":305.77,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":306.57,"end":346.161,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":307.07,"end":308.257,"terms":[]},{"text":"Your stack on the list.","start":308.557,"end":310.092,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Fill the list with your stack and flag the harness you lack.","start":310.642,"end":313.884,"terms":[]},{"text":"That's your next step.","start":314.184,"end":315.627,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Fill in the list of three harnesses with real names.","start":316.177,"end":319.221,"terms":[]},{"text":"Which runner, fixtures and reports you use.","start":319.521,"end":322.67,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you evaluate a model, with which dataset and scorers.","start":322.97,"end":326.862,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you use an agent, which rules, tools and permissions it has.","start":327.162,"end":331.263,"terms":[]},{"text":"Empty cells are information.","start":331.563,"end":333.62,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you lack the eval harness and your product already uses a model, that's the next step, and it's tomorrow's topic.","start":333.92,"end":340.61,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you lack the agent one, it can wait: it's productivity, not verification.","start":340.91,"end":346.161,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":346.961,"end":383.361,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":347.461,"end":348.566,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A product team fills in the list.","start":349.116,"end":351.22,"terms":[]},{"text":"Test harness: Playwright, login fixtures, HTML report in CI; complete.","start":351.52,"end":357.003,"terms":[]},{"text":"Eval harness: empty, even though the product has had an assistant answering customer queries for three months.","start":357.303,"end":363.888,"terms":[]},{"text":"Agent harness: two people use a coding agent with no shared rules.","start":364.188,"end":368.661,"terms":[]},{"text":"The diagnosis is clear.","start":368.961,"end":370.728,"terms":[]},{"text":"The riskiest feature, the assistant, has no measurement at all, while the team was debating which agent to adopt.","start":371.028,"end":377.149,"terms":[]},{"text":"They decide to build a twenty-case eval for the assistant first and then write a common rules file for the agents.","start":377.449,"end":383.361,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":384.161,"end":401.631,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":384.661,"end":386.324,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Using \"harness\" without saying what sits in the middle.","start":386.874,"end":389.814,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Letting the agent declare its own work done.","start":390.364,"end":393.095,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Investing in an agent harness while the test harness is brittle.","start":393.645,"end":397.142,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Evaluating a model with exact asserts instead of an eval harness.","start":397.692,"end":401.631,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":402.431,"end":421.328,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":402.931,"end":405.174,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"You can explain the three harnesses with the same definition.","start":405.724,"end":409.105,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Your list has concrete names in each cell or an explicit gap.","start":409.655,"end":413.582,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"You identified which one you lack and why it matters.","start":414.132,"end":417.444,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"You have the rule written down that the gate runs outside the agent.","start":417.994,"end":421.328,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":422.128,"end":432.879,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":422.628,"end":424.523,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Without an external gate, the agent reports success without having run anything.","start":425.073,"end":429.476,"terms":[]},{"text":"Verify with the exit code, not the summary.","start":429.776,"end":432.879,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":433.679,"end":446.229,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 7.","start":434.179,"end":435.528,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":435.828,"end":441.81,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 8: Your first eval harness with promptfoo.","start":442.36,"end":446.229,"terms":[{"start":50,"end":59,"say":"prompt foo"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d08-es","day":8,"lang":"es","title":"Tu primer eval harness con promptfoo","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d08-es.mp4","duration":384.735,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":14.054,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 8 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.452,"terms":[{"start":29,"end":31,"say":"cu a"},{"start":34,"end":36,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Tu primer eval harness con promptfoo.","start":4.002,"end":5.966,"terms":[{"start":15,"end":22,"say":"járnes"},{"start":27,"end":36,"say":"promptfú"}]}],[{"text":"Un eval harness real para un clasificador de bugs: prompt versionado, dataset, asserts, corridas repetidas y comparación entre dos modelos.","start":6.516,"end":14.054,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":15,"say":"járnes"},{"start":45,"end":49,"say":"bags"}]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":14.854,"end":33.806,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Hasta acá hablamos de evals como idea.","start":15.354,"end":17.307,"terms":[]},{"text":"Hoy construís uno que funciona, de punta a punta, sobre un problema que cualquier equipo de QA reconoce: clasificar reportes de bugs por severidad.","start":17.607,"end":24.959,"terms":[{"start":92,"end":94,"say":"cu a"},{"start":128,"end":132,"say":"bags"}]},{"text":"Es un buen primer caso porque la salida es chica, el criterio se puede discutir y los errores tienen consecuencias claras: un bug crítico clasificado como bajo se pierde en el backlog.","start":25.259,"end":33.806,"terms":[{"start":126,"end":129,"say":"bag"}]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":34.606,"end":50.667,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Vas a escribir el prompt como archivo, armar la configuración, etiquetar diez casos con tu propio criterio, correr todo varias veces y leer los fallos.","start":35.106,"end":42.771,"terms":[]},{"text":"La parte que más enseña no es la herramienta sino la lectura de fallos: ahí descubrís si el problema está en el modelo, en el prompt o en tus propias etiquetas.","start":43.071,"end":50.667,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":51.467,"end":60.737,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":51.967,"end":53.897,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El repo qa-ai-lab con promptfoo (día 4).","start":54.447,"end":56.899,"terms":[{"start":22,"end":31,"say":"promptfú"}]}],[{"text":"Ollama con llama3.2; opcional, un segundo modelo.","start":57.449,"end":60.737,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":6,"say":"olama"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":61.537,"end":66.859,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":62.037,"end":63.213,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":63.513,"end":66.859,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":67.659,"end":70.298,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":68.159,"end":70.298,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":71.098,"end":73.737,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":71.598,"end":73.737,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":74.537,"end":110.016,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":75.037,"end":75.933,"terms":[]},{"text":"El prompt como archivo.","start":76.233,"end":77.652,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Escribí el prompt en un archivo aparte, con las reglas de clasificación y el formato de salida.","start":78.202,"end":82.861,"terms":[]},{"text":"El prompt es código: se versiona.","start":83.161,"end":85.16,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Escribí el prompt en prompts/ con tres partes: el rol, las reglas de clasificación y el formato exacto de salida.","start":85.71,"end":91.448,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las reglas tienen que ser las tuyas, escritas como se las explicarías a alguien nuevo: qué hace que un bug sea crítico, qué lo hace bajo.","start":91.748,"end":98.043,"terms":[{"start":103,"end":106,"say":"bag"}]},{"text":"Cuanto más explícitas, menos interpreta el modelo.","start":98.343,"end":101.063,"terms":[]},{"text":"El formato de salida también va escrito, con los valores permitidos.","start":101.363,"end":104.767,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tenerlo en un archivo aparte permite ver en un diff qué palabra cambió cuando la calidad sube o baja.","start":105.067,"end":110.016,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":110.816,"end":145.051,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":111.316,"end":112.212,"terms":[]},{"text":"La configuración mínima.","start":112.512,"end":114.094,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Creá la config: prompt, provider y un defaultTest con el assert de formato que aplica a todos los casos.","start":114.644,"end":120.184,"terms":[{"start":24,"end":32,"say":"prováider"}]}],[{"text":"La configuración une tres cosas: qué prompt, contra qué provider y con qué casos.","start":120.734,"end":125.218,"terms":[{"start":56,"end":64,"say":"prováider"}]},{"text":"El bloque defaultTest es la pieza más útil para empezar: ahí va el assert de formato que se aplica a todos los casos sin repetirlo.","start":125.518,"end":132.174,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si la salida no es JSON válido con una severidad permitida, el caso falla ahí y no se evalúa nada más.","start":132.474,"end":137.689,"terms":[{"start":19,"end":23,"say":"yeison"}]},{"text":"Mantené la configuración corta.","start":137.989,"end":139.977,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un archivo que entra en una pantalla lo lee todo el equipo; uno de trescientas líneas, nadie.","start":140.277,"end":145.051,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":145.851,"end":182.55,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":146.351,"end":147.248,"terms":[]},{"text":"Diez casos etiquetados por vos.","start":147.548,"end":149.489,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cargá al menos 10 casos etiquetados por vos: 3 críticos, 3 bajos y 4 ambiguos.","start":150.039,"end":154.93,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los ambiguos son los que enseñan.","start":155.23,"end":157.067,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Elegí diez reportes reales o realistas y escribí la severidad esperada antes de correr nada.","start":157.617,"end":162.078,"terms":[]},{"text":"La mezcla importa: tres claramente críticos, tres claramente bajos y cuatro ambiguos.","start":162.378,"end":166.955,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los claros verifican que lo básico funciona.","start":167.255,"end":169.753,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los ambiguos son los que discriminan: un reporte que dice \"la app se cierra\" sin decir en qué flujo, o uno cosmético en la pantalla de pago.","start":170.053,"end":176.546,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si te cuesta etiquetar un caso, anotá por qué.","start":176.846,"end":179.519,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esa duda suele ser una regla que falta en el prompt.","start":179.819,"end":182.55,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":183.35,"end":217.923,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":183.85,"end":184.851,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tres corridas por caso.","start":185.151,"end":186.617,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Corré con --repeat 3.","start":187.167,"end":189.038,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un caso que pasa una vez de tres no está pasando.","start":189.338,"end":192.069,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Una sola corrida no dice nada sobre un sistema que varía.","start":192.619,"end":195.722,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con tres repeticiones cada caso queda en una de tres situaciones: pasa siempre, falla siempre o es inestable.","start":196.022,"end":201.353,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los inestables son los más interesantes, porque muestran dónde el prompt deja margen de interpretación.","start":201.653,"end":206.695,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tratá un caso que pasa una de tres veces como fallido: en producción, ese caso va a clasificar mal buena parte de las veces.","start":206.995,"end":213.453,"terms":[]},{"text":"Anotá el tiempo y el costo total de la corrida, que más adelante vas a necesitar.","start":213.753,"end":217.923,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":218.723,"end":256.375,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":219.223,"end":220.224,"terms":[]},{"text":"Leer los fallos uno por uno.","start":220.524,"end":222.199,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Abrí el visor y leé las fallas una por una.","start":222.749,"end":225.131,"terms":[]},{"text":"Clasificalas: error del modelo, error del prompt o error de tu etiqueta.","start":225.431,"end":229.231,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Abrí el visor y leé cada fallo con su entrada y su salida completas.","start":229.781,"end":233.417,"terms":[]},{"text":"Clasificalo en una de tres causas.","start":233.717,"end":235.751,"terms":[]},{"text":"Error del modelo: las reglas eran claras y no las siguió.","start":236.051,"end":239.073,"terms":[]},{"text":"Error del prompt: la regla no estaba o era ambigua.","start":239.373,"end":242.092,"terms":[]},{"text":"Error de etiquetado: al releer, la respuesta del modelo es defendible y tu etiqueta no.","start":242.392,"end":246.888,"terms":[]},{"text":"La tercera causa aparece más de lo que uno espera, y encontrarla mejora tu criterio.","start":247.188,"end":251.788,"terms":[]},{"text":"Corregí primero el prompt y las etiquetas, y recién después pensá en cambiar de modelo.","start":252.088,"end":256.375,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":257.175,"end":290.493,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":257.675,"end":258.641,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un segundo provider.","start":258.941,"end":260.325,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":19,"say":"prováider"}]}],[{"text":"Agregá un segundo provider y compará lado a lado calidad, latencia y costo.","start":260.875,"end":265.011,"terms":[{"start":18,"end":26,"say":"prováider"}]}],[{"text":"Agregá otro modelo a la lista de providers y corré de nuevo.","start":265.561,"end":268.64,"terms":[{"start":33,"end":42,"say":"prováiders"}]},{"text":"El visor muestra los dos lado a lado sobre los mismos casos.","start":268.94,"end":272.24,"terms":[]},{"text":"Compará tres cosas: tasa de aprobación, latencia y costo por corrida.","start":272.54,"end":276.583,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es frecuente que el modelo más grande gane por poco en calidad y pierda por mucho en las otras dos.","start":276.883,"end":281.588,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con diez casos no alcanza para una decisión definitiva, pero sí para descartar opciones y para tener, por primera vez, una comparación basada en tus datos y no en un ranking público.","start":281.888,"end":290.493,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":291.293,"end":324.954,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":291.793,"end":292.713,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Una QA lead arma el eval con diez reportes de su backlog.","start":293.263,"end":296.145,"terms":[{"start":4,"end":6,"say":"cu a"},{"start":7,"end":11,"say":"lid"}]},{"text":"En la primera corrida, con tres repeticiones, siete casos pasan siempre, uno falla siempre y dos son inestables.","start":296.445,"end":302.438,"terms":[]},{"text":"El que falla siempre es un error de etiquetado: ella lo había marcado como bajo, pero afecta al login y el modelo lo marcó crítico con buen motivo.","start":302.738,"end":309.614,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los dos inestables son reportes sin pasos de reproducción.","start":309.914,"end":313.028,"terms":[]},{"text":"Agrega al prompt una regla: \"si faltan datos para decidir, clasificá como medio y pedí los pasos\".","start":313.328,"end":318.52,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la segunda corrida, los diez casos pasan las tres veces.","start":318.82,"end":322.097,"terms":[]},{"text":"El cambio fue una línea de prompt y una etiqueta.","start":322.397,"end":324.954,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":325.754,"end":341.586,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":326.254,"end":327.777,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Etiquetar los casos después de ver la respuesta del modelo.","start":328.327,"end":331.093,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Usar solo casos fáciles y concluir que el prompt funciona.","start":331.643,"end":334.85,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Evaluar contenido cuando el formato ya falló.","start":335.4,"end":337.969,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cambiar de modelo antes de revisar el prompt y las etiquetas.","start":338.519,"end":341.586,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":342.386,"end":359.833,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":342.886,"end":345.153,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El prompt está en un archivo con reglas y formato de salida.","start":345.703,"end":348.701,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Hay diez casos etiquetados, cuatro de ellos ambiguos.","start":349.251,"end":352.179,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cada caso corrió tres veces y sabés cuáles son inestables.","start":352.729,"end":355.739,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cada fallo tiene una causa asignada: modelo, prompt o etiqueta.","start":356.289,"end":359.833,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":360.633,"end":369.723,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":361.133,"end":362.703,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Los modelos chicos agregan texto alrededor del JSON.","start":363.253,"end":366.262,"terms":[{"start":47,"end":51,"say":"yeison"}]},{"text":"Validá formato antes que contenido o vas a diagnosticar mal.","start":366.562,"end":369.723,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":370.523,"end":383.235,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 8.","start":371.023,"end":372.36,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":372.66,"end":377.899,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 9: Agent harness para QA: reglas, skills y MCP en tu repo de automation.","start":378.449,"end":383.235,"terms":[{"start":20,"end":25,"say":"éiyent"},{"start":26,"end":33,"say":"járnes"},{"start":39,"end":41,"say":"cu a"},{"start":78,"end":88,"say":"otoméishon"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d08-en","day":8,"lang":"en","title":"Your first eval harness with promptfoo","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d08-en.mp4","duration":428.609,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":15.029,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 8 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":3.8,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Your first eval harness with promptfoo.","start":4.35,"end":6.907,"terms":[{"start":29,"end":38,"say":"prompt foo"}]}],[{"text":"A real eval harness for a bug classifier: versioned prompt, dataset, asserts, repeated runs and a two-model comparison.","start":7.457,"end":15.029,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":15.829,"end":37.858,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"So far we've talked about evals as an idea.","start":16.329,"end":19.13,"terms":[]},{"text":"Today you build one that works, end to end, on a problem any QA team recognizes: classifying bug reports by severity.","start":19.43,"end":27.339,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's a good first case because the output is small, the criteria are debatable and mistakes have clear consequences: a critical bug classified as low gets lost in the backlog.","start":27.639,"end":37.858,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":38.658,"end":56.518,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"You will write the prompt as a file, build the config, label ten cases with your own judgment, run everything several times and read the failures.","start":39.158,"end":47.624,"terms":[]},{"text":"The part that teaches the most isn't the tool but reading failures: that's where you find out whether the problem lies in the model, in the prompt or in your own labels.","start":47.924,"end":56.518,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":57.318,"end":67.75,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":57.818,"end":59.516,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The qa-ai-lab repo with promptfoo (day 4).","start":60.066,"end":63.378,"terms":[{"start":24,"end":33,"say":"prompt foo"}]}],[{"text":"Ollama with llama3.2; optionally a second model.","start":63.928,"end":67.75,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":68.55,"end":74.847,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":69.05,"end":70.33,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":70.63,"end":74.847,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":75.647,"end":78.413,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":76.147,"end":78.413,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":79.213,"end":81.98,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":79.713,"end":81.98,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":82.78,"end":121.881,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":83.28,"end":84.316,"terms":[]},{"text":"The prompt as a file.","start":84.616,"end":86.116,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Write the prompt in its own file, with classification rules and output format.","start":86.666,"end":91.301,"terms":[]},{"text":"The prompt is code: version it.","start":91.601,"end":93.786,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Write the prompt under prompts/ in three parts: the role, the classification rules and the exact output format.","start":94.336,"end":100.817,"terms":[]},{"text":"The rules must be yours, written the way you'd explain them to someone new: what makes a bug critical, what makes it low.","start":101.117,"end":108.272,"terms":[]},{"text":"The more explicit they are, the less the model interprets.","start":108.572,"end":111.953,"terms":[]},{"text":"The output format is written down too, with the allowed values.","start":112.253,"end":116.087,"terms":[]},{"text":"Keeping it in a separate file lets you see in a diff which word changed when quality goes up or down.","start":116.387,"end":121.881,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":122.681,"end":161.688,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":123.181,"end":124.379,"terms":[]},{"text":"The minimal config.","start":124.679,"end":126.133,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Create the config: prompt, provider and a defaultTest with the format assert that applies to every case.","start":126.683,"end":132.758,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The config joins three things: which prompt, against which provider and with which cases.","start":133.308,"end":139.081,"terms":[]},{"text":"The defaultTest block is the most useful piece to start with: it holds the format assert that applies to every case without repeating it.","start":139.381,"end":147.092,"terms":[]},{"text":"If the output isn't valid JSON with an allowed severity, the case fails there and nothing else is evaluated.","start":147.392,"end":153.548,"terms":[]},{"text":"Keep the config short.","start":153.848,"end":155.349,"terms":[]},{"text":"A file that fits on one screen gets read by the whole team; a three-hundred-line one, by nobody.","start":155.649,"end":161.688,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":162.488,"end":203.506,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":162.988,"end":164.071,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ten cases labeled by you.","start":164.371,"end":166.37,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Load at least 10 cases labeled by you: 3 critical, 3 low and 4 ambiguous.","start":166.92,"end":172.322,"terms":[]},{"text":"The ambiguous ones teach the most.","start":172.622,"end":175.062,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pick ten real or realistic reports and write the expected severity before running anything.","start":175.612,"end":180.932,"terms":[]},{"text":"The mix matters: three clearly critical, three clearly low and four ambiguous.","start":181.232,"end":186.181,"terms":[]},{"text":"The clear ones verify that the basics work.","start":186.481,"end":189.154,"terms":[]},{"text":"The ambiguous ones discriminate: a report saying \"the app closes\" without saying in which flow, or a cosmetic one on the payment screen.","start":189.454,"end":197.421,"terms":[]},{"text":"If labeling a case is hard, note why.","start":197.721,"end":200.382,"terms":[]},{"text":"That doubt is usually a rule missing from the prompt.","start":200.682,"end":203.506,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":204.306,"end":242.931,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":204.806,"end":205.819,"terms":[]},{"text":"Three runs per case.","start":206.119,"end":207.631,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Run with --repeat 3.","start":208.181,"end":210.528,"terms":[]},{"text":"A case that passes one out of three times is not passing.","start":210.828,"end":214.453,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A single run says nothing about a system that varies.","start":215.003,"end":218.292,"terms":[]},{"text":"With three repetitions each case lands in one of three situations: always passes, always fails or is unstable.","start":218.592,"end":225.99,"terms":[]},{"text":"The unstable ones are the most interesting, because they show where the prompt leaves room for interpretation.","start":226.29,"end":232.016,"terms":[]},{"text":"Treat a case that passes one time in three as failed: in production that case will be misclassified a good part of the time.","start":232.316,"end":238.82,"terms":[]},{"text":"Note the total time and cost of the run, which you will need later.","start":239.12,"end":242.931,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":243.731,"end":285.376,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":244.231,"end":245.36,"terms":[]},{"text":"Reading failures one by one.","start":245.66,"end":247.787,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Open the viewer and read failures one by one.","start":248.337,"end":251.266,"terms":[]},{"text":"Classify them: model error, prompt error or labeling error.","start":251.566,"end":255.748,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Open the viewer and read each failure with its full input and output.","start":256.298,"end":260.352,"terms":[]},{"text":"Classify it under one of three causes.","start":260.652,"end":263.093,"terms":[]},{"text":"Model error: the rules were clear and it didn't follow them.","start":263.393,"end":266.728,"terms":[]},{"text":"Prompt error: the rule was missing or ambiguous.","start":267.028,"end":270.142,"terms":[]},{"text":"Labeling error: on rereading, the model's answer is defensible and your label isn't.","start":270.442,"end":275.541,"terms":[]},{"text":"The third cause shows up more than you'd expect, and finding it improves your judgment.","start":275.841,"end":280.616,"terms":[]},{"text":"Fix the prompt and the labels first, and only then think about changing models.","start":280.916,"end":285.376,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":286.176,"end":321.875,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":286.676,"end":287.863,"terms":[]},{"text":"A second provider.","start":288.163,"end":289.64,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Add a second provider and compare quality, latency and cost side by side.","start":290.19,"end":295.185,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Add another model to the providers list and run again.","start":295.735,"end":298.664,"terms":[]},{"text":"The viewer shows both side by side on the same cases.","start":298.964,"end":302.275,"terms":[]},{"text":"Compare three things: pass rate, latency and cost per run.","start":302.575,"end":306.676,"terms":[]},{"text":"Often the larger model wins by a little on quality and loses by a lot on the other two.","start":306.976,"end":311.646,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ten cases aren't enough for a final decision, but they are enough to rule options out and to have, for the first time, a comparison based on your data and not on a public leaderboard.","start":311.946,"end":321.875,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":322.675,"end":361.943,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":323.175,"end":324.281,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A QA lead builds the eval with ten reports from her backlog.","start":324.831,"end":328.711,"terms":[]},{"text":"In the first run, with three repetitions, seven cases always pass, one always fails and two are unstable.","start":329.011,"end":336.014,"terms":[]},{"text":"The one that always fails is a labeling error: she had marked it low, but it affects login and the model marked it critical with good reason.","start":336.314,"end":343.875,"terms":[]},{"text":"The two unstable ones are reports with no reproduction steps.","start":344.175,"end":348.392,"terms":[]},{"text":"She adds a rule to the prompt: \"if data is missing to decide, classify as medium and ask for the steps\".","start":348.692,"end":354.778,"terms":[]},{"text":"In the second run, all ten cases pass three times.","start":355.078,"end":358.75,"terms":[]},{"text":"The change was one prompt line and one label.","start":359.05,"end":361.943,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":362.743,"end":380.236,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":363.243,"end":364.906,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Labeling cases after seeing the model's answer.","start":365.456,"end":368.257,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Using only easy cases and concluding the prompt works.","start":368.807,"end":372.362,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Evaluating content when the format already failed.","start":372.912,"end":376.456,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Switching models before reviewing the prompt and the labels.","start":377.006,"end":380.236,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":381.036,"end":400.259,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":381.536,"end":383.779,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The prompt is in a file with rules and output format.","start":384.329,"end":387.49,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"There are ten labeled cases, four of them ambiguous.","start":388.04,"end":391.595,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each case ran three times and you know which are unstable.","start":392.145,"end":395.712,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each failure has an assigned cause: model, prompt or label.","start":396.262,"end":400.259,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":401.059,"end":410.81,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":401.559,"end":403.454,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Small models wrap JSON in extra text.","start":404.004,"end":406.874,"terms":[]},{"text":"Validate format before content or you'll misdiagnose.","start":407.174,"end":410.81,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":411.61,"end":427.109,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 8.","start":412.11,"end":413.379,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":413.679,"end":419.66,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 9: Agent harness for QA: rules, skills and MCP in your automation repo.","start":420.21,"end":427.109,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d09-es","day":9,"lang":"es","title":"Agent harness para QA: reglas, skills y MCP en tu repo de automation","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d09-es.mp4","duration":388.669,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":15.505,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 9 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.475,"terms":[{"start":29,"end":31,"say":"cu a"},{"start":34,"end":36,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Agent harness para QA: reglas, skills y MCP en tu repo de automation.","start":4.025,"end":7.94,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":5,"say":"éiyent"},{"start":6,"end":13,"say":"járnes"},{"start":19,"end":21,"say":"cu a"},{"start":58,"end":68,"say":"otoméishon"}]}],[{"text":"Cómo preparar un repo de automation para que cualquier agente de código escriba tests siguiendo tus convenciones, con herramientas y permisos acotados.","start":8.49,"end":15.505,"terms":[{"start":25,"end":35,"say":"otoméishon"}]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":16.305,"end":35.362,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Darle a un agente de código acceso a tu repositorio de automatización sin configurarlo es como sumar a alguien al equipo sin contarle cómo se trabaja.","start":16.805,"end":24.064,"terms":[]},{"text":"Va a escribir tests, pero con selectores CSS frágiles, sin tus Page Objects, con esperas fijas y sin correrlos.","start":24.364,"end":30.346,"terms":[{"start":63,"end":67,"say":"peich"}]},{"text":"Después la conclusión suele ser que \"el agente no sirve\", cuando el problema es que nadie le dio contexto.","start":30.646,"end":35.362,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":36.162,"end":53.721,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Esta guía arma ese contexto en cinco piezas: reglas, skills, herramientas, permisos y un gate.","start":36.662,"end":41.912,"terms":[{"start":89,"end":93,"say":"gueit"}]},{"text":"Cada una responde a un fallo concreto que vas a ver si no está.","start":42.212,"end":45.501,"terms":[]},{"text":"El resultado es un repo donde un agente puede producir un test que respeta tus convenciones, y donde vos podés comprobarlo con un comando en lugar de confiar en su palabra.","start":45.801,"end":53.721,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":54.521,"end":63.397,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":55.021,"end":56.951,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un repo con Playwright (día 4).","start":57.501,"end":59.396,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":22,"say":"pléirait"}]}],[{"text":"Un agente de código: Claude Code, Copilot agent, Cursor u otro.","start":59.946,"end":63.397,"terms":[{"start":21,"end":27,"say":"clod"},{"start":28,"end":32,"say":"coud"},{"start":42,"end":47,"say":"éiyent"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":64.197,"end":69.518,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":64.697,"end":65.872,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":66.172,"end":69.518,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":70.318,"end":72.957,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":70.818,"end":72.957,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":73.757,"end":113.114,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":74.257,"end":75.154,"terms":[]},{"text":"Reglas en la raíz del repo.","start":75.454,"end":76.966,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Reglas: creá AGENTS.md en la raíz con cómo correr, convenciones y qué significa \"terminado\".","start":77.516,"end":82.441,"terms":[{"start":13,"end":19,"say":"éiyents"}]},{"text":"Si usás Claude Code, el archivo equivalente es CLAUDE.md.","start":82.741,"end":86.064,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":14,"say":"clod"},{"start":15,"end":19,"say":"coud"},{"start":47,"end":53,"say":"clod"}]}],[{"text":"El archivo de reglas es lo primero que el agente lee en cada sesión, así que tiene que ser corto y concreto.","start":86.614,"end":91.911,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tres secciones alcanzan: cómo correr los tests, qué convenciones seguir y qué significa terminado.","start":92.211,"end":97.125,"terms":[]},{"text":"En convenciones, escribí lo que le marcarías a una persona en un code review: locators por rol, sin esperas fijas, un Page Object por pantalla.","start":97.425,"end":104.753,"terms":[{"start":65,"end":69,"say":"coud"},{"start":70,"end":76,"say":"riviú"},{"start":118,"end":122,"say":"peich"}]},{"text":"Evitá los párrafos de filosofía.","start":105.053,"end":106.96,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cada línea debería poder verificarse mirando un diff, y cada regla nueva debería nacer de un error que ya viste.","start":107.26,"end":113.114,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":113.914,"end":147.069,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":114.414,"end":115.31,"terms":[]},{"text":"Skills para lo que se repite.","start":115.61,"end":117.111,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Skills: pasá a un archivo los procedimientos que repetís, por ejemplo \"cómo escribir un test nuevo\" con tu estructura de Page Objects.","start":117.661,"end":124.037,"terms":[{"start":121,"end":125,"say":"peich"}]}],[{"text":"Hay procedimientos que no entran en una regla de una línea: cómo crear un test nuevo con su Page Object, cómo agregar un dato de prueba, cómo investigar un fallo en CI.","start":124.587,"end":132.45,"terms":[{"start":92,"end":96,"say":"peich"},{"start":165,"end":167,"say":"ci ai"}]},{"text":"Escribilos como archivos separados, paso a paso, con un ejemplo real del repo.","start":132.75,"end":136.746,"terms":[]},{"text":"El agente los carga cuando la tarea lo pide, sin ocupar contexto el resto del tiempo.","start":137.046,"end":141.275,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un buen indicio de que algo merece una skill es que ya se lo explicaste dos veces, a una persona o a un agente.","start":141.575,"end":147.069,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":147.869,"end":185.03,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":148.369,"end":149.266,"terms":[]},{"text":"Herramientas para ver la aplicación.","start":149.566,"end":151.507,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Herramientas: declará el servidor MCP de Playwright en .mcp.json para que el agente pueda abrir la app y mirar la página real.","start":152.057,"end":158.805,"terms":[{"start":41,"end":51,"say":"pléirait"}]}],[{"text":"Sin acceso a un navegador, el agente escribe selectores de memoria, adivinando la estructura de la página.","start":159.355,"end":164.757,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con el servidor MCP de Playwright puede abrir la aplicación, leer los roles y nombres reales de los elementos y escribir locators que existen.","start":165.057,"end":171.839,"terms":[{"start":23,"end":33,"say":"pléirait"}]},{"text":"Declararlo en .mcp.json dentro del repo hace que todo el equipo tenga la misma configuración.","start":172.139,"end":177.808,"terms":[]},{"text":"Apuntalo siempre a un entorno de prueba: la herramienta le da al agente la capacidad de hacer clic, y eso incluye botones que no querés que toque.","start":178.108,"end":185.03,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":185.83,"end":222.585,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":186.33,"end":187.331,"terms":[]},{"text":"Permisos antes del primer uso.","start":187.631,"end":189.538,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Permisos: negá lo destructivo (borrar, push, tocar .env) y pedí confirmación para instalar dependencias.","start":190.088,"end":195.895,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Definí qué no puede hacer antes de pedirle nada.","start":196.445,"end":198.967,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lo mínimo es denegar acciones destructivas o irreversibles: borrar archivos fuera de la carpeta de tests, hacer push, leer o modificar .env.","start":199.267,"end":206.747,"terms":[]},{"text":"Instalar dependencias debería pedir confirmación, porque cambia el proyecto para todos.","start":207.047,"end":211.763,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los permisos no reemplazan a la revisión, pero achican el daño posible de un error.","start":212.063,"end":216.477,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pensalo como los accesos de alguien que recién ingresa: puede proponer cualquier cosa y ejecutar pocas sin que alguien mire.","start":216.777,"end":222.585,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":223.385,"end":256.32,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":223.885,"end":224.886,"terms":[]},{"text":"El gate es un comando.","start":225.186,"end":226.628,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":7,"say":"gueit"}]}],[{"text":"Gate: \"terminado\" se verifica con un comando y su exit code, no con el resumen del agente.","start":227.178,"end":231.732,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":4,"say":"gueit"},{"start":55,"end":59,"say":"coud"}]}],[{"text":"Escribí en las reglas qué comando define que el trabajo está terminado, por ejemplo correr el test nuevo tres veces seguidas sin fallos.","start":232.282,"end":238.461,"terms":[]},{"text":"Y verificalo vos, o tu pipeline, mirando el código de salida.","start":238.761,"end":242.05,"terms":[{"start":23,"end":31,"say":"páiplain"}]},{"text":"Un agente puede escribir \"todos los tests pasan\" sin haberlos corrido, o habiéndolos corrido con un filtro que excluye el que falla.","start":242.35,"end":248.238,"terms":[]},{"text":"No es mala intención, es un resumen optimista.","start":248.538,"end":251.304,"terms":[]},{"text":"La diferencia entre un flujo confiable y uno que no lo es está en quién ejecuta la verificación final.","start":251.604,"end":256.32,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":257.12,"end":293.927,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":257.62,"end":258.587,"terms":[]},{"text":"Probar y realimentar.","start":258.887,"end":260.271,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Probalo: pedí un test nuevo y revisá si respetó locators, Page Objects y las tres corridas.","start":260.821,"end":265.56,"terms":[{"start":58,"end":62,"say":"peich"}]},{"text":"Lo que no respetó vuelve a AGENTS.md como regla más clara.","start":265.86,"end":269.091,"terms":[{"start":27,"end":33,"say":"éiyents"}]}],[{"text":"Pedile un test nuevo para un flujo simple y revisá el resultado contra tus reglas: ¿usó locators por rol?, ¿creó o reutilizó el Page Object?, ¿corrió las tres veces?","start":269.641,"end":278.205,"terms":[{"start":128,"end":132,"say":"peich"}]},{"text":"Cada cosa que haya salteado es una regla poco clara o que falta.","start":278.505,"end":281.747,"terms":[]},{"text":"Corregí el archivo, no solo el test, porque el archivo es lo que mejora la próxima sesión.","start":282.047,"end":286.589,"terms":[]},{"text":"Después de cuatro o cinco iteraciones, las reglas reflejan cómo trabaja tu equipo de verdad, y sirven también para sumar personas.","start":286.889,"end":293.927,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":294.727,"end":327.843,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":295.227,"end":296.147,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un equipo le pide al agente un test de \"agregar producto al carrito\" sin ninguna configuración.","start":296.697,"end":301.355,"terms":[]},{"text":"El resultado usa selectores como div.card:nth-child(3) button, una espera de dos segundos y ningún Page Object.","start":301.655,"end":307.823,"terms":[{"start":99,"end":103,"say":"peich"}]},{"text":"Escriben un archivo de reglas de veinte líneas, una skill para tests nuevos y declaran el servidor MCP.","start":308.123,"end":313.455,"terms":[]},{"text":"Repiten el pedido.","start":313.755,"end":314.965,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ahora el test usa getByRole, reutiliza el Page Object del carrito y el agente lo corre tres veces.","start":315.265,"end":320.341,"terms":[{"start":42,"end":46,"say":"peich"}]},{"text":"Se saltea una cosa: no agrega el test al tag de smoke.","start":320.641,"end":323.639,"terms":[{"start":48,"end":53,"say":"smouk"}]},{"text":"Suman esa línea a las reglas.","start":323.939,"end":325.741,"terms":[]},{"text":"La tercera vez sale completo.","start":326.041,"end":327.843,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":328.643,"end":345.613,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":329.143,"end":330.667,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Escribir un archivo de reglas largo y general que nadie puede verificar.","start":331.217,"end":334.795,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Corregir el test a mano sin actualizar la regla que faltaba.","start":335.345,"end":338.343,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Dar permisos amplios \"para probar\" en un repo real.","start":338.893,"end":341.601,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Aceptar el resumen del agente como evidencia de que los tests pasan.","start":342.151,"end":345.613,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":346.413,"end":364.185,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":346.913,"end":349.18,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El archivo de reglas tiene cómo correr, convenciones y definición de terminado.","start":349.73,"end":353.587,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Hay al menos una skill con un ejemplo real del repo.","start":354.137,"end":356.868,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El servidor MCP apunta a un entorno de prueba.","start":357.418,"end":360.079,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El test generado pasó tres veces en una corrida que ejecutaste vos.","start":360.629,"end":364.185,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":364.985,"end":374.133,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":365.485,"end":367.055,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Ante un test rojo, tiende a debilitar el assert en lugar de investigar.","start":367.605,"end":371.113,"terms":[]},{"text":"Hay que prohibirlo por escrito y revisarlo en el diff.","start":371.413,"end":374.133,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":374.933,"end":387.169,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 9.","start":375.433,"end":376.794,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":377.094,"end":382.333,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 10: Playwright MCP: un agente que navega tu app y propone tests.","start":382.883,"end":387.169,"terms":[{"start":21,"end":31,"say":"pléirait"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d09-en","day":9,"lang":"en","title":"Agent harness for QA: rules, skills and MCP in your automation repo","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d09-en.mp4","duration":442.505,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":17.525,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 9 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":3.974,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Agent harness for QA: rules, skills and MCP in your automation repo.","start":4.524,"end":9.74,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"How to prepare an automation repo so any coding agent writes tests following your conventions, with scoped tools and permissions.","start":10.29,"end":17.525,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":18.325,"end":41.98,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Giving a coding agent access to your automation repository without configuring it is like adding someone to the team without telling them how you work.","start":18.825,"end":27.187,"terms":[]},{"text":"It will write tests, but with brittle CSS selectors, without your Page Objects, with fixed waits and without running them.","start":27.487,"end":35.164,"terms":[]},{"text":"The conclusion afterwards is usually that \"the agent is no good\", when the problem is that nobody gave it context.","start":35.464,"end":41.98,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":42.78,"end":61.662,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This guide builds that context in five pieces: rules, skills, tools, permissions and a gate.","start":43.28,"end":49.122,"terms":[]},{"text":"Each one answers a concrete failure you'll see when it's missing.","start":49.422,"end":53.163,"terms":[]},{"text":"The result is a repo where an agent can produce a test that follows your conventions, and where you can confirm it with a command instead of taking its word.","start":53.463,"end":61.662,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":62.462,"end":72.627,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":62.962,"end":64.66,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A repo with Playwright (day 4).","start":65.21,"end":67.616,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A coding agent: Claude Code, Copilot agent, Cursor or another.","start":68.166,"end":72.627,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":73.427,"end":79.724,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":73.927,"end":75.207,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":75.507,"end":79.724,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":80.524,"end":83.29,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":81.024,"end":83.29,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":84.09,"end":129.24,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":84.59,"end":85.626,"terms":[]},{"text":"Rules at the repo root.","start":85.926,"end":87.519,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Rules: create AGENTS.md at the root with how to run, conventions and what \"done\" means.","start":88.069,"end":94.063,"terms":[]},{"text":"With Claude Code, the equivalent file is CLAUDE.md.","start":94.363,"end":98.173,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The rules file is the first thing the agent reads in every session, so it has to be short and concrete.","start":98.723,"end":104.636,"terms":[]},{"text":"Three sections are enough: how to run the tests, which conventions to follow and what done means.","start":104.936,"end":110.476,"terms":[]},{"text":"Under conventions, write what you would flag to a person in a code review: role-based locators, no fixed waits, one Page Object per screen.","start":110.776,"end":119.904,"terms":[]},{"text":"Avoid paragraphs of philosophy.","start":120.204,"end":122.552,"terms":[]},{"text":"Each line should be verifiable by looking at a diff, and each new rule should come from a mistake you've already seen.","start":122.852,"end":129.24,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":130.04,"end":168.443,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":130.54,"end":131.739,"terms":[]},{"text":"Skills for what repeats.","start":132.039,"end":133.76,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Skills: move repeated procedures into a file, for example \"how to write a new test\" with your Page Object structure.","start":134.31,"end":141.383,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Some procedures don't fit in a one-line rule: how to create a new test with its Page Object, how to add test data, how to investigate a CI failure.","start":141.933,"end":151.026,"terms":[]},{"text":"Write them as separate files, step by step, with a real example from the repo.","start":151.326,"end":156.216,"terms":[]},{"text":"The agent loads them when the task calls for it, without taking up context the rest of the time.","start":156.516,"end":161.883,"terms":[]},{"text":"A good sign that something deserves a skill is that you've already explained it twice, to a person or to an agent.","start":162.183,"end":168.443,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":169.243,"end":209.26,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":169.743,"end":170.825,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tools to see the application.","start":171.125,"end":173.171,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tools: declare the Playwright MCP server in .mcp.json so the agent can open the app and look at the real page.","start":173.721,"end":180.946,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Without access to a browser, the agent writes selectors from memory, guessing the page structure.","start":181.496,"end":187.28,"terms":[]},{"text":"With the Playwright MCP server it can open the application, read the real roles and names of elements and write locators that exist.","start":187.58,"end":195.373,"terms":[]},{"text":"Declaring it in .mcp.json inside the repo gives the whole team the same setup.","start":195.673,"end":201.225,"terms":[]},{"text":"Always point it at a test environment: the tool gives the agent the ability to click, and that includes buttons you don't want it to touch.","start":201.525,"end":209.26,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":210.06,"end":253.56,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":210.56,"end":211.573,"terms":[]},{"text":"Permissions before first use.","start":211.873,"end":214.162,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Permissions: deny destructive actions (delete, push, touching .env) and require confirmation to install dependencies.","start":214.712,"end":222.54,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Define what it can't do before asking for anything.","start":223.09,"end":226.111,"terms":[]},{"text":"The minimum is denying destructive or irreversible actions: deleting files outside the tests folder, pushing, reading or modifying .env.","start":226.411,"end":235.075,"terms":[]},{"text":"Installing dependencies should ask for confirmation, because it changes the project for everyone.","start":235.375,"end":241.113,"terms":[]},{"text":"Permissions don't replace review, but they shrink the possible damage of a mistake.","start":241.413,"end":246.199,"terms":[]},{"text":"Think of it as the access of someone who just joined: free to propose anything and to execute few things without someone looking.","start":246.499,"end":253.56,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":254.36,"end":293.972,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":254.86,"end":255.989,"terms":[]},{"text":"The gate is a command.","start":256.289,"end":257.801,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Gate: \"done\" is verified with a command and its exit code, not with the agent's summary.","start":258.351,"end":263.718,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Write in the rules which command defines that the work is done, for example running the new test three times in a row with no failures.","start":264.268,"end":271.654,"terms":[]},{"text":"And verify it yourself, or have your pipeline do it, by looking at the exit code.","start":271.954,"end":277.483,"terms":[]},{"text":"An agent can write \"all tests pass\" without having run them, or having run them with a filter that excludes the failing one.","start":277.783,"end":284.438,"terms":[]},{"text":"It isn't bad intent, it's an optimistic summary.","start":284.738,"end":287.91,"terms":[]},{"text":"The difference between a trustworthy flow and one that isn't lies in who runs the final verification.","start":288.21,"end":293.972,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":294.772,"end":336.826,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":295.272,"end":296.459,"terms":[]},{"text":"Try it and feed back.","start":296.759,"end":298.398,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Try it: ask for a new test and check whether it honored locators, Page Objects and the three runs.","start":298.948,"end":305.394,"terms":[]},{"text":"Whatever it missed goes back into AGENTS.md as a clearer rule.","start":305.694,"end":309.911,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ask for a new test for a simple flow and check the result against your rules: did it use role-based locators? did it create or reuse the Page Object? did it run three times?","start":310.461,"end":321.429,"terms":[]},{"text":"Whatever it skipped is an unclear or missing rule.","start":321.729,"end":324.588,"terms":[]},{"text":"Fix the file, not just the test, because the file is what improves the next session.","start":324.888,"end":329.929,"terms":[]},{"text":"After four or five iterations the rules reflect how your team really works, and they also help when onboarding people.","start":330.229,"end":336.826,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":337.626,"end":374.62,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":338.126,"end":339.232,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A team asks the agent for an \"add product to cart\" test with no setup at all.","start":339.782,"end":344.347,"terms":[]},{"text":"The result uses selectors such as div.card:nth-child(3) button, a two-second wait and no Page Object.","start":344.647,"end":351.86,"terms":[]},{"text":"They write a twenty-line rules file, a skill for new tests and declare the MCP server.","start":352.16,"end":357.375,"terms":[]},{"text":"They repeat the request.","start":357.675,"end":359.257,"terms":[]},{"text":"Now the test uses getByRole, reuses the cart Page Object and the agent runs it three times.","start":359.557,"end":365.678,"terms":[]},{"text":"It skips one thing: it doesn't add the test to the smoke tag.","start":365.978,"end":369.835,"terms":[]},{"text":"They add that line to the rules.","start":370.135,"end":372.053,"terms":[]},{"text":"The third time it comes out complete.","start":372.353,"end":374.62,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":375.42,"end":393.911,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":375.92,"end":377.583,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Writing a long, generic rules file nobody can verify.","start":378.133,"end":381.897,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Fixing the test by hand without updating the missing rule.","start":382.447,"end":385.793,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Granting broad permissions \"just to try\" on a real repo.","start":386.343,"end":389.771,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Accepting the agent's summary as evidence that tests pass.","start":390.321,"end":393.911,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":394.711,"end":414.421,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":395.211,"end":397.454,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The rules file has how to run, conventions and a definition of done.","start":398.004,"end":402.082,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"There is at least one skill with a real example from the repo.","start":402.632,"end":406.431,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The MCP server points to a test environment.","start":406.981,"end":409.898,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The generated test passed three times in a run you executed yourself.","start":410.448,"end":414.421,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":415.221,"end":425.461,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":415.721,"end":417.616,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Facing a red test, it tends to weaken the assertion instead of investigating.","start":418.166,"end":422.604,"terms":[]},{"text":"Forbid it in writing and check it in the diff.","start":422.904,"end":425.461,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":426.261,"end":441.005,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 9.","start":426.761,"end":428.145,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":428.445,"end":434.427,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 10: Playwright MCP: an agent that browses your app and proposes tests.","start":434.977,"end":441.005,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d10-es","day":10,"lang":"es","title":"Playwright MCP: un agente que navega tu app y propone tests","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d10-es.mp4","duration":393.549,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":14.762,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 10 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.394,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Playwright MCP: un agente que navega tu app y propone tests.","start":3.944,"end":7.162,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":10,"say":"pléirait"}]}],[{"text":"Conectá Playwright MCP a tu agente, hacé una exploración guiada y terminá con un plan de pruebas y un test que usa los roles reales de la página.","start":7.712,"end":14.762,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":18,"say":"pléirait"}]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":15.562,"end":33.33,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Durante años, generar tests de UI con IA significó pegar HTML en un chat y recibir selectores adivinados.","start":16.062,"end":22.055,"terms":[{"start":31,"end":33,"say":"u i"},{"start":38,"end":40,"say":"i a"}]},{"text":"El modelo nunca veía la aplicación funcionando: no sabía qué aparece después de un clic, qué botón queda deshabilitado ni cuánto tarda en cargar una lista.","start":22.355,"end":29.626,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los tests resultantes compilaban y fallaban en la primera corrida.","start":29.926,"end":33.33,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":34.13,"end":53.895,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"El servidor MCP de Playwright cambia ese punto de partida: le da al agente un navegador que puede manejar.","start":34.63,"end":39.973,"terms":[{"start":19,"end":29,"say":"pléirait"}]},{"text":"Navega, hace clic, completa formularios y lee el estado real de la página.","start":40.273,"end":44.107,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta guía muestra cómo usarlo con orden, primero para explorar y recién al final para escribir código, y qué límites ponerle, porque un agente que lee páginas también lee lo que alguien haya escrito en ellas.","start":44.407,"end":53.895,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":54.695,"end":68.129,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":55.195,"end":57.125,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Node.js 22+ y un agente compatible con MCP.","start":57.675,"end":61.01,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":7,"say":"noud yei es"}]}],[{"text":"App de práctica: https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc.","start":61.56,"end":65.707,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":40,"say":"pléirait"}]}],[{"text":"Solo ambientes y cuentas de prueba.","start":66.257,"end":68.129,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":68.929,"end":74.251,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":69.429,"end":70.604,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":70.904,"end":74.251,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":75.051,"end":77.69,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":75.551,"end":77.69,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":78.49,"end":114.444,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":78.99,"end":79.886,"terms":[]},{"text":"Conectar y comprobar.","start":80.186,"end":81.477,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Conectá el servidor MCP de Playwright a tu agente y verificá que aparezcan las herramientas del navegador.","start":82.027,"end":87.08,"terms":[{"start":27,"end":37,"say":"pléirait"}]}],[{"text":"Agregá el servidor a tu agente con el comando de la guía y, antes de pedir nada, listá las herramientas disponibles.","start":87.63,"end":93.508,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tienen que aparecer las de navegador: navegar, hacer clic, escribir, tomar un snapshot.","start":93.808,"end":98.478,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si no aparecen, el agente va a responder igual, pero de memoria, y vas a creer que exploró la página cuando no lo hizo.","start":98.778,"end":104.91,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esa comprobación de diez segundos evita la confusión más común.","start":105.21,"end":108.382,"terms":[]},{"text":"Después pedile algo trivial, como abrir una URL y decir el título, para confirmar que el navegador responde.","start":108.682,"end":114.444,"terms":[{"start":44,"end":47,"say":"u erre ele"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":115.244,"end":152.174,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":115.744,"end":116.64,"terms":[]},{"text":"Qué ve el agente.","start":116.94,"end":118.057,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Entendé qué ve el agente: un snapshot de accesibilidad con roles y nombres, no una imagen.","start":118.607,"end":123.208,"terms":[]},{"text":"Por eso sus locators salen mejor.","start":123.508,"end":125.426,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"No ve una captura de pantalla sino un snapshot de accesibilidad: un árbol de texto con el rol de cada elemento, su nombre accesible y su estado.","start":125.976,"end":133.084,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un botón aparece como botón con su etiqueta, un campo como textbox con su label.","start":133.384,"end":137.508,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tiene dos consecuencias.","start":137.808,"end":139.32,"terms":[]},{"text":"La primera es que los locators que propone se basan en roles y nombres, que son más estables que las clases CSS.","start":139.62,"end":145.277,"terms":[]},{"text":"La segunda es que una página con mala accesibilidad le resulta tan difícil como a un lector de pantalla, y ese ya es un hallazgo.","start":145.577,"end":152.174,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":152.974,"end":188.58,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":153.474,"end":154.37,"terms":[]},{"text":"Explorar sin escribir código.","start":154.67,"end":156.38,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Explorá primero, sin código.","start":156.93,"end":158.743,"terms":[]},{"text":"Usá el prompt de exploración y leé el reporte de roles, estados y posibles defectos.","start":159.043,"end":163.504,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pedile que recorra un flujo y que reporte lo que encuentra, con la instrucción explícita de no escribir tests todavía.","start":164.054,"end":169.421,"terms":[]},{"text":"El reporte debería listar los elementos por rol, los estados que observó, como listas vacías, errores o elementos deshabilitados, y lo que le pareció raro.","start":169.721,"end":177.537,"terms":[]},{"text":"Leelo como leerías las notas de alguien que usa el producto por primera vez.","start":177.837,"end":181.648,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sirve para descubrir estados que no tenías en mente.","start":181.948,"end":184.562,"terms":[]},{"text":"No sirve como lista de bugs: el agente no conoce tus reglas de negocio.","start":184.862,"end":188.58,"terms":[{"start":23,"end":27,"say":"bags"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":189.38,"end":223.442,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":189.88,"end":190.881,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un plan en Markdown, corregido por vos.","start":191.181,"end":193.425,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":19,"say":"márkdaun"}]}],[{"text":"Pedí un plan de pruebas en Markdown a partir de lo observado y corregilo vos: prioridades y casos faltantes.","start":193.975,"end":199.562,"terms":[{"start":27,"end":35,"say":"márkdaun"}]}],[{"text":"Con la exploración hecha, pedí un plan de pruebas en Markdown basado solo en lo observado.","start":200.112,"end":204.514,"terms":[{"start":53,"end":61,"say":"márkdaun"}]},{"text":"Un plan es barato de corregir y un conjunto de tests no.","start":204.814,"end":207.708,"terms":[]},{"text":"Revisalo con tres preguntas: qué casos faltan que solo conoce quien sabe del negocio, cuáles sobran porque repiten lo mismo y cuál es la prioridad real de cada uno.","start":208.008,"end":215.929,"terms":[]},{"text":"Guardá el plan corregido en el repo.","start":216.229,"end":218.228,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es la especificación de lo que vas a pedir después, y deja registro de qué decidiste probar y qué no.","start":218.528,"end":223.442,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":224.242,"end":256.144,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":224.742,"end":225.743,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un test por vez.","start":226.043,"end":227.241,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pedí un solo test del plan, con los roles que vio, y correlo tres veces.","start":227.791,"end":231.66,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pedí un solo test del plan, indicando que use los roles y nombres que vio en la página.","start":232.21,"end":236.52,"terms":[]},{"text":"Correlo tres veces seguidas antes de pedir el siguiente.","start":236.82,"end":239.447,"terms":[]},{"text":"Generar de a uno parece lento, pero cada test que pasa confirma que las convenciones se entendieron, y cada fallo se corrige una vez en lugar de veinte.","start":239.747,"end":246.959,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cuando un test falle, mirá primero si el problema es del test o de la aplicación.","start":247.259,"end":251.348,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si el agente explora y el test falla igual, puede que haya encontrado un defecto de verdad.","start":251.648,"end":256.144,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":256.944,"end":295.801,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":257.444,"end":258.41,"terms":[]},{"text":"Límites de seguridad.","start":258.71,"end":260.083,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Fijá límites: nunca producción ni credenciales reales, y recordá que el agente lee todo el contenido de la página, incluido texto malicioso.","start":260.633,"end":267.973,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Dos reglas fijas.","start":268.523,"end":269.838,"terms":[]},{"text":"Primera: nunca producción ni credenciales reales.","start":270.138,"end":273.031,"terms":[]},{"text":"El agente puede hacer clic en cualquier cosa que vea, incluidos los botones de borrar y de pagar.","start":273.331,"end":277.908,"terms":[]},{"text":"Segunda: todo el texto de la página entra a su contexto, y eso incluye contenido escrito por terceros, como comentarios, nombres de usuario o descripciones de producto.","start":278.208,"end":287.057,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si alguien escribió ahí una instrucción, el agente puede intentar seguirla.","start":287.357,"end":291.11,"terms":[]},{"text":"Usá un entorno de prueba con datos controlados y revisá cualquier acción que no hayas pedido.","start":291.41,"end":295.801,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":296.601,"end":331.26,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":297.101,"end":298.021,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Una tester conecta el servidor y le pide al agente explorar una aplicación de lista de tareas.","start":298.571,"end":303.218,"terms":[]},{"text":"El reporte lista un campo de texto, una casilla por tarea, tres filtros y un contador.","start":303.518,"end":308.269,"terms":[]},{"text":"Marca dos sospechas: el contador dice \"1 items left\" con una sola tarea, y no hay forma visible de editar una tarea.","start":308.569,"end":314.864,"terms":[]},{"text":"La primera es un defecto de texto menor; la segunda no es un defecto, se edita con doble clic y el agente no lo descubrió.","start":315.164,"end":321.308,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ella corrige el plan agregando el caso de edición, pide el primer test, \"agregar una tarea\", y pasa tres veces.","start":321.608,"end":327.822,"terms":[]},{"text":"En veinte minutos tiene un plan revisado y un test confiable.","start":328.122,"end":331.26,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":332.06,"end":349.343,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":332.56,"end":334.083,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pedir tests antes de explorar y de tener un plan revisado.","start":334.633,"end":337.724,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"No comprobar que las herramientas de navegador estén cargadas.","start":338.274,"end":341.284,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tratar el reporte de exploración como una lista de bugs confirmados.","start":341.834,"end":345.47,"terms":[{"start":51,"end":55,"say":"bags"}]}],[{"text":"Apuntar el agente a un entorno con datos o credenciales reales.","start":346.02,"end":349.343,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":350.143,"end":367.079,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":350.643,"end":352.91,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Las herramientas de navegador aparecen en la lista del agente.","start":353.46,"end":356.528,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Hay un reporte de exploración con roles, estados y sospechas.","start":357.078,"end":360.517,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El plan en Markdown está corregido y guardado en el repo.","start":361.067,"end":363.96,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":19,"say":"márkdaun"}]}],[{"text":"Un test generado pasó tres corridas seguidas.","start":364.51,"end":367.079,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":367.879,"end":378.676,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":368.379,"end":369.949,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Confunde comportamiento esperado con defecto cuando no conoce las reglas del producto.","start":370.499,"end":374.635,"terms":[]},{"text":"El reporte de exploración es una lista de sospechas, no de bugs.","start":374.935,"end":378.676,"terms":[{"start":59,"end":63,"say":"bags"}]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":379.476,"end":392.049,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 10.","start":379.976,"end":381.371,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":381.671,"end":386.91,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 11: Playwright Test Agents: planner, generator y healer en acción.","start":387.46,"end":392.049,"terms":[{"start":21,"end":31,"say":"pléirait"},{"start":37,"end":43,"say":"éiyents"},{"start":45,"end":52,"say":"pláner"},{"start":54,"end":63,"say":"yeneréitor"},{"start":66,"end":72,"say":"jíler"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d10-en","day":10,"lang":"en","title":"Playwright MCP: an agent that browses your app and proposes tests","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d10-en.mp4","duration":445.085,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":17.873,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 10 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":3.765,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Playwright MCP: an agent that browses your app and proposes tests.","start":4.315,"end":9.182,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Connect Playwright MCP to your agent, run a guided exploration and end with a test plan and one test built on the page's real roles.","start":9.732,"end":17.873,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":18.673,"end":38.648,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"For years, generating UI tests with AI meant pasting HTML into a chat and getting guessed selectors back.","start":19.173,"end":25.724,"terms":[]},{"text":"The model never saw the application running: it didn't know what appears after a click, which button ends up disabled or how long a list takes to load.","start":26.024,"end":34.816,"terms":[]},{"text":"The resulting tests compiled and failed on the first run.","start":35.116,"end":38.648,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":39.448,"end":61.918,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The Playwright MCP server changes that starting point: it gives the agent a browser it can drive.","start":39.948,"end":45.744,"terms":[]},{"text":"It navigates, clicks, fills in forms and reads the real state of the page.","start":46.044,"end":50.702,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide shows how to use it in order, first to explore and only at the end to write code, and which limits to set, because an agent that reads pages also reads whatever someone wrote on them.","start":51.002,"end":61.918,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":62.718,"end":78.625,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":63.218,"end":64.916,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Node.js 22+ and an MCP-capable agent.","start":65.466,"end":69.183,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Practice app: https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc.","start":69.733,"end":75.529,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Test environments and test accounts only.","start":76.079,"end":78.625,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":79.425,"end":85.721,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":79.925,"end":81.204,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":81.504,"end":85.721,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":86.521,"end":89.288,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":87.021,"end":89.288,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":90.088,"end":129.351,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":90.588,"end":91.624,"terms":[]},{"text":"Connect and confirm.","start":91.924,"end":93.505,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Connect the Playwright MCP server to your agent and confirm the browser tools show up.","start":94.055,"end":99.457,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Add the server to your agent with the command in the guide and, before asking for anything, list the available tools.","start":100.007,"end":106.697,"terms":[]},{"text":"The browser ones must show up: navigate, click, type, take a snapshot.","start":106.997,"end":112.27,"terms":[]},{"text":"If they don't, the agent will answer anyway, but from memory, and you'll believe it explored the page when it didn't.","start":112.57,"end":119.109,"terms":[]},{"text":"That ten-second check prevents the most common confusion.","start":119.409,"end":122.756,"terms":[]},{"text":"Then ask for something trivial, such as opening a URL and stating the title, to confirm the browser responds.","start":123.056,"end":129.351,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":130.151,"end":171.98,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":130.651,"end":131.849,"terms":[]},{"text":"What the agent sees.","start":132.149,"end":133.603,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Understand what the agent sees: an accessibility snapshot with roles and names, not an image.","start":134.153,"end":140.39,"terms":[]},{"text":"That's why its locators come out better.","start":140.69,"end":143.061,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"It doesn't see a screenshot but an accessibility snapshot: a text tree with each element's role, its accessible name and its state.","start":143.611,"end":151.091,"terms":[]},{"text":"A button appears as a button with its label, a field as a textbox with its label.","start":151.391,"end":156.258,"terms":[]},{"text":"That has two consequences.","start":156.558,"end":158.395,"terms":[]},{"text":"First, the locators it proposes are based on roles and names, which are more stable than CSS classes.","start":158.695,"end":165.234,"terms":[]},{"text":"Second, a page with poor accessibility is as hard for it as for a screen reader, and that is already a finding.","start":165.534,"end":171.98,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":172.78,"end":213.913,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":173.28,"end":174.363,"terms":[]},{"text":"Explore without writing code.","start":174.663,"end":176.639,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Explore first, no code.","start":177.189,"end":179.525,"terms":[]},{"text":"Use the exploration prompt and read the report of roles, states and possible defects.","start":179.825,"end":185.192,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ask it to walk through a flow and report what it finds, with the explicit instruction not to write tests yet.","start":185.742,"end":191.77,"terms":[]},{"text":"The report should list elements by role, the states it observed, such as empty lists, errors or disabled elements, and whatever seemed odd.","start":192.07,"end":201.059,"terms":[]},{"text":"Read it the way you'd read the notes of someone using the product for the first time.","start":201.359,"end":205.599,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's useful for discovering states you hadn't thought of.","start":205.899,"end":209.083,"terms":[]},{"text":"It isn't useful as a bug list: the agent doesn't know your business rules.","start":209.383,"end":213.913,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":214.713,"end":255.625,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":215.213,"end":216.226,"terms":[]},{"text":"A Markdown plan, corrected by you.","start":216.526,"end":219.164,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ask for a Markdown test plan based on what it observed and fix it yourself: priorities and missing cases.","start":219.714,"end":226.404,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"With exploration done, ask for a test plan in Markdown based only on what was observed.","start":226.954,"end":232.03,"terms":[]},{"text":"A plan is cheap to correct and a set of tests isn't.","start":232.33,"end":235.584,"terms":[]},{"text":"Review it with three questions: which cases are missing that only someone who knows the business would know, which are redundant because they repeat the same thing, and what the real priority of each one is.","start":235.884,"end":246.567,"terms":[]},{"text":"Save the corrected plan in the repo.","start":246.867,"end":249.134,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's the specification of what you'll ask for next, and it records what you decided to test and what you didn't.","start":249.434,"end":255.625,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":256.425,"end":295.606,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":256.925,"end":258.053,"terms":[]},{"text":"One test at a time.","start":258.353,"end":259.842,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ask for a single test from the plan, using the roles it saw, and run it three times.","start":260.392,"end":265.735,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ask for a single test from the plan, stating that it must use the roles and names it saw on the page.","start":266.285,"end":272.488,"terms":[]},{"text":"Run it three times in a row before asking for the next one.","start":272.788,"end":276.169,"terms":[]},{"text":"Generating one at a time looks slow, but each passing test confirms the conventions were understood, and each failure gets fixed once instead of twenty times.","start":276.469,"end":285.272,"terms":[]},{"text":"When a test fails, first check whether the problem is the test or the application.","start":285.572,"end":290.381,"terms":[]},{"text":"If the agent explored and the test still fails, it may have found a real defect.","start":290.681,"end":295.606,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":296.406,"end":336.924,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":296.906,"end":298.093,"terms":[]},{"text":"Safety limits.","start":298.393,"end":299.603,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Set limits: never production or real credentials, and remember the agent reads all page content, including malicious text.","start":300.153,"end":307.691,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Two fixed rules.","start":308.241,"end":309.648,"terms":[]},{"text":"First: never production or real credentials.","start":309.948,"end":312.842,"terms":[]},{"text":"The agent can click anything it sees, including delete and pay buttons.","start":313.142,"end":317.359,"terms":[]},{"text":"Second: all page text enters its context, and that includes content written by third parties, such as comments, user names or product descriptions.","start":317.659,"end":327.332,"terms":[]},{"text":"If someone wrote an instruction there, the agent may try to follow it.","start":327.632,"end":331.606,"terms":[]},{"text":"Use a test environment with controlled data and review any action you didn't ask for.","start":331.906,"end":336.924,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":337.724,"end":377.595,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":338.224,"end":339.329,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A tester connects the server and asks the agent to explore a to-do list application.","start":339.879,"end":345.049,"terms":[]},{"text":"The report lists a text field, a checkbox per task, three filters and a counter.","start":345.349,"end":350.576,"terms":[]},{"text":"It flags two suspicions: the counter says \"1 items left\" with a single task, and there is no visible way to edit a task.","start":350.876,"end":358.599,"terms":[]},{"text":"The first is a minor copy defect; the second isn't a defect, you edit by double-clicking and the agent didn't discover it.","start":358.899,"end":366.065,"terms":[]},{"text":"She corrects the plan by adding the edit case, asks for the first test, \"add a task\", and it passes three times.","start":366.365,"end":373.473,"terms":[]},{"text":"In twenty minutes she has a reviewed plan and a trustworthy test.","start":373.773,"end":377.595,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":378.395,"end":396.875,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":378.895,"end":380.558,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Asking for tests before exploring and having a reviewed plan.","start":381.108,"end":384.756,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Not confirming that the browser tools are loaded.","start":385.306,"end":388.2,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Treating the exploration report as a list of confirmed bugs.","start":388.75,"end":392.34,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pointing the agent at an environment with real data or credentials.","start":392.89,"end":396.875,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":397.675,"end":415.469,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":398.175,"end":400.418,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The browser tools show up in the agent's list.","start":400.968,"end":403.653,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"There is an exploration report with roles, states and suspicions.","start":404.203,"end":407.967,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The Markdown plan is corrected and saved in the repo.","start":408.517,"end":411.735,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"One generated test passed three runs in a row.","start":412.285,"end":415.469,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":416.269,"end":428.204,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":416.769,"end":418.664,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"It confuses expected behavior with defects when it doesn't know the product rules.","start":419.214,"end":424.163,"terms":[]},{"text":"The exploration report is a list of suspicions, not bugs.","start":424.463,"end":428.204,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":429.004,"end":443.585,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 10.","start":429.504,"end":430.888,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":431.188,"end":437.17,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 11: Playwright Test Agents: planner, generator and healer in action.","start":437.72,"end":443.585,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d11-es","day":11,"lang":"es","title":"Playwright Test Agents: planner, generator y healer en acción","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d11-es.mp4","duration":387.856,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":14.181,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 11 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.475,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Playwright Test Agents: planner, generator y healer en acción.","start":4.025,"end":7.592,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":10,"say":"pléirait"},{"start":16,"end":22,"say":"éiyents"},{"start":24,"end":31,"say":"pláner"},{"start":33,"end":42,"say":"yeneréitor"},{"start":45,"end":51,"say":"jíler"}]}],[{"text":"El ciclo completo planner, generator y healer en un repo real, y los dos puntos donde tiene que intervenir una persona.","start":8.142,"end":14.181,"terms":[{"start":18,"end":25,"say":"pláner"},{"start":27,"end":36,"say":"yeneréitor"},{"start":39,"end":45,"say":"jíler"}]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":14.981,"end":33.713,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Ayer el flujo de explorar, planear y generar lo armaste a mano, con prompts propios.","start":15.481,"end":19.908,"terms":[]},{"text":"Playwright trae ese mismo flujo empaquetado como tres agentes con responsabilidades separadas: uno planifica, otro genera y otro repara.","start":20.208,"end":27.281,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":10,"say":"pléirait"}]},{"text":"La ventaja no es que sean más inteligentes, sino que cada uno tiene instrucciones acotadas y deja un artefacto que el siguiente usa.","start":27.581,"end":33.713,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":34.513,"end":52.654,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"El riesgo también viene empaquetado.","start":35.013,"end":36.99,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un sistema que repara tests solo puede dejar la suite en verde por la razón equivocada.","start":37.29,"end":41.542,"terms":[{"start":48,"end":53,"say":"suit"}]},{"text":"Esta guía recorre los tres agentes y marca los dos puntos donde una persona tiene que intervenir sí o sí: antes de generar y después de reparar.","start":41.842,"end":48.532,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sin esas dos revisiones, lo que ganás en velocidad lo perdés en confianza.","start":48.832,"end":52.654,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":53.454,"end":64.512,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":53.954,"end":55.884,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Playwright actualizado (los agentes vienen desde la versión 1.56).","start":56.434,"end":60.465,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":10,"say":"pléirait"}]}],[{"text":"Un agente de código: Claude Code, VS Code con Copilot u OpenCode.","start":61.015,"end":64.512,"terms":[{"start":21,"end":27,"say":"clod"},{"start":28,"end":32,"say":"coud"},{"start":34,"end":36,"say":"vi es"},{"start":37,"end":41,"say":"coud"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":65.312,"end":70.634,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":65.812,"end":66.987,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":67.287,"end":70.634,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":71.434,"end":111.208,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":71.934,"end":72.83,"terms":[]},{"text":"Inicializar y commitear.","start":73.13,"end":74.77,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Actualizá Playwright y corré init-agents con el loop de tu agente.","start":75.32,"end":78.492,"terms":[{"start":10,"end":20,"say":"pléirait"},{"start":34,"end":40,"say":"éiyents"},{"start":48,"end":52,"say":"lup"}]},{"text":"Genera las definiciones de los tres agentes, un seed.spec.ts, la carpeta specs/ y la config del servidor MCP de tests.","start":78.792,"end":85.68,"terms":[{"start":48,"end":52,"say":"sid"}]},{"text":"Commiteá todo.","start":85.98,"end":87.015,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El comando de inicialización genera las definiciones de los tres agentes para el bucle que uses, un test semilla, la carpeta de planes y la configuración del servidor MCP de tests.","start":87.565,"end":96.357,"terms":[]},{"text":"Commiteá todo eso junto.","start":96.657,"end":98.25,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las definiciones son instrucciones en texto: conviene leerlas una vez para entender qué va a hacer cada agente y con qué herramientas.","start":98.55,"end":104.741,"terms":[]},{"text":"Al estar en el repo, todo el equipo usa las mismas, y cualquier ajuste que hagas queda en el historial igual que un cambio de código.","start":105.041,"end":111.208,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":112.008,"end":146.767,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":112.508,"end":113.405,"terms":[]},{"text":"El test semilla.","start":113.705,"end":114.81,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Escribí el seed test: deja la página en el estado inicial que necesitan todos los flujos (por ejemplo, logueado con un usuario de prueba).","start":115.36,"end":121.934,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":15,"say":"sid"}]}],[{"text":"El semilla es un test común que deja la aplicación en el estado desde el cual empiezan todos los flujos: sesión iniciada, datos mínimos cargados, pantalla inicial.","start":122.484,"end":130.509,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los agentes lo ejecutan antes de explorar o generar, así que también sirve como ejemplo de estilo: los tests generados tienden a parecerse a él.","start":130.809,"end":137.894,"terms":[]},{"text":"Escribilo con el mismo cuidado que tu mejor test.","start":138.194,"end":140.728,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si usa locators por rol y fixtures claras, lo que venga después hereda eso.","start":141.028,"end":145.071,"terms":[{"start":26,"end":34,"say":"fícschers"}]},{"text":"Si es descuidado, también.","start":145.371,"end":146.767,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":147.567,"end":182.662,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":148.067,"end":148.963,"terms":[]},{"text":"Planner y la primera intervención.","start":149.263,"end":151.17,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":7,"say":"pláner"}]}],[{"text":"Planner: pedile que explore un flujo y guarde el plan en specs/.","start":151.72,"end":154.915,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":7,"say":"pláner"}]},{"text":"Intervención 1: revisá el plan antes de generar nada.","start":155.215,"end":158.062,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El planner explora la aplicación a partir del semilla y escribe un plan en Markdown dentro de la carpeta de planes: escenarios, pasos y resultados esperados.","start":158.612,"end":166.44,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":10,"say":"pláner"},{"start":75,"end":83,"say":"márkdaun"}]},{"text":"Acá va la primera intervención humana, y es la más barata de toda la cadena.","start":166.74,"end":170.736,"terms":[]},{"text":"Leé el plan antes de generar nada.","start":171.036,"end":172.908,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sacá escenarios que no aportan, sumá los que dependen de reglas de negocio y corregí los resultados esperados que el planner supuso.","start":173.208,"end":179.678,"terms":[{"start":117,"end":124,"say":"pláner"}]},{"text":"Todo error que dejes pasar se multiplica en código.","start":179.978,"end":182.662,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":183.462,"end":217.083,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":183.962,"end":184.963,"terms":[]},{"text":"Generator.","start":185.263,"end":186.23,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":9,"say":"yeneréitor"}]}],[{"text":"Generator: pedile los tests del plan aprobado.","start":186.78,"end":189.267,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":9,"say":"yeneréitor"}]},{"text":"Verifica los selectores contra la página real mientras escribe.","start":189.567,"end":192.762,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El generator toma el plan aprobado y escribe los tests.","start":193.312,"end":196.229,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":12,"say":"yeneréitor"}]},{"text":"La diferencia con un generador clásico es que verifica contra la página viva: ejecuta los pasos mientras escribe y comprueba que cada locator encuentra su elemento.","start":196.529,"end":204.24,"terms":[]},{"text":"Por eso el resultado suele correr a la primera.","start":204.54,"end":207.004,"terms":[]},{"text":"Aun así, revisá las aserciones.","start":207.304,"end":209.408,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un test puede navegar bien todo el flujo y terminar verificando algo trivial, como que la URL cambió, en lugar de la regla que el escenario quería probar.","start":209.708,"end":217.083,"terms":[{"start":90,"end":93,"say":"u erre ele"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":217.883,"end":257.567,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":218.383,"end":219.385,"terms":[]},{"text":"Healer y la segunda intervención.","start":219.685,"end":221.522,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":6,"say":"jíler"}]}],[{"text":"Healer: rompé un locator a propósito y corré el healer.","start":222.072,"end":225,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":6,"say":"jíler"},{"start":48,"end":54,"say":"jíler"}]},{"text":"Intervención 2: revisá el diff.","start":225.3,"end":227.276,"terms":[]},{"text":"Arreglar un locator está bien; cambiar una aserción no.","start":227.576,"end":230.342,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Rompé un locator a propósito, por ejemplo cambiando el nombre de un botón, y corré el healer.","start":230.892,"end":235.539,"terms":[{"start":86,"end":92,"say":"jíler"}]},{"text":"Va a ejecutar el test, ver el fallo, inspeccionar la página y proponer una corrección.","start":235.839,"end":240.497,"terms":[]},{"text":"Acá va la segunda intervención: revisar el diff.","start":240.797,"end":243.737,"terms":[]},{"text":"Hay dos tipos de cambio.","start":244.037,"end":245.375,"terms":[]},{"text":"Actualizar un locator porque la interfaz cambió es mantenimiento legítimo.","start":245.675,"end":249.427,"terms":[]},{"text":"Modificar o quitar una aserción para que el test pase es ocultar un posible defecto.","start":249.727,"end":254.095,"terms":[]},{"text":"El primero se acepta; el segundo se rechaza y se investiga.","start":254.395,"end":257.567,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":258.367,"end":291.221,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":258.867,"end":259.834,"terms":[]},{"text":"Regenerar en cada upgrade.","start":260.134,"end":261.692,"terms":[{"start":18,"end":25,"say":"apgréid"}]}],[{"text":"En cada upgrade de Playwright, volvé a correr init-agents en el mismo PR para regenerar las definiciones.","start":262.242,"end":267.62,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":15,"say":"apgréid"},{"start":19,"end":29,"say":"pléirait"},{"start":51,"end":57,"say":"éiyents"},{"start":70,"end":72,"say":"pe erre"}]}],[{"text":"Las definiciones de los agentes están atadas a la versión de Playwright: cambian las herramientas disponibles y las instrucciones.","start":268.17,"end":274.326,"terms":[{"start":61,"end":71,"say":"pléirait"}]},{"text":"Cuando subas de versión, volvé a correr la inicialización en el mismo pull request y revisá qué cambió en las definiciones.","start":274.626,"end":280.225,"terms":[{"start":70,"end":82,"say":"pul ricuést"}]},{"text":"Si les habías hecho ajustes propios, vas a tener que reaplicarlos, así que conviene documentarlos en un archivo aparte.","start":280.525,"end":286.506,"terms":[]},{"text":"Dejar definiciones viejas con una versión nueva produce fallos difíciles de diagnosticar.","start":286.806,"end":291.221,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":292.021,"end":326.832,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":292.521,"end":293.441,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un equipo prueba el flujo de alta de usuario.","start":293.991,"end":296.373,"terms":[]},{"text":"El planner propone nueve escenarios.","start":296.673,"end":298.615,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":10,"say":"pláner"}]},{"text":"En la revisión sacan dos repetidos y agregan uno que no podía saber: los mails de un dominio bloqueado deben rechazarse.","start":298.915,"end":304.862,"terms":[]},{"text":"El generator escribe ocho tests y siete pasan a la primera.","start":305.162,"end":308.299,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":12,"say":"yeneréitor"}]},{"text":"Una semana después, un rediseño cambia el texto de un botón y fallan cuatro.","start":308.599,"end":312.445,"terms":[]},{"text":"El healer corrige los cuatro locators.","start":312.745,"end":314.86,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":9,"say":"jíler"}]},{"text":"En el diff, tres cambios son solo de locator.","start":315.16,"end":317.531,"terms":[]},{"text":"El cuarto además cambió una aserción de \"muestra el error\" a \"el botón está visible\".","start":317.831,"end":321.596,"terms":[]},{"text":"Rechazan ese cambio, investigan y descubren que el mensaje de error había desaparecido en el rediseño.","start":321.896,"end":326.832,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":327.632,"end":344.15,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":328.132,"end":329.656,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Generar tests sin haber leído y corregido el plan.","start":330.206,"end":332.902,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Aceptar el resultado del healer porque la suite quedó en verde.","start":333.452,"end":336.462,"terms":[{"start":25,"end":31,"say":"jíler"},{"start":42,"end":47,"say":"suit"}]}],[{"text":"Escribir un test semilla descuidado que después todos los tests imitan.","start":337.012,"end":340.404,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Subir Playwright sin regenerar las definiciones de los agentes.","start":340.954,"end":344.15,"terms":[{"start":6,"end":16,"say":"pléirait"}]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":344.95,"end":362.779,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":345.45,"end":347.716,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Las definiciones, el semilla y la configuración están commiteados.","start":348.266,"end":351.938,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El plan fue revisado por una persona antes de generar.","start":352.488,"end":355.347,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cada test generado tiene una aserción que prueba una regla.","start":355.897,"end":358.895,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El diff del healer se revisó separando locators de aserciones.","start":359.445,"end":362.779,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":18,"say":"jíler"}]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":363.579,"end":372.925,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":364.079,"end":365.649,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"El healer puede poner un test en verde modificando la aserción.","start":366.199,"end":369.499,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":9,"say":"jíler"}]},{"text":"Un test reparado sin revisar el diff es un falso verde.","start":369.799,"end":372.925,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":373.725,"end":386.356,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 11.","start":374.225,"end":375.632,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":375.932,"end":381.171,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 12: Triage de fallos con LLM: de 40 tests rojos a 3 causas.","start":381.721,"end":386.356,"terms":[{"start":21,"end":27,"say":"triásh"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d11-en","day":11,"lang":"en","title":"Playwright Test Agents: planner, generator and healer in action","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d11-en.mp4","duration":430.766,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":15.819,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 11 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.02,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Playwright Test Agents: planner, generator and healer in action.","start":4.57,"end":8.671,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The full planner, generator and healer cycle in a real repo, and the two points where a person must step in.","start":9.221,"end":15.819,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":16.619,"end":37.103,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Yesterday you built the explore, plan and generate flow by hand, with your own prompts.","start":17.119,"end":22.09,"terms":[]},{"text":"Playwright ships that same flow packaged as three agents with separate responsibilities: one plans, one generates and one heals.","start":22.39,"end":30.276,"terms":[]},{"text":"The advantage isn't that they're smarter, but that each has narrow instructions and leaves an artifact the next one uses.","start":30.576,"end":37.103,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":37.903,"end":57.902,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The risk comes packaged too.","start":38.403,"end":40.484,"terms":[]},{"text":"A system that repairs tests on its own can leave the suite green for the wrong reason.","start":40.784,"end":45.524,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide walks through the three agents and marks the two points where a person must step in no matter what: before generating and after healing.","start":45.824,"end":53.443,"terms":[]},{"text":"Without those two reviews, what you gain in speed you lose in trust.","start":53.743,"end":57.902,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":58.702,"end":70.619,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":59.202,"end":60.899,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Up-to-date Playwright (agents ship since version 1.56).","start":61.449,"end":65.376,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A coding agent: Claude Code, VS Code with Copilot or OpenCode.","start":65.926,"end":70.619,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":71.419,"end":77.716,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":71.919,"end":73.199,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":73.499,"end":77.716,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":78.516,"end":121.495,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":79.016,"end":80.052,"terms":[]},{"text":"Initialize and commit.","start":80.352,"end":82.003,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Upgrade Playwright and run init-agents with your agent's loop.","start":82.553,"end":86.213,"terms":[]},{"text":"It generates the three agent definitions, a seed.spec.ts, the specs/ folder and the test MCP server config.","start":86.513,"end":93.981,"terms":[]},{"text":"Commit everything.","start":94.281,"end":95.584,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The init command generates the three agent definitions for the loop you use, a seed test, the plans folder and the test MCP server config.","start":96.134,"end":104.333,"terms":[]},{"text":"Commit all of that together.","start":104.633,"end":106.354,"terms":[]},{"text":"The definitions are instructions in text: it's worth reading them once to understand what each agent will do and with which tools.","start":106.654,"end":113.924,"terms":[]},{"text":"Being in the repo, the whole team uses the same ones, and any adjustment you make stays in history just like a code change.","start":114.224,"end":121.495,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":122.295,"end":161.209,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":122.795,"end":123.993,"terms":[]},{"text":"The seed test.","start":124.293,"end":125.515,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Write the seed test: it leaves the page in the initial state every flow needs (for example, logged in as a test user).","start":126.065,"end":132.975,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The seed is an ordinary test that leaves the application in the state every flow starts from: session started, minimum data loaded, initial screen.","start":133.525,"end":141.91,"terms":[]},{"text":"The agents run it before exploring or generating, so it also serves as a style example: generated tests tend to resemble it.","start":142.21,"end":149.922,"terms":[]},{"text":"Write it with the same care as your best test.","start":150.222,"end":153,"terms":[]},{"text":"If it uses role-based locators and clear fixtures, whatever comes next inherits that.","start":153.3,"end":158.933,"terms":[]},{"text":"If it's sloppy, that too.","start":159.233,"end":161.209,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":162.009,"end":201.935,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":162.509,"end":163.592,"terms":[]},{"text":"Planner and the first intervention.","start":163.892,"end":165.88,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Planner: ask it to explore a flow and save the plan in specs/.","start":166.43,"end":170.287,"terms":[]},{"text":"Intervention 1: review the plan before generating anything.","start":170.587,"end":174.769,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The planner explores the application starting from the seed and writes a Markdown plan into the plans folder: scenarios, steps and expected results.","start":175.319,"end":183.843,"terms":[]},{"text":"This is the first human intervention, and it's the cheapest in the whole chain.","start":184.143,"end":188.36,"terms":[]},{"text":"Read the plan before generating anything.","start":188.66,"end":191.275,"terms":[]},{"text":"Remove scenarios that add nothing, add the ones that depend on business rules and fix expected results the planner assumed.","start":191.575,"end":198.567,"terms":[]},{"text":"Every error you let through gets multiplied in code.","start":198.867,"end":201.935,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":202.735,"end":242.01,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":203.235,"end":204.247,"terms":[]},{"text":"Generator.","start":204.547,"end":205.514,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Generator: ask for the tests from the approved plan.","start":206.064,"end":209.619,"terms":[]},{"text":"It checks selectors against the live page while writing.","start":209.919,"end":213.393,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The generator takes the approved plan and writes the tests.","start":213.943,"end":217.313,"terms":[]},{"text":"The difference from a classic generator is that it verifies against the live page: it executes the steps while writing and checks that each locator finds its element.","start":217.613,"end":226.323,"terms":[]},{"text":"That's why the result usually runs first time.","start":226.623,"end":229.597,"terms":[]},{"text":"Even so, review the assertions.","start":229.897,"end":232.292,"terms":[]},{"text":"A test can navigate the whole flow correctly and end up verifying something trivial, such as the URL having changed, instead of the rule the scenario meant to prove.","start":232.592,"end":242.01,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":242.81,"end":287.974,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":243.31,"end":244.439,"terms":[]},{"text":"Healer and the second intervention.","start":244.739,"end":246.866,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Healer: break a locator on purpose and run the healer.","start":247.416,"end":250.925,"terms":[]},{"text":"Intervention 2: review the diff.","start":251.225,"end":253.608,"terms":[]},{"text":"Fixing a locator is fine; changing an assertion is not.","start":253.908,"end":257.637,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Break a locator on purpose, for example by renaming a button, and run the healer.","start":258.187,"end":262.938,"terms":[]},{"text":"It will run the test, see the failure, inspect the page and propose a fix.","start":263.238,"end":267.827,"terms":[]},{"text":"This is the second intervention: reviewing the diff.","start":268.127,"end":271.369,"terms":[]},{"text":"There are two kinds of change.","start":271.669,"end":273.587,"terms":[]},{"text":"Updating a locator because the interface changed is legitimate maintenance.","start":273.887,"end":278.034,"terms":[]},{"text":"Modifying or removing an assertion so the test passes is hiding a possible defect.","start":278.334,"end":283.097,"terms":[]},{"text":"The first gets accepted; the second gets rejected and investigated.","start":283.397,"end":287.974,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":288.774,"end":323.589,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":289.274,"end":290.461,"terms":[]},{"text":"Regenerate on every upgrade.","start":290.761,"end":292.911,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"On every Playwright upgrade, rerun init-agents in the same PR to regenerate the definitions.","start":293.461,"end":298.979,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The agent definitions are tied to the Playwright version: available tools and instructions change.","start":299.529,"end":305.255,"terms":[]},{"text":"When you upgrade, rerun the initialization in the same pull request and review what changed in the definitions.","start":305.555,"end":311.525,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you had made your own adjustments, you'll have to reapply them, so it's worth documenting them in a separate file.","start":311.825,"end":318.178,"terms":[]},{"text":"Leaving old definitions with a new version produces failures that are hard to diagnose.","start":318.478,"end":323.589,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":324.389,"end":363.497,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":324.889,"end":325.995,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A team tests the user sign-up flow.","start":326.545,"end":329.044,"terms":[]},{"text":"The planner proposes nine scenarios.","start":329.344,"end":331.703,"terms":[]},{"text":"In review they remove two repeated ones and add one it couldn't know: emails from a blocked domain must be rejected.","start":332.003,"end":338.995,"terms":[]},{"text":"The generator writes eight tests and seven pass first time.","start":339.295,"end":342.792,"terms":[]},{"text":"A week later a redesign changes a button's text and four fail.","start":343.092,"end":346.949,"terms":[]},{"text":"The healer fixes the four locators.","start":347.249,"end":349.516,"terms":[]},{"text":"In the diff, three changes are locator-only.","start":349.816,"end":352.86,"terms":[]},{"text":"The fourth also changed an assertion from \"shows the error\" to \"the button is visible\".","start":353.16,"end":357.517,"terms":[]},{"text":"They reject that change, investigate and discover the error message had disappeared in the redesign.","start":357.817,"end":363.497,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":364.297,"end":383.159,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":364.797,"end":366.46,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Generating tests without having read and corrected the plan.","start":367.01,"end":370.577,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Accepting the healer's result because the suite turned green.","start":371.127,"end":374.659,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Writing a sloppy seed test that every later test imitates.","start":375.209,"end":379.019,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Upgrading Playwright without regenerating the agent definitions.","start":379.569,"end":383.159,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":383.959,"end":402.625,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":384.459,"end":386.703,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The definitions, the seed and the config are committed.","start":387.253,"end":390.622,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The plan was reviewed by a person before generating.","start":391.172,"end":394.31,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each generated test has an assertion that proves a rule.","start":394.86,"end":398.218,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The healer's diff was reviewed separating locators from assertions.","start":398.768,"end":402.625,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":403.425,"end":413.571,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":403.925,"end":405.82,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The healer can turn a test green by modifying the assertion.","start":406.37,"end":409.809,"terms":[]},{"text":"A healed test with an unreviewed diff is a false green.","start":410.109,"end":413.571,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":414.371,"end":429.266,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 11.","start":414.871,"end":416.221,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":416.521,"end":422.502,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 12: Failure triage with LLMs: from 40 red tests to 3 root causes.","start":423.052,"end":429.266,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d12-es","day":12,"lang":"es","title":"Triage de fallos con LLM: de 40 tests rojos a 3 causas","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d12-es.mp4","duration":385.104,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":14.437,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 12 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.44,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Triage de fallos con LLM: de 40 tests rojos a 3 causas.","start":3.99,"end":7.754,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":6,"say":"triásh"}]}],[{"text":"Un paso de triage que agrupa los fallos de una corrida por causa probable, con evidencia, y que dice \"no sé\" cuando no alcanza.","start":8.304,"end":14.437,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":17,"say":"triásh"}]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":15.237,"end":34.758,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un pipeline con cuarenta tests en rojo casi nunca tiene cuarenta problemas.","start":15.737,"end":19.675,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":11,"say":"páiplain"}]},{"text":"Tiene dos o tres: un servicio caído, un cambio de interfaz y, con suerte, un defecto real.","start":19.975,"end":24.866,"terms":[]},{"text":"El trabajo de quien hace el triage es descubrir esa agrupación, y es un trabajo lento y repetitivo: abrir fallo por fallo, leer el error y reconocer que ya lo viste diez veces esa mañana.","start":25.166,"end":34.758,"terms":[{"start":28,"end":34,"say":"triásh"}]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":35.558,"end":54.464,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Agrupar texto parecido es algo que un LLM hace bien y rápido.","start":36.058,"end":39.614,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lo que no hace bien es admitir que no sabe.","start":39.914,"end":42.343,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta guía arma un triage asistido que aprovecha lo primero y se protege de lo segundo: entrada estructurada y chica, salida con evidencia textual, una categoría \"desconocido\" obligatoria y una persona que confirma antes de cerrar nada.","start":42.643,"end":54.464,"terms":[{"start":18,"end":24,"say":"triásh"}]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":55.264,"end":62.886,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":55.764,"end":57.694,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Una suite de Playwright con algunos fallos.","start":58.244,"end":60.511,"terms":[{"start":4,"end":9,"say":"suit"},{"start":13,"end":23,"say":"pléirait"}]}],[{"text":"Traces activados en reintentos.","start":61.061,"end":62.886,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":6,"say":"tréises"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":63.686,"end":69.008,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":64.186,"end":65.361,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":65.661,"end":69.008,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":69.808,"end":72.446,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":70.308,"end":72.446,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":73.246,"end":75.885,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":73.746,"end":75.885,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":76.685,"end":111.373,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":77.185,"end":78.082,"terms":[]},{"text":"Resultados estructurados.","start":78.382,"end":79.998,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Sacá los resultados en formato estructurado con el reporter JSON y mantené los traces de los reintentos.","start":80.548,"end":85.926,"terms":[{"start":60,"end":64,"say":"yeison"},{"start":79,"end":85,"say":"tréises"}]}],[{"text":"El punto de partida es que tu runner emita los resultados en un formato que una máquina pueda leer, no solo un reporte HTML para personas.","start":86.476,"end":93.515,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":36,"say":"ráner"}]},{"text":"El reporter JSON de Playwright incluye para cada test su título, archivo, estado, error y reintentos.","start":93.815,"end":99.251,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":16,"say":"yeison"},{"start":20,"end":30,"say":"pléirait"}]},{"text":"Activá también los traces en el primer reintento: son la evidencia que vas a necesitar para verificar lo que el modelo proponga.","start":99.551,"end":105.521,"terms":[{"start":19,"end":25,"say":"tréises"}]},{"text":"Sin datos estructurados terminás pegando logs enteros en un chat, que es caro, lento y mezcla ruido con señal.","start":105.821,"end":111.373,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":112.173,"end":150.206,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":112.673,"end":113.57,"terms":[]},{"text":"Extraer solo lo necesario.","start":113.87,"end":115.602,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Extraé solo los fallos: título, archivo y los primeros 400 caracteres del error.","start":116.152,"end":120.961,"terms":[]},{"text":"Menos tokens, mejor agrupación.","start":121.261,"end":123.424,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un script corto recorre el JSON y se queda con los tests fallidos: título, archivo y los primeros cuatrocientos caracteres del error.","start":123.974,"end":130.489,"terms":[{"start":27,"end":31,"say":"yeison"}]},{"text":"Ese recorte tiene dos efectos.","start":130.789,"end":132.754,"terms":[]},{"text":"Baja el costo, porque un log completo puede tener miles de líneas irrelevantes.","start":133.054,"end":136.923,"terms":[]},{"text":"Y mejora el resultado, porque la primera parte del mensaje suele contener lo que distingue una causa de otra: el tipo de error, el locator, el código HTTP.","start":137.223,"end":145.178,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con stack traces enteros, el modelo tiende a agrupar por coincidencias superficiales del stack.","start":145.478,"end":150.206,"terms":[{"start":10,"end":16,"say":"tréises"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":151.006,"end":185.091,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":151.506,"end":152.403,"terms":[]},{"text":"El prompt de triage.","start":152.703,"end":153.971,"terms":[{"start":13,"end":19,"say":"triásh"}]}],[{"text":"Corré el prompt de triage.","start":154.521,"end":155.998,"terms":[{"start":19,"end":25,"say":"triásh"}]},{"text":"Exigí categoría, tests del grupo y la línea exacta de evidencia.","start":156.298,"end":159.772,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pedí tres cosas por grupo: una categoría de una lista cerrada, los tests que lo integran y la línea exacta del error que justifica la agrupación.","start":160.322,"end":167.267,"terms":[]},{"text":"La lista cerrada es importante: entorno, datos, cambio de interfaz, defecto probable y desconocido.","start":167.567,"end":173.189,"terms":[]},{"text":"La línea de evidencia tiene que ser una cita literal de la entrada, no una explicación.","start":173.489,"end":177.95,"terms":[]},{"text":"Así podés comprobar en segundos si el grupo tiene sentido, y un grupo cuya evidencia no aparece en los datos queda descartado de inmediato.","start":178.25,"end":185.091,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":185.891,"end":218.908,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":186.391,"end":187.392,"terms":[]},{"text":"Verificar el grupo más grande.","start":187.692,"end":189.599,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Verificá a mano el grupo más grande abriendo un trace con npx playwright show-trace.","start":190.149,"end":194.656,"terms":[{"start":48,"end":53,"say":"treis"},{"start":62,"end":72,"say":"pléirait"},{"start":78,"end":83,"say":"treis"}]}],[{"text":"Antes de actuar sobre el resultado, abrí el trace de un test del grupo más grande.","start":195.206,"end":199.458,"terms":[{"start":44,"end":49,"say":"treis"}]},{"text":"Mirá la línea de tiempo, las llamadas de red y la captura del momento del fallo.","start":199.758,"end":203.801,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si la causa propuesta coincide con lo que ves, el grupo queda confirmado y probablemente resolviste la mayor parte del pipeline en rojo.","start":204.101,"end":210.721,"terms":[{"start":119,"end":127,"say":"páiplain"}]},{"text":"Si no coincide, tomá el resultado completo con más cautela.","start":211.021,"end":214.089,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esa verificación lleva pocos minutos y es la que convierte una sugerencia en un diagnóstico.","start":214.389,"end":218.908,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":219.708,"end":251.714,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":220.208,"end":221.209,"terms":[]},{"text":"Guardar cada triage.","start":221.509,"end":222.777,"terms":[{"start":13,"end":19,"say":"triásh"}]}],[{"text":"Guardá cada triage con tu veredicto final.","start":223.327,"end":225.594,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":18,"say":"triásh"}]},{"text":"En pocas semanas tenés un dataset para evaluar el prompt.","start":225.894,"end":228.787,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Guardá la entrada, la salida del modelo y tu veredicto final en un archivo por corrida.","start":229.337,"end":233.693,"terms":[]},{"text":"Parece burocracia y es lo más valioso del proceso.","start":233.993,"end":236.806,"terms":[]},{"text":"En pocas semanas tenés decenas de casos reales etiquetados por alguien que conoce el sistema: exactamente el dataset que hace falta para evaluar el prompt de triage con el método del día ocho.","start":237.106,"end":246.094,"terms":[{"start":158,"end":164,"say":"triásh"}]},{"text":"También vas a ver patrones que un día suelto no muestra, como un test que cae siempre en la categoría de entorno.","start":246.394,"end":251.714,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":252.514,"end":285.148,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":253.014,"end":253.981,"terms":[]},{"text":"El cierre es humano.","start":254.281,"end":255.456,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"No automatices el cierre: el modelo propone, una persona confirma antes de poner en cuarentena o abrir un bug.","start":256.006,"end":261.454,"terms":[{"start":106,"end":109,"say":"bag"}]}],[{"text":"El modelo propone grupos y causas; una persona confirma antes de poner un test en cuarentena, abrir un bug o reintentar el pipeline.","start":262.004,"end":268.636,"terms":[{"start":103,"end":106,"say":"bag"},{"start":123,"end":131,"say":"páiplain"}]},{"text":"La razón es de costo del error.","start":268.936,"end":270.726,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cuarentenar por error un test que detectaba un defecto real deja pasar ese defecto.","start":271.026,"end":275.208,"terms":[]},{"text":"Abrir veinte bugs duplicados le quita credibilidad a QA.","start":275.508,"end":278.39,"terms":[{"start":13,"end":17,"say":"bags"},{"start":53,"end":55,"say":"cu a"}]},{"text":"Automatizar la propuesta ahorra la parte tediosa; automatizar la decisión traslada el riesgo a un sistema que no responde por ella.","start":278.69,"end":285.148,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":285.948,"end":325.055,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":286.448,"end":287.368,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un lunes el pipeline nocturno muestra cuarenta y dos fallos.","start":287.918,"end":290.951,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":20,"say":"páiplain"}]},{"text":"El script extrae los cuarenta y dos errores recortados y el prompt devuelve cuatro grupos.","start":291.251,"end":295.804,"terms":[]},{"text":"Treinta y uno comparten la línea de un error 503 del servicio de autenticación: entorno.","start":296.104,"end":301.157,"terms":[]},{"text":"Siete tienen un timeout esperando un botón cuyo nombre cambió: cambio de interfaz.","start":301.457,"end":305.407,"terms":[{"start":16,"end":23,"say":"táimaut"}]},{"text":"Tres muestran un total distinto del esperado en el carrito: defecto probable.","start":305.707,"end":309.657,"terms":[]},{"text":"Uno queda como desconocido.","start":309.957,"end":311.539,"terms":[]},{"text":"La QA abre un trace del primer grupo y confirma el 503.","start":311.839,"end":315.476,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":5,"say":"cu a"},{"start":14,"end":19,"say":"treis"}]},{"text":"Reintenta el pipeline cuando el servicio vuelve, actualiza un locator y abre un solo bug con tres tests como evidencia.","start":315.776,"end":321.618,"terms":[{"start":13,"end":21,"say":"páiplain"},{"start":85,"end":88,"say":"bag"}]},{"text":"El caso desconocido resulta ser un dato de prueba vencido.","start":321.918,"end":325.055,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":325.855,"end":343.232,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":326.355,"end":327.879,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pegar el log completo en lugar de los errores recortados.","start":328.429,"end":331.322,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"No ofrecer la categoría desconocido y obtener causas inventadas.","start":331.872,"end":335.555,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Aceptar la explicación del modelo sin abrir ningún trace.","start":336.105,"end":339.22,"terms":[{"start":51,"end":56,"say":"treis"}]}],[{"text":"Automatizar la cuarentena o la creación de bugs sin confirmación.","start":339.77,"end":343.232,"terms":[{"start":43,"end":47,"say":"bags"}]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":344.032,"end":360.863,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":344.532,"end":346.798,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El runner emite JSON y guarda traces en los reintentos.","start":347.348,"end":350.474,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":9,"say":"ráner"},{"start":16,"end":20,"say":"yeison"},{"start":30,"end":36,"say":"tréises"}]}],[{"text":"El script extrae título, archivo y error recortado de cada fallo.","start":351.024,"end":354.707,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cada grupo trae una línea de evidencia literal.","start":355.257,"end":357.814,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El triage quedó guardado con tu veredicto final.","start":358.364,"end":360.863,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":9,"say":"triásh"}]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":361.663,"end":370.393,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":362.163,"end":363.733,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Si no le das la salida \"desconocido\", fabrica una causa plausible.","start":364.283,"end":367.78,"terms":[]},{"text":"Una causa inventada es peor que ninguna.","start":368.08,"end":370.393,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":371.193,"end":383.604,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 12.","start":371.693,"end":373.077,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":373.377,"end":378.616,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 13: TDD con IA (1): red, green, refactor con un agente.","start":379.166,"end":383.604,"terms":[{"start":29,"end":31,"say":"i a"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d12-en","day":12,"lang":"en","title":"Failure triage with LLMs: from 40 red tests to 3 root causes","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d12-en.mp4","duration":424.833,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":16.829,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 12 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.067,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Failure triage with LLMs: from 40 red tests to 3 root causes.","start":4.617,"end":9.356,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A triage step that groups a run's failures by probable cause, with evidence, and says \"unknown\" when there isn't enough.","start":9.906,"end":16.829,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":17.629,"end":40.703,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"A pipeline with forty red tests almost never has forty problems.","start":18.129,"end":22.137,"terms":[]},{"text":"It has two or three: a service that's down, an interface change and, with luck, a real defect.","start":22.437,"end":28.743,"terms":[]},{"text":"The job of whoever does triage is to discover that grouping, and it's slow, repetitive work: opening failure after failure, reading the error and recognizing you've already seen it ten times that morning.","start":29.043,"end":40.703,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":41.503,"end":62.138,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Grouping similar text is something an LLM does well and fast.","start":42.003,"end":45.802,"terms":[]},{"text":"What it doesn't do well is admit it doesn't know.","start":46.102,"end":48.717,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide builds an assisted triage that uses the former and guards against the latter: small, structured input, output with textual evidence, a mandatory \"unknown\" category and a person who confirms before closing anything.","start":49.017,"end":62.138,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":62.938,"end":70.99,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":63.438,"end":65.136,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A Playwright suite with some failures.","start":65.686,"end":68.255,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Traces enabled on retries.","start":68.805,"end":70.99,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":71.79,"end":78.087,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":72.29,"end":73.57,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":73.87,"end":78.087,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":78.887,"end":81.653,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":79.387,"end":81.653,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":82.453,"end":85.22,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":82.953,"end":85.22,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":86.02,"end":122.983,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":86.52,"end":87.556,"terms":[]},{"text":"Structured results.","start":87.856,"end":89.182,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Emit results in a structured format with the JSON reporter and keep traces from retries.","start":89.732,"end":95.133,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The starting point is having your runner emit results in a format a machine can read, not just an HTML report for people.","start":95.683,"end":102.605,"terms":[]},{"text":"Playwright's JSON reporter includes each test's title, file, status, error and retries.","start":102.905,"end":109.131,"terms":[]},{"text":"Also enable traces on first retry: they are the evidence you'll need to verify whatever the model proposes.","start":109.431,"end":115.691,"terms":[]},{"text":"Without structured data you end up pasting whole logs into a chat, which is expensive, slow and mixes noise with signal.","start":115.991,"end":122.983,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":123.783,"end":165.404,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":124.283,"end":125.482,"terms":[]},{"text":"Extract only what's needed.","start":125.782,"end":127.642,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Extract failures only: title, file and the first 400 characters of the error.","start":128.192,"end":133.384,"terms":[]},{"text":"Fewer tokens, better grouping.","start":133.684,"end":135.602,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A short script walks the JSON and keeps the failed tests: title, file and the first four hundred characters of the error.","start":136.152,"end":143.353,"terms":[]},{"text":"That trimming has two effects.","start":143.653,"end":145.571,"terms":[]},{"text":"It lowers cost, because a full log can hold thousands of irrelevant lines.","start":145.871,"end":150.425,"terms":[]},{"text":"And it improves the result, because the first part of the message usually holds what tells one cause from another: the error type, the locator, the HTTP code.","start":150.725,"end":160.318,"terms":[]},{"text":"With full stack traces, the model tends to group by superficial stack matches.","start":160.618,"end":165.404,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":166.204,"end":202.901,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":166.704,"end":167.786,"terms":[]},{"text":"The triage prompt.","start":168.086,"end":169.389,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Run the triage prompt.","start":169.939,"end":171.59,"terms":[]},{"text":"Require category, tests in the group and the exact evidence line.","start":171.89,"end":176.13,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ask for three things per group: a category from a closed list, the tests in it and the exact error line justifying the grouping.","start":176.68,"end":183.916,"terms":[]},{"text":"The closed list matters: environment, data, interface change, probable defect and unknown.","start":184.216,"end":190.291,"terms":[]},{"text":"The evidence line must be a literal quote from the input, not an explanation.","start":190.591,"end":195.075,"terms":[]},{"text":"That way you can check in seconds whether the group makes sense, and a group whose evidence doesn't appear in the data gets discarded immediately.","start":195.375,"end":202.901,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":203.701,"end":239.318,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":204.201,"end":205.213,"terms":[]},{"text":"Verify the largest group.","start":205.513,"end":207.583,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Manually verify the largest group by opening a trace with npx playwright show-trace.","start":208.133,"end":213.209,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Before acting on the result, open the trace of one test from the largest group.","start":213.759,"end":218.58,"terms":[]},{"text":"Look at the timeline, the network calls and the screenshot at the moment of failure.","start":218.88,"end":223.352,"terms":[]},{"text":"If the proposed cause matches what you see, the group is confirmed and you've probably resolved most of the red pipeline.","start":223.652,"end":230.412,"terms":[]},{"text":"If it doesn't match, treat the whole result with more caution.","start":230.712,"end":234.151,"terms":[]},{"text":"That check takes a few minutes and is what turns a suggestion into a diagnosis.","start":234.451,"end":239.318,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":240.118,"end":276.606,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":240.618,"end":241.747,"terms":[]},{"text":"Save every triage.","start":242.047,"end":243.594,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Save each triage with your final verdict.","start":244.144,"end":246.747,"terms":[]},{"text":"In a few weeks you have a dataset to evaluate the prompt.","start":247.047,"end":250.324,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Save the input, the model's output and your final verdict in one file per run.","start":250.874,"end":255.752,"terms":[]},{"text":"It looks like bureaucracy and it's the most valuable part of the process.","start":256.052,"end":260.571,"terms":[]},{"text":"In a few weeks you have dozens of real cases labeled by someone who knows the system: exactly the dataset needed to evaluate the triage prompt with the day eight method.","start":260.871,"end":269.999,"terms":[]},{"text":"You'll also see patterns a single day doesn't show, such as a test that always lands in the environment category.","start":270.299,"end":276.606,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":277.406,"end":314.185,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":277.906,"end":279.093,"terms":[]},{"text":"Closure is human.","start":279.393,"end":280.754,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Don't automate closure: the model proposes, a person confirms before quarantining or filing a bug.","start":281.304,"end":287.623,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The model proposes groups and causes; a person confirms before quarantining a test, filing a bug or retrying the pipeline.","start":288.173,"end":295.942,"terms":[]},{"text":"The reason is the cost of error.","start":296.242,"end":298.149,"terms":[]},{"text":"Wrongly quarantining a test that was catching a real defect lets that defect through.","start":298.449,"end":303.037,"terms":[]},{"text":"Filing twenty duplicate bugs costs QA its credibility.","start":303.337,"end":306.997,"terms":[]},{"text":"Automating the proposal saves the tedious part; automating the decision moves the risk to a system that doesn't answer for it.","start":307.297,"end":314.185,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":314.985,"end":359.456,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":315.485,"end":316.59,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"On a Monday the nightly pipeline shows forty-two failures.","start":317.14,"end":320.649,"terms":[]},{"text":"The script extracts the forty-two trimmed errors and the prompt returns four groups.","start":320.949,"end":325.735,"terms":[]},{"text":"Thirty-one share the line of a 503 error from the authentication service: environment.","start":326.035,"end":331.517,"terms":[]},{"text":"Seven have a timeout waiting for a button whose name changed: interface change.","start":331.817,"end":336.522,"terms":[]},{"text":"Three show a cart total different from the expected one: probable defect.","start":336.822,"end":341.388,"terms":[]},{"text":"One stays as unknown.","start":341.688,"end":343.339,"terms":[]},{"text":"The QA opens a trace from the first group and confirms the 503.","start":343.639,"end":348.413,"terms":[]},{"text":"She retries the pipeline once the service is back, updates one locator and files a single bug with three tests as evidence.","start":348.713,"end":355.995,"terms":[]},{"text":"The unknown case turns out to be expired test data.","start":356.295,"end":359.456,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":360.256,"end":378.329,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":360.756,"end":362.419,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pasting the full log instead of the trimmed errors.","start":362.969,"end":365.862,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Not offering the unknown category and getting invented causes.","start":366.412,"end":370.153,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Accepting the model's explanation without opening any trace.","start":370.703,"end":374.34,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Automating quarantine or bug filing without confirmation.","start":374.89,"end":378.329,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":379.129,"end":396.923,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":379.629,"end":381.872,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The runner emits JSON and keeps traces on retries.","start":382.422,"end":385.885,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The script extracts title, file and trimmed error for each failure.","start":386.435,"end":390.698,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each group comes with a literal evidence line.","start":391.248,"end":393.967,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The triage is saved with your final verdict.","start":394.517,"end":396.923,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":397.723,"end":407.58,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":398.223,"end":400.118,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"If you don't give it the \"unknown\" exit, it fabricates a plausible cause.","start":400.668,"end":404.793,"terms":[]},{"text":"An invented cause is worse than none.","start":405.093,"end":407.58,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":408.38,"end":423.333,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 12.","start":408.88,"end":410.45,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":410.75,"end":416.732,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 13: TDD with AI (1): red, green, refactor with an agent.","start":417.282,"end":423.333,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d13-es","day":13,"lang":"es","title":"TDD con IA (1): red, green, refactor con un agente","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d13-es.mp4","duration":368.267,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":12.986,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 13 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.544,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"TDD con IA (1): red, green, refactor con un agente.","start":4.094,"end":7.197,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":10,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Tres ciclos de TDD donde vos escribís el test y el agente escribe la implementación mínima, sin tocar el test.","start":7.747,"end":12.986,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":13.786,"end":32.111,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"TDD siempre tuvo un costo de disciplina: escribir el test antes cuando la solución ya está en tu cabeza cuesta.","start":14.286,"end":19.931,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con un agente que escribe la implementación en segundos, la tentación se invierte.","start":20.231,"end":24.204,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lo fácil es pedir \"implementá esta función con sus tests\" y recibir todo junto, con tests que pasan porque fueron escritos mirando el código que tenían que verificar.","start":24.504,"end":32.111,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":32.911,"end":51.155,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"El ciclo rojo, verde, refactor ordena esa relación con una regla de autoría: el test es tuyo y la implementación es del agente.","start":33.411,"end":40.206,"terms":[]},{"text":"El test se vuelve la especificación ejecutable y también el control de calidad de lo que el agente produce.","start":40.506,"end":45.384,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta guía recorre el ciclo completo con una función chica, para que el hábito quede antes de aplicarlo a algo grande.","start":45.684,"end":51.155,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":51.955,"end":58.421,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":52.455,"end":54.385,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Node.js 22+, un agente de código y un repo vacío.","start":54.935,"end":58.421,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":7,"say":"noud yei es"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":59.221,"end":64.542,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":59.721,"end":60.896,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":61.196,"end":64.542,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":65.342,"end":67.981,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":65.842,"end":67.981,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":68.781,"end":71.42,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":69.281,"end":71.42,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":72.22,"end":104.913,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":72.72,"end":73.617,"terms":[]},{"text":"Rojo: un test, escrito por vos.","start":73.917,"end":75.835,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Rojo: escribí vos un solo test.","start":76.385,"end":78.501,"terms":[]},{"text":"El test es la especificación y es tuyo.","start":78.801,"end":80.998,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Escribí un solo test que describa un comportamiento concreto, con un nombre que se lea como una regla de negocio.","start":81.548,"end":86.74,"terms":[]},{"text":"Uno solo, no la suite completa.","start":87.04,"end":88.97,"terms":[{"start":16,"end":21,"say":"suit"}]},{"text":"Al escribirlo tomás decisiones de diseño que no conviene delegar: cómo se llama la función, qué recibe, qué devuelve y qué pasa con una entrada inválida.","start":89.27,"end":96.366,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ese es el trabajo de pensar.","start":96.666,"end":98.468,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si le pedís el test al agente, esas decisiones las toma él, y después vas a estar revisando una especificación que no escribiste.","start":98.768,"end":104.913,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":105.713,"end":139.298,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":106.213,"end":107.109,"terms":[]},{"text":"Fallar por la razón correcta.","start":107.409,"end":109.177,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Corré npx vitest run y confirmá que falla por el motivo correcto (la función no existe o devuelve otra cosa), no por un error de import.","start":109.727,"end":116.347,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Corré el test y leé el mensaje.","start":116.897,"end":118.513,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tiene que fallar porque la función no existe o porque devuelve otra cosa, no por un error de import, de configuración o de sintaxis.","start":118.813,"end":125.306,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es un paso que se saltea con frecuencia y que evita un problema clásico: un test que nunca pudo fallar por la razón que importa.","start":125.606,"end":131.831,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si el mensaje de error es confuso, mejoralo ahora.","start":132.131,"end":135.002,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ese mismo mensaje es lo que el agente va a leer para saber qué tiene que construir.","start":135.302,"end":139.298,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":140.098,"end":174.427,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":140.598,"end":141.495,"terms":[]},{"text":"Verde: lo mínimo, sin tocar tests.","start":141.795,"end":143.922,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Verde: pedí al agente el código mínimo para pasar ese test, con la instrucción explícita de no modificar tests.","start":144.472,"end":150.071,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pedile al agente el código mínimo para pasar ese test, con dos instrucciones explícitas: no modificar archivos de test y no agregar nada que el test no pida.","start":150.621,"end":158.356,"terms":[]},{"text":"La palabra mínimo importa.","start":158.656,"end":160.272,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sin ella vas a recibir validaciones, casos y parámetros opcionales que nadie especificó y que ningún test cubre.","start":160.572,"end":166.217,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es código que parece útil y que queda sin dueño: nadie sabe si funciona ni si debería existir.","start":166.517,"end":171.291,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si después hace falta, lo agregás con su propio test.","start":171.591,"end":174.427,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":175.227,"end":207.199,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":175.727,"end":176.728,"terms":[]},{"text":"Verificar el verde vos.","start":177.028,"end":178.517,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Verificá vos el verde corriendo el comando.","start":179.067,"end":181.287,"terms":[]},{"text":"Revisá el diff: solo debería haber cambiado src/discount.ts.","start":181.587,"end":185.792,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Corré el comando vos y mirá el resultado.","start":186.342,"end":188.47,"terms":[]},{"text":"Después revisá el diff.","start":188.77,"end":190.049,"terms":[]},{"text":"En este ejercicio solo debería haber cambiado el archivo de implementación.","start":190.349,"end":194.09,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si cambió el test, la configuración o apareció un archivo nuevo, preguntate por qué antes de seguir.","start":194.39,"end":199.385,"terms":[]},{"text":"Son treinta segundos que sostienen todo el método: el verde vale porque lo produjo un test que el agente no pudo ajustar y porque lo comprobaste por fuera de su resumen.","start":199.685,"end":207.199,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":207.999,"end":240.936,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":208.499,"end":209.501,"terms":[]},{"text":"Refactor con la red puesta.","start":209.801,"end":211.452,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Refactor: pedí una mejora de legibilidad manteniendo los tests en verde.","start":212.002,"end":215.731,"terms":[]},{"text":"Commit.","start":216.031,"end":216.765,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Con el test en verde, pedí una mejora de legibilidad sin cambio de comportamiento: nombres más claros, una constante en lugar de un número suelto, una función extraída.","start":217.315,"end":225.863,"terms":[]},{"text":"Volvé a correr los tests.","start":226.163,"end":227.57,"terms":[]},{"text":"Este es el paso donde el agente aporta más con menos riesgo, porque cualquier error lo detecta el test al instante.","start":227.87,"end":233.121,"terms":[]},{"text":"Commiteá al terminar.","start":233.421,"end":234.805,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si el refactor rompe algo y no es evidente por qué, descartarlo cuesta un comando, y volvés a un estado conocido.","start":235.105,"end":240.936,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":241.736,"end":273.324,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":242.236,"end":243.202,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un ciclo, un commit.","start":243.502,"end":244.735,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Repetí con el segundo y el tercer test.","start":245.285,"end":247.389,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un commit por ciclo deja un historial que se puede auditar.","start":247.689,"end":250.827,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Repetí el ciclo con el segundo y el tercer comportamiento, y hacé un commit por ciclo.","start":251.377,"end":255.803,"terms":[]},{"text":"El historial queda como una secuencia de reglas agregadas de a una, cada una con su test y su implementación.","start":256.103,"end":261.074,"terms":[]},{"text":"Eso sirve para revisar y para auditar: quien lea el historial puede ver qué se pidió, qué se construyó y en qué orden.","start":261.374,"end":267.159,"terms":[]},{"text":"También te da puntos de retorno baratos si un ciclo sale mal, algo que con cambios grandes generados de una vez no tenés.","start":267.459,"end":273.324,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":274.124,"end":312.874,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":274.624,"end":275.544,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"La función a construir aplica descuentos.","start":276.094,"end":278.454,"terms":[]},{"text":"Primer test, escrito a mano: un cupón del diez por ciento sobre cien devuelve noventa.","start":278.754,"end":282.959,"terms":[]},{"text":"Falla porque la función no existe.","start":283.259,"end":285.328,"terms":[]},{"text":"El agente escribe cuatro líneas y el test pasa; el diff toca un solo archivo.","start":285.628,"end":289.636,"terms":[]},{"text":"Refactor: extrae el cálculo del porcentaje.","start":289.936,"end":292.528,"terms":[]},{"text":"Commit.","start":292.828,"end":293.562,"terms":[]},{"text":"Segundo test: un descuento mayor al cien por ciento lanza un error.","start":293.862,"end":297.243,"terms":[]},{"text":"El agente agrega la validación.","start":297.543,"end":299.45,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tercer test: el resultado se redondea a dos decimales.","start":299.75,"end":302.852,"terms":[]},{"text":"En el segundo ciclo, el agente había sumado además soporte para cupones de monto fijo que nadie pidió.","start":303.152,"end":308.171,"terms":[]},{"text":"Se quita, porque ningún test lo exige.","start":308.471,"end":310.609,"terms":[]},{"text":"Quedan tres commits y tres reglas.","start":310.909,"end":312.874,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":313.674,"end":327.428,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":314.174,"end":315.698,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pedir implementación y tests en el mismo pedido.","start":316.248,"end":318.735,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"No comprobar que el test falla antes de implementar.","start":319.285,"end":321.818,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Dejar código extra que ningún test exige.","start":322.368,"end":324.774,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Juntar varios ciclos en un solo commit.","start":325.324,"end":327.428,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":328.228,"end":343.689,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":328.728,"end":330.995,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cada test fue escrito por vos antes que su implementación.","start":331.545,"end":334.613,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Viste cada test fallar por la razón esperada.","start":335.163,"end":337.708,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ningún diff de implementación tocó archivos de test.","start":338.258,"end":341.012,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El historial tiene un commit por ciclo.","start":341.562,"end":343.689,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":344.489,"end":353.301,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":344.989,"end":346.559,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Tiende a implementar de más: funciones y casos que ningún test pide.","start":347.109,"end":350.502,"terms":[]},{"text":"Código sin test que lo pida es código sin dueño.","start":350.802,"end":353.301,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":354.101,"end":366.767,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 13.","start":354.601,"end":355.997,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":356.297,"end":361.535,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 14: TDD con IA (2): guardrails para que el agente no haga trampa.","start":362.085,"end":366.767,"terms":[{"start":29,"end":31,"say":"i a"},{"start":37,"end":47,"say":"gárdreils"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d13-en","day":13,"lang":"en","title":"TDD with AI (1): red, green, refactor with an agent","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d13-en.mp4","duration":423.693,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":15.795,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 13 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.183,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"TDD with AI (1): red, green, refactor with an agent.","start":4.733,"end":8.788,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Three TDD cycles where you write the test and the agent writes the minimal implementation, without touching the test.","start":9.338,"end":15.795,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":16.595,"end":38.392,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"TDD always had a discipline cost: writing the test first when the solution is already in your head is hard.","start":17.095,"end":23.332,"terms":[]},{"text":"With an agent that writes the implementation in seconds, the temptation flips.","start":23.632,"end":28.465,"terms":[]},{"text":"The easy thing is to ask \"implement this function with its tests\" and get everything together, with tests that pass because they were written while looking at the code they had to verify.","start":28.765,"end":38.392,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":39.192,"end":60.571,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The red, green, refactor cycle orders that relationship with an authorship rule: the test is yours and the implementation is the agent's.","start":39.692,"end":47.624,"terms":[]},{"text":"The test becomes the executable specification and also the quality control for what the agent produces.","start":47.924,"end":54.417,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide walks the full cycle with a small function, so the habit sticks before you apply it to something big.","start":54.717,"end":60.571,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":61.371,"end":68.347,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":61.871,"end":63.569,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Node.js 22+, a coding agent and an empty repo.","start":64.119,"end":68.347,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":69.147,"end":75.444,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":69.647,"end":70.927,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":71.227,"end":75.444,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":76.244,"end":79.011,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":76.744,"end":79.011,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":79.811,"end":82.577,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":80.311,"end":82.577,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":83.377,"end":121.027,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":83.877,"end":84.913,"terms":[]},{"text":"Red: one test, written by you.","start":85.213,"end":88.176,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Red: you write a single test.","start":88.726,"end":90.958,"terms":[]},{"text":"The test is the specification and it's yours.","start":91.258,"end":94.105,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Write a single test describing a concrete behavior, with a name that reads like a business rule.","start":94.655,"end":99.824,"terms":[]},{"text":"One only, not the full suite.","start":100.124,"end":102.17,"terms":[]},{"text":"While writing it you make design decisions that shouldn't be delegated: what the function is called, what it takes, what it returns and what happens with invalid input.","start":102.47,"end":111.436,"terms":[]},{"text":"That is the thinking work.","start":111.736,"end":113.364,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you ask the agent for the test, it makes those decisions, and afterwards you'll be reviewing a specification you didn't write.","start":113.664,"end":121.027,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":121.827,"end":159.465,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":122.327,"end":123.526,"terms":[]},{"text":"Failing for the right reason.","start":123.826,"end":125.605,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Run npx vitest run and confirm it fails for the right reason (the function doesn't exist or returns something else), not an import error.","start":126.155,"end":134.075,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Run the test and read the message.","start":134.625,"end":136.729,"terms":[]},{"text":"It must fail because the function doesn't exist or returns something else, not because of an import, configuration or syntax error.","start":137.029,"end":144.996,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's a step often skipped and it prevents a classic problem: a test that could never fail for the reason that matters.","start":145.296,"end":151.766,"terms":[]},{"text":"If the error message is confusing, improve it now.","start":152.066,"end":155.238,"terms":[]},{"text":"That same message is what the agent will read to know what it has to build.","start":155.538,"end":159.465,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":160.265,"end":200.863,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":160.765,"end":161.847,"terms":[]},{"text":"Green: the minimum, without touching tests.","start":162.147,"end":165.087,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Green: ask the agent for the minimum code to pass that test, explicitly instructing it not to modify tests.","start":165.637,"end":172.257,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ask the agent for the minimum code to pass that test, with two explicit instructions: don't modify test files and don't add anything the test doesn't ask for.","start":172.807,"end":181.993,"terms":[]},{"text":"The word minimum matters.","start":182.293,"end":184.165,"terms":[]},{"text":"Without it you'll receive validations, cases and optional parameters nobody specified and no test covers.","start":184.465,"end":190.772,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's code that looks useful and ends up ownerless: nobody knows whether it works or whether it should exist.","start":191.072,"end":197.054,"terms":[]},{"text":"If it's needed later, you add it with its own test.","start":197.354,"end":200.863,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":201.663,"end":238.105,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":202.163,"end":203.175,"terms":[]},{"text":"Verify the green yourself.","start":203.475,"end":205.347,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Verify the green yourself by running the command.","start":205.897,"end":208.559,"terms":[]},{"text":"Review the diff: only src/discount.ts should have changed.","start":208.859,"end":213.424,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Run the command yourself and look at the result.","start":213.974,"end":216.45,"terms":[]},{"text":"Then review the diff.","start":216.75,"end":218.285,"terms":[]},{"text":"In this exercise only the implementation file should have changed.","start":218.585,"end":222.546,"terms":[]},{"text":"If the test changed, or the config, or a new file appeared, ask yourself why before going on.","start":222.846,"end":228.793,"terms":[]},{"text":"Those thirty seconds hold up the whole method: the green counts because it came from a test the agent couldn't adjust and because you confirmed it outside its summary.","start":229.093,"end":238.105,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":238.905,"end":276.915,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":239.405,"end":240.534,"terms":[]},{"text":"Refactor with the net in place.","start":240.834,"end":242.799,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Refactor: ask for a readability improvement keeping tests green.","start":243.349,"end":247.694,"terms":[]},{"text":"Commit.","start":247.994,"end":248.774,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"With the test green, ask for a readability improvement with no change in behavior: clearer names, a constant instead of a loose number, an extracted function.","start":249.324,"end":258.626,"terms":[]},{"text":"Run the tests again.","start":258.926,"end":260.392,"terms":[]},{"text":"This is the step where the agent contributes the most with the least risk, because any error is caught by the test instantly.","start":260.692,"end":267.324,"terms":[]},{"text":"Commit when done.","start":267.624,"end":269.008,"terms":[]},{"text":"If the refactor breaks something and it isn't obvious why, discarding it costs one command, and you're back to a known state.","start":269.308,"end":276.915,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":277.715,"end":315.062,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":278.215,"end":279.402,"terms":[]},{"text":"One cycle, one commit.","start":279.702,"end":281.632,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Repeat with the second and third tests.","start":282.182,"end":284.704,"terms":[]},{"text":"One commit per cycle leaves an auditable history.","start":285.004,"end":288.06,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Repeat the cycle with the second and third behaviors, and make one commit per cycle.","start":288.61,"end":293.616,"terms":[]},{"text":"The history becomes a sequence of rules added one at a time, each with its test and its implementation.","start":293.916,"end":300.165,"terms":[]},{"text":"That helps with reviewing and auditing: whoever reads the history can see what was asked, what was built and in what order.","start":300.465,"end":307.689,"terms":[]},{"text":"It also gives you cheap return points if a cycle goes wrong, something you don't have with large changes generated all at once.","start":307.989,"end":315.062,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":315.862,"end":361.45,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":316.362,"end":317.468,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The function to build applies discounts.","start":318.018,"end":320.447,"terms":[]},{"text":"First test, written by hand: a ten percent coupon on one hundred returns ninety.","start":320.747,"end":326.079,"terms":[]},{"text":"It fails because the function doesn't exist.","start":326.379,"end":329.214,"terms":[]},{"text":"The agent writes four lines and the test passes; the diff touches a single file.","start":329.514,"end":334.637,"terms":[]},{"text":"Refactor: it extracts the percentage calculation.","start":334.937,"end":338.434,"terms":[]},{"text":"Commit.","start":338.734,"end":339.514,"terms":[]},{"text":"Second test: a discount above one hundred percent throws an error.","start":339.814,"end":343.834,"terms":[]},{"text":"The agent adds the validation.","start":344.134,"end":346.215,"terms":[]},{"text":"Third test: the result is rounded to two decimals.","start":346.515,"end":349.745,"terms":[]},{"text":"In the second cycle the agent had also added support for fixed-amount coupons nobody asked for.","start":350.045,"end":355.562,"terms":[]},{"text":"It gets removed, because no test demands it.","start":355.862,"end":359.023,"terms":[]},{"text":"Three commits and three rules remain.","start":359.323,"end":361.45,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":362.25,"end":378.408,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":362.75,"end":364.413,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Asking for implementation and tests in the same request.","start":364.963,"end":368.414,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Not confirming the test fails before implementing.","start":368.964,"end":372.217,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Leaving extra code no test demands.","start":372.767,"end":375.15,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Squashing several cycles into a single commit.","start":375.7,"end":378.408,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":379.208,"end":395.481,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":379.708,"end":381.951,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each test was written by you before its implementation.","start":382.501,"end":385.72,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"You saw each test fail for the expected reason.","start":386.27,"end":389.036,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"No implementation diff touched test files.","start":389.586,"end":392.56,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The history has one commit per cycle.","start":393.11,"end":395.481,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":396.281,"end":406.869,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":396.781,"end":398.676,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"It tends to overbuild: functions and cases no test asks for.","start":399.226,"end":403.722,"terms":[]},{"text":"Code no test demands is code with no owner.","start":404.022,"end":406.869,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":407.669,"end":422.193,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 13.","start":408.169,"end":409.762,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":410.062,"end":416.044,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 14: TDD with AI (2): guardrails so the agent can't cheat.","start":416.594,"end":422.193,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d14-es","day":14,"lang":"es","title":"TDD con IA (2): guardrails para que el agente no haga trampa","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d14-es.mp4","duration":392.854,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":12.951,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 14 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.51,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"TDD con IA (2): guardrails para que el agente no haga trampa.","start":4.06,"end":7.464,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":10,"say":"i a"},{"start":16,"end":26,"say":"gárdreils"}]}],[{"text":"Las cinco trampas típicas de un agente frente a un test rojo y un control automático para cada una.","start":8.014,"end":12.951,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":13.751,"end":34.027,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Ayer el ciclo funcionó porque vos estabas mirando cada paso.","start":14.251,"end":17.539,"terms":[]},{"text":"En cuanto el agente trabaja más tiempo solo, aparece un problema conocido: su objetivo operativo es que los tests pasen, y hay varias formas de lograrlo sin que el código sea correcto.","start":17.839,"end":26.839,"terms":[]},{"text":"Puede editar el test, devolver justo el valor esperado, desactivar el caso molesto o simular la misma unidad que se suponía bajo prueba.","start":27.139,"end":34.027,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":34.827,"end":52.12,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"No es malicia.","start":35.327,"end":36.467,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es optimización hacia la métrica que le diste.","start":36.767,"end":39.208,"terms":[]},{"text":"La respuesta no es vigilarlo más sino hacer que cada atajo sea imposible o visible.","start":39.508,"end":43.679,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta guía empareja cinco trampas con cinco controles automáticos y los lleva al pipeline, de modo que la confianza en el verde no dependa de que alguien revise cada diff con lupa.","start":43.979,"end":52.12,"terms":[{"start":80,"end":88,"say":"páiplain"}]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":52.92,"end":57.911,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":53.42,"end":55.35,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El repo tdd-ai del día 13.","start":55.9,"end":57.911,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":58.711,"end":64.032,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":59.211,"end":60.386,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":60.686,"end":64.032,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":64.832,"end":67.471,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":65.332,"end":67.471,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":68.271,"end":70.91,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":68.771,"end":70.91,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Trampa y control","start":71.71,"end":102.028,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Trampa y control.","start":72.21,"end":73.443,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Editar el test para que pase: tests en solo lectura para el agente, más un guard en CI que falla si un PR de implementación toca tests.","start":73.993,"end":80.892,"terms":[{"start":75,"end":80,"say":"gard"},{"start":84,"end":86,"say":"ci ai"},{"start":103,"end":105,"say":"pe erre"}]}],[{"text":"Hardcodear el valor esperado: tests de propiedades en un archivo que el agente no ve (holdout).","start":81.442,"end":85.868,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Poner .skip o .only: regla de lint que prohíbe tests deshabilitados o enfocados.","start":86.418,"end":91.088,"terms":[{"start":15,"end":19,"say":"ónli"}]}],[{"text":"Mockear lo que se está probando: revisión de diff, ningún mock nuevo de la unidad bajo prueba.","start":91.638,"end":96.146,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tests que no prueban nada: mutation testing.","start":96.696,"end":99.194,"terms":[{"start":27,"end":35,"say":"miutéishon"}]},{"text":"Si los mutantes sobreviven, el test no valida.","start":99.494,"end":102.028,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":102.828,"end":136.495,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":103.328,"end":104.224,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tests de solo lectura.","start":104.524,"end":105.862,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Marcá los archivos de test como no editables en los permisos del agente.","start":106.412,"end":110.037,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El control más directo es que el agente no pueda escribir en los archivos de test.","start":110.587,"end":114.375,"terms":[]},{"text":"Casi todos los agentes de código permiten reglas de permisos por ruta: denegá la edición de los patrones de test y dejá libre la carpeta de implementación.","start":114.675,"end":122.061,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con eso, la trampa más frecuente deja de ser posible en lugar de depender de una instrucción en lenguaje natural.","start":122.361,"end":127.925,"terms":[]},{"text":"Una instrucción como \"no toques los tests\" se cumple casi siempre; un permiso denegado se cumple siempre.","start":128.225,"end":133.197,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cuando haga falta cambiar un test, lo hace una persona.","start":133.497,"end":136.495,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":137.295,"end":168.512,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":137.795,"end":138.691,"terms":[]},{"text":"El guard en CI.","start":138.991,"end":140.283,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":8,"say":"gard"},{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"ci ai"}]}],[{"text":"Agregá el guard de CI para PRs de implementación.","start":140.833,"end":143.715,"terms":[{"start":10,"end":15,"say":"gard"},{"start":19,"end":21,"say":"ci ai"},{"start":27,"end":30,"say":"pe erres"}]}],[{"text":"Los permisos locales protegen tu sesión, no las de todo el equipo.","start":144.265,"end":147.924,"terms":[]},{"text":"Por eso se suma un chequeo en el pipeline: si un pull request marcado como de implementación modifica archivos de test, falla.","start":148.224,"end":154.218,"terms":[{"start":33,"end":41,"say":"páiplain"},{"start":49,"end":61,"say":"pul ricuést"}]},{"text":"Es un script de pocas líneas que compara la lista de archivos cambiados contra un patrón.","start":154.518,"end":158.607,"terms":[]},{"text":"La idea de fondo es separar los cambios de especificación de los cambios de implementación.","start":158.907,"end":163.194,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un PR que toca las dos cosas no está prohibido, pero pasa a necesitar una revisión humana explícita.","start":163.494,"end":168.512,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":5,"say":"pe erre"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":169.312,"end":202.305,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":169.812,"end":170.709,"terms":[]},{"text":"Property tests fuera del contexto.","start":171.009,"end":172.985,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Escribí un test de propiedades por función crítica y dejalo fuera del contexto del agente.","start":173.535,"end":178.065,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un test con ejemplos fijos se puede satisfacer con un if que devuelva el valor esperado.","start":178.615,"end":182.844,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un property test no: genera cientos de entradas y verifica una propiedad general, por ejemplo que el total con descuento nunca supera al original ni es negativo.","start":183.144,"end":190.879,"terms":[]},{"text":"Escribí uno por función crítica y mantenelo fuera de lo que el agente lee, como un conjunto de validación apartado.","start":191.179,"end":196.58,"terms":[]},{"text":"Corre en CI, no en su sesión.","start":196.88,"end":198.891,"terms":[{"start":9,"end":11,"say":"ci ai"}]},{"text":"Si la implementación solo memorizó los ejemplos, falla ahí.","start":199.191,"end":202.305,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":203.105,"end":232.894,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":203.605,"end":204.607,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lint contra tests desactivados.","start":204.907,"end":206.778,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Activá la regla de lint contra .skip y .only.","start":207.328,"end":210.164,"terms":[{"start":40,"end":44,"say":"ónli"}]}],[{"text":"Agregar .skip a un test que falla o dejar un .only que hace que el resto no corra son cambios de una palabra que pasan desapercibidos en un diff largo.","start":210.714,"end":217.915,"terms":[{"start":46,"end":50,"say":"ónli"}]},{"text":"Una regla de lint los detecta sin esfuerzo y de forma determinista.","start":218.215,"end":221.584,"terms":[]},{"text":"Activala como error, no como advertencia, y corré el lint en el pipeline.","start":221.884,"end":225.59,"terms":[{"start":64,"end":72,"say":"páiplain"}]},{"text":"Si tu equipo necesita desactivar un test temporalmente, que sea con un mecanismo distinto que exija un comentario con el motivo y un ticket asociado.","start":225.89,"end":232.894,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":233.694,"end":267.825,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":234.194,"end":235.195,"terms":[]},{"text":"Mutation testing sobre lo tocado.","start":235.495,"end":237.587,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":8,"say":"miutéishon"}]}],[{"text":"Corré Stryker y mirá el mutation score de los archivos que tocó el agente.","start":238.137,"end":241.855,"terms":[{"start":6,"end":13,"say":"stráiker"},{"start":24,"end":32,"say":"miutéishon"}]}],[{"text":"Cobertura alta no significa que los tests verifiquen algo.","start":242.405,"end":245.577,"terms":[]},{"text":"Mutation testing lo mide directamente: introduce cambios chicos en el código, como invertir una condición o cambiar un operador, y comprueba si algún test falla.","start":245.877,"end":253.601,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":8,"say":"miutéishon"}]},{"text":"Si el mutante sobrevive, ese comportamiento no está protegido.","start":253.901,"end":257.27,"terms":[]},{"text":"Correrlo sobre todo el proyecto es lento, así que limitalo a los archivos que cambió el PR.","start":257.57,"end":262.112,"terms":[{"start":88,"end":90,"say":"pe erre"}]},{"text":"Mirá los mutantes sobrevivientes en la lógica de negocio: cada uno es un test que falta o una aserción débil.","start":262.412,"end":267.825,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":268.625,"end":300.713,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":269.125,"end":270.091,"terms":[]},{"text":"Todo en el pipeline.","start":270.391,"end":271.625,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":19,"say":"páiplain"}]}],[{"text":"Sumá todo al pipeline: si un control falla, el PR no se mergea.","start":272.175,"end":275.904,"terms":[{"start":13,"end":21,"say":"páiplain"},{"start":47,"end":49,"say":"pe erre"}]}],[{"text":"Los cinco controles valen cuando bloquean.","start":276.454,"end":278.744,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sumalos como pasos obligatorios: el guard de archivos, el lint, la suite normal, los property tests apartados y mutation testing con un umbral mínimo para los archivos tocados.","start":279.044,"end":288.16,"terms":[{"start":36,"end":41,"say":"gard"},{"start":67,"end":72,"say":"suit"},{"start":112,"end":120,"say":"miutéishon"}]},{"text":"Si alguno falla, el PR no se mergea.","start":288.46,"end":290.762,"terms":[{"start":20,"end":22,"say":"pe erre"}]},{"text":"Ordená los pasos del más rápido al más lento para que los errores baratos aparezcan primero.","start":291.062,"end":295.534,"terms":[]},{"text":"El resultado es que el verde vuelve a significar algo, lo haya escrito una persona o un agente.","start":295.834,"end":300.713,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":301.513,"end":335.012,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":302.013,"end":302.933,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un agente trabaja una hora sobre un módulo de precios y entrega un PR con todos los tests en verde y cobertura del noventa y cinco por ciento.","start":303.483,"end":310.08,"terms":[{"start":67,"end":69,"say":"pe erre"}]},{"text":"El pipeline con guardrails cuenta otra historia.","start":310.38,"end":312.89,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":11,"say":"páiplain"},{"start":16,"end":26,"say":"gárdreils"}]},{"text":"El guard de archivos pasa.","start":313.19,"end":314.772,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":8,"say":"gard"}]},{"text":"El lint encuentra un .skip agregado a un test de redondeo.","start":315.072,"end":318.256,"terms":[]},{"text":"El property test apartado falla con un precio de cero.","start":318.556,"end":321.496,"terms":[]},{"text":"Mutation testing muestra que sobrevive la mitad de los mutantes en la función de impuestos: los tests la ejecutaban sin verificar el resultado.","start":321.796,"end":328.799,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":8,"say":"miutéishon"}]},{"text":"Tres hallazgos que una revisión rápida del diff no habría visto.","start":329.099,"end":332.329,"terms":[]},{"text":"El PR vuelve con tres pedidos concretos.","start":332.629,"end":335.012,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":5,"say":"pe erre"}]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":335.812,"end":351.552,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":336.312,"end":337.836,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Confiar en una instrucción en texto en lugar de un permiso denegado.","start":338.386,"end":341.79,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Medir la calidad de los tests solo con cobertura.","start":342.34,"end":344.862,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Dejar los property tests al alcance del agente.","start":345.412,"end":347.934,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tener los controles como advertencias que no bloquean el merge.","start":348.484,"end":351.552,"terms":[{"start":57,"end":62,"say":"merch"}]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":352.352,"end":369.008,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":352.852,"end":355.118,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El agente no puede editar archivos de test.","start":355.668,"end":357.958,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El pipeline falla si un PR de implementación toca tests.","start":358.508,"end":361.564,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":11,"say":"páiplain"},{"start":24,"end":26,"say":"pe erre"}]}],[{"text":"Hay un property test apartado por cada función crítica.","start":362.114,"end":365.112,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Mutation testing corre sobre los archivos tocados con un umbral.","start":365.662,"end":369.008,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":8,"say":"miutéishon"}]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":369.808,"end":378.957,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":370.308,"end":371.878,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Produce suites con cobertura total que casi no detectan mutantes.","start":372.428,"end":375.867,"terms":[]},{"text":"Hay que medir si el test falla cuando el código está mal.","start":376.167,"end":378.957,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":379.757,"end":391.354,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 14.","start":380.257,"end":381.815,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":382.115,"end":387.354,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 15: Cómo setear modelos para pruebas de calidad.","start":387.904,"end":391.354,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d14-en","day":14,"lang":"en","title":"TDD with AI (2): guardrails so the agent can't cheat","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d14-en.mp4","duration":432.085,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":14.065,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 14 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":3.986,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"TDD with AI (2): guardrails so the agent can't cheat.","start":4.536,"end":8.381,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The five typical ways an agent cheats on a red test and an automatic control for each.","start":8.931,"end":14.065,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":14.865,"end":38.183,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Yesterday the cycle worked because you were watching every step.","start":15.365,"end":18.596,"terms":[]},{"text":"As soon as the agent works longer on its own, a known problem appears: its operating goal is for tests to pass, and there are several ways to achieve that without the code being correct.","start":18.896,"end":29.568,"terms":[]},{"text":"It can edit the test, return exactly the expected value, disable the annoying case or mock the very unit that was supposed to be under test.","start":29.868,"end":38.183,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":38.983,"end":59.597,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"It isn't malice.","start":39.483,"end":40.867,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's optimization toward the metric you gave it.","start":41.167,"end":43.782,"terms":[]},{"text":"The answer isn't watching it more closely but making every shortcut impossible or visible.","start":44.082,"end":49.147,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide pairs five cheats with five automatic controls and takes them into the pipeline, so that trust in green doesn't depend on someone reviewing every diff with a magnifying glass.","start":49.447,"end":59.597,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":60.397,"end":65.771,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":60.897,"end":62.594,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The tdd-ai repo from day 13.","start":63.144,"end":65.771,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":66.571,"end":72.868,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":67.071,"end":68.35,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":68.65,"end":72.868,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":73.668,"end":76.434,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":74.168,"end":76.434,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":77.234,"end":80.001,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":77.734,"end":80.001,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cheat and control","start":80.801,"end":115.716,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Cheat and control.","start":81.301,"end":82.673,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Editing the test so it passes: read-only tests for the agent, plus a CI guard that fails if an implementation PR touches tests.","start":83.223,"end":91.26,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Hardcoding the expected value: property-based tests in a file the agent never sees (holdout).","start":91.81,"end":97.629,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Adding .skip or .only: a lint rule banning disabled or focused tests.","start":98.179,"end":103.128,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Mocking the unit under test: diff review, no new mock of the unit under test.","start":103.678,"end":108.684,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tests that test nothing: mutation testing.","start":109.234,"end":112.36,"terms":[]},{"text":"If mutants survive, the test doesn't validate.","start":112.66,"end":115.716,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":116.516,"end":153.085,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":117.016,"end":118.052,"terms":[]},{"text":"Read-only tests.","start":118.352,"end":119.864,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Mark test files as non-editable in the agent's permissions.","start":120.414,"end":124.062,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The most direct control is that the agent can't write to test files.","start":124.612,"end":128.492,"terms":[]},{"text":"Almost every coding agent supports path-based permission rules: deny edits to the test patterns and leave the implementation folder open.","start":128.792,"end":136.434,"terms":[]},{"text":"With that, the most frequent cheat stops being possible instead of depending on a natural-language instruction.","start":136.734,"end":142.797,"terms":[]},{"text":"An instruction such as \"don't touch the tests\" is followed almost always; a denied permission is followed always.","start":143.097,"end":149.799,"terms":[]},{"text":"When a test needs to change, a person does it.","start":150.099,"end":153.085,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":153.885,"end":188.586,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":154.385,"end":155.584,"terms":[]},{"text":"The CI guard.","start":155.884,"end":157.164,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Add the CI guard for implementation PRs.","start":157.714,"end":160.955,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Local permissions protect your session, not the whole team's.","start":161.505,"end":165.49,"terms":[]},{"text":"That's why you add a check in the pipeline: if a pull request labeled as implementation modifies test files, it fails.","start":165.79,"end":172.991,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's a script of a few lines comparing the list of changed files against a pattern.","start":173.291,"end":177.868,"terms":[]},{"text":"The underlying idea is to separate specification changes from implementation changes.","start":178.168,"end":183.291,"terms":[]},{"text":"A PR touching both isn't forbidden, but it now requires an explicit human review.","start":183.591,"end":188.586,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":189.386,"end":227.453,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":189.886,"end":190.968,"terms":[]},{"text":"Property tests out of context.","start":191.268,"end":193.256,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Write one property test per critical function and keep it out of the agent's context.","start":193.806,"end":198.627,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A test with fixed examples can be satisfied by an if returning the expected value.","start":199.177,"end":204.056,"terms":[]},{"text":"A property test can't: it generates hundreds of inputs and checks a general property, for example that the discounted total never exceeds the original and is never negative.","start":204.356,"end":213.635,"terms":[]},{"text":"Write one per critical function and keep it out of what the agent reads, as a held-out validation set.","start":213.935,"end":219.545,"terms":[]},{"text":"It runs in CI, not in the agent's session.","start":219.845,"end":222.878,"terms":[]},{"text":"If the implementation only memorized the examples, it fails there.","start":223.178,"end":227.453,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":228.253,"end":260.259,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":228.753,"end":229.765,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lint against disabled tests.","start":230.065,"end":231.96,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Enable the lint rule against .skip and .only.","start":232.51,"end":235.497,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Adding .skip to a failing test or leaving an .only that stops the rest from running are one-word changes that go unnoticed in a long diff.","start":236.047,"end":243.886,"terms":[]},{"text":"A lint rule catches them effortlessly and deterministically.","start":244.186,"end":247.66,"terms":[]},{"text":"Enable it as an error, not a warning, and run lint in the pipeline.","start":247.96,"end":252.084,"terms":[]},{"text":"If your team needs to disable a test temporarily, do it through a different mechanism that requires a comment with the reason and a linked ticket.","start":252.384,"end":260.259,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":261.059,"end":298.023,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":261.559,"end":262.687,"terms":[]},{"text":"Mutation testing on what was touched.","start":262.987,"end":265.242,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Run Stryker and check the mutation score for files the agent touched.","start":265.792,"end":269.94,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"High coverage doesn't mean tests verify anything.","start":270.49,"end":273.523,"terms":[]},{"text":"Mutation testing measures that directly: it introduces small changes in the code, such as flipping a condition or swapping an operator, and checks whether any test fails.","start":273.823,"end":283.438,"terms":[]},{"text":"If the mutant survives, that behavior isn't protected.","start":283.738,"end":287.096,"terms":[]},{"text":"Running it across the whole project is slow, so limit it to the files the PR changed.","start":287.396,"end":292.263,"terms":[]},{"text":"Look at surviving mutants in business logic: each one is a missing test or a weak assertion.","start":292.563,"end":298.023,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":298.823,"end":334.22,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":299.323,"end":300.51,"terms":[]},{"text":"Everything in the pipeline.","start":300.81,"end":302.426,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Add everything to the pipeline: if a control fails, the PR doesn't merge.","start":302.976,"end":307.75,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The five controls count when they block.","start":308.3,"end":310.648,"terms":[]},{"text":"Add them as required steps: the file guard, lint, the normal suite, the held-out property tests and mutation testing with a minimum threshold for touched files.","start":310.948,"end":320.935,"terms":[]},{"text":"If any fails, the PR doesn't merge.","start":321.235,"end":323.85,"terms":[]},{"text":"Order the steps from fastest to slowest so cheap errors show up first.","start":324.15,"end":328.6,"terms":[]},{"text":"The result is that green means something again, whether a person or an agent wrote the code.","start":328.9,"end":334.22,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":335.02,"end":370.225,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":335.52,"end":336.625,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"An agent works for an hour on a pricing module and delivers a PR with all tests green and ninety-five percent coverage.","start":337.175,"end":344.097,"terms":[]},{"text":"The pipeline with guardrails tells a different story.","start":344.397,"end":347.488,"terms":[]},{"text":"The file guard passes.","start":347.788,"end":349.521,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lint finds a .skip added to a rounding test.","start":349.821,"end":352.97,"terms":[]},{"text":"The held-out property test fails on a price of zero.","start":353.27,"end":356.465,"terms":[]},{"text":"Mutation testing shows half the mutants in the tax function survive: the tests executed it without verifying the result.","start":356.765,"end":363.664,"terms":[]},{"text":"Three findings a quick diff review wouldn't have seen.","start":363.964,"end":366.881,"terms":[]},{"text":"The PR goes back with three concrete requests.","start":367.181,"end":370.225,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":371.025,"end":387.38,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":371.525,"end":373.188,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Relying on a text instruction instead of a denied permission.","start":373.738,"end":377.154,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Measuring test quality with coverage alone.","start":377.704,"end":380.4,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Leaving property tests within the agent's reach.","start":380.95,"end":383.774,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Having the controls as warnings that don't block the merge.","start":384.324,"end":387.38,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":388.18,"end":405.858,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":388.68,"end":390.923,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The agent can't edit test files.","start":391.473,"end":393.902,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The pipeline fails if an implementation PR touches tests.","start":394.452,"end":398.159,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"There is a held-out property test per critical function.","start":398.709,"end":401.869,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Mutation testing runs on touched files with a threshold.","start":402.419,"end":405.858,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":406.658,"end":416.631,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":407.158,"end":409.053,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"It produces full-coverage suites that barely catch mutants.","start":409.603,"end":413.379,"terms":[]},{"text":"Measure whether the test fails when the code is wrong.","start":413.679,"end":416.631,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":417.431,"end":430.585,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 14.","start":417.931,"end":419.594,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":419.894,"end":425.876,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 15: How to set up models for quality testing.","start":426.426,"end":430.585,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d15-es","day":15,"lang":"es","title":"Cómo setear modelos para pruebas de calidad","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d15-es.mp4","duration":378.991,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":14.53,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 15 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.568,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Cómo setear modelos para pruebas de calidad.","start":4.118,"end":6.28,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Configurar un modelo para pruebas de forma reproducible: versión fija, parámetros explícitos, salida estructurada y un juez separado del modelo evaluado.","start":6.83,"end":14.53,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":15.33,"end":34.584,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Cuando un test con un modelo da resultados distintos de un día para el otro, la primera sospecha suele ser el modelo.","start":15.83,"end":21.498,"terms":[]},{"text":"La segunda debería ser la configuración.","start":21.798,"end":24.25,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un alias que apunta a la última versión, una temperatura que quedó en el valor por defecto, un límite de tokens que corta la respuesta o un juez que cambió sin aviso explican buena parte de las diferencias inexplicables.","start":24.55,"end":34.584,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":35.384,"end":53.732,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Configurar un modelo para pruebas de calidad es tratarlo como cualquier otra dependencia de un entorno de test: versión fija, parámetros explícitos y registro de con qué corrió cada cosa.","start":35.884,"end":45.024,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta guía recorre las seis decisiones que hacen que dos corridas sean comparables, y deja en claro qué significa reproducibilidad cuando el sistema bajo prueba no es determinista.","start":45.324,"end":53.732,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":54.532,"end":63.373,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":55.032,"end":56.962,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El eval harness del día 8.","start":57.512,"end":59.233,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":15,"say":"járnes"}]}],[{"text":"Opcional: API key de un proveedor para el segundo modelo y el juez.","start":59.783,"end":63.373,"terms":[{"start":14,"end":17,"say":"ki"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":64.173,"end":69.494,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":64.673,"end":65.848,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":66.148,"end":69.494,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":70.294,"end":106.795,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":70.794,"end":71.691,"terms":[]},{"text":"El rol define la configuración.","start":71.991,"end":73.991,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Definí el rol del modelo en la prueba: sistema bajo prueba, generador (datos o tests) o juez.","start":74.541,"end":79.176,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cada rol se configura distinto.","start":79.476,"end":81.359,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un mismo modelo puede cumplir tres roles en tu proceso, y cada uno pide otra cosa.","start":81.909,"end":86.207,"terms":[]},{"text":"Como sistema bajo prueba, se configura igual que en producción, porque querés medir lo que el usuario recibe.","start":86.507,"end":91.978,"terms":[]},{"text":"Como generador de datos o tests, puede usar más temperatura para producir variedad.","start":92.278,"end":96.704,"terms":[]},{"text":"Como juez, necesita la configuración más estable posible.","start":97.004,"end":100.258,"terms":[]},{"text":"Mezclar roles en una sola configuración es un error frecuente: terminás midiendo el producto con parámetros que el producto no usa.","start":100.558,"end":106.795,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":107.595,"end":142.086,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":108.095,"end":108.992,"terms":[]},{"text":"Versión exacta, sin alias.","start":109.292,"end":111.024,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Fijá la versión exacta.","start":111.574,"end":113.109,"terms":[]},{"text":"Nada de alias tipo \"latest\": si el proveedor actualiza el modelo, tus resultados cambian sin que toques nada.","start":113.409,"end":118.776,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Los proveedores ofrecen alias que apuntan a la versión más reciente de un modelo.","start":119.326,"end":123.264,"terms":[]},{"text":"Son cómodos para desarrollar y malos para medir: el día que el alias cambia, tus resultados cambian sin que hayas tocado nada, y no tenés forma de saber por qué.","start":123.564,"end":131.032,"terms":[]},{"text":"Usá siempre el identificador completo con su versión o fecha.","start":131.332,"end":134.562,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cuando quieras actualizar, hacelo como un cambio deliberado: corré el eval con la versión vieja y con la nueva, compará y recién después cambiá el identificador.","start":134.862,"end":142.086,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":142.886,"end":183.427,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":143.386,"end":144.283,"terms":[]},{"text":"Parámetros a la vista.","start":144.583,"end":145.99,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Parámetros explícitos: temperatura baja para clasificar y juzgar, límite de tokens de salida y seed si el proveedor lo soporta.","start":146.54,"end":153.091,"terms":[{"start":95,"end":99,"say":"sid"}]},{"text":"Si un modelo no expone algún parámetro, anotalo.","start":153.391,"end":156.006,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Escribí en la configuración los parámetros que afectan la salida, aunque coincidan con los valores por defecto: temperatura, límite de tokens de salida y semilla si el proveedor la soporta.","start":156.556,"end":165.463,"terms":[]},{"text":"Para clasificar y juzgar, temperatura baja.","start":165.763,"end":168.366,"terms":[]},{"text":"El límite de tokens merece atención: una respuesta cortada a la mitad puede parecer un fallo de formato cuando es un límite mal puesto.","start":168.666,"end":175.519,"terms":[]},{"text":"Algunos modelos no exponen ciertos parámetros o los ignoran.","start":175.819,"end":178.991,"terms":[]},{"text":"Anotalo también, para no atribuirle a la configuración un control que no tenés.","start":179.291,"end":183.427,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":184.227,"end":218.579,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":184.727,"end":185.728,"terms":[]},{"text":"Salida estructurada.","start":186.028,"end":187.331,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Salida estructurada: pedí JSON con schema.","start":187.881,"end":190.357,"terms":[{"start":26,"end":30,"say":"yeison"},{"start":35,"end":41,"say":"skima"}]},{"text":"Te ahorra buena parte de los fallos de formato.","start":190.657,"end":192.888,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Si tu código va a parsear la respuesta, pedila en JSON con un schema, usando el mecanismo de salida estructurada del proveedor cuando exista.","start":193.438,"end":200.442,"terms":[{"start":50,"end":54,"say":"yeison"},{"start":62,"end":68,"say":"skima"}]},{"text":"Reduce una clase entera de fallos: texto antes o después del JSON, campos con otro nombre, valores fuera de la lista permitida.","start":200.742,"end":207.374,"terms":[{"start":61,"end":65,"say":"yeison"}]},{"text":"No reemplaza a la validación de tu lado, que sigue siendo necesaria, pero cambia la proporción.","start":207.674,"end":212.32,"terms":[]},{"text":"Vas a dedicar los evals a medir si el contenido es correcto, en lugar de gastar casos en descubrir que el formato vino roto.","start":212.62,"end":218.579,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":219.379,"end":252.081,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":219.879,"end":220.88,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un juez separado.","start":221.18,"end":222.274,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Juez separado: el modelo que evalúa no debería ser el mismo que se evalúa, y conviene que sea igual o más capaz.","start":222.824,"end":228.33,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El modelo que califica no debería ser el mismo que está siendo calificado, y conviene que sea igual o más capaz.","start":228.88,"end":234.525,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un modelo que evalúa su propia salida tiende a encontrarla razonable, porque comparte los mismos sesgos que la produjeron.","start":234.825,"end":240.528,"terms":[]},{"text":"Declaralo como un provider aparte, con su versión fija y su etiqueta.","start":240.828,"end":244.546,"terms":[{"start":18,"end":26,"say":"prováider"}]},{"text":"Cambiar el juez es cambiar el instrumento de medición: después de hacerlo, las métricas nuevas no son comparables con las anteriores hasta volver a calibrar.","start":244.846,"end":252.081,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":252.881,"end":286.989,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":253.381,"end":254.348,"terms":[]},{"text":"La ficha de corrida.","start":254.648,"end":255.892,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Registrá la ficha de cada corrida: modelo y versión, versión del prompt, parámetros, fecha y dataset.","start":256.442,"end":262.169,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sin ficha no hay comparación válida.","start":262.469,"end":264.526,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cada corrida debería dejar un registro con modelo y versión, versión del prompt, parámetros, fecha, dataset y, si hay, juez.","start":265.076,"end":271.882,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es un archivo chico que se guarda junto a los resultados.","start":272.182,"end":275.192,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sin esa ficha, una comparación entre dos corridas no dice nada, porque no sabés qué más cambió entre una y otra.","start":275.492,"end":281.068,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con ella podés responder la pregunta que siempre llega: por qué la semana pasada daba noventa y hoy da ochenta y cuatro.","start":281.368,"end":286.989,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":287.789,"end":319.627,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":288.289,"end":289.209,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un equipo nota que su eval de clasificación bajó cinco puntos sin que nadie tocara el prompt.","start":289.759,"end":294.336,"terms":[]},{"text":"Buscan las fichas de corrida y no existen.","start":294.636,"end":296.914,"terms":[]},{"text":"Reconstruyen lo que pueden: el provider usaba un alias y el proveedor había publicado una versión nueva esa semana.","start":297.214,"end":302.674,"terms":[{"start":31,"end":39,"say":"prováider"}]},{"text":"Fijan la versión anterior, corren de nuevo y el resultado vuelve al valor original.","start":302.974,"end":307.121,"terms":[]},{"text":"Después corren la versión nueva a propósito: es mejor en los casos ambiguos y peor en dos casos críticos.","start":307.421,"end":312.497,"terms":[]},{"text":"Deciden no actualizar todavía.","start":312.797,"end":314.704,"terms":[]},{"text":"A partir de ahí, cada corrida guarda su ficha y el identificador del modelo incluye la fecha.","start":315.004,"end":319.627,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":320.427,"end":335.749,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":320.927,"end":322.451,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Usar un alias de versión en evals o en CI.","start":323.001,"end":325.5,"terms":[{"start":39,"end":41,"say":"ci ai"}]}],[{"text":"Evaluar el producto con parámetros distintos a los de producción.","start":326.05,"end":329.338,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Usar como juez el mismo modelo que se evalúa.","start":329.888,"end":332.271,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Comparar dos corridas sin saber qué cambió entre ellas.","start":332.821,"end":335.749,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":336.549,"end":353.182,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":337.049,"end":339.315,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cada provider tiene un rol y una etiqueta que lo dice.","start":339.865,"end":342.713,"terms":[{"start":5,"end":13,"say":"prováider"}]}],[{"text":"Todos los modelos están fijados a una versión exacta.","start":343.263,"end":346.237,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Temperatura, límite de tokens y semilla están escritos.","start":346.787,"end":349.913,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cada corrida guarda su ficha junto a los resultados.","start":350.463,"end":353.182,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":353.982,"end":364.025,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":354.482,"end":356.052,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Temperatura cero no garantiza la misma respuesta.","start":356.602,"end":359.368,"terms":[]},{"text":"La reproducibilidad en LLMs es estadística: se mide con corridas repetidas.","start":359.668,"end":364.025,"terms":[{"start":23,"end":27,"say":"ele ele emes"}]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":364.825,"end":377.491,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 15.","start":365.325,"end":366.709,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":367.009,"end":372.248,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 16: LLM como juez: rúbricas y calibración contra humanos.","start":372.798,"end":377.491,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d15-en","day":15,"lang":"en","title":"How to set up models for quality testing","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d15-en.mp4","duration":425.071,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":17.27,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 15 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.334,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"How to set up models for quality testing.","start":4.884,"end":7.487,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Configuring a model for testing reproducibly: pinned version, explicit parameters, structured output and a judge separate from the model under test.","start":8.037,"end":17.27,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":18.07,"end":39.24,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"When a test involving a model gives different results from one day to the next, the first suspect is usually the model.","start":18.57,"end":25.062,"terms":[]},{"text":"The second should be the configuration.","start":25.362,"end":27.582,"terms":[]},{"text":"An alias pointing to the latest version, a temperature left at its default, a token limit that cuts the answer off or a judge that changed without notice explain a good share of the unexplained differences.","start":27.882,"end":39.24,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":40.04,"end":60.059,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Setting up a model for quality testing means treating it like any other dependency of a test environment: pinned version, explicit parameters and a record of what each thing ran with.","start":40.54,"end":50.608,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide walks through the six decisions that make two runs comparable, and makes clear what reproducibility means when the system under test isn't deterministic.","start":50.908,"end":60.059,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":60.859,"end":70.234,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":61.359,"end":63.057,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The eval harness from day 8.","start":63.607,"end":65.502,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Optional: a provider API key for the second model and the judge.","start":66.052,"end":70.234,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":71.034,"end":77.331,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":71.534,"end":72.814,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":73.114,"end":77.331,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":78.131,"end":120.228,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":78.631,"end":79.667,"terms":[]},{"text":"The role defines the config.","start":79.967,"end":81.943,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Define the model's role in the test: system under test, generator (data or tests) or judge.","start":82.493,"end":88.15,"terms":[]},{"text":"Each role is configured differently.","start":88.45,"end":90.682,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The same model can play three roles in your process, and each asks for something different.","start":91.232,"end":95.96,"terms":[]},{"text":"As the system under test, it's configured the same as in production, because you want to measure what the user gets.","start":96.26,"end":102.601,"terms":[]},{"text":"As a generator of data or tests, it can use more temperature to produce variety.","start":102.901,"end":108.163,"terms":[]},{"text":"As a judge, it needs the most stable configuration possible.","start":108.463,"end":112.483,"terms":[]},{"text":"Mixing roles in a single config is a frequent mistake: you end up measuring the product with parameters the product doesn't use.","start":112.783,"end":120.228,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":121.028,"end":160.813,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":121.528,"end":122.726,"terms":[]},{"text":"Exact version, no aliases.","start":123.026,"end":125.537,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pin the exact version.","start":126.087,"end":127.738,"terms":[]},{"text":"No \"latest\" aliases: if the provider updates the model, your results change without you touching anything.","start":128.038,"end":134.229,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Providers offer aliases pointing to a model's most recent version.","start":134.779,"end":138.705,"terms":[]},{"text":"They're convenient for development and bad for measuring: the day the alias moves, your results change without you touching anything, and you have no way of knowing why.","start":139.005,"end":148.006,"terms":[]},{"text":"Always use the full identifier with its version or date.","start":148.306,"end":151.768,"terms":[]},{"text":"When you want to upgrade, do it as a deliberate change: run the eval with the old and the new version, compare, and only then change the identifier.","start":152.068,"end":160.813,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":161.613,"end":205.045,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":162.113,"end":163.195,"terms":[]},{"text":"Parameters in plain sight.","start":163.495,"end":165.379,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Explicit parameters: low temperature for classifying and judging, an output token limit and a seed if the provider supports it.","start":165.929,"end":173.141,"terms":[]},{"text":"If a model doesn't expose a parameter, write that down.","start":173.441,"end":176.892,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Write into the config the parameters that affect the output, even when they match the defaults: temperature, output token limit and seed if the provider supports it.","start":177.442,"end":186.547,"terms":[]},{"text":"For classifying and judging, low temperature.","start":186.847,"end":189.613,"terms":[]},{"text":"The token limit deserves attention: an answer cut off halfway can look like a format failure when it's a badly set limit.","start":189.913,"end":196.545,"terms":[]},{"text":"Some models don't expose certain parameters or ignore them.","start":196.845,"end":200.435,"terms":[]},{"text":"Note that too, so you don't credit the config with control you don't have.","start":200.735,"end":205.045,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":205.845,"end":247.093,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":206.345,"end":207.357,"terms":[]},{"text":"Structured output.","start":207.657,"end":209.135,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Structured output: request JSON with a schema.","start":209.685,"end":212.903,"terms":[]},{"text":"It removes a good share of your format failures.","start":213.203,"end":216.294,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"If your code is going to parse the answer, request it as JSON with a schema, using the provider's structured output mechanism where one exists.","start":216.844,"end":225.043,"terms":[]},{"text":"It reduces a whole class of failures: text before or after the JSON, fields with another name, values outside the allowed list.","start":225.343,"end":233.949,"terms":[]},{"text":"It doesn't replace validation on your side, which is still necessary, but it changes the proportion.","start":234.249,"end":239.917,"terms":[]},{"text":"You'll spend evals measuring whether content is correct, instead of spending cases discovering the format came back broken.","start":240.217,"end":247.093,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":247.893,"end":282.964,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":248.393,"end":249.522,"terms":[]},{"text":"A separate judge.","start":249.822,"end":251.136,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Separate judge: the grading model shouldn't be the one being graded, and should be equally or more capable.","start":251.686,"end":257.761,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The model that grades shouldn't be the one being graded, and it should be equally or more capable.","start":258.311,"end":263.434,"terms":[]},{"text":"A model evaluating its own output tends to find it reasonable, because it shares the same biases that produced it.","start":263.734,"end":270.098,"terms":[]},{"text":"Declare it as a separate provider, with its pinned version and its label.","start":270.398,"end":274.639,"terms":[]},{"text":"Changing the judge is changing the measuring instrument: after doing so, new metrics aren't comparable with earlier ones until you recalibrate.","start":274.939,"end":282.964,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":283.764,"end":322.493,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":284.264,"end":285.451,"terms":[]},{"text":"The run card.","start":285.751,"end":286.891,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Record a run card for each run: model and version, prompt version, parameters, date and dataset.","start":287.441,"end":294.119,"terms":[]},{"text":"No card, no valid comparison.","start":294.419,"end":297.116,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each run should leave a record with model and version, prompt version, parameters, date, dataset and, if any, judge.","start":297.666,"end":305.911,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's a small file stored next to the results.","start":306.211,"end":308.896,"terms":[]},{"text":"Without that card, a comparison between two runs says nothing, because you don't know what else changed between them.","start":309.196,"end":316.118,"terms":[]},{"text":"With it you can answer the question that always arrives: why it scored ninety last week and eighty-four today.","start":316.418,"end":322.493,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":323.293,"end":359.415,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":323.793,"end":324.898,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A team notices their classification eval dropped five points with nobody touching the prompt.","start":325.448,"end":330.478,"terms":[]},{"text":"They look for the run cards and there are none.","start":330.778,"end":333.137,"terms":[]},{"text":"They reconstruct what they can: the provider used an alias and the vendor had released a new version that week.","start":333.437,"end":339.547,"terms":[]},{"text":"They pin the previous version, run again and the result returns to its original value.","start":339.847,"end":345.167,"terms":[]},{"text":"Then they run the new version on purpose: it's better on ambiguous cases and worse on two critical ones.","start":345.467,"end":351.704,"terms":[]},{"text":"They decide not to upgrade yet.","start":352.004,"end":354.039,"terms":[]},{"text":"From then on, every run stores its card and the model identifier includes the date.","start":354.339,"end":359.415,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":360.215,"end":378.125,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":360.715,"end":362.378,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Using a version alias in evals or in CI.","start":362.928,"end":365.891,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Evaluating the product with parameters different from production.","start":366.441,"end":370.275,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Using the model under evaluation as its own judge.","start":370.825,"end":374.09,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Comparing two runs without knowing what changed between them.","start":374.64,"end":378.125,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":378.925,"end":395.675,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":379.425,"end":381.669,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each provider has a role and a label that states it.","start":382.219,"end":385.101,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Every model is pinned to an exact version.","start":385.651,"end":388.347,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Temperature, token limit and seed are written down.","start":388.897,"end":392.208,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each run stores its card next to the results.","start":392.758,"end":395.675,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":396.475,"end":407.922,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":396.975,"end":398.87,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Temperature zero doesn't guarantee the same answer.","start":399.42,"end":402.627,"terms":[]},{"text":"Reproducibility with LLMs is statistical: you measure it with repeated runs.","start":402.927,"end":407.922,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":408.722,"end":423.571,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 15.","start":409.222,"end":410.989,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":411.289,"end":417.271,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 16: LLM-as-a-judge: rubrics and calibration against humans.","start":417.821,"end":423.571,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d16-es","day":16,"lang":"es","title":"LLM como juez: rúbricas y calibración contra humanos","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d16-es.mp4","duration":374.354,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":13.705,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 16 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.661,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"LLM como juez: rúbricas y calibración contra humanos.","start":4.211,"end":7.441,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cómo escribir una rúbrica observable para un modelo juez y medir cuánto coincide con tu criterio antes de confiar en él.","start":7.991,"end":13.705,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":14.505,"end":30.88,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Hay cualidades de una respuesta que ninguna regla captura: si la explicación es clara, si el tono corresponde, si contesta lo que se preguntó.","start":15.005,"end":21.846,"terms":[]},{"text":"Revisarlas a mano no escala más allá de unas decenas de casos.","start":22.146,"end":25.423,"terms":[]},{"text":"La solución habitual es pedirle a otro modelo que las califique, lo que se conoce como LLM como juez.","start":25.723,"end":30.88,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":31.68,"end":52.839,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"El problema es que ese juez es otro sistema no determinista con sus propios errores, y sus veredictos vienen acompañados de justificaciones muy convincentes.","start":32.18,"end":39.718,"terms":[]},{"text":"Usarlo sin calibrar equivale a medir con un instrumento que nunca comparaste contra un patrón.","start":40.018,"end":44.665,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta guía muestra cómo escribir una rúbrica que se pueda verificar, cómo medir el acuerdo entre el juez y vos, y cuándo ese acuerdo alcanza para confiarle un gate.","start":44.965,"end":52.839,"terms":[{"start":158,"end":162,"say":"gueit"}]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":53.639,"end":66.829,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":54.139,"end":56.069,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un eval harness con juez configurado (días 8 y 15).","start":56.619,"end":59.524,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":15,"say":"járnes"}]}],[{"text":"30 salidas reales del modelo evaluado.","start":60.074,"end":62.248,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Una planilla con columnas: id, veredicto humano, veredicto del juez, motivo.","start":62.798,"end":66.829,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":67.629,"end":72.95,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":68.129,"end":69.304,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":69.604,"end":72.95,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":73.75,"end":109.108,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":74.25,"end":75.147,"terms":[]},{"text":"Criterios observables.","start":75.447,"end":76.878,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Escribí criterios observables.","start":77.428,"end":79.299,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"¿Es buena la respuesta?\" no sirve.","start":79.599,"end":81.776,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"Menciona el componente del reporte\" sí.","start":82.076,"end":84.284,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Una rúbrica funciona cuando dos personas que la leen llegan al mismo veredicto sobre la misma respuesta.","start":84.834,"end":89.922,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"¿Es buena la respuesta?\" no cumple.","start":90.222,"end":92.364,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"Menciona el componente que figura en el reporte\" sí, porque se puede comprobar mirando el texto.","start":92.664,"end":97.31,"terms":[]},{"text":"Escribí pocos criterios, cada uno verificable, y definí el resultado como pasa o falla con todos los criterios cumplidos.","start":97.61,"end":103.836,"terms":[]},{"text":"Evitá las escalas del uno al cinco: nadie sabe qué separa un tres de un cuatro, y el juez tampoco.","start":104.136,"end":109.108,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":109.908,"end":144.272,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":110.408,"end":111.304,"terms":[]},{"text":"Treinta etiquetas tuyas, primero.","start":111.604,"end":113.615,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Etiquetá vos 30 salidas a mano, pasa o falla, con el motivo.","start":114.165,"end":117.5,"terms":[]},{"text":"Hacelo antes de mirar lo que dice el juez.","start":117.8,"end":119.997,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tomá treinta salidas reales del sistema y etiquetalas a mano, pasa o falla, anotando el motivo en una línea.","start":120.547,"end":126.006,"terms":[]},{"text":"Hacelo antes de correr el juez y sin mirar lo que diría.","start":126.306,"end":128.991,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si ves primero su veredicto, tu etiqueta se contamina y el acuerdo que midas después va a salir inflado.","start":129.291,"end":134.402,"terms":[]},{"text":"Treinta casos son un piso razonable para empezar.","start":134.702,"end":137.433,"terms":[]},{"text":"Buscá que haya aprobados y rechazados en proporciones parecidas: con casi todos aprobados no vas a saber si el juez detecta los malos.","start":137.733,"end":144.272,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":145.072,"end":173.769,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":145.572,"end":146.468,"terms":[]},{"text":"Correr el juez sobre los mismos casos.","start":146.768,"end":148.884,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Corré el juez sobre las mismas 30.","start":149.434,"end":151.364,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ejecutá el juez con la rúbrica sobre esas mismas treinta salidas, con la configuración estable que definiste ayer.","start":151.914,"end":157.431,"terms":[]},{"text":"Guardá el veredicto y la justificación de cada caso.","start":157.731,"end":160.404,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si podés, corré dos o tres veces para ver si el juez es consistente consigo mismo.","start":160.704,"end":165.026,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un juez que cambia de veredicto sobre la misma entrada entre corridas tiene un problema anterior al acuerdo con vos: la rúbrica deja demasiado margen o la configuración no es estable.","start":165.326,"end":173.769,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":174.569,"end":211.291,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":175.069,"end":176.07,"terms":[]},{"text":"Acuerdo y dirección.","start":176.37,"end":177.731,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Calculá la coincidencia: casos donde coinciden dividido el total.","start":178.281,"end":181.592,"terms":[]},{"text":"Y mirá el sentido del desacuerdo: ¿el juez es más blando o más duro que vos?","start":181.892,"end":185.529,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El cálculo es simple: casos donde coinciden dividido por el total.","start":186.079,"end":189.448,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pero el número solo no alcanza.","start":189.748,"end":191.597,"terms":[]},{"text":"Mirá la dirección del desacuerdo.","start":191.897,"end":193.734,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si el juez aprueba casos que vos rechazaste, es indulgente y tu gate va a dejar pasar defectos.","start":194.034,"end":198.89,"terms":[{"start":64,"end":68,"say":"gueit"}]},{"text":"Si rechaza casos que vos aprobaste, es estricto y va a bloquear trabajo válido.","start":199.19,"end":203.43,"terms":[]},{"text":"Son riesgos distintos y se corrigen distinto.","start":203.73,"end":206.113,"terms":[]},{"text":"Armá la tabla de dos por dos, vos contra el juez, y leé las dos celdas de desacuerdo por separado.","start":206.413,"end":211.291,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":212.091,"end":245.806,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":212.591,"end":213.593,"terms":[]},{"text":"Corregir la rúbrica.","start":213.893,"end":215.137,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Arreglá la rúbrica donde no coinciden: un criterio más preciso o un ejemplo de pasa y uno de falla.","start":215.687,"end":220.706,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Leé cada desacuerdo y preguntate quién tiene razón.","start":221.256,"end":224.056,"terms":[]},{"text":"A veces es el juez, y corregís tu etiqueta.","start":224.356,"end":226.785,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lo más frecuente es que el criterio fuera ambiguo.","start":227.085,"end":229.596,"terms":[]},{"text":"Hay dos arreglos que funcionan: reescribir el criterio para que sea más preciso, o agregar a la rúbrica un ejemplo que pasa y uno que falla.","start":229.896,"end":236.888,"terms":[]},{"text":"No agregues diez ejemplos, porque el juez empieza a copiar los ejemplos en lugar de aplicar el criterio.","start":237.188,"end":241.915,"terms":[]},{"text":"Y no uses como ejemplo ninguno de los treinta casos que después vas a medir.","start":242.215,"end":245.806,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":246.606,"end":280.413,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":247.106,"end":248.072,"terms":[]},{"text":"Medir de nuevo y fijar un mínimo.","start":248.372,"end":250.139,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Volvé a medir.","start":250.689,"end":251.748,"terms":[]},{"text":"Definí un mínimo de coincidencia para usar el juez en CI y repetí la calibración cada vez que cambies de modelo juez.","start":252.048,"end":257.705,"terms":[{"start":54,"end":56,"say":"ci ai"}]}],[{"text":"Corré otra vez y recalculá.","start":258.255,"end":259.883,"terms":[]},{"text":"Decidí por adelantado qué acuerdo mínimo necesitás para usar el juez en CI y dejalo escrito.","start":260.183,"end":264.934,"terms":[{"start":72,"end":74,"say":"ci ai"}]},{"text":"Si no llegás, el juez sirve para ordenar qué revisar a mano, pero no para bloquear un merge.","start":265.234,"end":269.776,"terms":[{"start":86,"end":91,"say":"merch"}]},{"text":"La calibración vence: cada vez que cambies el modelo juez, su versión o la rúbrica, volvé a medir contra tus etiquetas.","start":270.076,"end":275.965,"terms":[]},{"text":"Guardá esas treinta etiquetas como un activo, porque son tu patrón de referencia.","start":276.265,"end":280.413,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":281.213,"end":316.233,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":281.713,"end":282.632,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un equipo evalúa resúmenes de tickets de soporte.","start":283.182,"end":285.844,"terms":[]},{"text":"Etiquetan treinta a mano: dieciocho pasan y doce fallan.","start":286.144,"end":289.223,"terms":[]},{"text":"El juez coincide en veintitrés, un acuerdo del setenta y siete por ciento.","start":289.523,"end":293.473,"terms":[]},{"text":"De los siete desacuerdos, seis son casos que el juez aprobó y ellos no: es indulgente.","start":293.773,"end":298.211,"terms":[]},{"text":"Al leerlos encuentran el patrón.","start":298.511,"end":300.29,"terms":[]},{"text":"El criterio decía \"incluye el problema del cliente\" y el juez aceptaba menciones vagas.","start":300.59,"end":304.807,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lo reescriben como \"nombra el producto y el síntoma concreto\" y agregan un ejemplo de cada veredicto.","start":305.107,"end":309.997,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la segunda medición coinciden veintiocho de treinta.","start":310.297,"end":313.109,"terms":[]},{"text":"Fijan ese nivel como mínimo para usarlo en el pipeline.","start":313.409,"end":316.233,"terms":[{"start":46,"end":54,"say":"páiplain"}]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":317.033,"end":331.461,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":317.533,"end":319.057,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Etiquetar después de ver el veredicto del juez.","start":319.607,"end":321.943,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Usar escalas numéricas en lugar de pasa o falla.","start":322.493,"end":325.177,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Mirar solo el porcentaje de acuerdo y no su dirección.","start":325.727,"end":328.714,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cambiar el modelo juez sin volver a calibrar.","start":329.264,"end":331.461,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":332.261,"end":349.544,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":332.761,"end":335.027,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"La rúbrica tiene criterios que se verifican mirando el texto.","start":335.577,"end":338.703,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Hay treinta casos etiquetados a mano antes de correr el juez.","start":339.253,"end":342.356,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Calculaste el acuerdo y sabés si el juez es indulgente o estricto.","start":342.906,"end":346.24,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Hay un acuerdo mínimo escrito para usarlo en CI.","start":346.79,"end":349.544,"terms":[{"start":45,"end":47,"say":"ci ai"}]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":350.344,"end":359.063,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":350.844,"end":352.414,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"El juez explica muy bien veredictos incorrectos.","start":352.964,"end":355.568,"terms":[]},{"text":"No confíes en la justificación: medí la coincidencia.","start":355.868,"end":359.063,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":359.863,"end":372.854,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 16.","start":360.363,"end":362.049,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":362.349,"end":367.588,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 17: Golden datasets: cómo etiquetar, balancear y versionar.","start":368.138,"end":372.854,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d16-en","day":16,"lang":"en","title":"LLM-as-a-judge: rubrics and calibration against humans","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d16-en.mp4","duration":418.449,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":15.958,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 16 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.404,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"LLM-as-a-judge: rubrics and calibration against humans.","start":4.954,"end":8.834,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"How to write an observable rubric for a judge model and measure how much it agrees with your judgment before trusting it.","start":9.384,"end":15.958,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":16.758,"end":35.42,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Some qualities of an answer are captured by no rule: whether the explanation is clear, whether the tone fits, whether it answers what was asked.","start":17.258,"end":25.817,"terms":[]},{"text":"Reviewing them by hand doesn't scale beyond a few dozen cases.","start":26.117,"end":30.021,"terms":[]},{"text":"The usual solution is asking another model to grade them, known as LLM as a judge.","start":30.321,"end":35.42,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":36.22,"end":58.702,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The problem is that this judge is another non-deterministic system with its own errors, and its verdicts come with very convincing justifications.","start":36.72,"end":44.513,"terms":[]},{"text":"Using it uncalibrated is like measuring with an instrument you never compared against a standard.","start":44.813,"end":49.878,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide shows how to write a rubric that can be verified, how to measure agreement between the judge and you, and when that agreement is enough to trust it with a gate.","start":50.178,"end":58.702,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":59.502,"end":74.596,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":60.002,"end":61.7,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"An eval harness with a judge configured (days 8 and 15).","start":62.25,"end":65.956,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"30 real outputs from the model under test.","start":66.506,"end":69.469,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A sheet with columns: id, human verdict, judge verdict, reason.","start":70.019,"end":74.596,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":75.396,"end":81.693,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":75.896,"end":77.176,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":77.476,"end":81.693,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":82.493,"end":121.704,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":82.993,"end":84.029,"terms":[]},{"text":"Observable criteria.","start":84.329,"end":85.968,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Write observable criteria.","start":86.518,"end":88.587,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"Is the answer good?\" doesn't work.","start":88.887,"end":91.25,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"Mentions the component from the report\" does.","start":91.55,"end":94.013,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A rubric works when two people reading it reach the same verdict on the same answer.","start":94.563,"end":99.233,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"Is the answer good?\" doesn't qualify.","start":99.533,"end":102.22,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"Mentions the component named in the report\" does, because it can be checked by looking at the text.","start":102.52,"end":108.073,"terms":[]},{"text":"Write few criteria, each verifiable, and define the result as pass or fail with all criteria met.","start":108.373,"end":114.958,"terms":[]},{"text":"Avoid one-to-five scales: nobody knows what separates a three from a four, and neither does the judge.","start":115.258,"end":121.704,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":122.504,"end":162.929,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":123.004,"end":124.203,"terms":[]},{"text":"Thirty labels of your own, first.","start":124.503,"end":126.793,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Hand-label 30 outputs yourself, pass or fail, with the reason.","start":127.343,"end":131.548,"terms":[]},{"text":"Do it before looking at what the judge says.","start":131.848,"end":134.463,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Take thirty real outputs from the system and label them by hand, pass or fail, noting the reason in one line.","start":135.013,"end":141.413,"terms":[]},{"text":"Do it before running the judge and without looking at what it would say.","start":141.713,"end":145.477,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you see its verdict first, your label gets contaminated and the agreement you measure afterwards will come out inflated.","start":145.777,"end":152.525,"terms":[]},{"text":"Thirty cases are a reasonable floor to start.","start":152.825,"end":155.498,"terms":[]},{"text":"Aim for passes and fails in similar proportions: with almost everything passing you won't know whether the judge detects the bad ones.","start":155.798,"end":162.929,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":163.729,"end":194.969,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":164.229,"end":165.311,"terms":[]},{"text":"Run the judge on the same cases.","start":165.611,"end":167.727,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Run the judge on the same 30.","start":168.277,"end":170.3,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Run the judge with the rubric on those same thirty outputs, with the stable configuration you defined yesterday.","start":170.85,"end":176.588,"terms":[]},{"text":"Save the verdict and justification for each case.","start":176.888,"end":179.781,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you can, run two or three times to see whether the judge is consistent with itself.","start":180.081,"end":185.146,"terms":[]},{"text":"A judge that changes its verdict on the same input between runs has a problem that comes before agreement with you: the rubric leaves too much room or the config isn't stable.","start":185.446,"end":194.969,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":195.769,"end":236.299,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":196.269,"end":197.281,"terms":[]},{"text":"Agreement and direction.","start":197.581,"end":199.314,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Compute agreement: matching cases divided by total.","start":199.864,"end":203.257,"terms":[]},{"text":"And check the direction of disagreement: is the judge more lenient or stricter than you?","start":203.557,"end":208.261,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The calculation is simple: matching cases divided by the total.","start":208.811,"end":212.726,"terms":[]},{"text":"But the number alone isn't enough.","start":213.026,"end":215.084,"terms":[]},{"text":"Look at the direction of disagreement.","start":215.384,"end":217.326,"terms":[]},{"text":"If the judge passes cases you failed, it's lenient and your gate will let defects through.","start":217.626,"end":223.05,"terms":[]},{"text":"If it fails cases you passed, it's strict and will block valid work.","start":223.35,"end":227.799,"terms":[]},{"text":"Those are different risks and they're fixed differently.","start":228.099,"end":230.737,"terms":[]},{"text":"Build the two-by-two table, you against the judge, and read the two disagreement cells separately.","start":231.037,"end":236.299,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":237.099,"end":274.563,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":237.599,"end":238.728,"terms":[]},{"text":"Fix the rubric.","start":239.028,"end":240.192,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Fix the rubric where you disagree: a sharper criterion or one pass and one fail example.","start":240.742,"end":246.549,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Read each disagreement and ask who is right.","start":247.099,"end":249.877,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sometimes it's the judge, and you correct your label.","start":250.177,"end":253.314,"terms":[]},{"text":"Most often the criterion was ambiguous.","start":253.614,"end":256.38,"terms":[]},{"text":"Two fixes work: rewriting the criterion to make it sharper, or adding to the rubric one example that passes and one that fails.","start":256.68,"end":263.892,"terms":[]},{"text":"Don't add ten examples, because the judge starts copying the examples instead of applying the criterion.","start":264.192,"end":270.104,"terms":[]},{"text":"And don't use as an example any of the thirty cases you'll measure afterwards.","start":270.404,"end":274.563,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":275.363,"end":313.35,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":275.863,"end":277.05,"terms":[]},{"text":"Measure again and set a minimum.","start":277.35,"end":279.292,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Measure again.","start":279.842,"end":281.017,"terms":[]},{"text":"Set a minimum agreement to use the judge in CI and recalibrate whenever you change the judge model.","start":281.317,"end":286.823,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Run again and recompute.","start":287.373,"end":289.338,"terms":[]},{"text":"Decide in advance which minimum agreement you need to use the judge in CI and write it down.","start":289.638,"end":294.876,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you don't reach it, the judge is useful for ordering what to review by hand, but not for blocking a merge.","start":295.176,"end":301.506,"terms":[]},{"text":"Calibration expires: every time you change the judge model, its version or the rubric, measure again against your labels.","start":301.806,"end":308.775,"terms":[]},{"text":"Keep those thirty labels as an asset, because they are your reference standard.","start":309.075,"end":313.35,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":314.15,"end":354.024,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":314.65,"end":315.756,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A team evaluates summaries of support tickets.","start":316.306,"end":319.315,"terms":[]},{"text":"They label thirty by hand: eighteen pass and twelve fail.","start":319.615,"end":323.356,"terms":[]},{"text":"The judge matches on twenty-three, an agreement of seventy-seven percent.","start":323.656,"end":327.827,"terms":[]},{"text":"Of the seven disagreements, six are cases the judge passed and they didn't: it's lenient.","start":328.127,"end":333.075,"terms":[]},{"text":"Reading them, they find the pattern.","start":333.375,"end":335.572,"terms":[]},{"text":"The criterion said \"includes the customer's problem\" and the judge accepted vague mentions.","start":335.872,"end":340.763,"terms":[]},{"text":"They rewrite it as \"names the product and the concrete symptom\" and add one example of each verdict.","start":341.063,"end":346.673,"terms":[]},{"text":"In the second measurement, twenty-eight out of thirty match.","start":346.973,"end":350.226,"terms":[]},{"text":"They set that level as the minimum for using it in the pipeline.","start":350.526,"end":354.024,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":354.824,"end":371.155,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":355.324,"end":356.986,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Labeling after seeing the judge's verdict.","start":357.536,"end":360.047,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Using numeric scales instead of pass or fail.","start":360.597,"end":363.92,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Looking only at the agreement percentage and not its direction.","start":364.47,"end":367.99,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Changing the judge model without recalibrating.","start":368.54,"end":371.155,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":371.955,"end":391.515,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":372.455,"end":374.699,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The rubric has criteria that can be checked by looking at the text.","start":375.249,"end":378.932,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"There are thirty cases labeled by hand before running the judge.","start":379.482,"end":383.153,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"You computed agreement and know whether the judge is lenient or strict.","start":383.703,"end":387.618,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"There is a written minimum agreement for using it in CI.","start":388.168,"end":391.515,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":392.315,"end":401.532,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":392.815,"end":394.71,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The judge explains wrong verdicts very well.","start":395.26,"end":398.258,"terms":[]},{"text":"Don't trust the justification: measure agreement.","start":398.558,"end":401.532,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":402.332,"end":416.949,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 16.","start":402.832,"end":404.623,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":404.923,"end":410.905,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 17: Golden datasets: how to label, balance and version.","start":411.455,"end":416.949,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d17-es","day":17,"lang":"es","title":"Golden datasets: cómo etiquetar, balancear y versionar","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d17-es.mp4","duration":397.256,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":13.148,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 17 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.649,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Golden datasets: cómo etiquetar, balancear y versionar.","start":4.199,"end":7.58,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un golden dataset chico pero confiable: con guía de etiquetado, tres particiones y versionado en Git.","start":8.13,"end":13.148,"terms":[{"start":97,"end":100,"say":"guit"}]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":13.948,"end":34.016,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Todo lo que medimos en los últimos días depende de una pieza que suele recibir poca atención: el conjunto de casos con su respuesta correcta.","start":14.448,"end":21.034,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si las etiquetas están mal, el mejor harness del mundo mide con precisión algo equivocado.","start":21.334,"end":25.829,"terms":[{"start":37,"end":44,"say":"járnes"}]},{"text":"Si el conjunto solo tiene casos fáciles, cualquier modelo parece excelente.","start":26.129,"end":29.963,"terms":[]},{"text":"Y si los casos se filtran al prompt, el número deja de significar algo.","start":30.263,"end":34.016,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":34.816,"end":53.548,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un golden dataset es ese conjunto tratado con el cuidado que merece un instrumento de medición.","start":35.316,"end":40.288,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta guía cubre cómo juntar los casos, cómo etiquetarlos con criterio compartido, cómo balancearlos, cómo separarlos para no engañarte y cómo versionarlos.","start":40.588,"end":48.508,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es trabajo artesanal, y es el que más mejora la calidad de todas las decisiones posteriores.","start":48.808,"end":53.548,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":54.348,"end":67.073,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":54.848,"end":56.778,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El repo qa-ai-lab, carpeta data/.","start":57.328,"end":59.176,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Entre 50 y 100 casos reales anonimizados o sintéticos.","start":59.726,"end":63.084,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Idealmente, una segunda persona para etiquetar una muestra.","start":63.634,"end":67.073,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":67.873,"end":73.195,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":68.373,"end":69.549,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":69.849,"end":73.195,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":73.995,"end":76.634,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":74.495,"end":76.634,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":77.434,"end":114.711,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":77.934,"end":78.83,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tres fuentes de casos.","start":79.13,"end":80.515,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Juntá casos de tres fuentes: uso real anonimizado, casos borde y casos adversos (relleno, duplicados, entradas vacías).","start":81.065,"end":87.755,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"La primera fuente es el uso real, anonimizado: muestra cómo son de verdad las entradas, con sus errores de tipeo y sus ambigüedades.","start":88.305,"end":95.076,"terms":[]},{"text":"La segunda son los casos de borde que vos sabés que existen aunque aparezcan poco: valores límite, formatos raros, entradas muy largas.","start":95.376,"end":102.437,"terms":[]},{"text":"La tercera son los casos adversos, pensados para molestar: texto de relleno, duplicados, entradas vacías o en otro idioma.","start":102.737,"end":109.567,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un dataset hecho solo con la primera fuente describe el promedio y no dice nada sobre los fallos.","start":109.867,"end":114.711,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":115.511,"end":150.188,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":116.011,"end":116.907,"terms":[]},{"text":"La guía de etiquetado.","start":117.207,"end":118.533,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Escribí la guía de etiquetado: definición de cada etiqueta, dos ejemplos por etiqueta y qué hacer en caso de duda.","start":119.083,"end":124.763,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Antes de etiquetar en volumen, escribí una guía corta: la definición de cada etiqueta, dos ejemplos por etiqueta y qué hacer ante la duda.","start":125.313,"end":132.212,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esa última parte es la que más se olvida.","start":132.512,"end":134.709,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sin una regla para los casos dudosos, cada persona resuelve distinto y el dataset acumula inconsistencias.","start":135.009,"end":140.596,"terms":[]},{"text":"La guía también obliga a tomar decisiones de negocio que estaban implícitas.","start":140.896,"end":144.672,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si escribir la definición de una etiqueta resulta difícil, es probable que el sistema tampoco pueda aplicarla.","start":144.972,"end":150.188,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":150.988,"end":186.909,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":151.488,"end":152.384,"terms":[]},{"text":"Dos personas, veinte casos.","start":152.684,"end":154.753,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Dos personas etiquetan por separado una muestra de 20.","start":155.303,"end":158.499,"terms":[]},{"text":"Donde no coinciden, la guía está incompleta: corregila.","start":158.799,"end":161.983,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tomá una muestra de veinte casos y que dos personas la etiqueten por separado, sin verse las respuestas.","start":162.533,"end":167.736,"terms":[]},{"text":"Después comparen.","start":168.036,"end":169.2,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los desacuerdos no son errores de las personas: son huecos de la guía.","start":169.5,"end":173.044,"terms":[]},{"text":"Discutan cada uno, decidan y actualicen la guía con la regla nueva o con un ejemplo.","start":173.344,"end":177.387,"terms":[]},{"text":"Repitan hasta que los desacuerdos sean pocos.","start":177.687,"end":179.988,"terms":[]},{"text":"Recién ahí conviene etiquetar el resto.","start":180.288,"end":182.566,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ese acuerdo entre personas marca además el techo razonable de cualquier modelo.","start":182.866,"end":186.909,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":187.709,"end":224.244,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":188.209,"end":189.21,"terms":[]},{"text":"Balance.","start":189.51,"end":190.395,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Balanceá: que todas las clases y todos los tipos difíciles estén representados.","start":190.945,"end":195.081,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si el 90% es fácil, tu accuracy miente.","start":195.381,"end":198.379,"terms":[{"start":23,"end":31,"say":"ákiurasi"}]}],[{"text":"Contá cuántos casos hay por clase y por tipo de dificultad.","start":198.929,"end":202.078,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si el noventa por ciento es fácil, un sistema que resuelve solo lo fácil saca noventa y parece listo para producción.","start":202.378,"end":208.174,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cada clase y cada tipo difícil necesita representación suficiente como para que su tasa de aciertos diga algo.","start":208.474,"end":213.829,"terms":[]},{"text":"Eso implica sobrerrepresentar a propósito los casos raros.","start":214.129,"end":217.522,"terms":[]},{"text":"Está bien: un dataset de evaluación no busca reflejar las proporciones de producción, sino exponer dónde falla el sistema.","start":217.822,"end":224.244,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":225.044,"end":261.568,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":225.544,"end":226.546,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tres particiones.","start":226.846,"end":228.125,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Partí en tres: dev para iterar, test que nunca se usa para ajustar ni aparece en ejemplos del prompt, y regresión con todo lo que alguna vez falló.","start":228.675,"end":236.166,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Separá el conjunto en tres.","start":236.716,"end":238.344,"terms":[]},{"text":"Dev es para iterar: ahí probás prompts y mirás fallos todas las veces que quieras.","start":238.644,"end":242.78,"terms":[]},{"text":"Test no se usa para ajustar nada ni se cita en los ejemplos del prompt, y se consulta solo para tomar una decisión.","start":243.08,"end":248.899,"terms":[]},{"text":"Regresión junta todo lo que alguna vez falló y se arregló, para que no vuelva.","start":249.199,"end":252.871,"terms":[]},{"text":"La disciplina con test es lo difícil.","start":253.171,"end":255.333,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cada vez que ajustás algo mirando esos resultados, deja de medir cómo se va a comportar el sistema con casos nuevos.","start":255.633,"end":261.568,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":262.368,"end":298.265,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":262.868,"end":263.834,"terms":[]},{"text":"Versionado.","start":264.134,"end":265.031,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Versioná en JSONL con CHANGELOG.","start":265.581,"end":267.755,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":17,"say":"yeison ele"},{"start":22,"end":31,"say":"chéinchlog"}]},{"text":"Corregir una etiqueta es un commit, y después de corregir se recalculan las métricas anteriores.","start":268.055,"end":272.957,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Guardá cada partición como JSONL, un caso por línea con un identificador estable, y llevá un CHANGELOG.","start":273.507,"end":279.361,"terms":[{"start":27,"end":32,"say":"yeison ele"},{"start":93,"end":102,"say":"chéinchlog"}]},{"text":"Corregir una etiqueta es un commit con su motivo, igual que un cambio de código.","start":279.661,"end":283.704,"terms":[]},{"text":"Después de una corrección hay que recalcular las métricas anteriores, porque el instrumento cambió y los números viejos ya no son comparables.","start":284.004,"end":290.566,"terms":[]},{"text":"Anotá la versión del dataset en la ficha de cada corrida.","start":290.866,"end":293.794,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sin ese dato, una mejora de dos puntos puede ser solo una etiqueta corregida.","start":294.094,"end":298.265,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":299.065,"end":335.548,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":299.565,"end":300.485,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un equipo arma un dataset para un sistema que decide si un proveedor nuevo ya existe en el catálogo.","start":301.035,"end":305.751,"terms":[]},{"text":"Juntan ciento veinte casos: sesenta de uso real anonimizado, cuarenta de borde y veinte adversos.","start":306.051,"end":311.371,"terms":[]},{"text":"Dos personas etiquetan veinte y discrepan en seis.","start":311.671,"end":314.46,"terms":[]},{"text":"El origen es una sola pregunta sin responder: si una sucursal cuenta como el mismo proveedor.","start":314.76,"end":319.801,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lo deciden, lo agregan a la guía y en la segunda ronda discrepan en uno.","start":320.101,"end":323.831,"terms":[]},{"text":"Al contar, descubren que solo ocho casos son del tipo \"nombre casi idéntico, empresa distinta\", el error más costoso.","start":324.131,"end":330.298,"terms":[]},{"text":"Suman doce más.","start":330.598,"end":331.785,"terms":[]},{"text":"Parten en sesenta de dev, cuarenta de test y veinte de regresión.","start":332.085,"end":335.548,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":336.348,"end":353.666,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":336.848,"end":338.371,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Dejar que el modelo genere las etiquetas contra las que se lo evalúa.","start":338.921,"end":342.093,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Etiquetar todo con una sola persona y sin guía escrita.","start":342.643,"end":345.676,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ajustar el prompt mirando los resultados del conjunto de test.","start":346.226,"end":349.549,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Corregir etiquetas sin registro y comparar métricas viejas con nuevas.","start":350.099,"end":353.666,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":354.466,"end":372.319,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":354.966,"end":357.233,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El dataset tiene casos reales, de borde y adversos.","start":357.783,"end":360.932,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Hay una guía de etiquetado con regla para los casos dudosos.","start":361.482,"end":364.491,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Dos personas etiquetaron una muestra y resolvieron los desacuerdos.","start":365.041,"end":368.55,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Existen dev, test y regresión en JSONL con CHANGELOG.","start":369.1,"end":372.319,"terms":[{"start":33,"end":38,"say":"yeison ele"},{"start":43,"end":52,"say":"chéinchlog"}]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":373.119,"end":382.151,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":373.619,"end":375.189,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Un modelo evaluado contra etiquetas que él mismo generó siempre sale bien.","start":375.739,"end":379.422,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las etiquetas del test las pone una persona.","start":379.722,"end":382.151,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":382.951,"end":395.756,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 17.","start":383.451,"end":385.09,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":385.39,"end":390.629,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 18: Cómo testear un modelo: accuracy, N corridas y rangos.","start":391.179,"end":395.756,"terms":[{"start":45,"end":53,"say":"ákiurasi"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d17-en","day":17,"lang":"en","title":"Golden datasets: how to label, balance and version","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d17-en.mp4","duration":428.533,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":14.46,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 17 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.311,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Golden datasets: how to label, balance and version.","start":4.861,"end":8.335,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A small but reliable golden dataset: with a labeling guide, three splits and Git versioning.","start":8.885,"end":14.46,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":15.26,"end":37.58,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Everything we measured over the last few days depends on a piece that usually gets little attention: the set of cases with their correct answer.","start":15.76,"end":23.634,"terms":[]},{"text":"If the labels are wrong, the best harness in the world measures the wrong thing precisely.","start":23.934,"end":29.127,"terms":[]},{"text":"If the set has only easy cases, any model looks excellent.","start":29.427,"end":33.133,"terms":[]},{"text":"And if cases leak into the prompt, the number stops meaning anything.","start":33.433,"end":37.58,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":38.38,"end":58.378,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"A golden dataset is that set treated with the care a measuring instrument deserves.","start":38.88,"end":43.48,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide covers how to gather the cases, how to label them with shared criteria, how to balance them, how to split them so you don't fool yourself and how to version them.","start":43.78,"end":53.477,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's craft work, and it's what most improves the quality of every later decision.","start":53.777,"end":58.378,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":59.178,"end":72.263,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":59.678,"end":61.375,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The qa-ai-lab repo, data/ folder.","start":61.925,"end":64.645,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"50 to 100 anonymized real or synthetic cases.","start":65.195,"end":68.854,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ideally, a second person to label a sample.","start":69.404,"end":72.263,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":73.063,"end":79.36,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":73.563,"end":74.843,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":75.143,"end":79.36,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":80.16,"end":82.926,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":80.66,"end":82.926,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":83.726,"end":126.46,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":84.226,"end":85.262,"terms":[]},{"text":"Three sources of cases.","start":85.562,"end":87.353,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Collect cases from three sources: anonymized real usage, edge cases and adverse cases (filler, duplicates, empty inputs).","start":87.903,"end":96.427,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The first source is real usage, anonymized: it shows what inputs are really like, with their typos and ambiguities.","start":96.977,"end":104.132,"terms":[]},{"text":"The second is the edge cases you know exist even though they rarely show up: boundary values, odd formats, very long inputs.","start":104.432,"end":112.259,"terms":[]},{"text":"The third is adverse cases, designed to cause trouble: filler text, duplicates, empty inputs or ones in another language.","start":112.559,"end":120.132,"terms":[]},{"text":"A dataset built only from the first source describes the average and says nothing about the failures.","start":120.432,"end":126.46,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":127.26,"end":164.619,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":127.76,"end":128.958,"terms":[]},{"text":"The labeling guide.","start":129.258,"end":130.677,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Write the labeling guide: definition of each label, two examples per label and what to do when in doubt.","start":131.227,"end":137.093,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Before labeling in volume, write a short guide: the definition of each label, two examples per label and what to do when in doubt.","start":137.643,"end":145.018,"terms":[]},{"text":"That last part is the one most often forgotten.","start":145.318,"end":148.293,"terms":[]},{"text":"Without a rule for doubtful cases, each person resolves them differently and the dataset piles up inconsistencies.","start":148.593,"end":155.469,"terms":[]},{"text":"The guide also forces business decisions that were implicit.","start":155.769,"end":159.312,"terms":[]},{"text":"If writing a label's definition proves hard, the system probably can't apply it either.","start":159.612,"end":164.619,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":165.419,"end":203.372,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":165.919,"end":167.001,"terms":[]},{"text":"Two people, twenty cases.","start":167.301,"end":169.382,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Two people independently label a sample of 20.","start":169.932,"end":173.023,"terms":[]},{"text":"Where they disagree, the guide is incomplete: fix it.","start":173.323,"end":176.541,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Take a sample of twenty cases and have two people label it separately, without seeing each other's answers.","start":177.091,"end":182.911,"terms":[]},{"text":"Then compare.","start":183.211,"end":184.363,"terms":[]},{"text":"Disagreements aren't the people's errors: they're gaps in the guide.","start":184.663,"end":188.357,"terms":[]},{"text":"Discuss each one, decide and update the guide with the new rule or an example.","start":188.657,"end":193.397,"terms":[]},{"text":"Repeat until disagreements are few.","start":193.697,"end":196.172,"terms":[]},{"text":"Only then is it worth labeling the rest.","start":196.472,"end":198.727,"terms":[]},{"text":"That agreement between people also marks the reasonable ceiling for any model.","start":199.027,"end":203.372,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":204.172,"end":242.542,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":204.672,"end":205.685,"terms":[]},{"text":"Balance.","start":205.985,"end":206.939,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Balance: every class and every hard type must be represented.","start":207.489,"end":211.683,"terms":[]},{"text":"If 90% is easy, your accuracy lies.","start":211.983,"end":215.028,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Count how many cases there are per class and per difficulty type.","start":215.578,"end":219.075,"terms":[]},{"text":"If ninety percent is easy, a system that solves only the easy part scores ninety and looks ready for production.","start":219.375,"end":225.693,"terms":[]},{"text":"Each class and each hard type needs enough representation for its hit rate to mean something.","start":225.993,"end":231.383,"terms":[]},{"text":"That implies over-representing rare cases on purpose.","start":231.683,"end":234.902,"terms":[]},{"text":"That's fine: an evaluation dataset doesn't aim to mirror production proportions, but to expose where the system fails.","start":235.202,"end":242.542,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":243.342,"end":284.022,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":243.842,"end":244.971,"terms":[]},{"text":"Three splits.","start":245.271,"end":246.376,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Split in three: dev to iterate, test never used for tuning nor quoted in prompt examples, and regression with everything that ever failed.","start":246.926,"end":254.893,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Split the set in three.","start":255.443,"end":256.92,"terms":[]},{"text":"Dev is for iterating: there you try prompts and look at failures as often as you like.","start":257.22,"end":262.331,"terms":[]},{"text":"Test isn't used for tuning anything nor quoted in prompt examples, and is consulted only to make a decision.","start":262.631,"end":268.625,"terms":[]},{"text":"Regression collects everything that ever failed and got fixed, so it doesn't come back.","start":268.925,"end":273.862,"terms":[]},{"text":"Discipline with test is the hard part.","start":274.162,"end":276.73,"terms":[]},{"text":"Every time you adjust something while looking at those results, it stops measuring how the system will behave on new cases.","start":277.03,"end":284.022,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":284.822,"end":321.868,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":285.322,"end":286.509,"terms":[]},{"text":"Versioning.","start":286.809,"end":287.636,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Version as JSONL with a CHANGELOG.","start":288.186,"end":290.603,"terms":[]},{"text":"Fixing a label is a commit, and after a fix you recompute earlier metrics.","start":290.903,"end":295.445,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Store each split as JSONL, one case per line with a stable identifier, and keep a CHANGELOG.","start":295.995,"end":301.861,"terms":[]},{"text":"Fixing a label is a commit with its reason, just like a code change.","start":302.161,"end":306.134,"terms":[]},{"text":"After a fix, earlier metrics must be recomputed, because the instrument changed and the old numbers are no longer comparable.","start":306.434,"end":313.774,"terms":[]},{"text":"Note the dataset version on each run card.","start":314.074,"end":316.782,"terms":[]},{"text":"Without that fact, a two-point improvement may be nothing more than a corrected label.","start":317.082,"end":321.868,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":322.668,"end":363.342,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":323.168,"end":324.274,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A team builds a dataset for a system that decides whether a new supplier already exists in the catalog.","start":324.824,"end":330.771,"terms":[]},{"text":"They gather one hundred and twenty cases: sixty from anonymized real usage, forty edge cases and twenty adverse.","start":331.071,"end":337.528,"terms":[]},{"text":"Two people label twenty and disagree on six.","start":337.828,"end":340.594,"terms":[]},{"text":"The origin is a single unanswered question: whether a branch counts as the same supplier.","start":340.894,"end":346.04,"terms":[]},{"text":"They decide it, add it to the guide and disagree on one in the second round.","start":346.34,"end":350.267,"terms":[]},{"text":"Counting, they discover only eight cases are of the \"nearly identical name, different company\" type, the costliest error.","start":350.567,"end":357.466,"terms":[]},{"text":"They add twelve more.","start":357.766,"end":359.278,"terms":[]},{"text":"They split into sixty dev, forty test and twenty regression.","start":359.578,"end":363.342,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":364.142,"end":382.471,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":364.642,"end":366.305,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Letting the model generate the labels it's evaluated against.","start":366.855,"end":370.236,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Labeling everything with a single person and no written guide.","start":370.786,"end":374.179,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tuning the prompt while looking at test set results.","start":374.729,"end":377.785,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Fixing labels with no record and comparing old metrics with new ones.","start":378.335,"end":382.471,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":383.271,"end":402.075,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":383.771,"end":386.014,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The dataset has real, edge and adverse cases.","start":386.564,"end":389.933,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"There is a labeling guide with a rule for doubtful cases.","start":390.483,"end":393.667,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Two people labeled a sample and resolved their disagreements.","start":394.217,"end":397.54,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Dev, test and regression exist as JSONL with a CHANGELOG.","start":398.09,"end":402.075,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":402.875,"end":411.733,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":403.375,"end":405.27,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A model graded against labels it generated always looks good.","start":405.82,"end":409.387,"terms":[]},{"text":"Test labels come from a person.","start":409.687,"end":411.733,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":412.533,"end":427.033,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 17.","start":413.033,"end":414.719,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":415.019,"end":421.001,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 18: How to test a model: accuracy, N runs and ranges.","start":421.551,"end":427.033,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d18-es","day":18,"lang":"es","title":"Cómo testear un modelo: accuracy, N corridas y rangos","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d18-es.mp4","duration":391.037,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":16.039,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 18 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.73,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Cómo testear un modelo: accuracy, N corridas y rangos.","start":4.28,"end":7.568,"terms":[{"start":24,"end":32,"say":"ákiurasi"}]}],[{"text":"Cómo medir un modelo que toma decisiones (un agente que mapea nombres desordenados contra un catálogo maestro) con corridas repetidas, rangos y métricas por tipo de error.","start":8.118,"end":16.039,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":16.839,"end":33.252,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"\"¿Qué modelo es mejor para esto?\" es una pregunta que casi siempre se responde con un ranking público o con la impresión de quien lo probó una tarde.","start":17.339,"end":24.02,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ninguna de las dos cosas dice cómo se va a comportar en tu tarea, con tus datos y tu prompt.","start":24.32,"end":28.746,"terms":[]},{"text":"Y cuando alguien lo mide, suele hacerlo con una sola corrida y reportar un solo número.","start":29.046,"end":33.252,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":34.052,"end":56.848,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Probar un modelo en serio se parece más a un experimento que a un test.","start":34.552,"end":38.026,"terms":[]},{"text":"Hay una tarea definida, un conjunto fijo, varias repeticiones y un resultado con su rango.","start":38.326,"end":43.286,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta guía usa un caso de mapeo de registros para mostrar el método completo: qué medir además del accuracy, cómo distinguir una diferencia real del ruido y cómo decidir cuando el modelo más caro no es claramente el mejor.","start":43.586,"end":54.049,"terms":[{"start":98,"end":106,"say":"ákiurasi"}]},{"text":"Las cifras que aparecen son ilustrativas.","start":54.349,"end":56.848,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":57.648,"end":67.452,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":58.148,"end":60.078,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El golden dataset del día 17 (unos 100 casos de test).","start":60.628,"end":63.73,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un eval harness con versión de modelo fija (días 8 y 15).","start":64.28,"end":67.452,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":15,"say":"járnes"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":68.252,"end":73.574,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":68.752,"end":69.927,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":70.227,"end":73.574,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":74.374,"end":77.013,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":74.874,"end":77.013,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":77.813,"end":114.197,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":78.313,"end":79.209,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tarea y acciones definidas.","start":79.509,"end":81.358,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Definí la tarea y las acciones posibles con precisión.","start":81.908,"end":84.906,"terms":[]},{"text":"En este caso: mapear a un registro existente, aprobar como nuevo o mandar a revisión.","start":85.206,"end":89.829,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Escribí con precisión qué recibe el modelo y cuáles son las únicas salidas válidas.","start":90.379,"end":94.457,"terms":[]},{"text":"En el caso de ejemplo llega un registro nuevo y hay tres acciones posibles: mapearlo a uno existente, aprobarlo como nuevo o enviarlo a revisión humana.","start":94.757,"end":101.818,"terms":[]},{"text":"Definir las acciones como una lista cerrada vuelve medible la tarea.","start":102.118,"end":105.523,"terms":[]},{"text":"También obliga a decidir qué se espera en cada situación dudosa.","start":105.823,"end":109.053,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si la definición es vaga, vas a estar midiendo la interpretación del modelo y no su capacidad.","start":109.353,"end":114.197,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":114.997,"end":150.441,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":115.497,"end":116.394,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cinco corridas por configuración.","start":116.694,"end":118.716,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Corré cada configuración 5 veces sobre el mismo set de test.","start":119.266,"end":122.682,"terms":[]},{"text":"Una sola corrida no es una medición.","start":122.982,"end":125.11,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Corré cada configuración cinco veces sobre el mismo conjunto de test, sin cambiar nada entre corridas.","start":125.66,"end":130.852,"terms":[]},{"text":"El mismo modelo con la misma entrada puede moverse varios puntos de una corrida a otra.","start":131.152,"end":135.276,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si medís una sola vez, no sabés si viste su mejor día, el peor o uno típico.","start":135.576,"end":140.025,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cinco repeticiones es un compromiso razonable entre costo e información.","start":140.325,"end":144.264,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si los resultados quedan muy dispersos, esa dispersión ya es un hallazgo sobre la estabilidad del modelo en tu tarea.","start":144.564,"end":150.441,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":151.241,"end":184.758,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":151.741,"end":152.638,"terms":[]},{"text":"Media y rango.","start":152.938,"end":154.032,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Reportá media y rango (mínimo y máximo).","start":154.582,"end":156.837,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si dos configuraciones se pisan en el rango, la diferencia es ruido.","start":157.137,"end":160.982,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Reportá el promedio junto con el mínimo y el máximo de las cinco corridas.","start":161.532,"end":165.273,"terms":[]},{"text":"Después aplicá una regla simple: si los rangos de dos configuraciones se superponen, no podés afirmar que una sea mejor.","start":165.573,"end":171.787,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esa diferencia es compatible con el ruido.","start":172.087,"end":174.493,"terms":[]},{"text":"Muchas decisiones de cambiar de modelo o de prompt se toman sobre diferencias de uno o dos puntos que desaparecen al repetir la medición.","start":174.793,"end":180.914,"terms":[]},{"text":"Mostrar el rango en la tabla evita esa discusión antes de que empiece.","start":181.214,"end":184.758,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":185.558,"end":222.824,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":186.058,"end":187.059,"terms":[]},{"text":"Más allá del accuracy.","start":187.359,"end":188.871,"terms":[{"start":13,"end":21,"say":"ákiurasi"}]}],[{"text":"Mirá más allá del accuracy: precisión por acción y cantidad de errores caros por corrida.","start":189.421,"end":193.754,"terms":[{"start":18,"end":26,"say":"ákiurasi"}]},{"text":"Mapear mal a un registro existente ensucia datos; mandar a revisión de más solo cuesta tiempo.","start":194.054,"end":198.898,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El accuracy trata todos los errores como iguales, y no lo son.","start":199.448,"end":202.864,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":11,"say":"ákiurasi"}]},{"text":"En el ejemplo, mapear un registro a uno existente que no corresponde contamina los datos y es difícil de detectar después.","start":203.164,"end":209.041,"terms":[]},{"text":"Enviar de más a revisión solo cuesta tiempo de una persona.","start":209.341,"end":212.525,"terms":[]},{"text":"Por eso conviene mirar la precisión por acción y, sobre todo, contar los errores costosos por corrida.","start":212.825,"end":217.75,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un modelo con un poco menos de accuracy y la mitad de errores costosos puede ser la mejor elección.","start":218.05,"end":222.824,"terms":[{"start":31,"end":39,"say":"ákiurasi"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":223.624,"end":255.352,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":224.124,"end":225.126,"terms":[]},{"text":"Mismas condiciones, calidad contra costo.","start":225.426,"end":227.913,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Compará modelos con el mismo prompt y dataset, calidad contra costo por llamada.","start":228.463,"end":232.54,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Para comparar modelos, mantené fijo todo lo demás: el mismo prompt, el mismo dataset, los mismos parámetros y el mismo juez si lo hay.","start":233.09,"end":240.129,"terms":[]},{"text":"Agregá a la tabla el costo por llamada y la latencia.","start":240.429,"end":243.113,"terms":[]},{"text":"La comparación útil no es qué modelo tiene el número más alto sino cuánta calidad adicional obtenés por cada unidad de costo.","start":243.413,"end":249.43,"terms":[]},{"text":"A veces esa calidad adicional existe, y a veces queda dentro del margen de ruido, pagando varias veces más.","start":249.73,"end":255.352,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":256.152,"end":289.296,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":256.652,"end":257.618,"terms":[]},{"text":"Decidir con la tabla completa.","start":257.918,"end":259.755,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Decidí con la tabla completa.","start":260.305,"end":262.049,"terms":[]},{"text":"El modelo más caro no siempre gana, y a veces gana por menos que el ruido.","start":262.349,"end":266.125,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"La decisión sale de leer todas las columnas juntas: media, rango, errores costosos, costo y latencia.","start":266.675,"end":272.204,"terms":[]},{"text":"No siempre gana el más caro, y cuando gana puede hacerlo por menos que el ruido.","start":272.504,"end":276.315,"terms":[]},{"text":"Dejá la decisión escrita junto con la tabla y la ficha de corrida.","start":276.615,"end":279.868,"terms":[]},{"text":"Dentro de tres meses, cuando salga un modelo nuevo, vas a poder repetir exactamente el mismo experimento y comparar contra una referencia propia, en lugar de empezar la discusión de cero.","start":280.168,"end":289.296,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":290.096,"end":329.445,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":290.596,"end":291.516,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Con cifras inventadas para ilustrar el método: un equipo compara tres configuraciones sobre cuarenta casos de test, cinco corridas cada una.","start":292.066,"end":299.36,"terms":[]},{"text":"El modelo chico promedia 71% con rango de 68 a 75 y seis errores costosos por corrida.","start":299.66,"end":305.758,"terms":[]},{"text":"El mediano, 84% con rango de 81 a 86 y dos errores costosos.","start":306.058,"end":311.145,"terms":[]},{"text":"El grande, 85% con rango de 82 a 88, también dos errores costosos y un costo cinco veces mayor que el mediano.","start":311.445,"end":318.809,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los rangos del mediano y del grande se superponen casi por completo, así que la diferencia de un punto no es evidencia.","start":319.109,"end":325.102,"terms":[]},{"text":"Eligen el mediano y documentan que el grande no justificó su costo en esta tarea.","start":325.402,"end":329.445,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":330.245,"end":345.021,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":330.745,"end":332.269,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Decidir con una sola corrida por modelo.","start":332.819,"end":335.12,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Reportar un promedio sin su rango.","start":335.67,"end":337.635,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Mirar solo accuracy cuando los errores tienen costos distintos.","start":338.185,"end":341.589,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":19,"say":"ákiurasi"}]}],[{"text":"Comparar modelos con prompts o datasets diferentes.","start":342.139,"end":345.021,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":345.821,"end":363.685,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":346.321,"end":348.588,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"La tarea tiene una lista cerrada de acciones válidas.","start":349.138,"end":351.996,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cada configuración corrió cinco veces sobre el mismo test.","start":352.546,"end":355.625,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"La tabla muestra media, rango, errores costosos, costo y latencia.","start":356.175,"end":359.998,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"La decisión quedó escrita junto a la tabla y la ficha de corrida.","start":360.548,"end":363.685,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":364.485,"end":375.979,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":364.985,"end":366.555,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"El mismo modelo con la misma entrada varía varios puntos entre corridas, y algunos modelos fallan directamente con contextos grandes.","start":367.105,"end":373.482,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un número suelto no describe a un modelo.","start":373.782,"end":375.979,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":376.779,"end":389.537,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 18.","start":377.279,"end":378.895,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":379.195,"end":384.434,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 19: Costo como métrica de calidad: prefiltro top-K antes del modelo.","start":384.984,"end":389.537,"terms":[{"start":62,"end":67,"say":"top ca"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d18-en","day":18,"lang":"en","title":"How to test a model: accuracy, N runs and ranges","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d18-en.mp4","duration":433.321,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":17.223,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 18 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.16,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"How to test a model: accuracy, N runs and ranges.","start":4.71,"end":8.497,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"How to measure a decision-making model (an agent mapping messy names to a master catalog) with repeated runs, ranges and per-error-type metrics.","start":9.047,"end":17.223,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":18.023,"end":37.768,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"\"Which model is best for this?\" is a question almost always answered with a public leaderboard or the impression of whoever tried it one afternoon.","start":18.523,"end":26.702,"terms":[]},{"text":"Neither says how it will behave on your task, with your data and your prompt.","start":27.002,"end":31.602,"terms":[]},{"text":"And when someone does measure it, they usually do it with a single run and report a single number.","start":31.902,"end":37.768,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":38.568,"end":62.804,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Testing a model seriously is closer to an experiment than to a test.","start":39.068,"end":43.192,"terms":[]},{"text":"There is a defined task, a fixed set, several repetitions and a result with its range.","start":43.492,"end":48.893,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide uses a record-mapping case to show the full method: what to measure besides accuracy, how to tell a real difference from noise and how to decide when the most expensive model isn't clearly the best.","start":49.193,"end":60.318,"terms":[]},{"text":"The figures shown are illustrative.","start":60.618,"end":62.804,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":63.604,"end":74.674,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":64.104,"end":65.801,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The golden dataset from day 17 (about 100 test cases).","start":66.351,"end":70.243,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"An eval harness with a pinned model version (days 8 and 15).","start":70.793,"end":74.674,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":75.474,"end":81.77,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":75.974,"end":77.253,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":77.553,"end":81.77,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":82.57,"end":85.337,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":83.07,"end":85.337,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":86.137,"end":127.757,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":86.637,"end":87.673,"terms":[]},{"text":"Task and actions defined.","start":87.973,"end":89.891,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Define the task and possible actions precisely.","start":90.441,"end":93.625,"terms":[]},{"text":"In this case: map to an existing record, approve as new or send to review.","start":93.925,"end":98.804,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Write down precisely what the model receives and which are the only valid outputs.","start":99.354,"end":103.733,"terms":[]},{"text":"In the sample case a new record arrives and there are three possible actions: map it to an existing one, approve it as new or send it to human review.","start":104.033,"end":112.801,"terms":[]},{"text":"Defining the actions as a closed list makes the task measurable.","start":113.101,"end":116.808,"terms":[]},{"text":"It also forces a decision on what is expected in each doubtful situation.","start":117.108,"end":121.812,"terms":[]},{"text":"If the definition is vague, you'll be measuring the model's interpretation and not its capability.","start":122.112,"end":127.757,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":128.557,"end":166.59,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":129.057,"end":130.256,"terms":[]},{"text":"Five runs per configuration.","start":130.556,"end":132.614,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Run each configuration 5 times on the same test set.","start":133.164,"end":136.591,"terms":[]},{"text":"A single run isn't a measurement.","start":136.891,"end":138.925,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Run each configuration five times on the same test set, changing nothing between runs.","start":139.475,"end":144.586,"terms":[]},{"text":"The same model on the same input can move several points from one run to the next.","start":144.886,"end":149.243,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you measure once, you don't know whether you saw its best day, its worst or a typical one.","start":149.543,"end":154.956,"terms":[]},{"text":"Five repetitions is a reasonable trade-off between cost and information.","start":155.256,"end":160.111,"terms":[]},{"text":"If results come out widely spread, that spread is already a finding about the model's stability on your task.","start":160.411,"end":166.59,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":167.39,"end":203.705,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":167.89,"end":168.973,"terms":[]},{"text":"Mean and range.","start":169.273,"end":170.611,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Report mean and range (min and max).","start":171.161,"end":173.857,"terms":[]},{"text":"If two configurations overlap in range, the difference is noise.","start":174.157,"end":178.084,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Report the mean together with the minimum and maximum of the five runs.","start":178.634,"end":182.99,"terms":[]},{"text":"Then apply a simple rule: if the ranges of two configurations overlap, you can't claim one is better.","start":183.29,"end":189.608,"terms":[]},{"text":"That difference is compatible with noise.","start":189.908,"end":192.43,"terms":[]},{"text":"Many decisions to switch model or prompt are made on one- or two-point differences that vanish when the measurement is repeated.","start":192.73,"end":199.339,"terms":[]},{"text":"Showing the range in the table avoids that argument before it starts.","start":199.639,"end":203.705,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":204.505,"end":245.87,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":205.005,"end":206.018,"terms":[]},{"text":"Beyond accuracy.","start":206.318,"end":207.841,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Look beyond accuracy: per-action precision and number of costly errors per run.","start":208.391,"end":213.479,"terms":[]},{"text":"A wrong map to an existing record pollutes data; over-sending to review only costs time.","start":213.779,"end":219.506,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Accuracy treats all errors as equal, and they aren't.","start":220.056,"end":223.193,"terms":[]},{"text":"In the example, mapping a record to an existing one that doesn't correspond pollutes the data and is hard to detect later.","start":223.493,"end":230.531,"terms":[]},{"text":"Over-sending to review only costs a person's time.","start":230.831,"end":234.166,"terms":[]},{"text":"That's why it's worth looking at per-action precision and, above all, counting costly errors per run.","start":234.466,"end":240.215,"terms":[]},{"text":"A model with slightly lower accuracy and half the costly errors may be the best choice.","start":240.515,"end":245.87,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":246.67,"end":283.46,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":247.17,"end":248.299,"terms":[]},{"text":"Same conditions, quality against cost.","start":248.599,"end":251.365,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Compare models with the same prompt and dataset, quality against cost per call.","start":251.915,"end":256.538,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"To compare models, hold everything else fixed: the same prompt, the same dataset, the same parameters and the same judge if there is one.","start":257.088,"end":265.845,"terms":[]},{"text":"Add cost per call and latency to the table.","start":266.145,"end":268.992,"terms":[]},{"text":"The useful comparison isn't which model has the highest number but how much additional quality you get per unit of cost.","start":269.292,"end":276.284,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sometimes that additional quality exists, and sometimes it sits within the noise margin, at several times the price.","start":276.584,"end":283.46,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":284.26,"end":323.255,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":284.76,"end":285.946,"terms":[]},{"text":"Decide with the full table.","start":286.246,"end":288.002,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Decide with the full table.","start":288.552,"end":290.308,"terms":[]},{"text":"The most expensive model doesn't always win, and sometimes wins by less than the noise.","start":290.608,"end":295.858,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The decision comes from reading all the columns together: mean, range, costly errors, cost and latency.","start":296.408,"end":303.226,"terms":[]},{"text":"The most expensive doesn't always win, and when it does it may win by less than the noise.","start":303.526,"end":308.556,"terms":[]},{"text":"Leave the decision in writing together with the table and the run card.","start":308.856,"end":312.434,"terms":[]},{"text":"Three months from now, when a new model comes out, you'll be able to repeat exactly the same experiment and compare against a reference of your own, instead of starting the discussion from zero.","start":312.734,"end":323.255,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":324.055,"end":366.225,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":324.555,"end":325.661,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"With made-up figures to illustrate the method: a team compares three configurations on forty test cases, five runs each.","start":326.211,"end":333.447,"terms":[]},{"text":"The small model averages 71% with a range of 68 to 75 and six costly errors per run.","start":333.747,"end":340.204,"terms":[]},{"text":"The medium one, 84% with a range of 81 to 86 and two costly errors.","start":340.504,"end":346.463,"terms":[]},{"text":"The large one, 85% with a range of 82 to 88, also two costly errors and five times the cost of the medium one.","start":346.763,"end":354.742,"terms":[]},{"text":"The medium and large ranges overlap almost entirely, so the one-point difference isn't evidence.","start":355.042,"end":360.849,"terms":[]},{"text":"They choose the medium one and document that the large one didn't justify its cost on this task.","start":361.149,"end":366.225,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":367.025,"end":382.382,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":367.525,"end":369.188,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Deciding with a single run per model.","start":369.738,"end":371.97,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Reporting a mean without its range.","start":372.52,"end":374.636,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Looking only at accuracy when errors have different costs.","start":375.186,"end":378.718,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Comparing models with different prompts or datasets.","start":379.268,"end":382.382,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":383.182,"end":402.938,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":383.682,"end":385.925,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The task has a closed list of valid actions.","start":386.475,"end":389.531,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each configuration ran five times on the same test set.","start":390.081,"end":393.637,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The table shows mean, range, costly errors, cost and latency.","start":394.187,"end":398.914,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The decision is written down next to the table and the run card.","start":399.464,"end":402.938,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":403.738,"end":416.497,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":404.238,"end":406.133,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The same model on the same input varies several points between runs, and some models fail outright on large contexts.","start":406.683,"end":413.722,"terms":[]},{"text":"A single number doesn't describe a model.","start":414.022,"end":416.497,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":417.297,"end":431.821,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 18.","start":417.797,"end":419.356,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":419.656,"end":425.637,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 19: Cost as a quality metric: top-K prefiltering before the model.","start":426.187,"end":431.821,"terms":[{"start":48,"end":53,"say":"top K"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d19-es","day":19,"lang":"es","title":"Costo como métrica de calidad: prefiltro top-K antes del modelo","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d19-es.mp4","duration":380.724,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":14.193,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 19 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.661,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Costo como métrica de calidad: prefiltro top-K antes del modelo.","start":4.211,"end":7.65,"terms":[{"start":41,"end":46,"say":"top ca"}]}],[{"text":"Cómo reducir los tokens de entrada de un agente con un prefiltro local de candidatos y demostrar con mediciones que la calidad no baja.","start":8.2,"end":14.193,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":14.993,"end":32.773,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"En QA estamos acostumbrados a que la calidad se mida en defectos.","start":15.493,"end":18.816,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":5,"say":"cu a"}]},{"text":"Con sistemas que usan modelos, el costo se vuelve una dimensión de calidad igual de concreta: una funcionalidad que acierta pero cuesta demasiado por llamada no se puede sostener en producción, y una que es barata y falla tampoco sirve.","start":19.116,"end":30.23,"terms":[]},{"text":"Alguien tiene que medir las dos cosas juntas.","start":30.53,"end":32.773,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":33.573,"end":51.631,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"El caso más común de costo innecesario es mandar al modelo todo el contexto disponible, por las dudas.","start":34.073,"end":39.346,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta guía muestra una técnica para evitarlo: un prefiltro local que elige los K candidatos más relevantes antes de llamar al modelo.","start":39.646,"end":45.988,"terms":[]},{"text":"Y muestra cómo probarlo, porque un prefiltro mal medido baja el costo y también la calidad sin que nadie lo note.","start":46.288,"end":51.631,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":52.431,"end":62.12,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":52.931,"end":54.861,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El caso del día 18: nombres a mapear contra un catálogo.","start":55.411,"end":58.909,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un set etiquetado con la respuesta correcta por nombre.","start":59.459,"end":62.12,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":62.92,"end":68.241,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":63.42,"end":64.595,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":64.895,"end":68.241,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":69.041,"end":102.731,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":69.541,"end":70.438,"terms":[]},{"text":"La línea de base.","start":70.738,"end":72.029,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Medí el punto de partida: tokens de entrada por llamada y qué parte es contexto fijo (por ejemplo, el catálogo entero).","start":72.579,"end":78.491,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Antes de optimizar, medí.","start":79.041,"end":80.786,"terms":[]},{"text":"Para una muestra de llamadas, registrá cuántos tokens de entrada usa cada una y cuántos corresponden a contexto fijo que se repite siempre, como un catálogo entero o un documento completo.","start":81.086,"end":89.819,"terms":[]},{"text":"En muchos sistemas esa parte fija es la mayor parte del costo.","start":90.119,"end":93.407,"terms":[]},{"text":"Anotá también la calidad actual con el eval que ya tenés.","start":93.707,"end":96.601,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sin esos dos números, tokens y calidad, no vas a poder decir después si el cambio fue una mejora o solo un cambio.","start":96.901,"end":102.731,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":103.531,"end":139.463,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":104.031,"end":104.928,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un prefiltro local y simple.","start":105.228,"end":106.914,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Armá un prefiltro local que elija los K candidatos más parecidos.","start":107.464,"end":111.043,"terms":[]},{"text":"Empezá por uno léxico simple: es gratis y no llama a ningún modelo.","start":111.343,"end":115.026,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El prefiltro recibe la entrada y la lista completa de candidatos, y devuelve los K más parecidos.","start":115.576,"end":120.629,"terms":[]},{"text":"Empezá con un método léxico, por ejemplo similitud por trigramas de caracteres: se escribe en pocas líneas, corre en milisegundos y no llama a ningún modelo.","start":120.929,"end":128.524,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es además determinista, así que se puede probar con tests clásicos.","start":128.824,"end":132.368,"terms":[]},{"text":"La tentación es saltar directo a algo más sofisticado.","start":132.668,"end":135.619,"terms":[]},{"text":"Resistila hasta tener un número que muestre que lo simple no alcanza.","start":135.919,"end":139.463,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":140.263,"end":174.848,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":140.763,"end":141.66,"terms":[]},{"text":"Recall del prefiltro.","start":141.96,"end":143.297,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":6,"say":"ricól"}]}],[{"text":"Medí el recall del prefiltro: en cuántos casos etiquetados la respuesta correcta queda entre los K.","start":143.847,"end":148.564,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":14,"say":"ricól"}]},{"text":"Si no está ahí, el modelo no puede acertar.","start":148.864,"end":151.119,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Esta es la medición central.","start":151.669,"end":153.401,"terms":[]},{"text":"Para cada caso etiquetado, verificá si la respuesta correcta está entre los K candidatos que devolvió el prefiltro.","start":153.701,"end":159.404,"terms":[]},{"text":"El porcentaje de casos donde está es el recall.","start":159.704,"end":162.284,"terms":[{"start":40,"end":46,"say":"ricól"}]},{"text":"Funciona como techo de todo el sistema: si la respuesta correcta no llega al contexto, el modelo no tiene forma de elegirla, por bueno que sea.","start":162.584,"end":169.437,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es un test determinista, barato y rápido, y te dice si el problema está antes del modelo o en el modelo.","start":169.737,"end":174.848,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":175.648,"end":210.048,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":176.148,"end":177.149,"terms":[]},{"text":"Comparar métodos y valores de K.","start":177.449,"end":179.321,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Compará métodos (léxico contra embeddings) y valores de K con ese mismo recall.","start":179.871,"end":183.716,"terms":[{"start":72,"end":78,"say":"ricól"}]},{"text":"No des por hecho que embeddings gana.","start":184.016,"end":185.877,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Con el recall como métrica podés comparar opciones de forma justa: método léxico contra embeddings, y distintos valores de K para cada uno.","start":186.427,"end":193.244,"terms":[{"start":7,"end":13,"say":"ricól"}]},{"text":"Armá una tabla.","start":193.544,"end":194.766,"terms":[]},{"text":"No asumas que los embeddings ganan.","start":195.066,"end":196.938,"terms":[]},{"text":"En tareas con nombres propios, códigos o abreviaturas, la similitud léxica suele rendir igual o mejor, y además es gratis.","start":197.238,"end":203.881,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los embeddings ayudan cuando la relación es de significado y no de escritura.","start":204.181,"end":208.004,"terms":[]},{"text":"Dejá que tus datos lo decidan.","start":208.304,"end":210.048,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":210.848,"end":247.43,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":211.348,"end":212.349,"terms":[]},{"text":"El eval completo con cada K.","start":212.649,"end":214.416,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Corré el eval completo, 5 veces, con lista entera y con cada K.","start":214.966,"end":218.58,"terms":[]},{"text":"Compará accuracy, errores caros, tokens y costo.","start":218.88,"end":221.773,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":16,"say":"ákiurasi"}]}],[{"text":"El recall mide el prefiltro, no el resultado final.","start":222.323,"end":225.17,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":9,"say":"ricól"}]},{"text":"Corré el eval de punta a punta, cinco veces, con la lista completa y con cada valor de K.","start":225.47,"end":230.14,"terms":[]},{"text":"Compará accuracy, errores costosos, tokens y costo.","start":230.44,"end":233.554,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":16,"say":"ákiurasi"}]},{"text":"Vas a ver algo que suele sorprender: más contexto no siempre da mejor resultado.","start":233.854,"end":238.025,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con muchos candidatos parecidos en el prompt, el modelo se confunde más.","start":238.325,"end":242.089,"terms":[]},{"text":"Hay un punto a partir del cual agregar candidatos empeora la calidad y sube el costo al mismo tiempo.","start":242.389,"end":247.43,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":248.23,"end":281.874,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":248.73,"end":249.697,"terms":[]},{"text":"Elegir el K más chico que alcanza.","start":249.997,"end":252.043,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Elegí el K más chico que mantiene recall y accuracy.","start":252.593,"end":255.335,"terms":[{"start":34,"end":40,"say":"ricól"},{"start":43,"end":51,"say":"ákiurasi"}]},{"text":"Más candidatos parecidos pueden confundir al modelo y empeorar el resultado.","start":255.635,"end":259.504,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"La regla de decisión es elegir el menor K que mantiene el recall y el accuracy dentro del rango de la línea de base.","start":260.054,"end":265.653,"terms":[{"start":58,"end":64,"say":"ricól"},{"start":70,"end":78,"say":"ákiurasi"}]},{"text":"Documentá la tabla completa y el valor elegido.","start":265.953,"end":268.591,"terms":[]},{"text":"Agregá el recall del prefiltro como test permanente en CI: si alguien cambia el método o el catálogo crece mucho, el recall puede bajar sin aviso.","start":268.891,"end":276.324,"terms":[{"start":10,"end":16,"say":"ricól"},{"start":55,"end":57,"say":"ci ai"},{"start":117,"end":123,"say":"ricól"}]},{"text":"Ese test tarda segundos, y protege una pieza que, cuando falla, hace fallar a todo lo que viene después.","start":276.624,"end":281.874,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":282.674,"end":321.663,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":283.174,"end":284.094,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Con números ilustrativos: un sistema envía en cada llamada un catálogo de dos mil registros, unos sesenta mil tokens de entrada.","start":284.644,"end":291.102,"terms":[]},{"text":"El equipo arma un prefiltro por trigramas y mide el recall sobre ochenta casos etiquetados.","start":291.402,"end":296.141,"terms":[{"start":52,"end":58,"say":"ricól"}]},{"text":"Con K igual a cinco, la respuesta correcta está en el 90% de los casos; con diez, en el 97%; con veinte, en el 99%; con cincuenta, también en el 99%.","start":296.441,"end":306.115,"terms":[]},{"text":"Prueban embeddings y con K igual a veinte obtienen 98%: no mejora.","start":306.415,"end":310.574,"terms":[]},{"text":"En el eval completo, K igual a veinte da el mismo accuracy que la lista entera, con un error costoso menos por corrida y alrededor de un tres por ciento de los tokens.","start":310.874,"end":318.794,"terms":[{"start":50,"end":58,"say":"ákiurasi"}]},{"text":"Con cincuenta, el accuracy baja dos puntos.","start":319.094,"end":321.663,"terms":[{"start":18,"end":26,"say":"ákiurasi"}]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":322.463,"end":337.413,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":322.963,"end":324.486,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Reducir el contexto sin medir el recall del prefiltro.","start":325.036,"end":327.814,"terms":[{"start":33,"end":39,"say":"ricól"}]}],[{"text":"Asumir que los embeddings superan a un método léxico.","start":328.364,"end":331.165,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Evaluar solo el costo y no la calidad de punta a punta.","start":331.715,"end":334.631,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Elegir un K grande \"para estar seguros\".","start":335.181,"end":337.413,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":338.213,"end":354.812,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":338.713,"end":340.98,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tenés tokens de entrada y calidad de la línea de base.","start":341.53,"end":344.284,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El recall del prefiltro está medido para varios valores de K.","start":344.834,"end":347.994,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":9,"say":"ricól"}]}],[{"text":"El eval completo corrió con la lista entera y con cada K.","start":348.544,"end":351.531,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El recall del prefiltro corre como test en CI.","start":352.081,"end":354.812,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":9,"say":"ricól"},{"start":43,"end":45,"say":"ci ai"}]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":355.612,"end":365.689,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":356.112,"end":357.682,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Con muchos candidatos parecidos en contexto, el modelo elige mal con más frecuencia.","start":358.232,"end":362.634,"terms":[]},{"text":"El contexto se diseña y se mide, no se acumula.","start":362.934,"end":365.689,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":366.489,"end":379.224,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 19.","start":366.989,"end":368.57,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":368.87,"end":374.109,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 20: Regresión de prompts: versionar, comparar y no romper lo que andaba.","start":374.659,"end":379.224,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d19-en","day":19,"lang":"en","title":"Cost as a quality metric: top-K prefiltering before the model","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d19-en.mp4","duration":421.173,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":15.679,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 19 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.09,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cost as a quality metric: top-K prefiltering before the model.","start":4.64,"end":8.404,"terms":[{"start":26,"end":31,"say":"top K"}]}],[{"text":"How to cut an agent's input tokens with a local candidate prefilter and prove with measurements that quality doesn't drop.","start":8.954,"end":15.679,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":16.479,"end":35.408,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"In QA we're used to quality being measured in defects.","start":16.979,"end":20.093,"terms":[]},{"text":"With systems that use models, cost becomes an equally concrete quality dimension: a feature that gets it right but costs too much per call can't be sustained in production, and one that's cheap and fails is no good either.","start":20.393,"end":32.958,"terms":[]},{"text":"Someone has to measure both together.","start":33.258,"end":35.408,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":36.208,"end":57.204,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common case of unnecessary cost is sending the model all the available context, just in case.","start":36.708,"end":42.481,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide shows a technique to avoid it: a local prefilter that picks the K most relevant candidates before calling the model.","start":42.781,"end":49.97,"terms":[]},{"text":"And it shows how to test it, because a badly measured prefilter lowers cost and quality alike without anyone noticing.","start":50.27,"end":57.204,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":58.004,"end":67.902,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":58.504,"end":60.202,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The day 18 case: names to map against a catalog.","start":60.752,"end":64.551,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A labeled set with the correct answer per name.","start":65.101,"end":67.902,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":68.702,"end":74.998,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":69.202,"end":70.481,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":70.781,"end":74.998,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":75.798,"end":113.97,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":76.298,"end":77.334,"terms":[]},{"text":"The baseline.","start":77.634,"end":78.647,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Measure the baseline: input tokens per call and how much is fixed context (for example, the whole catalog).","start":79.197,"end":85.724,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Before optimizing, measure.","start":86.274,"end":88.309,"terms":[]},{"text":"For a sample of calls, record how many input tokens each one uses and how many correspond to fixed context repeated every time, such as a whole catalog or a full document.","start":88.609,"end":98.77,"terms":[]},{"text":"In many systems that fixed part is most of the cost.","start":99.07,"end":102.242,"terms":[]},{"text":"Also note current quality with the eval you already have.","start":102.542,"end":105.958,"terms":[]},{"text":"Without those two numbers, tokens and quality, you won't be able to say afterwards whether the change was an improvement or merely a change.","start":106.258,"end":113.97,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":114.77,"end":154.138,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":115.27,"end":116.468,"terms":[]},{"text":"A simple local prefilter.","start":116.768,"end":118.687,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Build a local prefilter that picks the K most similar candidates.","start":119.237,"end":122.931,"terms":[]},{"text":"Start with a simple lexical one: it's free and calls no model.","start":123.231,"end":127.193,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The prefilter takes the input and the full candidate list, and returns the K most similar.","start":127.743,"end":132.889,"terms":[]},{"text":"Start with a lexical method, for example character trigram similarity: it takes a few lines to write, runs in milliseconds and calls no model.","start":133.189,"end":141.667,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's also deterministic, so it can be tested with classic tests.","start":141.967,"end":146.393,"terms":[]},{"text":"The temptation is to jump straight to something more sophisticated.","start":146.693,"end":150.12,"terms":[]},{"text":"Resist it until you have a number showing the simple approach falls short.","start":150.42,"end":154.138,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":154.938,"end":194.864,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":155.438,"end":156.52,"terms":[]},{"text":"Prefilter recall.","start":156.82,"end":158.147,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Measure prefilter recall: in how many labeled cases the correct answer is among the K.","start":158.697,"end":163.912,"terms":[]},{"text":"If it isn't there, the model can't get it right.","start":164.212,"end":167.28,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"This is the central measurement.","start":167.83,"end":169.504,"terms":[]},{"text":"For each labeled case, check whether the correct answer is among the K candidates the prefilter returned.","start":169.804,"end":175.74,"terms":[]},{"text":"The percentage of cases where it is, is recall.","start":176.04,"end":179.189,"terms":[]},{"text":"It works as the ceiling for the whole system: if the correct answer doesn't reach the context, the model has no way of choosing it, however good it is.","start":179.489,"end":188.024,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's a deterministic, cheap, fast test, and it tells you whether the problem lies before the model or in the model.","start":188.324,"end":194.864,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":195.664,"end":231.933,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":196.164,"end":197.176,"terms":[]},{"text":"Compare methods and K values.","start":197.476,"end":199.662,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Compare methods (lexical versus embeddings) and K values on that same recall.","start":200.212,"end":204.893,"terms":[]},{"text":"Don't assume embeddings win.","start":205.193,"end":207.065,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"With recall as the metric you can compare options fairly: lexical method against embeddings, and different K values for each.","start":207.615,"end":214.711,"terms":[]},{"text":"Build a table.","start":215.011,"end":216.105,"terms":[]},{"text":"Don't assume embeddings win.","start":216.405,"end":218.277,"terms":[]},{"text":"In tasks with proper names, codes or abbreviations, lexical similarity often performs as well or better, and it's free.","start":218.577,"end":225.697,"terms":[]},{"text":"Embeddings help when the relationship is one of meaning rather than spelling.","start":225.997,"end":229.796,"terms":[]},{"text":"Let your data decide.","start":230.096,"end":231.933,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":232.733,"end":274.691,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":233.233,"end":234.361,"terms":[]},{"text":"The full eval with each K.","start":234.661,"end":236.568,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Run the full eval, 5 times, with the full list and with each K.","start":237.118,"end":241.3,"terms":[]},{"text":"Compare accuracy, costly errors, tokens and cost.","start":241.6,"end":245.423,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Recall measures the prefilter, not the final result.","start":245.973,"end":249.412,"terms":[]},{"text":"Run the eval end to end, five times, with the full list and with each K value.","start":249.712,"end":254.556,"terms":[]},{"text":"Compare accuracy, costly errors, tokens and cost.","start":254.856,"end":258.678,"terms":[]},{"text":"You'll see something that tends to surprise: more context doesn't always give a better result.","start":258.978,"end":264.437,"terms":[]},{"text":"With many similar candidates in the prompt, the model gets confused more.","start":264.737,"end":269.059,"terms":[]},{"text":"There's a point beyond which adding candidates worsens quality and raises cost at the same time.","start":269.359,"end":274.691,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":275.491,"end":313.488,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":275.991,"end":277.177,"terms":[]},{"text":"Choose the smallest K that suffices.","start":277.477,"end":279.93,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pick the smallest K that keeps recall and accuracy.","start":280.48,"end":283.942,"terms":[]},{"text":"More similar candidates can confuse the model and make results worse.","start":284.242,"end":288.308,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The decision rule is choosing the smallest K that keeps recall and accuracy within the baseline range.","start":288.858,"end":294.724,"terms":[]},{"text":"Document the full table and the chosen value.","start":295.024,"end":297.859,"terms":[]},{"text":"Add prefilter recall as a permanent test in CI: if someone changes the method or the catalog grows a lot, recall can drop without notice.","start":298.159,"end":306.846,"terms":[]},{"text":"That test takes seconds, and it protects a piece that, when it fails, makes everything after it fail.","start":307.146,"end":313.488,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":314.288,"end":357.677,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":314.788,"end":315.894,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"With illustrative numbers: a system sends a catalog of two thousand records on every call, about sixty thousand input tokens.","start":316.444,"end":323.656,"terms":[]},{"text":"The team builds a trigram prefilter and measures recall on eighty labeled cases.","start":323.956,"end":328.672,"terms":[]},{"text":"With K equal to five, the correct answer is present in 90% of cases; with ten, in 97%; with twenty, in 99%; with fifty, also 99%.","start":328.972,"end":339.772,"terms":[]},{"text":"They try embeddings and with K equal to twenty get 98%: no improvement.","start":340.072,"end":344.963,"terms":[]},{"text":"In the full eval, K equal to twenty gives the same accuracy as the full list, with one fewer costly error per run and around three percent of the tokens.","start":345.263,"end":354.158,"terms":[]},{"text":"With fifty, accuracy drops two points.","start":354.458,"end":357.677,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":358.477,"end":374.054,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":358.977,"end":360.64,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Shrinking context without measuring prefilter recall.","start":361.19,"end":364.409,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Assuming embeddings beat a lexical method.","start":364.959,"end":367.504,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Evaluating only cost and not end-to-end quality.","start":368.054,"end":371.365,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Choosing a large K \"to be safe\".","start":371.915,"end":374.054,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":374.854,"end":392.173,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":375.354,"end":377.597,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"You have baseline input tokens and quality.","start":378.147,"end":381.227,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Prefilter recall is measured for several K values.","start":381.777,"end":384.995,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The full eval ran with the full list and with each K.","start":385.545,"end":388.683,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Prefilter recall runs as a test in CI.","start":389.233,"end":392.173,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":392.973,"end":403.99,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":393.473,"end":395.368,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"With many similar candidates in context, the model picks wrong more often.","start":395.918,"end":400.506,"terms":[]},{"text":"Context is designed and measured, not piled up.","start":400.806,"end":403.99,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":404.79,"end":419.673,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 19.","start":405.29,"end":406.872,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":407.172,"end":413.153,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 20: Prompt regression: version, compare and don't break what worked.","start":413.703,"end":419.673,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d20-es","day":20,"lang":"es","title":"Regresión de prompts: versionar, comparar y no romper lo que andaba","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d20-es.mp4","duration":378.21,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":14.576,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 20 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.51,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Regresión de prompts: versionar, comparar y no romper lo que andaba.","start":4.06,"end":7.603,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tratar cada cambio de prompt como un cambio de código: versión, comparación lado a lado y un set de regresión que decide el merge.","start":8.153,"end":14.576,"terms":[{"start":124,"end":129,"say":"merch"}]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":15.376,"end":35.618,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un prompt en producción es código que se modifica seguido y casi nunca se prueba.","start":15.876,"end":19.954,"terms":[]},{"text":"Alguien encuentra un caso que falla, agrega una frase para arreglarlo, lo prueba con ese mismo caso, ve que funciona y lo publica.","start":20.254,"end":26.758,"terms":[]},{"text":"Una semana después aparecen tres casos que antes andaban y ahora no.","start":27.058,"end":30.532,"terms":[]},{"text":"Nadie conecta una cosa con la otra, porque no hay registro de qué cambió ni de cómo estaba antes.","start":30.832,"end":35.618,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":36.418,"end":54.847,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Es un problema clásico de regresión, y se resuelve con las herramientas clásicas adaptadas: versiones, cambios chicos, comparación lado a lado y un conjunto de regresión que crece.","start":36.918,"end":46.093,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta guía arma ese flujo y agrega dos cuidados propios de los modelos: mirar los errores por tipo y vigilar que los ejemplos del prompt no se filtren desde el conjunto de test.","start":46.393,"end":54.847,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":55.647,"end":64.79,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":56.147,"end":58.077,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un golden dataset con test y regression (día 17).","start":58.627,"end":61.648,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Dos versiones del prompt en archivos separados.","start":62.198,"end":64.79,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":65.59,"end":70.912,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":66.09,"end":67.265,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":67.565,"end":70.912,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":71.712,"end":74.351,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":72.212,"end":74.351,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":75.151,"end":77.789,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":75.651,"end":77.789,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":78.589,"end":112.849,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":79.089,"end":79.986,"terms":[]},{"text":"Una versión, un archivo, una línea de changelog.","start":80.286,"end":83.18,"terms":[{"start":38,"end":47,"say":"chéinchlog"}]}],[{"text":"Cada versión del prompt es un archivo, con una línea de changelog que diga qué regla cambió y por qué.","start":83.73,"end":88.655,"terms":[{"start":56,"end":65,"say":"chéinchlog"}]}],[{"text":"Cada versión del prompt es un archivo propio con su número, y cada una tiene una línea en el changelog que dice qué regla cambió y por qué.","start":89.205,"end":95.79,"terms":[{"start":93,"end":102,"say":"chéinchlog"}]},{"text":"No sobrescribas el archivo anterior, porque lo vas a necesitar para comparar.","start":96.09,"end":100.122,"terms":[]},{"text":"El porqué importa tanto como el qué.","start":100.422,"end":102.351,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"Se agregó regla para quejas de rendimiento\" no alcanza.","start":102.651,"end":105.429,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sumale qué caso la motivó.","start":105.729,"end":107.31,"terms":[]},{"text":"Dentro de unos meses, alguien va a querer quitar esa regla, y sin el motivo no va a saber qué puede romper.","start":107.61,"end":112.849,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":113.649,"end":146.76,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":114.149,"end":115.046,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un cambio por versión.","start":115.346,"end":116.753,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un cambio por versión.","start":117.303,"end":118.711,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si cambiás tres cosas juntas, no sabés cuál movió el resultado.","start":119.011,"end":122.392,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Si en una misma versión reescribís una regla, agregás un ejemplo y cambiás el formato de salida, y el resultado se mueve, no sabés cuál de las tres cosas lo movió.","start":122.942,"end":130.932,"terms":[]},{"text":"Puede incluso que una haya mejorado y otra empeorado, y veas un empate.","start":131.232,"end":134.706,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un cambio por versión hace que cada medición tenga una sola explicación posible.","start":135.006,"end":139.037,"terms":[]},{"text":"Parece más lento.","start":139.337,"end":140.594,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la práctica es más rápido, porque no perdés tiempo desarmando cambios mezclados para encontrar el que causó el problema.","start":140.894,"end":146.76,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":147.56,"end":178.986,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":148.06,"end":148.956,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lado a lado, cinco veces.","start":149.256,"end":150.989,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Corré las dos versiones lado a lado sobre el set de test, 5 veces, y sobre el de regresión.","start":151.539,"end":156.592,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Configurá el eval con las dos versiones como prompts del mismo archivo, de modo que corran sobre los mismos casos.","start":157.142,"end":162.705,"terms":[]},{"text":"Usá el conjunto de test y también el de regresión, con cinco repeticiones.","start":163.005,"end":166.863,"terms":[]},{"text":"El visor muestra las dos columnas y permite filtrar los casos donde una versión pasa y la otra no.","start":167.163,"end":171.972,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esos casos son los que hay que leer.","start":172.272,"end":173.993,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ahí aparece lo que arreglaste, lo que rompiste y lo que cambió de respuesta sin cambiar de veredicto.","start":174.293,"end":178.986,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":179.786,"end":212.954,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":180.286,"end":181.287,"terms":[]},{"text":"Movimiento por tipo de error.","start":181.587,"end":183.261,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Mirá el movimiento por tipo de error, no solo el total.","start":183.811,"end":186.728,"terms":[]},{"text":"Arreglar un tipo suele subir otro.","start":187.028,"end":189.028,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El total puede quedar igual mientras por debajo cambia todo.","start":189.578,"end":192.564,"terms":[]},{"text":"Clasificá los fallos por tipo y compará la cantidad de cada tipo entre versiones.","start":192.864,"end":197.139,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es habitual que una regla nueva arregle una categoría y empeore otra.","start":197.439,"end":201.11,"terms":[]},{"text":"Una instrucción que vuelve al modelo más cauto con las quejas, por ejemplo, puede hacer que marque de menos los bloqueos reales.","start":201.41,"end":207.334,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si solo mirás el porcentaje global, ese intercambio queda oculto, y lo vas a descubrir en producción.","start":207.634,"end":212.954,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":213.754,"end":244.448,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":214.254,"end":215.255,"terms":[]},{"text":"Fugas entre prompt y test.","start":215.555,"end":217.183,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Controlá fugas: los ejemplos que ponés en el prompt no pueden ser casos del set de test.","start":217.733,"end":222.194,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Los ejemplos que ponés en el prompt no pueden ser casos del conjunto de test, ni casi iguales.","start":222.744,"end":227.635,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si lo son, el modelo ve la respuesta antes de la pregunta y la métrica sube sin que haya mejorado nada.","start":227.935,"end":232.848,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es una fuga fácil de cometer: el caso que falla es justo el que uno quiere usar de ejemplo.","start":233.148,"end":237.783,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sacá los ejemplos del conjunto de dev o escribilos desde cero, y verificá con una búsqueda de texto que ninguno aparezca en test.","start":238.083,"end":244.448,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":245.248,"end":280.158,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":245.748,"end":246.714,"terms":[]},{"text":"La regla de merge.","start":247.014,"end":248.236,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":17,"say":"merch"}]}],[{"text":"Regla de merge: test mejora por encima del ruido y regresión no baja.","start":248.786,"end":252.492,"terms":[{"start":9,"end":14,"say":"merch"}]},{"text":"Después, los casos recién arreglados entran al set de regresión.","start":252.792,"end":256.406,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Definí por adelantado cuándo una versión nueva reemplaza a la anterior: el conjunto de test mejora por encima del ruido, es decir sin superposición de rangos, y el de regresión no baja.","start":256.956,"end":265.979,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si cumple, se mergea.","start":266.279,"end":267.629,"terms":[]},{"text":"Después, los casos recién arreglados pasan al conjunto de regresión.","start":267.929,"end":271.658,"terms":[]},{"text":"Así cada problema resuelto queda protegido para siempre.","start":271.958,"end":274.921,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si no cumple, la versión se descarta o se sigue trabajando, pero no se publica \"porque parece mejor\".","start":275.221,"end":280.158,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":280.958,"end":319.241,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":281.458,"end":282.378,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un clasificador de tickets marca como bloqueante cualquier queja de lentitud.","start":282.928,"end":286.866,"terms":[]},{"text":"El equipo crea la versión siete con una sola regla nueva: las quejas de rendimiento sin un flujo bloqueado son de severidad media.","start":287.166,"end":293.16,"terms":[]},{"text":"La corren contra la versión seis, cinco veces.","start":293.46,"end":296.353,"terms":[]},{"text":"En test, el promedio sube de 82 a 88 sin superposición de rangos.","start":296.653,"end":300.905,"terms":[]},{"text":"En regresión, baja un caso: un ticket que decía \"tarda tanto que no puedo pagar\" ahora sale como medio.","start":301.205,"end":306.583,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es un bloqueo real.","start":306.883,"end":308.047,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ajustan la redacción de la regla en una versión ocho, vuelven a correr, y regresión queda completa.","start":308.347,"end":313.098,"terms":[]},{"text":"Mergean la ocho y suman cuatro casos nuevos al conjunto de regresión.","start":313.398,"end":317.185,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las cifras son ilustrativas.","start":317.485,"end":319.241,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":320.041,"end":335.154,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":320.541,"end":322.065,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Sobrescribir el prompt sin conservar la versión anterior.","start":322.615,"end":325.717,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Meter varios cambios en una misma versión.","start":326.267,"end":328.65,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Probar el cambio solo con el caso que lo motivó.","start":329.2,"end":331.629,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Usar casos del conjunto de test como ejemplos del prompt.","start":332.179,"end":335.154,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":335.954,"end":353.702,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":336.454,"end":338.72,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cada versión del prompt es un archivo con su línea de changelog.","start":339.27,"end":342.605,"terms":[{"start":54,"end":63,"say":"chéinchlog"}]}],[{"text":"La versión nueva tiene un solo cambio respecto de la anterior.","start":343.155,"end":346.118,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Las dos versiones corrieron lado a lado en test y en regresión.","start":346.668,"end":350.049,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Los casos arreglados se sumaron al conjunto de regresión.","start":350.599,"end":353.702,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":354.502,"end":363.174,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":355.002,"end":356.572,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"El cumplimiento de una regla del prompt es probabilístico.","start":357.122,"end":360.143,"terms":[]},{"text":"Hay que medir en qué porcentaje de corridas se respeta.","start":360.443,"end":363.174,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":363.974,"end":376.71,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 20.","start":364.474,"end":365.905,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":366.205,"end":371.443,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 21: Testing de RAG: retrieval, fidelidad y respuesta por separado.","start":371.993,"end":376.71,"terms":[{"start":37,"end":46,"say":"retríval"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d20-en","day":20,"lang":"en","title":"Prompt regression: version, compare and don't break what worked","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d20-en.mp4","duration":409.498,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":15.981,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 20 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.067,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Prompt regression: version, compare and don't break what worked.","start":4.617,"end":8.37,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Treating every prompt change as a code change: version, side-by-side comparison and a regression set that decides the merge.","start":8.92,"end":15.981,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":16.781,"end":39.554,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"A prompt in production is code that gets modified often and almost never tested.","start":17.281,"end":22.113,"terms":[]},{"text":"Someone finds a failing case, adds a sentence to fix it, tries it on that same case, sees it works and ships it.","start":22.413,"end":29.591,"terms":[]},{"text":"A week later three cases that used to work no longer do.","start":29.891,"end":33.33,"terms":[]},{"text":"Nobody connects one thing with the other, because there's no record of what changed or how it was before.","start":33.63,"end":39.554,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":40.354,"end":60.2,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"It's a classic regression problem, and it's solved with the classic tools adapted: versions, small changes, side-by-side comparison and a regression set that grows.","start":40.854,"end":50.83,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide builds that flow and adds two cautions specific to models: looking at errors by type and watching that prompt examples don't leak from the test set.","start":51.13,"end":60.2,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":61,"end":70.096,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":61.5,"end":63.197,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A golden dataset with test and regression (day 17).","start":63.747,"end":67.105,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Two prompt versions in separate files.","start":67.655,"end":70.096,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":70.896,"end":77.193,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":71.396,"end":72.676,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":72.976,"end":77.193,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":77.993,"end":80.759,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":78.493,"end":80.759,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":81.559,"end":84.326,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":82.059,"end":84.326,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":85.126,"end":121.824,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":85.626,"end":86.662,"terms":[]},{"text":"One version, one file, one changelog line.","start":86.962,"end":90.215,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each prompt version is a file, with a changelog line stating which rule changed and why.","start":90.765,"end":95.981,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each prompt version is its own file with its number, and each has a changelog line stating which rule changed and why.","start":96.531,"end":103.372,"terms":[]},{"text":"Don't overwrite the previous file, because you'll need it to compare.","start":103.672,"end":107.459,"terms":[]},{"text":"The why matters as much as the what.","start":107.759,"end":110.188,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"Added rule for performance complaints\" isn't enough.","start":110.488,"end":113.486,"terms":[]},{"text":"Add which case motivated it.","start":113.786,"end":115.739,"terms":[]},{"text":"Months from now someone will want to remove that rule, and without the reason they won't know what it might break.","start":116.039,"end":121.824,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":122.624,"end":159.484,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":123.124,"end":124.322,"terms":[]},{"text":"One change per version.","start":124.622,"end":126.227,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"One change per version.","start":126.777,"end":128.382,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you change three things at once, you don't know which one moved the result.","start":128.682,"end":132.98,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"If in a single version you rewrite a rule, add an example and change the output format, and the result moves, you don't know which of the three moved it.","start":133.53,"end":141.787,"terms":[]},{"text":"One may even have improved things and another worsened them, and you see a tie.","start":142.087,"end":146.386,"terms":[]},{"text":"One change per version means each measurement has a single possible explanation.","start":146.686,"end":151.298,"terms":[]},{"text":"It looks slower.","start":151.598,"end":152.9,"terms":[]},{"text":"In practice it's faster, because you don't waste time untangling mixed changes to find the one that caused the problem.","start":153.2,"end":159.484,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":160.284,"end":194.961,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":160.784,"end":161.866,"terms":[]},{"text":"Side by side, five times.","start":162.166,"end":164.421,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Run both versions side by side on the test set, 5 times, and on the regression set.","start":164.971,"end":170.048,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Set up the eval with both versions as prompts in the same file, so they run on the same cases.","start":170.598,"end":175.883,"terms":[]},{"text":"Use the test set and the regression set as well, with five repetitions.","start":176.183,"end":180.655,"terms":[]},{"text":"The viewer shows the two columns and lets you filter the cases where one version passes and the other doesn't.","start":180.955,"end":186.902,"terms":[]},{"text":"Those are the cases to read.","start":187.202,"end":188.981,"terms":[]},{"text":"That's where you see what you fixed, what you broke and what changed its answer without changing its verdict.","start":189.281,"end":194.961,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":195.761,"end":231.693,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":196.261,"end":197.274,"terms":[]},{"text":"Movement by error type.","start":197.574,"end":199.248,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Look at movement per error type, not just the total.","start":199.798,"end":203.028,"terms":[]},{"text":"Fixing one type often raises another.","start":203.328,"end":205.676,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The total can stay the same while everything shifts underneath.","start":206.226,"end":209.932,"terms":[]},{"text":"Classify failures by type and compare the count of each type across versions.","start":210.232,"end":214.717,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's common for a new rule to fix one category and worsen another.","start":215.017,"end":218.99,"terms":[]},{"text":"An instruction making the model more cautious with complaints, for example, can make it under-flag real blockers.","start":219.29,"end":225.631,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you look only at the overall percentage, that trade stays hidden, and you'll discover it in production.","start":225.931,"end":231.693,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":232.493,"end":266.089,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":232.993,"end":234.121,"terms":[]},{"text":"Leaks between prompt and test.","start":234.421,"end":236.433,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Check for leakage: examples you put in the prompt can't be test set cases.","start":236.983,"end":241.478,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The examples you put in the prompt can't be test set cases, nor nearly identical ones.","start":242.028,"end":247.604,"terms":[]},{"text":"If they are, the model sees the answer before the question and the metric rises with nothing having improved.","start":247.904,"end":253.514,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's an easy leak to commit: the failing case is exactly the one you want to use as an example.","start":253.814,"end":259.192,"terms":[]},{"text":"Take examples from the dev set or write them from scratch, and verify with a text search that none appears in test.","start":259.492,"end":266.089,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":266.889,"end":305.41,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":267.389,"end":268.576,"terms":[]},{"text":"The merge rule.","start":268.876,"end":270.156,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Merge rule: test improves beyond noise and regression doesn't drop.","start":270.706,"end":275.062,"terms":[]},{"text":"Then the newly fixed cases join the regression set.","start":275.362,"end":278.523,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Define in advance when a new version replaces the previous one: the test set improves beyond noise, meaning no overlapping ranges, and the regression set doesn't drop.","start":279.073,"end":288.828,"terms":[]},{"text":"If it qualifies, it merges.","start":289.128,"end":291.243,"terms":[]},{"text":"Afterwards, the newly fixed cases join the regression set.","start":291.543,"end":295.052,"terms":[]},{"text":"That way every solved problem stays protected for good.","start":295.352,"end":298.756,"terms":[]},{"text":"If it doesn't qualify, the version is discarded or worked on further, but it doesn't ship \"because it looks better\".","start":299.056,"end":305.41,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":306.21,"end":346.733,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":306.71,"end":307.815,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A ticket classifier marks any slowness complaint as blocking.","start":308.365,"end":311.874,"terms":[]},{"text":"The team creates version seven with a single new rule: performance complaints with no blocked flow are medium severity.","start":312.174,"end":318.887,"terms":[]},{"text":"They run it against version six, five times.","start":319.187,"end":322.174,"terms":[]},{"text":"On test, the mean rises from 82 to 88 with no overlapping ranges.","start":322.474,"end":327.434,"terms":[]},{"text":"On regression, one case drops: a ticket saying \"it takes so long I can't pay\" now comes out as medium.","start":327.734,"end":333.936,"terms":[]},{"text":"That's a real blocker.","start":334.236,"end":335.713,"terms":[]},{"text":"They adjust the rule's wording in a version eight, run again, and regression is complete.","start":336.013,"end":340.683,"terms":[]},{"text":"They merge eight and add four new cases to the regression set.","start":340.983,"end":344.724,"terms":[]},{"text":"The figures are illustrative.","start":345.024,"end":346.733,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":347.533,"end":363.784,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":348.033,"end":349.696,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Overwriting the prompt without keeping the previous version.","start":350.246,"end":353.477,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Putting several changes into a single version.","start":354.027,"end":356.583,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Trying the change only on the case that motivated it.","start":357.133,"end":360.131,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Using test set cases as prompt examples.","start":360.681,"end":363.784,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":364.584,"end":382.599,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":365.084,"end":367.327,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each prompt version is a file with its changelog line.","start":367.877,"end":371.165,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The new version has a single change from the previous one.","start":371.715,"end":375.05,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Both versions ran side by side on test and on regression.","start":375.6,"end":379.446,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The fixed cases were added to the regression set.","start":379.996,"end":382.599,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":383.399,"end":392.152,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":383.899,"end":385.794,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Adherence to a prompt rule is probabilistic.","start":386.344,"end":389.191,"terms":[]},{"text":"Measure in what percentage of runs it's honored.","start":389.491,"end":392.152,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":392.952,"end":407.998,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 20.","start":393.452,"end":394.848,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":395.148,"end":401.13,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 21: Testing RAG: retrieval, faithfulness and answer, separately.","start":401.68,"end":407.998,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d21-es","day":21,"lang":"es","title":"Testing de RAG: retrieval, fidelidad y respuesta por separado","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d21-es.mp4","duration":398.729,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":15.017,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 21 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.649,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Testing de RAG: retrieval, fidelidad y respuesta por separado.","start":4.199,"end":7.719,"terms":[{"start":16,"end":25,"say":"retríval"}]}],[{"text":"Cómo probar un sistema RAG en tres capas para saber, cuando falla, si el problema está en la búsqueda, en la generación o en la base de conocimiento.","start":8.269,"end":15.017,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":15.817,"end":34.155,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un sistema RAG responde preguntas buscando primero en tus documentos y generando después una respuesta con lo que encontró.","start":16.317,"end":22.021,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cuando contesta mal, la reacción habitual es retocar el prompt.","start":22.321,"end":25.609,"terms":[]},{"text":"A veces funciona.","start":25.909,"end":27.223,"terms":[]},{"text":"Muchas veces no, porque el problema estaba antes: el documento correcto nunca llegó al modelo y ninguna instrucción puede compensar eso.","start":27.523,"end":34.155,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":34.955,"end":54.302,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Probar un RAG de punta a punta, con una sola métrica, mezcla dos sistemas distintos: un buscador y un generador.","start":35.455,"end":41.46,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta guía los separa en tres capas que se miden por separado, y agrega los casos negativos, donde la respuesta correcta es admitir que la información no está.","start":41.76,"end":49.472,"terms":[]},{"text":"El resultado es un diagnóstico que dice dónde hay que arreglar, no solo que algo anda mal.","start":49.772,"end":54.302,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":55.102,"end":72.786,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":55.602,"end":57.532,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Python 3.10+ y una app RAG que exponga la respuesta y los fragmentos recuperados.","start":58.082,"end":62.694,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":6,"say":"páiton"}]}],[{"text":"20 preguntas con respuesta esperada y documento fuente.","start":63.244,"end":66.08,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"DeepEval usa por defecto un modelo de OpenAI como juez (requiere OPENAI_API_KEY); su documentación explica cómo usar otro.","start":66.63,"end":72.786,"terms":[{"start":38,"end":44,"say":"open ei ai"},{"start":76,"end":79,"say":"ki"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":73.586,"end":78.907,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":74.086,"end":75.261,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":75.561,"end":78.907,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":79.707,"end":82.346,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":80.207,"end":82.346,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":83.146,"end":122.666,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":83.646,"end":84.543,"terms":[]},{"text":"Capa uno: recuperación.","start":84.843,"end":86.389,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Capa 1, retrieval: para cada pregunta, verificá si el documento correcto aparece entre los fragmentos recuperados.","start":86.939,"end":92.956,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":17,"say":"retríval"}]},{"text":"Es determinista y no necesita un modelo.","start":93.256,"end":95.464,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Para cada pregunta del dataset, anotá qué documento contiene la respuesta.","start":96.014,"end":99.872,"terms":[]},{"text":"El test verifica si ese documento aparece entre los fragmentos recuperados.","start":100.172,"end":104.203,"terms":[]},{"text":"No hace falta ningún modelo para evaluarlo: es una comparación de identificadores, determinista y rápida.","start":104.503,"end":110.299,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta métrica es el techo del sistema.","start":110.599,"end":112.703,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si el fragmento correcto aparece en siete de cada diez preguntas, la respuesta final no puede acertar con fundamento en más de siete.","start":113.003,"end":119.136,"terms":[]},{"text":"Empezar por acá evita semanas de ajustes en el lugar equivocado.","start":119.436,"end":122.666,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":123.466,"end":160.93,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":123.966,"end":124.862,"terms":[]},{"text":"Capa dos: fidelidad.","start":125.162,"end":126.512,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Capa 2, fidelidad: cada afirmación de la respuesta tiene que estar respaldada por los fragmentos recuperados.","start":127.062,"end":132.579,"terms":[]},{"text":"Acá se detectan las invenciones.","start":132.879,"end":134.867,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"La fidelidad pregunta si cada afirmación de la respuesta está respaldada por los fragmentos recuperados.","start":135.417,"end":140.412,"terms":[]},{"text":"No evalúa si la respuesta es verdadera en general, sino si sale del contexto que el sistema tenía.","start":140.712,"end":145.695,"terms":[]},{"text":"Acá se detectan las invenciones: un dato que el modelo agregó de su conocimiento previo, una cifra alterada, una condición que el documento no menciona.","start":145.995,"end":153.533,"terms":[]},{"text":"Se mide con un juez que descompone la respuesta en afirmaciones y verifica cada una.","start":153.833,"end":157.702,"terms":[]},{"text":"Para cifras y fechas, sumá chequeos deterministas.","start":158.002,"end":160.93,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":161.73,"end":193.353,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":162.23,"end":163.127,"terms":[]},{"text":"Capa tres: respuesta.","start":163.427,"end":164.846,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Capa 3, respuesta: ¿responde lo que se preguntó y coincide con lo esperado?","start":165.396,"end":169.404,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Recién en la tercera capa se evalúa si la respuesta contesta lo que se preguntó y coincide con lo esperado.","start":169.954,"end":175.03,"terms":[]},{"text":"Una respuesta puede ser fiel al contexto y no servir: habla del tema, cita bien el documento y no responde la pregunta concreta.","start":175.33,"end":181.718,"terms":[]},{"text":"O puede responder correctamente por conocimiento general sin apoyarse en tus documentos, lo cual es un riesgo distinto.","start":182.018,"end":187.965,"terms":[]},{"text":"Por eso las tres capas se miran juntas: cada combinación de resultados apunta a un problema diferente.","start":188.265,"end":193.353,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":194.153,"end":227.622,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":194.653,"end":195.654,"terms":[]},{"text":"Casos negativos.","start":195.954,"end":197.315,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Sumá casos negativos: preguntas cuya respuesta no está en la base.","start":197.865,"end":201.408,"terms":[]},{"text":"El sistema tiene que decir que no sabe.","start":201.708,"end":203.871,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Agregá preguntas cuya respuesta no está en la base de conocimiento.","start":204.421,"end":207.697,"terms":[]},{"text":"El comportamiento esperado es que el sistema lo diga, en lugar de armar una respuesta plausible con fragmentos vagamente relacionados.","start":207.997,"end":214.443,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es el caso que más daño hace en producción y el que menos se prueba, porque los datasets suelen construirse a partir de los documentos existentes.","start":214.743,"end":221.468,"terms":[]},{"text":"Incluí también preguntas casi cubiertas: el documento habla del tema, pero no trae el dato específico que se pide.","start":221.768,"end":227.622,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":228.422,"end":264.946,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":228.922,"end":229.923,"terms":[]},{"text":"Diagnosticar por capa.","start":230.223,"end":231.666,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Diagnosticá por capa: retrieval bajo se arregla en chunking, índice o búsqueda; fidelidad baja se arregla en prompt o modelo.","start":232.216,"end":238.151,"terms":[{"start":22,"end":31,"say":"retríval"},{"start":51,"end":59,"say":"chánkin"}]},{"text":"Cambiar el prompt no arregla un mal retrieval.","start":238.451,"end":240.764,"terms":[{"start":36,"end":45,"say":"retríval"}]}],[{"text":"Con las tres métricas separadas, cada arreglo tiene su lugar.","start":241.314,"end":244.382,"terms":[]},{"text":"Recuperación baja se corrige en el tamaño de los fragmentos, en el índice o en la búsqueda.","start":244.682,"end":248.945,"terms":[]},{"text":"Fidelidad baja con buena recuperación se corrige en el prompt o cambiando de modelo.","start":249.245,"end":253.172,"terms":[]},{"text":"Respuesta baja con las otras dos altas suele ser un problema de formato o de instrucciones.","start":253.472,"end":257.979,"terms":[]},{"text":"La regla práctica es no tocar el prompt hasta confirmar que la recuperación funciona: cambiar el prompt no arregla una búsqueda que no encuentra.","start":258.279,"end":264.946,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":265.746,"end":296.533,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":266.246,"end":267.212,"terms":[]},{"text":"Métricas en CI.","start":267.512,"end":268.873,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"ci ai"}]}],[{"text":"Llevá las tres métricas a CI con umbrales y seguí su evolución en cada cambio de documentos.","start":269.423,"end":273.849,"terms":[{"start":26,"end":28,"say":"ci ai"}]}],[{"text":"Llevá las tres métricas al pipeline con un umbral para cada una, y corré el eval cada vez que cambien los documentos, no solo el código.","start":274.399,"end":280.648,"terms":[{"start":27,"end":35,"say":"páiplain"}]},{"text":"En un RAG, la base de conocimiento es parte del sistema.","start":280.948,"end":284.027,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un documento nuevo que contradice a otro, o una reorganización de carpetas, puede bajar la recuperación sin que nadie haya tocado una línea.","start":284.327,"end":290.924,"terms":[]},{"text":"Registrá la evolución de las métricas con cada cambio de contenido para poder ubicar cuándo empezó una caída.","start":291.224,"end":296.533,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":297.333,"end":339.54,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":297.833,"end":298.753,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un asistente interno responde preguntas sobre políticas de la empresa.","start":299.303,"end":302.951,"terms":[]},{"text":"El equipo arma cuarenta preguntas con su documento fuente y diez negativas.","start":303.251,"end":306.957,"terms":[]},{"text":"Primera medición, con cifras ilustrativas: recuperación 65%, fidelidad 92%, respuesta 60%.","start":307.257,"end":314.62,"terms":[]},{"text":"El equipo venía ajustando el prompt hacía dos semanas.","start":314.92,"end":317.988,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los números muestran que el generador es fiel a lo que recibe y que el problema está en la búsqueda.","start":318.288,"end":322.853,"terms":[]},{"text":"Revisan y encuentran que los fragmentos cortaban las tablas por la mitad.","start":323.153,"end":326.662,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cambian la estrategia de fragmentación y la recuperación sube a 90%; la respuesta, a 84%, sin tocar el prompt.","start":326.962,"end":333.989,"terms":[]},{"text":"En las negativas, tres de diez todavía inventan.","start":334.289,"end":336.997,"terms":[]},{"text":"Eso sí se corrige con una instrucción.","start":337.297,"end":339.54,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":340.34,"end":356.625,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":340.84,"end":342.363,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Medir solo la respuesta final y no saber qué capa falla.","start":342.913,"end":345.772,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ajustar el prompt cuando el problema es de recuperación.","start":346.322,"end":349.355,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"No incluir preguntas sin respuesta en la base de conocimiento.","start":349.905,"end":352.799,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Correr el eval solo cuando cambia el código y no los documentos.","start":353.349,"end":356.625,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":357.425,"end":373.699,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":357.925,"end":360.192,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cada pregunta tiene anotado su documento fuente.","start":360.742,"end":363.287,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Recuperación, fidelidad y respuesta se miden por separado.","start":363.837,"end":366.928,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El dataset incluye casos negativos y casi cubiertos.","start":367.478,"end":370.557,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El eval corre en CI ante cambios de documentos.","start":371.107,"end":373.699,"terms":[{"start":17,"end":19,"say":"ci ai"}]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":374.499,"end":382.59,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":374.999,"end":376.569,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Con la fuente correcta en contexto, igual altera cifras.","start":377.119,"end":380.163,"terms":[]},{"text":"La fidelidad se mide, no se supone.","start":380.463,"end":382.59,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":383.39,"end":397.229,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 21.","start":383.89,"end":385.484,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":385.784,"end":391.022,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 22: Modelo para pruebas de seguridad (1): threat model con OWASP LLM y Agentic Top 10.","start":391.572,"end":397.229,"terms":[{"start":59,"end":65,"say":"tret"},{"start":76,"end":81,"say":"ouasp"},{"start":88,"end":95,"say":"ayéntic"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d21-en","day":21,"lang":"en","title":"Testing RAG: retrieval, faithfulness and answer, separately","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d21-en.mp4","duration":440.548,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":18.582,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 21 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.195,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Testing RAG: retrieval, faithfulness and answer, separately.","start":4.745,"end":9.623,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"How to test a RAG system in three layers so that, when it fails, you know whether the problem is retrieval, generation or the knowledge base.","start":10.173,"end":18.582,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":19.382,"end":40.483,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"A RAG system answers questions by first searching your documents and then generating an answer with what it found.","start":19.882,"end":26.711,"terms":[]},{"text":"When it answers badly, the usual reaction is to tweak the prompt.","start":27.011,"end":30.833,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sometimes that works.","start":31.133,"end":32.599,"terms":[]},{"text":"Often it doesn't, because the problem came earlier: the right document never reached the model, and no instruction can make up for that.","start":32.899,"end":40.483,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":41.283,"end":63.103,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Testing a RAG end to end, with a single metric, mixes two different systems: a search engine and a generator.","start":41.783,"end":48.995,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide splits them into three layers measured separately, and adds negative cases, where the correct answer is admitting the information isn't there.","start":49.295,"end":57.657,"terms":[]},{"text":"The result is a diagnosis that says where to fix things, not just that something is wrong.","start":57.957,"end":63.103,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":63.903,"end":84.036,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":64.403,"end":66.1,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Python 3.10+ and a RAG app exposing the answer and the retrieved chunks.","start":66.65,"end":72.319,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"20 questions with expected answer and source document.","start":72.869,"end":76.238,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"DeepEval defaults to an OpenAI model as judge (needs OPENAI_API_KEY); its docs explain how to use another.","start":76.788,"end":84.036,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":84.836,"end":91.132,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":85.336,"end":86.615,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":86.915,"end":91.132,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":91.932,"end":94.699,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":92.432,"end":94.699,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":95.499,"end":136.691,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":95.999,"end":97.035,"terms":[]},{"text":"Layer one: retrieval.","start":97.335,"end":99.183,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Layer 1, retrieval: for each question, check whether the right document shows up among the retrieved chunks.","start":99.733,"end":105.936,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's deterministic and needs no model.","start":106.236,"end":108.7,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"For each question in the dataset, note which document holds the answer.","start":109.25,"end":113.269,"terms":[]},{"text":"The test checks whether that document shows up among the retrieved chunks.","start":113.569,"end":117.636,"terms":[]},{"text":"No model is needed to evaluate it: it's a comparison of identifiers, deterministic and fast.","start":117.936,"end":124.022,"terms":[]},{"text":"This metric is the system's ceiling.","start":124.322,"end":126.67,"terms":[]},{"text":"If the right chunk shows up for seven questions in ten, the final answer can't be right with grounding in more than seven.","start":126.97,"end":133.207,"terms":[]},{"text":"Starting here avoids weeks of adjustments in the wrong place.","start":133.507,"end":136.691,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":137.491,"end":178.554,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":137.991,"end":139.189,"terms":[]},{"text":"Layer two: faithfulness.","start":139.489,"end":141.674,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Layer 2, faithfulness: every claim in the answer must be supported by the retrieved chunks.","start":142.224,"end":147.533,"terms":[]},{"text":"This is where fabrications get caught.","start":147.833,"end":150.146,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Faithfulness asks whether every claim in the answer is supported by the retrieved chunks.","start":150.696,"end":155.4,"terms":[]},{"text":"It doesn't evaluate whether the answer is true in general, but whether it comes from the context the system had.","start":155.7,"end":161.752,"terms":[]},{"text":"This is where fabrications get caught: a fact the model added from prior knowledge, an altered figure, a condition the document doesn't mention.","start":162.052,"end":170.309,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's measured with a judge that breaks the answer into claims and verifies each one.","start":170.609,"end":175.151,"terms":[]},{"text":"For figures and dates, add deterministic checks.","start":175.451,"end":178.554,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":179.354,"end":215.981,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":179.854,"end":180.936,"terms":[]},{"text":"Layer three: answer.","start":181.236,"end":183.131,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Layer 3, answer: does it answer what was asked and match what's expected?","start":183.681,"end":188.583,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Only in the third layer do you evaluate whether the answer addresses what was asked and matches what's expected.","start":189.133,"end":195.556,"terms":[]},{"text":"An answer can be faithful to the context and still useless: it talks about the topic, quotes the document well and doesn't answer the specific question.","start":195.856,"end":204.23,"terms":[]},{"text":"Or it can answer correctly from general knowledge without relying on your documents, which is a different risk.","start":204.53,"end":210.361,"terms":[]},{"text":"That's why the three layers are read together: each combination of results points to a different problem.","start":210.661,"end":215.981,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":216.781,"end":253.443,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":217.281,"end":218.293,"terms":[]},{"text":"Negative cases.","start":218.593,"end":220.059,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Add negative cases: questions whose answer isn't in the knowledge base.","start":220.609,"end":224.93,"terms":[]},{"text":"The system must say it doesn't know.","start":225.23,"end":227.578,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Add questions whose answer isn't in the knowledge base.","start":228.128,"end":231.242,"terms":[]},{"text":"The expected behavior is for the system to say so, instead of assembling a plausible answer from vaguely related chunks.","start":231.542,"end":238.302,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's the case that does the most damage in production and the least tested, because datasets tend to be built from existing documents.","start":238.602,"end":245.594,"terms":[]},{"text":"Also include nearly covered questions: the document discusses the topic but doesn't carry the specific fact being asked for.","start":245.894,"end":253.443,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":254.243,"end":294.9,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":254.743,"end":255.872,"terms":[]},{"text":"Diagnose per layer.","start":256.172,"end":257.649,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Diagnose per layer: low retrieval is fixed in chunking, index or search; low faithfulness is fixed in prompt or model.","start":258.199,"end":265.864,"terms":[]},{"text":"Changing the prompt doesn't fix bad retrieval.","start":266.164,"end":268.906,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"With the three metrics separated, each fix has its place.","start":269.456,"end":272.849,"terms":[]},{"text":"Low retrieval is fixed in chunk size, the index or the search.","start":273.149,"end":276.995,"terms":[]},{"text":"Low faithfulness with good retrieval is fixed in the prompt or by changing models.","start":277.295,"end":281.79,"terms":[]},{"text":"A low answer score with the other two high is usually a format or instructions problem.","start":282.09,"end":287.074,"terms":[]},{"text":"The practical rule is not to touch the prompt until you've confirmed retrieval works: changing the prompt doesn't fix a search that doesn't find.","start":287.374,"end":294.9,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":295.7,"end":329.459,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":296.2,"end":297.387,"terms":[]},{"text":"Metrics in CI.","start":297.687,"end":299.245,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Bring the three metrics into CI with thresholds and track them on every document change.","start":299.795,"end":304.964,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Bring the three metrics into the pipeline with a threshold for each, and run the eval every time documents change, not just code.","start":305.514,"end":312.993,"terms":[]},{"text":"In a RAG, the knowledge base is part of the system.","start":313.293,"end":316.431,"terms":[]},{"text":"A new document contradicting another, or a folder reorganization, can lower retrieval without anyone having touched a line.","start":316.731,"end":324.199,"terms":[]},{"text":"Record how the metrics evolve with each content change so you can locate when a drop began.","start":324.499,"end":329.459,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":330.259,"end":374.707,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":330.759,"end":331.864,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"An internal assistant answers questions about company policies.","start":332.414,"end":336.225,"terms":[]},{"text":"The team builds forty questions with their source document and ten negatives.","start":336.525,"end":340.661,"terms":[]},{"text":"First measurement, with illustrative figures: retrieval 65%, faithfulness 92%, answer 60%.","start":340.961,"end":348.556,"terms":[]},{"text":"The team had been tuning the prompt for two weeks.","start":348.856,"end":351.529,"terms":[]},{"text":"The numbers show the generator is faithful to what it receives and that the problem lies in search.","start":351.829,"end":356.743,"terms":[]},{"text":"They check and find chunks were cutting tables in half.","start":357.043,"end":359.925,"terms":[]},{"text":"They change the chunking strategy and retrieval rises to 90%; the answer score, to 84%, without touching the prompt.","start":360.225,"end":368.157,"terms":[]},{"text":"On the negatives, three out of ten still make things up.","start":368.457,"end":371.722,"terms":[]},{"text":"That one does get fixed with an instruction.","start":372.022,"end":374.707,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":375.507,"end":393.406,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":376.007,"end":377.669,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Measuring only the final answer and not knowing which layer fails.","start":378.219,"end":381.949,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tuning the prompt when the problem is retrieval.","start":382.499,"end":385.149,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Not including questions with no answer in the knowledge base.","start":385.699,"end":389.254,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Running the eval only when code changes and not documents.","start":389.804,"end":393.406,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":394.206,"end":411.617,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":394.706,"end":396.949,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each question has its source document noted.","start":397.499,"end":400.091,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Retrieval, faithfulness and answer are measured separately.","start":400.641,"end":404.149,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The dataset includes negative and nearly covered cases.","start":404.699,"end":408.15,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The eval runs in CI on document changes.","start":408.7,"end":411.617,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":412.417,"end":421.519,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":412.917,"end":414.812,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"With the right source in context, it still alters figures.","start":415.362,"end":418.882,"terms":[]},{"text":"Faithfulness is measured, not assumed.","start":419.182,"end":421.519,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":422.319,"end":439.048,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 21.","start":422.819,"end":424.609,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":424.909,"end":430.891,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 22: A model for security testing (1): threat modeling with the OWASP LLM and Agentic Top 10.","start":431.441,"end":439.048,"terms":[{"start":81,"end":86,"say":"oh wasp"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d22-es","day":22,"lang":"es","title":"Modelo para pruebas de seguridad (1): threat model con OWASP LLM y Agentic Top 10","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d22-es.mp4","duration":436.495,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":14.309,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 22 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.672,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Modelo para pruebas de seguridad (1): threat model con OWASP LLM y Agentic Top 10.","start":4.222,"end":8.706,"terms":[{"start":38,"end":44,"say":"tret"},{"start":55,"end":60,"say":"ouasp"},{"start":67,"end":74,"say":"ayéntic"}]}],[{"text":"Cómo armar el modelo de amenazas de una feature con IA y convertirlo en casos de abuso que se pueden probar.","start":9.256,"end":14.309,"terms":[{"start":40,"end":47,"say":"fícher"},{"start":52,"end":54,"say":"i a"}]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":15.109,"end":32.389,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Las pruebas de seguridad de una aplicación con modelos suelen empezar por el final: alguien corre una herramienta de ataques, obtiene un reporte largo y no sabe qué hacer con él.","start":15.609,"end":23.936,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sin un modelo de amenazas, no hay forma de decidir cuáles de esos hallazgos importan para tu sistema ni qué riesgos quedaron sin probar porque ninguna herramienta los cubre.","start":24.236,"end":32.389,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":33.189,"end":54.313,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"El threat modeling es la parte de pensar antes de atacar.","start":33.689,"end":36.594,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":9,"say":"tret"}]},{"text":"Se dibuja el sistema, se marca dónde entra texto que no controlás y se recorren listas de riesgos conocidos para ver cuáles aplican.","start":36.894,"end":43.05,"terms":[]},{"text":"OWASP mantiene dos que sirven de guía: una para aplicaciones con LLM y otra para aplicaciones agénticas.","start":43.35,"end":48.612,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":5,"say":"ouasp"}]},{"text":"Esta guía te lleva del dibujo a una tabla de amenazas con casos de abuso concretos y una decisión por cada uno.","start":48.912,"end":54.313,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":55.113,"end":74.608,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":55.613,"end":57.543,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Una feature con IA propia o de práctica (un asistente de soporte sirve).","start":58.093,"end":61.788,"terms":[{"start":4,"end":11,"say":"fícher"},{"start":16,"end":18,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Las listas vigentes del OWASP GenAI Security Project (genai.owasp.org): Top 10 for LLM Applications, edición 2026, y Top 10 for Agentic Applications.","start":62.338,"end":71.698,"terms":[{"start":24,"end":29,"say":"ouasp"},{"start":30,"end":35,"say":"yen ei ai"},{"start":36,"end":44,"say":"sekiúriti"},{"start":128,"end":135,"say":"ayéntic"}]}],[{"text":"Solo sistemas propios o con autorización.","start":72.248,"end":74.608,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo de tabla de amenazas","start":75.408,"end":111.592,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Ejemplo de tabla de amenazas.","start":75.908,"end":77.698,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Chat: el mensaje del usuario es entrada no confiable.","start":78.248,"end":81.258,"terms":[]},{"text":"Riesgo: Prompt Injection.","start":81.558,"end":83.197,"terms":[{"start":15,"end":24,"say":"inyékshon"}]},{"text":"Caso de abuso: el usuario pide ignorar las reglas y ver datos de otro cliente.","start":83.497,"end":87.552,"terms":[]},{"text":"Se cubre con un test.","start":87.852,"end":89.166,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"RAG: un documento subido es entrada no confiable.","start":89.716,"end":92.575,"terms":[]},{"text":"Riesgo: Prompt Injection indirecta.","start":92.875,"end":95.026,"terms":[{"start":15,"end":24,"say":"inyékshon"}]},{"text":"Caso de abuso: un documento trae instrucciones ocultas.","start":95.326,"end":98.416,"terms":[]},{"text":"Se cubre con test y control.","start":98.716,"end":100.403,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tool de envío de email: la salida del modelo dispara una acción.","start":100.953,"end":104.067,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":4,"say":"tul"},{"start":17,"end":22,"say":"iméil"}]},{"text":"Riesgo: Excessive Agency.","start":104.367,"end":106.227,"terms":[]},{"text":"Caso de abuso: el modelo envía un correo sin aprobación.","start":106.527,"end":109.478,"terms":[]},{"text":"Se cubre con un control de diseño.","start":109.778,"end":111.592,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":112.392,"end":150.691,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":112.892,"end":113.789,"terms":[]},{"text":"Dibujar el sistema.","start":114.089,"end":115.369,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Dibujá el sistema: entradas (usuario, documentos, web, herramientas), modelo, salidas y acciones que puede ejecutar.","start":115.919,"end":122.04,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Empezá por un diagrama simple con cuatro tipos de elementos: las entradas, el modelo, las salidas y las acciones.","start":122.59,"end":128.548,"terms":[]},{"text":"En entradas, listá todo lo que llega al modelo: mensajes del usuario, documentos recuperados, páginas web, resultados de herramientas e instrucciones del sistema.","start":128.848,"end":137.013,"terms":[]},{"text":"En acciones, todo lo que el sistema puede hacer además de responder: consultar una base, enviar un mail, modificar un registro.","start":137.313,"end":143.817,"terms":[]},{"text":"No hace falta una herramienta especial.","start":144.117,"end":146.279,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lo que importa es que el dibujo lo haga o lo valide alguien que conoce la arquitectura real.","start":146.579,"end":150.691,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":151.491,"end":187.353,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":151.991,"end":152.888,"terms":[]},{"text":"Fronteras de confianza.","start":153.188,"end":154.607,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Marcá las fronteras de confianza: todo lugar donde el modelo lee texto que no controla tu equipo.","start":155.157,"end":159.873,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Sobre el diagrama, marcá cada lugar donde el modelo lee texto que tu equipo no escribió ni controla.","start":160.423,"end":165.314,"terms":[]},{"text":"El mensaje del usuario es el caso evidente.","start":165.614,"end":168.182,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los menos evidentes son los que más problemas traen: un documento subido por un cliente, el contenido de una página web, un campo de texto libre en un ticket, la respuesta de una API de terceros.","start":168.482,"end":177.947,"terms":[]},{"text":"Para un modelo, todo eso es texto en su contexto, y no distingue de forma confiable entre un dato y una instrucción.","start":178.247,"end":184.391,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cada frontera es un punto de entrada posible.","start":184.691,"end":187.353,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":188.153,"end":228.112,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":188.653,"end":189.549,"terms":[]},{"text":"El Top 10 para aplicaciones con LLM.","start":189.849,"end":192.185,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Recorré el Top 10 de LLM Applications y marcá qué riesgos aplican.","start":192.735,"end":196.279,"terms":[]},{"text":"Prompt Injection sigue primero en la edición 2026 y Sensitive Information Disclosure segundo.","start":196.579,"end":201.76,"terms":[{"start":7,"end":16,"say":"inyékshon"}]}],[{"text":"Recorré la lista riesgo por riesgo y, para cada uno, preguntá si aplica a tu diagrama.","start":202.31,"end":206.887,"terms":[]},{"text":"La inyección de prompts y la divulgación de información sensible encabezan la lista, y aplican a casi cualquier sistema.","start":207.187,"end":212.96,"terms":[]},{"text":"Otros dependen de la arquitectura: los riesgos de la cadena de suministro aplican si usás modelos o componentes de terceros; el manejo inseguro de la salida, si lo que genera el modelo termina ejecutándose o mostrándose sin validar.","start":213.26,"end":224.628,"terms":[]},{"text":"Marcá aplica o no aplica, con una línea de justificación.","start":224.928,"end":228.112,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":228.912,"end":264.077,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":229.412,"end":230.413,"terms":[]},{"text":"El Top 10 para aplicaciones agénticas.","start":230.713,"end":233.038,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Si el sistema tiene herramientas, memoria o actúa solo, sumá el Top 10 de Agentic Applications: ahí el riesgo ya no es lo que el modelo dice sino lo que hace.","start":233.588,"end":240.963,"terms":[{"start":74,"end":81,"say":"ayéntic"}]}],[{"text":"Si tu sistema tiene herramientas, memoria o actúa sin aprobación en cada paso, sumá la segunda lista.","start":241.513,"end":246.473,"terms":[]},{"text":"El cambio de fondo es que el riesgo deja de ser lo que el modelo dice y pasa a ser lo que hace.","start":246.773,"end":251.094,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un texto incorrecto se puede corregir; un mail enviado o un registro borrado, no.","start":251.394,"end":255.472,"terms":[]},{"text":"Acá aparecen riesgos como el abuso de herramientas, el exceso de permisos y la manipulación de la memoria.","start":255.772,"end":261.011,"terms":[]},{"text":"Revisá cada herramienta de tu diagrama contra la lista.","start":261.311,"end":264.077,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":264.877,"end":298.683,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":265.377,"end":266.378,"terms":[]},{"text":"Casos de abuso concretos.","start":266.678,"end":268.306,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Por cada riesgo que aplica, escribí un caso de abuso concreto: quién, por dónde entra y qué logra.","start":268.856,"end":273.955,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Para cada riesgo que aplica, escribí un caso de abuso con tres partes: quién, por qué punto de entrada y qué logra.","start":274.505,"end":280.266,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"Inyección de prompts\" es una categoría.","start":280.566,"end":282.856,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"Un cliente sube un PDF con texto oculto que le indica al asistente incluir en su respuesta los datos de otro cliente\" es un caso de abuso.","start":283.156,"end":289.626,"terms":[]},{"text":"La diferencia es que el segundo se puede convertir en un test.","start":289.926,"end":293.063,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si no podés escribir el caso concreto, todavía no entendiste cómo ese riesgo se manifiesta en tu sistema.","start":293.363,"end":298.683,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":299.483,"end":337.318,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":299.983,"end":300.949,"terms":[]},{"text":"Priorizar y decidir.","start":301.249,"end":302.668,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Priorizá por impacto y exposición, y decidí qué se cubre con tests automatizados (día 23) y qué necesita un control de diseño: permisos mínimos o aprobación humana.","start":303.218,"end":311.429,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ordená los casos por impacto y exposición: qué tan grave sería y qué tan fácil es llegar a ese punto de entrada.","start":311.979,"end":317.949,"terms":[]},{"text":"Después tomá una decisión para cada uno.","start":318.249,"end":320.377,"terms":[]},{"text":"Algunos se cubren con tests automatizados, que es el tema de mañana.","start":320.677,"end":324.267,"terms":[]},{"text":"Otros no se resuelven probando sino diseñando: quitarle una herramienta al agente, exigir aprobación humana antes de una acción irreversible, limitar los destinos posibles.","start":324.567,"end":332.87,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un control de diseño vale más que cien tests de un riesgo que no debería existir.","start":333.17,"end":337.318,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":338.118,"end":373.811,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":338.618,"end":339.538,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un equipo modela un asistente de soporte que lee tickets, consulta una base de pedidos y puede enviar mails.","start":340.088,"end":345.419,"terms":[]},{"text":"El diagrama muestra tres fronteras de confianza: el mensaje del usuario, los adjuntos del ticket y las respuestas de la API de envíos.","start":345.719,"end":352.107,"terms":[]},{"text":"De la primera lista aplican seis riesgos; de la segunda, cuatro.","start":352.407,"end":355.986,"terms":[]},{"text":"Escriben nueve casos de abuso.","start":356.286,"end":357.995,"terms":[]},{"text":"El de mayor impacto: un adjunto con instrucciones ocultas hace que el asistente envíe por mail los datos de un pedido ajeno.","start":358.295,"end":364.138,"terms":[]},{"text":"Deciden dos cosas.","start":364.438,"end":365.845,"terms":[]},{"text":"La herramienta de mail solo puede escribirle al remitente del ticket, un control de diseño.","start":366.145,"end":370.478,"terms":[]},{"text":"Y los otros ocho casos pasan a tests automatizados.","start":370.778,"end":373.811,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":374.611,"end":392.522,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":375.111,"end":376.635,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Correr una herramienta de ataques antes de tener el modelo de amenazas.","start":377.185,"end":380.705,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Considerar solo el mensaje del usuario como entrada no confiable.","start":381.255,"end":384.683,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Dejar los riesgos como categorías, sin casos de abuso concretos.","start":385.233,"end":388.811,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Querer cubrir con tests lo que se resuelve quitando un permiso.","start":389.361,"end":392.522,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":393.322,"end":411.65,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":393.822,"end":396.088,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El diagrama fue validado por alguien que conoce la arquitectura.","start":396.638,"end":399.706,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Todas las fronteras de confianza están marcadas.","start":400.256,"end":402.824,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cada riesgo aplicable tiene un caso de abuso con quién, por dónde y qué logra.","start":403.374,"end":407.197,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cada caso tiene una decisión: test, control de diseño o riesgo aceptado.","start":407.747,"end":411.65,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":412.45,"end":421.285,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":412.95,"end":414.52,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Un threat model generado sin tu arquitectura es una lista genérica.","start":415.07,"end":418.73,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":9,"say":"tret"}]},{"text":"El dibujo del sistema lo hace quien lo conoce.","start":419.03,"end":421.285,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":422.085,"end":434.995,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 22.","start":422.585,"end":424.201,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":424.501,"end":429.74,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 23: Modelo para pruebas de seguridad (2): red teaming automatizado.","start":430.29,"end":434.995,"terms":[{"start":59,"end":70,"say":"red tíming"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d22-en","day":22,"lang":"en","title":"A model for security testing (1): threat modeling with the OWASP LLM and Agentic Top 10","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d22-en.mp4","duration":482.343,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":16.12,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 22 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.299,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A model for security testing (1): threat modeling with the OWASP LLM and Agentic Top 10.","start":4.849,"end":10.796,"terms":[{"start":59,"end":64,"say":"oh wasp"}]}],[{"text":"How to build the threat model of an AI feature and turn it into testable abuse cases.","start":11.346,"end":16.12,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":16.92,"end":35.872,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Security testing of an application with models tends to start at the end: someone runs an attack tool, gets a long report and doesn't know what to do with it.","start":17.42,"end":26.398,"terms":[]},{"text":"Without a threat model, there's no way to decide which of those findings matter for your system, nor which risks went untested because no tool covers them.","start":26.698,"end":35.872,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":36.672,"end":59.782,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Threat modeling is the thinking part before attacking.","start":37.172,"end":40.205,"terms":[]},{"text":"You draw the system, mark where text you don't control comes in and walk through lists of known risks to see which apply.","start":40.505,"end":47.195,"terms":[]},{"text":"OWASP maintains two that serve as a guide: one for LLM applications and one for agentic applications.","start":47.495,"end":53.86,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":5,"say":"oh wasp"}]},{"text":"This guide takes you from the drawing to a threat table with concrete abuse cases and a decision for each.","start":54.16,"end":59.782,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":60.582,"end":83.257,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":61.082,"end":62.779,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"An AI feature of your own or a practice one (a support assistant works).","start":63.329,"end":67.674,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The current OWASP GenAI Security Project lists (genai.owasp.org): Top 10 for LLM Applications, 2026 edition, and Top 10 for Agentic Applications.","start":68.224,"end":79.546,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":17,"say":"oh wasp"}]}],[{"text":"Only systems you own or are authorized to test.","start":80.096,"end":83.257,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Sample threat table","start":84.057,"end":125.884,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Sample threat table.","start":84.557,"end":86.15,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Chat: the user message is untrusted input.","start":86.7,"end":89.756,"terms":[]},{"text":"Risk: Prompt Injection.","start":90.056,"end":92.079,"terms":[]},{"text":"Abuse case: the user asks to ignore the rules and see another customer's data.","start":92.379,"end":97.583,"terms":[]},{"text":"Covered by a test.","start":97.883,"end":99.406,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"RAG: an uploaded document is untrusted input.","start":99.956,"end":103.361,"terms":[]},{"text":"Risk: indirect Prompt Injection.","start":103.661,"end":106.461,"terms":[]},{"text":"Abuse case: a document carries hidden instructions.","start":106.761,"end":110.444,"terms":[]},{"text":"Covered by a test plus a control.","start":110.744,"end":113.057,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Email tool: model output triggers an action.","start":113.607,"end":116.756,"terms":[]},{"text":"Risk: Excessive Agency.","start":117.056,"end":119.323,"terms":[]},{"text":"Abuse case: the model sends an email without approval.","start":119.623,"end":123.608,"terms":[]},{"text":"Covered by a design control.","start":123.908,"end":125.884,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":126.684,"end":169.43,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":127.184,"end":128.22,"terms":[]},{"text":"Draw the system.","start":128.52,"end":129.742,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Draw the system: inputs (user, documents, web, tools), model, outputs and the actions it can execute.","start":130.292,"end":137.771,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Start with a simple diagram with four kinds of elements: inputs, the model, outputs and actions.","start":138.321,"end":144.222,"terms":[]},{"text":"Under inputs, list everything reaching the model: user messages, retrieved documents, web pages, tool results and system instructions.","start":144.522,"end":153.255,"terms":[]},{"text":"Under actions, everything the system can do besides answering: query a database, send an email, modify a record.","start":153.555,"end":160.953,"terms":[]},{"text":"No special tool is needed.","start":161.253,"end":163.218,"terms":[]},{"text":"What matters is that the drawing is made or validated by someone who knows the real architecture.","start":163.518,"end":169.43,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":170.23,"end":207.844,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":170.73,"end":171.928,"terms":[]},{"text":"Trust boundaries.","start":172.228,"end":173.764,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Mark trust boundaries: every place where the model reads text your team doesn't control.","start":174.314,"end":179.564,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"On the diagram, mark every place where the model reads text your team neither wrote nor controls.","start":180.114,"end":185.515,"terms":[]},{"text":"The user message is the obvious case.","start":185.815,"end":188.302,"terms":[]},{"text":"The less obvious ones bring the most trouble: a document uploaded by a customer, the content of a web page, a free-text field in a ticket, a third-party API response.","start":188.602,"end":198.949,"terms":[]},{"text":"To a model, all of that is text in its context, and it doesn't reliably tell data from an instruction.","start":199.249,"end":204.964,"terms":[]},{"text":"Every boundary is a possible entry point.","start":205.264,"end":207.844,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":208.644,"end":253.817,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":209.144,"end":210.227,"terms":[]},{"text":"The Top 10 for LLM applications.","start":210.527,"end":213.014,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Walk the Top 10 for LLM Applications and flag which risks apply.","start":213.564,"end":218.036,"terms":[]},{"text":"Prompt Injection stays first in the 2026 edition and Sensitive Information Disclosure second.","start":218.336,"end":224.736,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Walk the list risk by risk and, for each one, ask whether it applies to your diagram.","start":225.286,"end":230.931,"terms":[]},{"text":"Prompt injection and sensitive information disclosure head the list, and apply to almost any system.","start":231.231,"end":237.201,"terms":[]},{"text":"Others depend on the architecture: supply chain risks apply if you use third-party models or components; insecure output handling, if what the model generates ends up being executed or displayed without validation.","start":237.501,"end":249.718,"terms":[]},{"text":"Mark applies or doesn't apply, with one line of justification.","start":250.018,"end":253.817,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":254.617,"end":294.901,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":255.117,"end":256.129,"terms":[]},{"text":"The Top 10 for agentic applications.","start":256.429,"end":259.009,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"If the system has tools, memory or acts on its own, add the Top 10 for Agentic Applications: there the risk is no longer what the model says but what it does.","start":259.559,"end":269.639,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"If your system has tools, memory or acts without approval at each step, add the second list.","start":270.189,"end":275.951,"terms":[]},{"text":"The underlying shift is that the risk stops being what the model says and becomes what it does.","start":276.251,"end":281.42,"terms":[]},{"text":"Wrong text can be corrected; a sent email or a deleted record can't.","start":281.72,"end":285.995,"terms":[]},{"text":"This is where risks such as tool misuse, excessive permissions and memory manipulation appear.","start":286.295,"end":291.766,"terms":[]},{"text":"Check each tool in your diagram against the list.","start":292.066,"end":294.901,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":295.701,"end":333.292,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":296.201,"end":297.33,"terms":[]},{"text":"Concrete abuse cases.","start":297.63,"end":299.339,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"For each applicable risk, write a concrete abuse case: who, through which entry point, achieving what.","start":299.889,"end":306.01,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"For each applicable risk, write an abuse case with three parts: who, through which entry point and achieving what.","start":306.56,"end":313.285,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"Prompt injection\" is a category.","start":313.585,"end":315.608,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"A customer uploads a PDF with hidden text instructing the assistant to include another customer's data in its answer\" is an abuse case.","start":315.908,"end":323.631,"terms":[]},{"text":"The difference is that the second can be turned into a test.","start":323.931,"end":327.463,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you can't write the concrete case, you haven't yet understood how that risk shows up in your system.","start":327.763,"end":333.292,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":334.092,"end":376.792,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":334.592,"end":335.779,"terms":[]},{"text":"Prioritize and decide.","start":336.079,"end":337.963,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Prioritize by impact and exposure, and decide what gets automated tests (day 23) and what needs a design control: least privilege or human approval.","start":338.513,"end":348.244,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Order the cases by impact and exposure: how serious it would be and how easy it is to reach that entry point.","start":348.794,"end":355.229,"terms":[]},{"text":"Then make a decision for each.","start":355.529,"end":357.563,"terms":[]},{"text":"Some get covered by automated tests, which is tomorrow's topic.","start":357.863,"end":361.604,"terms":[]},{"text":"Others aren't solved by testing but by design: removing a tool from the agent, requiring human approval before an irreversible action, limiting possible destinations.","start":361.904,"end":371.938,"terms":[]},{"text":"A design control is worth more than a hundred tests of a risk that shouldn't exist.","start":372.238,"end":376.792,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":377.592,"end":415.746,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":378.092,"end":379.197,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A team models a support assistant that reads tickets, queries an orders database and can send emails.","start":379.747,"end":386.066,"terms":[]},{"text":"The diagram shows three trust boundaries: the user message, ticket attachments and the shipping API's responses.","start":386.366,"end":392.812,"terms":[]},{"text":"Six risks from the first list apply; four from the second.","start":393.112,"end":396.865,"terms":[]},{"text":"They write nine abuse cases.","start":397.165,"end":399.153,"terms":[]},{"text":"The highest-impact one: an attachment with hidden instructions makes the assistant email the data of someone else's order.","start":399.453,"end":406.189,"terms":[]},{"text":"They decide two things.","start":406.489,"end":408.14,"terms":[]},{"text":"The email tool can only write to the ticket's sender, a design control.","start":408.44,"end":412.437,"terms":[]},{"text":"And the other eight cases become automated tests.","start":412.737,"end":415.746,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":416.546,"end":434.77,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":417.046,"end":418.709,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Running an attack tool before having the threat model.","start":419.259,"end":421.92,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Considering only the user message as untrusted input.","start":422.47,"end":425.724,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Leaving risks as categories, with no concrete abuse cases.","start":426.274,"end":430.294,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Trying to cover with tests what is solved by removing a permission.","start":430.844,"end":434.77,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":435.57,"end":455.257,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":436.07,"end":438.314,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The diagram was validated by someone who knows the architecture.","start":438.864,"end":442.593,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"All trust boundaries are marked.","start":443.143,"end":445.131,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each applicable risk has an abuse case with who, where and what it achieves.","start":445.681,"end":450.281,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each case has a decision: test, design control or accepted risk.","start":450.831,"end":455.257,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":456.057,"end":465.832,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":456.557,"end":458.452,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A threat model generated without your architecture is a generic list.","start":459.002,"end":462.906,"terms":[]},{"text":"The system drawing comes from whoever knows it.","start":463.206,"end":465.832,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":466.632,"end":480.843,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 22.","start":467.132,"end":468.76,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":469.06,"end":475.042,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 23: A model for security testing (2): automated red teaming.","start":475.592,"end":480.843,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d23-es","day":23,"lang":"es","title":"Modelo para pruebas de seguridad (2): red teaming automatizado","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d23-es.mp4","duration":412.218,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":15.853,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 23 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.556,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Modelo para pruebas de seguridad (2): red teaming automatizado.","start":4.106,"end":7.545,"terms":[{"start":38,"end":49,"say":"red tíming"}]}],[{"text":"Un esquema de tres modelos (atacante, objetivo y evaluador) que genera pruebas adversas a partir de tu threat model y reporta la tasa de éxito por categoría.","start":8.095,"end":15.853,"terms":[{"start":103,"end":109,"say":"tret"}]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":16.653,"end":33.667,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Con el modelo de amenazas de ayer tenés una lista de casos de abuso.","start":17.153,"end":20.662,"terms":[]},{"text":"Probarlos a mano, escribiendo variantes de ataques una por una, funciona para cinco casos y no para quinientos.","start":20.962,"end":26.421,"terms":[]},{"text":"Y los ataques reales no vienen de a uno: vienen reformulados, traducidos, repartidos en varios mensajes o disfrazados de otra cosa.","start":26.721,"end":33.667,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":34.467,"end":56.635,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"El red teaming automatizado usa un modelo para generar esas variantes en volumen, las envía contra tu aplicación y usa otro modelo para decidir si el ataque tuvo éxito.","start":34.967,"end":43.294,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":14,"say":"red tíming"}]},{"text":"Es potente y tiene límites claros: cuesta, genera contenido adverso y su veredicto también se equivoca.","start":43.594,"end":48.949,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta guía muestra cómo configurarlo a partir de tu modelo de amenazas, cómo leer el reporte sin tomarlo como verdad y dónde ubicarlo en tu ciclo de pruebas.","start":49.249,"end":56.635,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":57.435,"end":73.523,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":57.935,"end":59.865,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Solo contra sistemas propios o con autorización escrita, y en ambientes de prueba.","start":60.415,"end":64.505,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El threat model del día 22.","start":65.055,"end":66.95,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":9,"say":"tret"}]}],[{"text":"Tu asistente corriendo en local o staging con un endpoint HTTP.","start":67.5,"end":70.811,"terms":[{"start":34,"end":41,"say":"stéiyin"}]}],[{"text":"Node.js 22+ y promptfoo.","start":71.361,"end":73.523,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":7,"say":"noud yei es"},{"start":14,"end":23,"say":"promptfú"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":74.323,"end":79.645,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":74.823,"end":75.998,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":76.298,"end":79.645,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":80.445,"end":83.084,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":80.945,"end":83.084,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":83.884,"end":119.827,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":84.384,"end":85.28,"terms":[]},{"text":"Autorización y entorno.","start":85.58,"end":87.197,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Confirmá autorización y ambiente.","start":87.747,"end":89.758,"terms":[]},{"text":"El red teaming genera contenido adverso: va contra staging, con datos de prueba.","start":90.058,"end":94.275,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":14,"say":"red tíming"},{"start":51,"end":58,"say":"stéiyin"}]}],[{"text":"Antes de correr nada, confirmá por escrito que tenés autorización para atacar ese sistema, y apuntá a un entorno de staging con datos de prueba.","start":94.825,"end":101.724,"terms":[{"start":116,"end":123,"say":"stéiyin"}]},{"text":"El red teaming genera contenido adverso a propósito y puede disparar acciones reales si el sistema tiene herramientas conectadas.","start":102.024,"end":108.11,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":14,"say":"red tíming"}]},{"text":"Contra producción puede enviar mensajes, modificar registros o activar alertas de abuso del proveedor.","start":108.41,"end":113.625,"terms":[]},{"text":"Avisale también al equipo que opera el entorno.","start":113.925,"end":116.355,"terms":[]},{"text":"Una corrida que nadie esperaba se parece mucho a un incidente.","start":116.655,"end":119.827,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":120.627,"end":157.824,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":121.127,"end":122.023,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tres roles.","start":122.323,"end":123.301,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Entendé el esquema: un modelo atacante genera entradas adversas, tu aplicación es el objetivo, y un evaluador decide si el ataque tuvo éxito.","start":123.851,"end":131.377,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El esquema tiene tres piezas.","start":131.927,"end":133.729,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un modelo atacante genera entradas adversas.","start":134.029,"end":136.598,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tu aplicación es el objetivo y las recibe como si vinieran de un usuario.","start":136.898,"end":140.825,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un evaluador decide, para cada intercambio, si el ataque logró lo que buscaba.","start":141.125,"end":144.958,"terms":[]},{"text":"Entender esto ayuda a interpretar los resultados, porque dos de las tres piezas son modelos, con sus propios errores.","start":145.258,"end":151.124,"terms":[]},{"text":"Una tasa de éxito de ataque depende tanto de tu aplicación como de lo creativo que fue el atacante y de lo estricto que fue el evaluador.","start":151.424,"end":157.824,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":158.624,"end":195.566,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":159.124,"end":160.02,"terms":[]},{"text":"El propósito, con sus límites.","start":160.32,"end":162.216,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Describí el propósito del sistema con precisión, incluido lo que no debe hacer.","start":162.766,"end":166.762,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los ataques se generan en relación a esa descripción.","start":167.062,"end":170.014,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"La configuración pide una descripción del sistema.","start":170.564,"end":173.213,"terms":[]},{"text":"De todo el archivo, es el campo que más influye en la calidad de los ataques.","start":173.513,"end":177.486,"terms":[]},{"text":"Escribí qué hace la aplicación, para quién y, sobre todo, qué no debe hacer nunca: revelar datos de otros clientes, dar asesoramiento médico, ejecutar acciones sin confirmación.","start":177.786,"end":187.054,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los ataques se generan en relación con esa descripción.","start":187.354,"end":190.294,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con un propósito vago recibís ataques genéricos, y un reporte que dice poco sobre tus riesgos.","start":190.594,"end":195.566,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":196.366,"end":237.708,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":196.866,"end":197.867,"terms":[]},{"text":"Plugins y estrategias desde el threat model.","start":198.167,"end":200.375,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":7,"say":"pláguins"},{"start":31,"end":37,"say":"tret"}]}],[{"text":"Elegí plugins a partir de tu threat model, no todos.","start":200.925,"end":203.923,"terms":[{"start":6,"end":13,"say":"pláguins"},{"start":29,"end":35,"say":"tret"}]},{"text":"Cada plugin es una categoría de riesgo; cada estrategia, una forma de entregar el ataque.","start":204.223,"end":208.986,"terms":[{"start":5,"end":11,"say":"pláguin"}]},{"text":"Los nombres cambian entre versiones: redteam init te ofrece los vigentes.","start":209.286,"end":213.073,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cada plugin representa una categoría de riesgo y cada estrategia, una forma de entregar el ataque.","start":213.623,"end":218.711,"terms":[{"start":5,"end":11,"say":"pláguin"}]},{"text":"La tentación es activar todo.","start":219.011,"end":220.929,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es caro, lento y produce un reporte imposible de leer.","start":221.229,"end":224.344,"terms":[]},{"text":"Elegí los plugins que corresponden a los riesgos que marcaste como aplicables ayer, y dos o tres estrategias.","start":224.644,"end":230.138,"terms":[{"start":10,"end":17,"say":"pláguins"}]},{"text":"Los nombres cambian entre versiones de la herramienta, así que usá el comando de inicialización para ver los vigentes en lugar de copiarlos de un tutorial viejo.","start":230.438,"end":237.708,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":238.508,"end":278.283,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":239.008,"end":240.009,"terms":[]},{"text":"Leer el reporte y las conversaciones.","start":240.309,"end":242.471,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Corré y leé el reporte por categoría: tasa de éxito de ataque.","start":243.021,"end":246.356,"terms":[]},{"text":"Después abrí las conversaciones: el evaluador también da falsos positivos.","start":246.656,"end":250.745,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El reporte muestra una tasa de éxito de ataque por categoría.","start":251.295,"end":254.607,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sirve para saber dónde mirar, no para sacar conclusiones.","start":254.907,"end":258.183,"terms":[]},{"text":"Abrí las conversaciones marcadas como exitosas y leelas.","start":258.483,"end":261.516,"terms":[]},{"text":"Vas a encontrar hallazgos reales, y también falsos positivos: casos donde la aplicación se negó de forma correcta y el evaluador igual lo contó como fallo.","start":261.816,"end":269.238,"terms":[]},{"text":"Revisá además una muestra de los marcados como resistidos, porque los falsos negativos existen.","start":269.538,"end":274.428,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un hallazgo es una conversación que una persona leyó y confirmó.","start":274.728,"end":278.283,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":279.083,"end":311.961,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":279.583,"end":280.55,"terms":[]},{"text":"Corregir, repetir y programar.","start":280.85,"end":282.687,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Arreglá, volvé a correr y programalo: nocturno o por release.","start":283.237,"end":286.618,"terms":[{"start":53,"end":60,"say":"rilís"}]},{"text":"Por costo no conviene en cada PR.","start":286.918,"end":288.883,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"pe erre"}]}],[{"text":"Para cada hallazgo confirmado, decidí el arreglo, que puede ser un cambio de prompt, un filtro o un control de diseño, y volvé a correr la misma configuración.","start":289.433,"end":297.365,"terms":[]},{"text":"Guardá los ataques que tuvieron éxito como casos de regresión deterministas, para probarlos en cada cambio sin tener que generar nada.","start":297.665,"end":304.366,"terms":[]},{"text":"La corrida completa de red teaming va de forma programada, por noche o por release.","start":304.666,"end":308.733,"terms":[{"start":23,"end":34,"say":"red tíming"},{"start":75,"end":82,"say":"rilís"}]},{"text":"Por costo y por duración no conviene en cada pull request.","start":309.033,"end":311.961,"terms":[{"start":45,"end":57,"say":"pul ricuést"}]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":312.761,"end":349.069,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":313.261,"end":314.181,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un equipo corre red teaming contra el asistente de soporte de ayer, en staging, con cuatro plugins elegidos desde su modelo de amenazas.","start":314.731,"end":321.432,"terms":[{"start":16,"end":27,"say":"red tíming"},{"start":71,"end":78,"say":"stéiyin"},{"start":91,"end":98,"say":"pláguins"}]},{"text":"El reporte marca doce ataques exitosos sobre doscientos.","start":321.732,"end":324.753,"terms":[]},{"text":"Al leer las conversaciones, cinco son falsos positivos: el asistente se negó y ofreció derivar a una persona.","start":325.053,"end":331.244,"terms":[]},{"text":"Quedan siete reales.","start":331.544,"end":332.87,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cuatro logran que revele parte de sus instrucciones internas y tres consiguen datos de un pedido cambiando el número en un mensaje reformulado.","start":333.17,"end":339.802,"terms":[]},{"text":"El segundo grupo es grave.","start":340.102,"end":341.87,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lo corrigen validando en el código que el pedido pertenezca al usuario, no en el prompt.","start":342.17,"end":346.352,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la segunda corrida ese grupo queda en cero.","start":346.652,"end":349.069,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":349.869,"end":366.956,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":350.369,"end":351.893,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Correr contra producción o sin autorización escrita.","start":352.443,"end":355.209,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Activar todos los plugins en lugar de los del modelo de amenazas.","start":355.759,"end":359.07,"terms":[{"start":18,"end":25,"say":"pláguins"}]}],[{"text":"Reportar la tasa de éxito sin haber leído las conversaciones.","start":359.62,"end":362.943,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Arreglar con una frase en el prompt lo que requiere un control en código.","start":363.493,"end":366.956,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":367.756,"end":385.341,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":368.256,"end":370.522,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Hay autorización escrita y el objetivo es staging con datos de prueba.","start":371.072,"end":374.616,"terms":[{"start":42,"end":49,"say":"stéiyin"}]}],[{"text":"El propósito describe qué no debe hacer el sistema.","start":375.166,"end":377.839,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Los plugins salen de los riesgos aplicables del día anterior.","start":378.389,"end":381.422,"terms":[{"start":4,"end":11,"say":"pláguins"}]}],[{"text":"Cada hallazgo reportado fue leído y confirmado por una persona.","start":381.972,"end":385.341,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":386.141,"end":395.87,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":386.641,"end":388.211,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"El modelo evaluador produce falsos positivos y falsos negativos.","start":388.761,"end":392.375,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un reporte de red teaming sin revisión humana no es un hallazgo.","start":392.675,"end":395.87,"terms":[{"start":14,"end":25,"say":"red tíming"}]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":396.67,"end":410.718,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 23.","start":397.17,"end":398.763,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":399.063,"end":404.302,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 24: Seguridad (3): prompt injection en agentes con herramientas y cómo probar los controles.","start":404.852,"end":410.718,"terms":[{"start":43,"end":52,"say":"inyékshon"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d23-en","day":23,"lang":"en","title":"A model for security testing (2): automated red teaming","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d23-en.mp4","duration":452.376,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":17.316,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 23 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.229,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A model for security testing (2): automated red teaming.","start":4.779,"end":8.451,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A three-model scheme (attacker, target and grader) that generates adversarial tests from your threat model and reports the success rate per category.","start":9.001,"end":17.316,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":18.116,"end":37.08,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"With yesterday's threat model you have a list of abuse cases.","start":18.616,"end":22.195,"terms":[]},{"text":"Testing them by hand, writing attack variants one at a time, works for five cases and not for five hundred.","start":22.495,"end":28.709,"terms":[]},{"text":"And real attacks don't come one at a time: they come rephrased, translated, spread over several messages or disguised as something else.","start":29.009,"end":37.08,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":37.88,"end":63.95,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Automated red teaming uses a model to generate those variants in volume, sends them against your application and uses another model to decide whether the attack succeeded.","start":38.38,"end":47.88,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's powerful and has clear limits: it costs money, it generates adversarial content and its verdict is also wrong at times.","start":48.18,"end":56.019,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide shows how to configure it from your threat model, how to read the report without taking it as truth and where to place it in your testing cycle.","start":56.319,"end":63.95,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":64.75,"end":83.891,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":65.25,"end":66.947,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Only against systems you own or have written authorization for, in test environments.","start":67.497,"end":72.748,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The threat model from day 22.","start":73.298,"end":75.645,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Your assistant running locally or in staging with an HTTP endpoint.","start":76.195,"end":80.262,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Node.js 22+ and promptfoo.","start":80.812,"end":83.891,"terms":[{"start":16,"end":25,"say":"prompt foo"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":84.691,"end":90.988,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":85.191,"end":86.471,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":86.771,"end":90.988,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":91.788,"end":94.554,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":92.288,"end":94.554,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":95.354,"end":135.477,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":95.854,"end":96.89,"terms":[]},{"text":"Authorization and environment.","start":97.19,"end":99.132,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Confirm authorization and environment.","start":99.682,"end":102.088,"terms":[]},{"text":"Red teaming generates adversarial content: it targets staging, with test data.","start":102.388,"end":107.499,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Before running anything, confirm in writing that you're authorized to attack that system, and target a staging environment with test data.","start":108.049,"end":115.423,"terms":[]},{"text":"Red teaming generates adversarial content on purpose and can trigger real actions if the system has tools connected.","start":115.723,"end":122.808,"terms":[]},{"text":"Against production it can send messages, modify records or trip the provider's abuse alerts.","start":123.108,"end":128.974,"terms":[]},{"text":"Also tell the team operating the environment.","start":129.274,"end":131.958,"terms":[]},{"text":"A run nobody expected looks a lot like an incident.","start":132.258,"end":135.477,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":136.277,"end":177.317,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":136.777,"end":137.975,"terms":[]},{"text":"Three roles.","start":138.275,"end":139.288,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Understand the scheme: an attacker model generates adversarial inputs, your app is the target, and a grader decides whether the attack succeeded.","start":139.838,"end":148.293,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The scheme has three pieces.","start":148.843,"end":150.819,"terms":[]},{"text":"An attacker model generates adversarial inputs.","start":151.119,"end":154.198,"terms":[]},{"text":"Your application is the target and receives them as if they came from a user.","start":154.498,"end":158.774,"terms":[]},{"text":"A grader decides, for each exchange, whether the attack achieved what it sought.","start":159.074,"end":163.813,"terms":[]},{"text":"Understanding this helps interpret results, because two of the three pieces are models, with their own errors.","start":164.113,"end":170.211,"terms":[]},{"text":"An attack success rate depends as much on your application as on how creative the attacker was and how strict the grader was.","start":170.511,"end":177.317,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":178.117,"end":218.96,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":178.617,"end":179.699,"terms":[]},{"text":"The purpose, with its limits.","start":179.999,"end":182.034,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Describe the system's purpose precisely, including what it must not do.","start":182.584,"end":187.172,"terms":[]},{"text":"Attacks are generated relative to that description.","start":187.472,"end":190.644,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The config asks for a description of the system.","start":191.194,"end":194.03,"terms":[]},{"text":"Of the whole file, it's the field with the most influence on attack quality.","start":194.33,"end":198.814,"terms":[]},{"text":"Write what the application does, for whom and, above all, what it must never do: reveal other customers' data, give medical advice, execute actions without confirmation.","start":199.114,"end":210.274,"terms":[]},{"text":"Attacks are generated relative to that description.","start":210.574,"end":213.746,"terms":[]},{"text":"With a vague purpose you get generic attacks, and a report that says little about your risks.","start":214.046,"end":218.96,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":219.76,"end":264.132,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":220.26,"end":221.272,"terms":[]},{"text":"Plugins and strategies from the threat model.","start":221.572,"end":224.292,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pick plugins from your threat model, not all of them.","start":224.842,"end":228.072,"terms":[]},{"text":"Each plugin is a risk category; each strategy, a way of delivering the attack.","start":228.372,"end":233.425,"terms":[]},{"text":"Names change between versions: redteam init offers the current ones.","start":233.725,"end":238.07,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each plugin represents a risk category and each strategy, a way of delivering the attack.","start":238.62,"end":244.009,"terms":[]},{"text":"The temptation is to enable everything.","start":244.309,"end":246.727,"terms":[]},{"text":"That's expensive, slow and produces an unreadable report.","start":247.027,"end":250.849,"terms":[]},{"text":"Choose the plugins matching the risks you marked as applicable yesterday, and two or three strategies.","start":251.149,"end":256.423,"terms":[]},{"text":"Names change between versions of the tool, so use the init command to see the current ones instead of copying them from an old tutorial.","start":256.723,"end":264.132,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":264.932,"end":307.088,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":265.432,"end":266.561,"terms":[]},{"text":"Read the report and the conversations.","start":266.861,"end":269.116,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Run and read the report per category: attack success rate.","start":269.666,"end":273.338,"terms":[]},{"text":"Then open the conversations: the grader produces false positives too.","start":273.638,"end":278.122,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The report shows an attack success rate per category.","start":278.672,"end":281.879,"terms":[]},{"text":"It tells you where to look, not what to conclude.","start":282.179,"end":285.328,"terms":[]},{"text":"Open the conversations flagged as successful and read them.","start":285.628,"end":288.974,"terms":[]},{"text":"You'll find real findings, and false positives too: cases where the application refused correctly and the grader still counted it as a failure.","start":289.274,"end":297.427,"terms":[]},{"text":"Also review a sample of those flagged as resisted, because false negatives exist.","start":297.727,"end":303.337,"terms":[]},{"text":"A finding is a conversation a person read and confirmed.","start":303.637,"end":307.088,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":307.888,"end":343.343,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":308.388,"end":309.575,"terms":[]},{"text":"Fix, repeat and schedule.","start":309.875,"end":311.746,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Fix, rerun and schedule it: nightly or per release.","start":312.296,"end":315.852,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cost makes it a poor fit for every PR.","start":316.152,"end":318.755,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"For each confirmed finding, decide the fix, which may be a prompt change, a filter or a design control, and rerun the same config.","start":319.305,"end":326.947,"terms":[]},{"text":"Save the attacks that succeeded as deterministic regression cases, so you can test them on every change without generating anything.","start":327.247,"end":334.668,"terms":[]},{"text":"The full red teaming run goes on a schedule, nightly or per release.","start":334.968,"end":338.907,"terms":[]},{"text":"Because of cost and duration it isn't a good fit for every pull request.","start":339.207,"end":343.343,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":344.143,"end":384.352,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":344.643,"end":345.748,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A team runs red teaming against yesterday's support assistant, in staging, with four plugins chosen from their threat model.","start":346.298,"end":353.325,"terms":[]},{"text":"The report flags twelve successful attacks out of two hundred.","start":353.625,"end":357.378,"terms":[]},{"text":"Reading the conversations, five are false positives: the assistant refused and offered to hand over to a person.","start":357.678,"end":364.588,"terms":[]},{"text":"Seven real ones remain.","start":364.888,"end":366.725,"terms":[]},{"text":"Four get it to reveal part of its internal instructions and three obtain data from an order by changing the number in a rephrased message.","start":367.025,"end":374.203,"terms":[]},{"text":"The second group is serious.","start":374.503,"end":376.398,"terms":[]},{"text":"They fix it by validating in code that the order belongs to the user, not in the prompt.","start":376.698,"end":381.53,"terms":[]},{"text":"In the second run that group is at zero.","start":381.83,"end":384.352,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":385.152,"end":403.783,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":385.652,"end":387.315,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Running against production or without written authorization.","start":387.865,"end":391.235,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Enabling every plugin instead of those from the threat model.","start":391.785,"end":395.235,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Reporting the success rate without having read the conversations.","start":395.785,"end":399.433,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Fixing with a sentence in the prompt what requires a control in code.","start":399.983,"end":403.783,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":404.583,"end":422.911,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":405.083,"end":407.326,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"There is written authorization and the target is staging with test data.","start":407.876,"end":411.64,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The purpose describes what the system must not do.","start":412.19,"end":415.246,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The plugins come from the previous day's applicable risks.","start":415.796,"end":418.98,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each reported finding was read and confirmed by a person.","start":419.53,"end":422.911,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":423.711,"end":434.206,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":424.211,"end":426.106,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The grader model produces false positives and false negatives.","start":426.656,"end":430.246,"terms":[]},{"text":"A red teaming report with no human review isn't a finding.","start":430.546,"end":434.206,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":435.006,"end":450.876,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 23.","start":435.506,"end":437.239,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":437.539,"end":443.52,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 24: Security (3): prompt injection in tool-using agents and how to test the controls.","start":444.07,"end":450.876,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d24-es","day":24,"lang":"es","title":"Seguridad (3): prompt injection en agentes con herramientas y cómo probar los controles","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d24-es.mp4","duration":414.516,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":15.052,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 24 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.846,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Seguridad (3): prompt injection en agentes con herramientas y cómo probar los controles.","start":4.396,"end":9.066,"terms":[{"start":22,"end":31,"say":"inyékshon"}]}],[{"text":"Tests que verifican que un agente no ejecuta acciones indebidas cuando el contenido que lee trae instrucciones inyectadas.","start":9.616,"end":15.052,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":15.852,"end":36.292,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Una inyección de prompts en un chatbot que solo conversa produce, en el peor caso, un texto inapropiado.","start":16.352,"end":21.881,"terms":[]},{"text":"La misma inyección en un agente con herramientas puede terminar en una acción: un mail enviado, un registro modificado, datos que salen de la organización.","start":22.181,"end":29.765,"terms":[]},{"text":"Y no hace falta que el atacante hable con el agente.","start":30.065,"end":32.726,"terms":[]},{"text":"Alcanza con dejar la instrucción en un lugar que el agente vaya a leer.","start":33.026,"end":36.292,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":37.092,"end":54.337,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"No existe hoy una defensa que garantice que un modelo ignore instrucciones maliciosas en su contexto.","start":37.592,"end":42.679,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lo que sí existe son controles alrededor del modelo que limitan lo que puede pasar cuando la inyección funciona.","start":42.979,"end":48.276,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta guía muestra cómo probar esos controles con tests deterministas que miran las acciones del agente y no sus palabras.","start":48.576,"end":54.337,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":55.137,"end":66.103,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":55.637,"end":57.567,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un agente con herramientas en ambiente de prueba, con herramientas falsas o en modo simulado.","start":58.117,"end":62.276,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Acceso al registro de llamadas a herramientas de cada ejecución.","start":62.826,"end":66.103,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":66.903,"end":72.224,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":67.403,"end":68.578,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":68.878,"end":72.224,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Controles y cómo se prueban","start":73.024,"end":95.776,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Controles y cómo se prueban.","start":73.524,"end":75.199,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Permisos mínimos por herramienta: el agente de soporte no tiene la herramienta de borrado disponible.","start":75.749,"end":80.523,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Aprobación humana en acciones irreversibles: la llamada queda en estado pendiente, no ejecutada.","start":81.073,"end":85.778,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Lista de destinos permitidos: un envío a un dominio no listado se rechaza.","start":86.328,"end":90.057,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Salida de herramientas tratada como dato: texto con instrucciones en un resultado no dispara acciones.","start":90.607,"end":95.776,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":96.576,"end":131.126,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":97.076,"end":97.973,"terms":[]},{"text":"Inventario de herramientas.","start":98.273,"end":99.889,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Hacé el inventario: qué herramientas tiene el agente y qué puede hacer cada una (leer, escribir, enviar).","start":100.439,"end":105.353,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Listá cada herramienta que el agente tiene disponible y clasificá lo que puede hacer en tres capacidades: leer, escribir y enviar hacia afuera.","start":105.903,"end":112.419,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sé específico.","start":112.719,"end":113.906,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"Acceso a la base de datos\" no alcanza.","start":114.206,"end":116.472,"terms":[]},{"text":"Decí qué tablas, y con qué operaciones.","start":116.772,"end":119.108,"terms":[]},{"text":"Incluí las herramientas que vienen por defecto y las que agrega cada servidor MCP conectado, que suelen ser más de las que uno recuerda.","start":119.408,"end":125.611,"terms":[]},{"text":"El inventario suele dar una primera sorpresa: herramientas que el agente tiene y que la tarea no necesita.","start":125.911,"end":131.126,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":131.926,"end":169.761,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":132.426,"end":133.323,"terms":[]},{"text":"La combinación peligrosa.","start":133.623,"end":135.355,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Buscá la combinación peligrosa: acceso a datos privados, lectura de contenido no confiable y capacidad de comunicarse hacia afuera.","start":135.905,"end":142.421,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con las tres juntas, una inyección puede sacar datos.","start":142.721,"end":145.87,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Buscá si el agente reúne tres condiciones a la vez: acceso a datos privados, lectura de contenido no confiable y capacidad de comunicarse hacia afuera.","start":146.42,"end":154.016,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cada una por separado es manejable.","start":154.316,"end":156.373,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las tres juntas permiten que una instrucción escondida en un documento le indique leer algo privado y enviarlo a un tercero.","start":156.673,"end":162.493,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si tu agente las reúne, la medida más efectiva es romper la combinación quitando una de las tres para esa tarea, antes de pensar en filtros.","start":162.793,"end":169.761,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":170.561,"end":206.979,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":171.061,"end":171.958,"terms":[]},{"text":"Fixtures con inyecciones en cada canal.","start":172.258,"end":174.304,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":8,"say":"fícschers"}]}],[{"text":"Armá fixtures con instrucciones inyectadas en cada canal que el agente lee: ticket, documento, página web, resultado de herramienta.","start":174.854,"end":181.625,"terms":[{"start":5,"end":13,"say":"fícschers"}]}],[{"text":"Armá datos de prueba con instrucciones inyectadas en todos los lugares que el agente lee: el cuerpo de un ticket, un documento adjunto, el contenido de una página web, el resultado de una herramienta.","start":182.175,"end":191.965,"terms":[]},{"text":"Usá instrucciones inofensivas pero verificables, como llamar a una herramienta marcadora que no hace nada.","start":192.265,"end":197.388,"terms":[]},{"text":"Variá el formato: texto directo, texto dentro de un comentario, instrucciones que simulan venir del sistema.","start":197.688,"end":203.251,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cada fixture corresponde a un caso de abuso del modelo de amenazas.","start":203.551,"end":206.979,"terms":[{"start":5,"end":12,"say":"fícscher"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":207.779,"end":241.492,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":208.279,"end":209.28,"terms":[]},{"text":"Aserciones sobre acciones.","start":209.58,"end":211.266,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Afirmá sobre acciones, no sobre palabras: el test pasa si la llamada a la herramienta no ocurrió, sin importar lo que el agente diga.","start":211.816,"end":218.193,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El test no evalúa lo que el agente responde.","start":218.743,"end":221.009,"terms":[]},{"text":"Evalúa si ocurrió la llamada a la herramienta prohibida.","start":221.309,"end":223.97,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un agente puede escribir \"no voy a hacer eso\" y aun así ejecutar la acción, o lo contrario.","start":224.27,"end":228.824,"terms":[]},{"text":"Registrá las llamadas a herramientas y verificá que la lista no contenga la acción prohibida, o que el destinatario esté dentro de los permitidos.","start":229.124,"end":235.93,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es una aserción clásica y determinista sobre un registro de llamadas, que no necesita un modelo juez.","start":236.23,"end":241.492,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":242.292,"end":275.391,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":242.792,"end":243.793,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cada control por separado.","start":244.093,"end":245.71,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Probá cada control por separado, con las herramientas en modo simulado.","start":246.26,"end":249.745,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Probá cada control de forma aislada, con las herramientas en modo simulado para que nada salga de verdad.","start":250.295,"end":255.348,"terms":[]},{"text":"Mínimo privilegio: la herramienta peligrosa directamente no figura entre las disponibles.","start":255.648,"end":260.156,"terms":[]},{"text":"Aprobación humana: la acción irreversible queda pendiente y no se ejecuta.","start":260.456,"end":264.173,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lista de destinos permitidos: un envío a un dominio no listado se rechaza.","start":264.473,"end":268.203,"terms":[]},{"text":"Salida de herramienta tratada como dato: un resultado con instrucciones no dispara acciones.","start":268.503,"end":273.266,"terms":[]},{"text":"Aislados, sabés cuál falló.","start":273.566,"end":275.391,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":276.191,"end":313.004,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":276.691,"end":277.657,"terms":[]},{"text":"MCP y regresión.","start":277.957,"end":279.446,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Si usás MCP: revisá las descripciones de las herramientas de terceros (también son texto que el modelo lee) y fijá versiones de los servidores.","start":279.996,"end":286.825,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sumá todo a la regresión.","start":287.125,"end":288.707,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Las descripciones de las herramientas de un servidor MCP también son texto que el modelo lee, y un servidor de terceros podría incluir instrucciones ahí.","start":289.257,"end":296.701,"terms":[]},{"text":"Revisá las descripciones antes de conectar un servidor y fijá su versión, para que no cambien sin que lo sepas.","start":297.001,"end":302.565,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sumá todos estos tests al conjunto de regresión y correlos ante cualquier cambio de herramientas, permisos o prompt.","start":302.865,"end":308.499,"terms":[]},{"text":"Como la resistencia del modelo es probabilística, repetí cada caso varias veces.","start":308.799,"end":313.004,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":313.804,"end":350.427,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":314.304,"end":315.224,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un agente de soporte lee tickets, consulta pedidos y envía mails.","start":315.774,"end":319.306,"terms":[]},{"text":"El inventario muestra que además tiene una herramienta para borrar pedidos que nunca usa.","start":319.606,"end":323.637,"terms":[]},{"text":"La quitan.","start":323.937,"end":324.857,"terms":[]},{"text":"El agente reúne las tres condiciones peligrosas, así que restringen el mail al remitente del ticket.","start":325.157,"end":329.932,"terms":[]},{"text":"Arman seis fixtures con inyecciones y corren cada uno diez veces con las herramientas simuladas.","start":330.232,"end":334.867,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":19,"say":"fícschers"}]},{"text":"En cincuenta y ocho de sesenta corridas el agente ignora la instrucción.","start":335.167,"end":338.826,"terms":[]},{"text":"En dos intenta enviar un mail a una dirección externa, y la lista de destinos lo rechaza.","start":339.126,"end":343.587,"terms":[]},{"text":"El modelo cedió dos veces y el control funcionó las dos.","start":343.887,"end":346.781,"terms":[]},{"text":"El test verifica el control, que es lo que tiene que cumplirse siempre.","start":347.081,"end":350.427,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":351.227,"end":367.547,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":351.727,"end":353.251,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Evaluar lo que el agente dice en lugar de lo que ejecuta.","start":353.801,"end":356.462,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Probar inyecciones solo en el mensaje del usuario.","start":357.012,"end":359.487,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Confiar en una instrucción del prompt como único control.","start":360.037,"end":363.024,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Conectar servidores MCP de terceros sin revisar ni fijar versión.","start":363.574,"end":367.547,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":368.347,"end":386.838,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":368.847,"end":371.114,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Hay un inventario de herramientas con leer, escribir y enviar.","start":371.664,"end":374.836,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Sabés si el agente reúne la combinación peligrosa y cómo se rompe.","start":375.386,"end":378.686,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Existen fixtures con inyecciones en cada canal de lectura.","start":379.236,"end":382.443,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":16,"say":"fícschers"}]}],[{"text":"Cada control tiene un test que verifica acciones, repetido varias veces.","start":382.993,"end":386.838,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":387.638,"end":398.644,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":388.138,"end":389.708,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"La resistencia a la inyección es probabilística: un agente que resiste nueve veces puede ceder la décima.","start":390.258,"end":395.381,"terms":[]},{"text":"Por eso las acciones sensibles se limitan con permisos.","start":395.681,"end":398.644,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":399.444,"end":413.016,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 24.","start":399.944,"end":401.63,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":401.93,"end":407.169,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 25: Modelo para pruebas de performance (1): las métricas que importan en un LLM.","start":407.719,"end":413.016,"terms":[{"start":44,"end":55,"say":"perfórmans"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d24-en","day":24,"lang":"en","title":"Security (3): prompt injection in tool-using agents and how to test the controls","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d24-en.mp4","duration":444.597,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":17.119,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 24 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.264,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Security (3): prompt injection in tool-using agents and how to test the controls.","start":4.814,"end":9.821,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tests that verify an agent doesn't execute improper actions when the content it reads carries injected instructions.","start":10.371,"end":17.119,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":17.919,"end":38.66,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"A prompt injection in a chatbot that only talks produces, at worst, inappropriate text.","start":18.419,"end":24.157,"terms":[]},{"text":"The same injection in a tool-using agent can end in an action: an email sent, a record modified, data leaving the organization.","start":24.457,"end":32.18,"terms":[]},{"text":"And the attacker doesn't need to talk to the agent.","start":32.48,"end":35.014,"terms":[]},{"text":"Leaving the instruction somewhere the agent will read is enough.","start":35.314,"end":38.66,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":39.46,"end":57.983,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"No defense today guarantees that a model will ignore malicious instructions in its context.","start":39.96,"end":45.292,"terms":[]},{"text":"What does exist are controls around the model that limit what can happen when the injection works.","start":45.592,"end":51.051,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide shows how to test those controls with deterministic tests that look at the agent's actions and not its words.","start":51.351,"end":57.983,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":58.783,"end":69.168,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":59.283,"end":60.98,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A tool-using agent in a test environment, with fake or simulated tools.","start":61.53,"end":66.212,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Access to each run's tool-call log.","start":66.762,"end":69.168,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":69.968,"end":76.265,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":70.468,"end":71.748,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":72.048,"end":76.265,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Controls and how to test them","start":77.065,"end":100.722,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Controls and how to test them.","start":77.565,"end":79.634,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Least privilege per tool: the support agent doesn't have the delete tool available.","start":80.184,"end":84.993,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Human approval for irreversible actions: the call stays pending, not executed.","start":85.543,"end":90.712,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Destination allowlist: a send to an unlisted domain is rejected.","start":91.262,"end":94.991,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tool output treated as data: text with instructions in a result triggers no actions.","start":95.541,"end":100.722,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":101.522,"end":138.615,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":102.022,"end":103.058,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tool inventory.","start":103.358,"end":104.557,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Take inventory: which tools the agent has and what each can do (read, write, send).","start":105.107,"end":110.218,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"List every tool the agent has available and classify what it can do into three capabilities: read, write and send outward.","start":110.768,"end":118.131,"terms":[]},{"text":"Be specific.","start":118.431,"end":119.641,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"Database access\" isn't enough.","start":119.941,"end":122.08,"terms":[]},{"text":"State which tables, and with which operations.","start":122.38,"end":125.262,"terms":[]},{"text":"Include the tools that come by default and the ones each connected MCP server adds, which tend to be more than you remember.","start":125.562,"end":132.867,"terms":[]},{"text":"The inventory usually brings a first surprise: tools the agent has that the task doesn't need.","start":133.167,"end":138.615,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":139.415,"end":180.93,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":139.915,"end":141.113,"terms":[]},{"text":"The dangerous combination.","start":141.413,"end":143.215,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Look for the dangerous combination: access to private data, reading untrusted content and the ability to communicate outward.","start":143.765,"end":150.827,"terms":[]},{"text":"With all three, an injection can exfiltrate data.","start":151.127,"end":154.392,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Check whether the agent meets three conditions at once: access to private data, reading untrusted content and the ability to communicate outward.","start":154.942,"end":163.095,"terms":[]},{"text":"Each one alone is manageable.","start":163.395,"end":165.383,"terms":[]},{"text":"All three together allow an instruction hidden in a document to tell it to read something private and send it to a third party.","start":165.683,"end":172.129,"terms":[]},{"text":"If your agent meets them, the most effective measure is breaking the combination by removing one of the three for that task, before thinking about filters.","start":172.429,"end":180.93,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":181.73,"end":220.04,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":182.23,"end":183.312,"terms":[]},{"text":"Fixtures with injections in every channel.","start":183.612,"end":186.03,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Build fixtures with injected instructions in every channel the agent reads: ticket, document, web page, tool result.","start":186.58,"end":193.757,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Build test data with injected instructions in every place the agent reads: the body of a ticket, an attached document, the content of a web page, a tool result.","start":194.307,"end":203.54,"terms":[]},{"text":"Use harmless but verifiable instructions, such as calling a marker tool that does nothing.","start":203.84,"end":209.056,"terms":[]},{"text":"Vary the format: direct text, text inside a comment, instructions pretending to come from the system.","start":209.356,"end":216.069,"terms":[]},{"text":"Each fixture corresponds to an abuse case from the threat model.","start":216.369,"end":220.04,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":220.84,"end":255.877,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":221.34,"end":222.353,"terms":[]},{"text":"Assertions on actions.","start":222.653,"end":224.327,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Assert on actions, not words: the test passes if the tool call didn't happen, regardless of what the agent says.","start":224.877,"end":231.416,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The test doesn't evaluate what the agent replies.","start":231.966,"end":234.999,"terms":[]},{"text":"It evaluates whether the call to the forbidden tool happened.","start":235.299,"end":238.448,"terms":[]},{"text":"An agent can write \"I won't do that\" and still execute the action, or the reverse.","start":238.748,"end":243.534,"terms":[]},{"text":"Record the tool calls and verify the list doesn't contain the forbidden action, or that the recipient is among the allowed ones.","start":243.834,"end":250.454,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's a classic, deterministic assertion over a call log, needing no judge model.","start":250.754,"end":255.877,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":256.677,"end":292.609,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":257.177,"end":258.306,"terms":[]},{"text":"Each control separately.","start":258.606,"end":260.303,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Test each control separately, with tools in simulated mode.","start":260.853,"end":264.455,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Test each control in isolation, with tools in simulated mode so nothing really goes out.","start":265.005,"end":270.36,"terms":[]},{"text":"Least privilege: the dangerous tool simply isn't among the available ones.","start":270.66,"end":275.144,"terms":[]},{"text":"Human approval: the irreversible action stays pending and isn't executed.","start":275.444,"end":280.474,"terms":[]},{"text":"Destination allowlist: a send to an unlisted domain is rejected.","start":280.774,"end":284.503,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tool output treated as data: a result with instructions triggers no actions.","start":284.803,"end":289.775,"terms":[]},{"text":"Isolated, you know which one failed.","start":290.075,"end":292.609,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":293.409,"end":331.673,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":293.909,"end":295.095,"terms":[]},{"text":"MCP and regression.","start":295.395,"end":297.279,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"With MCP: review third-party tool descriptions (they're also text the model reads) and pin server versions.","start":297.829,"end":304.31,"terms":[]},{"text":"Add everything to regression.","start":304.61,"end":306.284,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tool descriptions from an MCP server are also text the model reads, and a third-party server could include instructions there.","start":306.834,"end":314.14,"terms":[]},{"text":"Review descriptions before connecting a server and pin its version, so they don't change without you knowing.","start":314.44,"end":320.421,"terms":[]},{"text":"Add all these tests to the regression set and run them on any change to tools, permissions or prompt.","start":320.721,"end":326.32,"terms":[]},{"text":"Because the model's resistance is probabilistic, repeat each case several times.","start":326.62,"end":331.673,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":332.473,"end":373.937,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":332.973,"end":334.079,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A support agent reads tickets, queries orders and sends emails.","start":334.629,"end":338.834,"terms":[]},{"text":"The inventory shows it also has a tool for deleting orders that it never uses.","start":339.134,"end":344.257,"terms":[]},{"text":"They remove it.","start":344.557,"end":345.511,"terms":[]},{"text":"The agent meets the three dangerous conditions, so they restrict email to the ticket's sender.","start":345.811,"end":350.864,"terms":[]},{"text":"They build six fixtures with injections and run each ten times with simulated tools.","start":351.164,"end":356.078,"terms":[]},{"text":"In fifty-eight of sixty runs the agent ignores the instruction.","start":356.378,"end":360.049,"terms":[]},{"text":"In two it tries to send an email to an external address, and the allowlist rejects it.","start":360.349,"end":365.518,"terms":[]},{"text":"The model yielded twice and the control worked both times.","start":365.818,"end":369.281,"terms":[]},{"text":"The test verifies the control, which is what must hold every time.","start":369.581,"end":373.937,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":374.737,"end":392.764,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":375.237,"end":376.9,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Evaluating what the agent says instead of what it executes.","start":377.45,"end":381.168,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Testing injections only in the user message.","start":381.718,"end":384.495,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Relying on a prompt instruction as the only control.","start":385.045,"end":387.997,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Connecting third-party MCP servers without reviewing or pinning versions.","start":388.547,"end":392.764,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":393.564,"end":413.82,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":394.064,"end":396.307,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"There is a tool inventory with read, write and send.","start":396.857,"end":400.285,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"You know whether the agent has the dangerous combination and how it's broken.","start":400.835,"end":404.727,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Fixtures with injections exist for every reading channel.","start":405.277,"end":408.612,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each control has a test verifying actions, repeated several times.","start":409.162,"end":413.82,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":414.62,"end":427.263,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":415.12,"end":417.015,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Injection resistance is probabilistic: an agent that resists nine times can yield on the tenth.","start":417.565,"end":423.523,"terms":[]},{"text":"That's why sensitive actions are limited with permissions.","start":423.823,"end":427.263,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":428.063,"end":443.097,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 24.","start":428.563,"end":430.318,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":430.618,"end":436.6,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 25: A model for performance testing (1): the metrics that matter for an LLM.","start":437.15,"end":443.097,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d25-es","day":25,"lang":"es","title":"Modelo para pruebas de performance (1): las métricas que importan en un LLM","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d25-es.mp4","duration":426.42,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":15.54,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 25 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.602,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Modelo para pruebas de performance (1): las métricas que importan en un LLM.","start":4.152,"end":8.288,"terms":[{"start":23,"end":34,"say":"perfórmans"}]}],[{"text":"El modelo de performance de una feature con LLM: perfil de carga, métricas propias de generación de texto y objetivos por percentil.","start":8.838,"end":15.54,"terms":[{"start":13,"end":24,"say":"perfórmans"},{"start":32,"end":39,"say":"fícher"}]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":16.34,"end":36.071,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Quien viene de pruebas de performance tradicionales llega a un endpoint con un modelo y aplica lo que sabe: requests por segundo, latencia promedio y tasa de errores.","start":16.84,"end":24.819,"terms":[{"start":26,"end":37,"say":"perfórmans"},{"start":108,"end":116,"say":"ricuésts"}]},{"text":"Los números salen, y describen mal el sistema.","start":25.119,"end":27.71,"terms":[]},{"text":"Dos requests al mismo endpoint pueden generar veinte tokens o dos mil, y tardar medio segundo o cuarenta.","start":28.01,"end":33.319,"terms":[{"start":4,"end":12,"say":"ricuésts"}]},{"text":"Promediarlos mezcla cosas que no se parecen.","start":33.619,"end":36.071,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":36.871,"end":56.033,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"La unidad de trabajo de un modelo es el token.","start":37.371,"end":39.788,"terms":[]},{"text":"La experiencia del usuario depende de cuándo llega el primero y de qué tan fluido llega el resto.","start":40.088,"end":44.573,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta guía presenta las métricas que describen eso, cómo definir objetivos por percentil y cómo medir una línea de base con un solo usuario.","start":44.873,"end":51.795,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sin esa base, cualquier prueba de carga posterior es un número sin referencia.","start":52.095,"end":56.033,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":56.833,"end":65.384,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":57.333,"end":59.263,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un endpoint compatible con la API de OpenAI (Ollama local sirve).","start":59.813,"end":63.148,"terms":[{"start":37,"end":43,"say":"open ei ai"},{"start":45,"end":51,"say":"olama"}]}],[{"text":"Python 3.10+.","start":63.698,"end":65.384,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":6,"say":"páiton"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":66.184,"end":71.505,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":66.684,"end":67.859,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":68.159,"end":71.505,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":72.305,"end":74.944,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":72.805,"end":74.944,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Las métricas","start":75.744,"end":114.449,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Las métricas.","start":76.244,"end":77.315,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"TTFT (time to first token): tiempo hasta el primer token.","start":77.865,"end":81.188,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es lo que el usuario siente como \"respondió\".","start":81.488,"end":83.615,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"ITL (inter-token latency): tiempo entre tokens.","start":84.165,"end":86.978,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":3,"say":"i te ele"},{"start":17,"end":24,"say":"léitensi"}]},{"text":"Mide la fluidez del texto en streaming.","start":87.278,"end":89.323,"terms":[{"start":29,"end":38,"say":"estrímin"}]}],[{"text":"Output tokens/s: velocidad de generación.","start":89.873,"end":92.767,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":6,"say":"áutput"}]},{"text":"Es la capacidad real del sistema.","start":93.067,"end":94.881,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Latencia total p95 y p99: la cola es la que genera quejas.","start":95.431,"end":99.648,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tasa de error (429, 5xx, timeouts): límites de cuota y saturación.","start":100.198,"end":105.506,"terms":[{"start":25,"end":33,"say":"táimauts"}]}],[{"text":"Goodput: porcentaje de requests que cumple todos los objetivos a la vez.","start":106.056,"end":109.693,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":7,"say":"gúdput"},{"start":23,"end":31,"say":"ricuésts"}]}],[{"text":"Costo por request: tokens de entrada y salida.","start":110.243,"end":112.579,"terms":[{"start":10,"end":17,"say":"ricuést"}]},{"text":"La carga también se paga.","start":112.879,"end":114.449,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":115.249,"end":154.767,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":115.749,"end":116.646,"terms":[]},{"text":"Por qué las métricas clásicas engañan.","start":116.946,"end":119.096,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Entendé por qué las métricas clásicas engañan: dos requests al mismo endpoint pueden generar 20 o 2.000 tokens.","start":119.646,"end":125.651,"terms":[{"start":51,"end":59,"say":"ricuésts"}]},{"text":"Requests por segundo y latencia promedio mezclan cosas distintas.","start":125.951,"end":129.344,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":8,"say":"ricuésts"}]}],[{"text":"En una API tradicional, las requests al mismo endpoint cuestan más o menos lo mismo.","start":129.894,"end":134.32,"terms":[{"start":28,"end":36,"say":"ricuésts"}]},{"text":"Con un modelo, el costo de cada request depende de cuántos tokens entran y, sobre todo, de cuántos salen, porque la generación es secuencial.","start":134.62,"end":141.635,"terms":[{"start":32,"end":39,"say":"ricuést"}]},{"text":"Diez requests por segundo con respuestas cortas y con respuestas largas son dos cargas completamente distintas para el mismo servidor.","start":141.935,"end":148.102,"terms":[{"start":5,"end":13,"say":"ricuésts"}]},{"text":"Si reportás solo requests por segundo y latencia promedio, dos pruebas con igual resultado pueden haber medido cosas diferentes.","start":148.402,"end":154.767,"terms":[{"start":17,"end":25,"say":"ricuésts"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":155.567,"end":192.093,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":156.067,"end":156.964,"terms":[]},{"text":"El perfil de carga.","start":157.264,"end":158.497,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Definí el perfil de carga: tamaños de prompt, largo de salida esperado, con o sin streaming, y concurrencia en hora pico.","start":159.047,"end":165.168,"terms":[{"start":82,"end":91,"say":"estrímin"}]}],[{"text":"Antes de medir, describí la carga real.","start":165.718,"end":167.915,"terms":[]},{"text":"Qué tamaños de prompt llegan y en qué proporción.","start":168.215,"end":170.83,"terms":[]},{"text":"Qué largo de respuesta se espera.","start":171.13,"end":172.874,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si se usa streaming o se espera la respuesta entera.","start":173.174,"end":175.673,"terms":[{"start":10,"end":19,"say":"estrímin"}]},{"text":"Cuál es la concurrencia pico.","start":175.973,"end":177.485,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sacá estos datos de los registros de producción si existen, o estimalos con el equipo de producto si no.","start":177.785,"end":183.117,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un perfil con tres o cuatro tipos de request y sus proporciones es suficiente.","start":183.417,"end":187.401,"terms":[{"start":37,"end":44,"say":"ricuést"}]},{"text":"Sin perfil vas a probar con un prompt cualquiera y obtener números que no predicen nada.","start":187.701,"end":192.093,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":192.893,"end":228.858,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":193.393,"end":194.289,"terms":[]},{"text":"Objetivos por percentil.","start":194.589,"end":196.24,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Fijá objetivos por percentil, no por promedio: por ejemplo TTFT p95, latencia total p95 y tasa de error máxima.","start":196.79,"end":204.223,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un promedio esconde la cola, y la cola es donde están los usuarios que se quejan.","start":204.773,"end":208.712,"terms":[]},{"text":"Definí los objetivos como percentiles: por ejemplo, tiempo al primer token en el percentil noventa y cinco, latencia total en el percentil noventa y cinco y una tasa de error máxima.","start":209.012,"end":218.117,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los valores salen de la experiencia que el producto necesita, no de lo que el sistema da hoy.","start":218.417,"end":222.82,"terms":[]},{"text":"Escribilos antes de medir.","start":223.12,"end":224.69,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si los definís después, se van a parecer sospechosamente a lo que mediste.","start":224.99,"end":228.858,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":229.658,"end":265.229,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":230.158,"end":231.159,"terms":[]},{"text":"La línea de base con un usuario.","start":231.459,"end":233.424,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Medí la línea base con un solo usuario usando el script.","start":233.974,"end":236.856,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si con un usuario ya no cumplís, no hay prueba de carga que hacer todavía.","start":237.156,"end":240.99,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Corré el script con un solo usuario y sin concurrencia, varias veces por cada tipo de request del perfil.","start":241.54,"end":246.732,"terms":[{"start":86,"end":93,"say":"ricuést"}]},{"text":"Eso te da el mejor caso: lo que el sistema hace cuando no compite con nadie.","start":247.032,"end":250.878,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si con un usuario no cumplís los objetivos, no hay prueba de carga que correr todavía, porque el problema está en el modelo elegido, en el tamaño del prompt o en la infraestructura, y la carga solo lo va a empeorar.","start":251.178,"end":261.142,"terms":[]},{"text":"La línea de base es además la referencia para leer todo lo que viene después.","start":261.442,"end":265.229,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":266.029,"end":297.443,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":266.529,"end":267.53,"terms":[]},{"text":"Goodput.","start":267.83,"end":268.576,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":7,"say":"gúdput"}]}],[{"text":"Sumá goodput: el porcentaje de requests que cumple todos los objetivos a la vez.","start":269.126,"end":273.146,"terms":[{"start":5,"end":12,"say":"gúdput"},{"start":31,"end":39,"say":"ricuésts"}]}],[{"text":"El goodput es el porcentaje de requests que cumplen todos los objetivos a la vez: primer token a tiempo, latencia total dentro del límite y sin error.","start":273.696,"end":280.954,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":10,"say":"gúdput"},{"start":31,"end":39,"say":"ricuésts"}]},{"text":"Importa porque las métricas por separado pueden verse bien mientras la experiencia es mala.","start":281.254,"end":285.483,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un sistema puede sostener muchas requests por segundo y tener un goodput bajo si buena parte de ellas llega tarde.","start":285.783,"end":291.208,"terms":[{"start":33,"end":41,"say":"ricuésts"},{"start":65,"end":72,"say":"gúdput"}]},{"text":"Es el número que mejor resume si la carga se está atendiendo de forma aceptable, y sirve para comparar configuraciones.","start":291.508,"end":297.443,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":298.243,"end":331.192,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":298.743,"end":299.709,"terms":[]},{"text":"El costo por request.","start":300.009,"end":301.324,"terms":[{"start":13,"end":20,"say":"ricuést"}]}],[{"text":"Anotá costo por request.","start":301.874,"end":303.397,"terms":[{"start":16,"end":23,"say":"ricuést"}]},{"text":"Una prueba de carga contra una API paga consume presupuesto real.","start":303.697,"end":307.137,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Registrá los tokens de entrada y de salida de cada request y convertilos en costo.","start":307.687,"end":311.555,"terms":[{"start":51,"end":58,"say":"ricuést"}]},{"text":"Tiene dos usos.","start":311.855,"end":313.054,"terms":[]},{"text":"El primero es de producto: saber cuánto cuesta atender a un usuario con la carga esperada.","start":313.354,"end":317.826,"terms":[]},{"text":"El segundo es práctico.","start":318.126,"end":319.696,"terms":[]},{"text":"Una prueba de carga contra una API paga consume presupuesto real, y una prueba mal dimensionada puede gastar en una hora lo previsto para un mes.","start":319.996,"end":327.023,"terms":[]},{"text":"Estimá el costo de la prueba antes de correrla y usá una clave con tope de gasto.","start":327.323,"end":331.192,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":331.992,"end":365.605,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":332.492,"end":333.411,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Con valores ilustrativos: un equipo define tres tipos de request para su asistente, corta, media y larga, en proporción de sesenta, treinta y diez.","start":333.961,"end":341.673,"terms":[{"start":57,"end":64,"say":"ricuést"}]},{"text":"Los objetivos son primer token en menos de un segundo y medio y latencia total bajo doce segundos, ambos en el percentil noventa y cinco.","start":341.973,"end":348.559,"terms":[]},{"text":"La línea de base con un usuario muestra que las cortas y las medias cumplen.","start":348.859,"end":352.483,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las largas tardan dieciocho segundos en total, aunque el primer token llega a tiempo.","start":352.783,"end":357.105,"terms":[]},{"text":"Antes de cualquier prueba de carga ya hay un hallazgo.","start":357.405,"end":360.206,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lo discuten con producto y acuerdan limitar el largo de esas respuestas, lo que las deja en once segundos.","start":360.506,"end":365.605,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":366.405,"end":382.249,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":366.905,"end":368.429,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Reportar solo requests por segundo y latencia promedio.","start":368.979,"end":371.965,"terms":[{"start":14,"end":22,"say":"ricuésts"}]}],[{"text":"Probar con un único prompt que no representa la carga real.","start":372.515,"end":375.594,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Fijar los objetivos después de ver las mediciones.","start":376.144,"end":378.713,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Lanzar una prueba de carga sin estimar cuánto va a costar.","start":379.263,"end":382.249,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":383.049,"end":399.95,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":383.549,"end":385.816,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El perfil de carga tiene tipos de request y proporciones.","start":386.366,"end":389.317,"terms":[{"start":34,"end":41,"say":"ricuést"}]}],[{"text":"Los objetivos están escritos por percentil antes de medir.","start":389.867,"end":393.005,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Hay una línea de base con un usuario para cada tipo de request.","start":393.555,"end":396.878,"terms":[{"start":55,"end":62,"say":"ricuést"}]}],[{"text":"Cada medición incluye tokens y costo por request.","start":397.428,"end":399.95,"terms":[{"start":41,"end":48,"say":"ricuést"}]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":400.75,"end":410.769,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":401.25,"end":402.82,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Los asistentes proponen por defecto métricas de API clásica (RPS, promedio).","start":403.37,"end":407.61,"terms":[]},{"text":"Hay que pedir explícitamente métricas de generación.","start":407.91,"end":410.769,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":411.569,"end":424.92,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 25.","start":412.069,"end":413.72,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":414.02,"end":419.259,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 26: Performance (2): prueba de carga a un endpoint LLM con k6.","start":419.809,"end":424.92,"terms":[{"start":21,"end":32,"say":"perfórmans"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d25-en","day":25,"lang":"en","title":"A model for performance testing (1): the metrics that matter for an LLM","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d25-en.mp4","duration":474.647,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":16.782,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 25 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.357,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A model for performance testing (1): the metrics that matter for an LLM.","start":4.907,"end":9.24,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The performance model of an LLM feature: workload profile, text-generation-specific metrics and percentile targets.","start":9.79,"end":16.782,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":17.582,"end":40.494,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Someone coming from traditional performance testing arrives at an endpoint with a model and applies what they know: requests per second, average latency and error rate.","start":18.082,"end":27.535,"terms":[]},{"text":"The numbers come out, and they describe the system poorly.","start":27.835,"end":31.147,"terms":[]},{"text":"Two requests to the same endpoint can generate twenty tokens or two thousand, and take half a second or forty.","start":31.447,"end":37.301,"terms":[]},{"text":"Averaging them blends things that aren't alike.","start":37.601,"end":40.494,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":41.294,"end":62.813,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"A model's unit of work is the token.","start":41.794,"end":44.2,"terms":[]},{"text":"The user's experience depends on when the first one arrives and on how smoothly the rest follow.","start":44.5,"end":50.261,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide introduces the metrics that describe that, how to define targets per percentile and how to measure a single-user baseline.","start":50.561,"end":58.134,"terms":[]},{"text":"Without that baseline, any later load test is a number with no reference.","start":58.434,"end":62.813,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":63.613,"end":72.861,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":64.113,"end":65.811,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"An OpenAI-compatible endpoint (local Ollama works).","start":66.361,"end":69.893,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Python 3.10+.","start":70.443,"end":72.861,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":73.661,"end":79.957,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":74.161,"end":75.44,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":75.74,"end":79.957,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":80.757,"end":83.524,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":81.257,"end":83.524,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The metrics","start":84.324,"end":128.613,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The metrics.","start":84.824,"end":85.883,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"TTFT (time to first token): time until the first token.","start":86.433,"end":90.43,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's what the user feels as \"it answered\".","start":90.73,"end":93.228,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"ITL (inter-token latency): time between tokens.","start":93.778,"end":97.252,"terms":[]},{"text":"It measures how smooth streaming text is.","start":97.552,"end":100.307,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Output tokens/s: generation speed.","start":100.857,"end":104.017,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's the system's real capacity.","start":104.317,"end":106.607,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Total latency p95 and p99: the tail is what generates complaints.","start":107.157,"end":112.372,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Error rate (429, 5xx, timeouts): quota limits and saturation.","start":112.922,"end":118.173,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Goodput: percentage of requests meeting all targets at once.","start":118.723,"end":122.754,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":7,"say":"good put"}]}],[{"text":"Cost per request: input and output tokens.","start":123.304,"end":126.662,"terms":[]},{"text":"Load is paid for too.","start":126.962,"end":128.613,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":129.413,"end":173.935,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":129.913,"end":130.949,"terms":[]},{"text":"Why classic metrics mislead.","start":131.249,"end":133.272,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Understand why classic metrics mislead: two requests to the same endpoint can generate 20 or 2,000 tokens.","start":133.822,"end":140.814,"terms":[]},{"text":"Requests per second and average latency blend different things.","start":141.114,"end":145.226,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"In a traditional API, requests to the same endpoint cost roughly the same.","start":145.776,"end":150.736,"terms":[]},{"text":"With a model, each request's cost depends on how many tokens go in and, above all, how many come out, because generation is sequential.","start":151.036,"end":159.514,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ten requests per second with short answers and with long answers are two completely different loads for the same server.","start":159.814,"end":166.562,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you report only requests per second and average latency, two tests with the same result may have measured different things.","start":166.862,"end":173.935,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":174.735,"end":216.427,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":175.235,"end":176.434,"terms":[]},{"text":"The workload profile.","start":176.734,"end":178.35,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Define the workload profile: prompt sizes, expected output length, streaming or not, and peak concurrency.","start":178.9,"end":186.031,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Before measuring, describe the real load.","start":186.581,"end":189.231,"terms":[]},{"text":"Which prompt sizes arrive and in what proportion.","start":189.531,"end":192.552,"terms":[]},{"text":"What answer length is expected.","start":192.852,"end":194.968,"terms":[]},{"text":"Whether streaming is used or the full answer is awaited.","start":195.268,"end":198.638,"terms":[]},{"text":"What peak concurrency is.","start":198.938,"end":200.705,"terms":[]},{"text":"Take this from production logs if they exist, or estimate it with the product team if not.","start":201.005,"end":206.232,"terms":[]},{"text":"A profile with three or four request types and their proportions is enough.","start":206.532,"end":210.668,"terms":[]},{"text":"Without a profile you'll test with an arbitrary prompt and get numbers that predict nothing.","start":210.968,"end":216.427,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":217.227,"end":256.618,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":217.727,"end":218.809,"terms":[]},{"text":"Targets per percentile.","start":219.109,"end":220.923,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Set targets per percentile, not average: for example TTFT p95, total latency p95 and a maximum error rate.","start":221.473,"end":229.858,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"An average hides the tail, and the tail is where the complaining users are.","start":230.408,"end":234.614,"terms":[]},{"text":"Define targets as percentiles: for example, time to first token at the ninety-fifth percentile, total latency at the ninety-fifth percentile and a maximum error rate.","start":234.914,"end":244.274,"terms":[]},{"text":"The values come from the experience the product needs, not from what the system delivers today.","start":244.574,"end":250.01,"terms":[]},{"text":"Write them before measuring.","start":250.31,"end":251.973,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you define them afterwards, they'll look suspiciously like what you measured.","start":252.273,"end":256.618,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":257.418,"end":295.949,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":257.918,"end":258.93,"terms":[]},{"text":"The single-user baseline.","start":259.23,"end":261.091,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Measure the single-user baseline with the script.","start":261.641,"end":264.546,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you miss targets with one user, there's no load test to run yet.","start":264.846,"end":268.749,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Run the script with a single user and no concurrency, several times for each request type in the profile.","start":269.299,"end":275.595,"terms":[]},{"text":"That gives you the best case: what the system does when competing with nobody.","start":275.895,"end":280.332,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you miss targets with one user, there's no load test to run yet, because the problem lies in the chosen model, the prompt size or the infrastructure, and load will only make it worse.","start":280.632,"end":291.583,"terms":[]},{"text":"The baseline is also the reference for reading everything that comes next.","start":291.883,"end":295.949,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":296.749,"end":332.215,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":297.249,"end":298.378,"terms":[]},{"text":"Goodput.","start":298.678,"end":299.598,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":7,"say":"good put"}]}],[{"text":"Add goodput: the percentage of requests meeting all targets at once.","start":300.148,"end":304.678,"terms":[{"start":4,"end":11,"say":"good put"}]}],[{"text":"Goodput is the percentage of requests meeting all targets at once: first token on time, total latency within the limit and no error.","start":305.228,"end":313.137,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":7,"say":"good put"}]},{"text":"It matters because the separate metrics can look fine while the experience is poor.","start":313.437,"end":318.328,"terms":[]},{"text":"A system can sustain many requests per second and have low goodput if a good share of them arrive late.","start":318.628,"end":324.598,"terms":[{"start":59,"end":66,"say":"good put"}]},{"text":"It's the number that best sums up whether load is being served acceptably, and it's useful for comparing configurations.","start":324.898,"end":332.215,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":333.015,"end":369.551,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":333.515,"end":334.702,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cost per request.","start":335.002,"end":336.479,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Record cost per request.","start":337.029,"end":339.005,"terms":[]},{"text":"A load test against a paid API consumes real budget.","start":339.305,"end":342.953,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Record each request's input and output tokens and convert them into cost.","start":343.503,"end":347.802,"terms":[]},{"text":"It has two uses.","start":348.102,"end":349.474,"terms":[]},{"text":"The first is product-related: knowing what it costs to serve a user at the expected load.","start":349.774,"end":355.036,"terms":[]},{"text":"The second is practical.","start":355.336,"end":356.999,"terms":[]},{"text":"A load test against a paid API consumes real budget, and a badly sized test can spend in an hour what was planned for a month.","start":357.299,"end":365.127,"terms":[]},{"text":"Estimate the test's cost before running it and use a key with a spending cap.","start":365.427,"end":369.551,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":370.351,"end":408.748,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":370.851,"end":371.957,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"With illustrative values: a team defines three request types for its assistant, short, medium and long, in a sixty, thirty, ten proportion.","start":372.507,"end":381.344,"terms":[]},{"text":"The targets are first token in under a second and a half and total latency under twelve seconds, both at the ninety-fifth percentile.","start":381.644,"end":389.379,"terms":[]},{"text":"The single-user baseline shows the short and medium ones comply.","start":389.679,"end":393.548,"terms":[]},{"text":"The long ones take eighteen seconds in total, even though the first token arrives on time.","start":393.848,"end":399.098,"terms":[]},{"text":"Before any load test there's already a finding.","start":399.398,"end":402.292,"terms":[]},{"text":"They discuss it with product and agree to limit the length of those answers, which brings them to eleven seconds.","start":402.592,"end":408.748,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":409.548,"end":427.203,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":410.048,"end":411.711,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Reporting only requests per second and average latency.","start":412.261,"end":415.874,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Testing with a single prompt that doesn't represent the real load.","start":416.424,"end":420.002,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Setting targets after seeing the measurements.","start":420.552,"end":422.97,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Launching a load test without estimating what it will cost.","start":423.52,"end":427.203,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":428.003,"end":445.925,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":428.503,"end":430.746,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The workload profile has request types and proportions.","start":431.296,"end":434.631,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Targets are written per percentile before measuring.","start":435.181,"end":438.086,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"There is a single-user baseline for each request type.","start":438.636,"end":441.657,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each measurement includes tokens and cost per request.","start":442.207,"end":445.925,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":446.725,"end":457.359,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":447.225,"end":449.12,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Assistants default to classic API metrics (RPS, averages).","start":449.67,"end":453.922,"terms":[]},{"text":"You have to explicitly ask for generation metrics.","start":454.222,"end":457.359,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":458.159,"end":473.147,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 25.","start":458.659,"end":460.462,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":460.762,"end":466.743,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 26: Performance (2): load testing an LLM endpoint with k6.","start":467.293,"end":473.147,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d26-es","day":26,"lang":"es","title":"Performance (2): prueba de carga a un endpoint LLM con k6","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d26-es.mp4","duration":410.813,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":15.342,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 26 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.73,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Performance (2): prueba de carga a un endpoint LLM con k6.","start":4.28,"end":7.859,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":11,"say":"perfórmans"}]}],[{"text":"Una prueba de carga escalonada contra un endpoint LLM, con métricas de tokens y goodput, para encontrar el punto donde el sistema deja de escalar.","start":8.409,"end":15.342,"terms":[{"start":80,"end":87,"say":"gúdput"}]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":16.142,"end":30.949,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Con la línea de base y los objetivos de ayer ya podés hacer la pregunta que le importa a quien opera el sistema: cuántos usuarios soporta antes de que la experiencia se degrade.","start":16.642,"end":24.517,"terms":[]},{"text":"La respuesta es una curva con un punto de quiebre, y encontrarlo requiere cargar de forma gradual y mirar las métricas correctas.","start":24.817,"end":30.949,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":31.749,"end":52.932,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"k6 sirve bien para esto.","start":32.249,"end":33.959,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los scripts son código, corren en local y en CI, y los umbrales convierten la prueba en un criterio de pasa o falla.","start":34.259,"end":39.799,"terms":[{"start":45,"end":47,"say":"ci ai"}]},{"text":"Pero una prueba de carga contra un modelo tiene trampas propias: el caché que maquilla los resultados, los límites de cuota del proveedor que parecen saturación y los timeouts por defecto que cortan respuestas largas.","start":40.099,"end":50.121,"terms":[{"start":167,"end":175,"say":"táimauts"}]},{"text":"Esta guía arma la prueba evitando esas tres.","start":50.421,"end":52.932,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":53.732,"end":74.765,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":54.232,"end":56.162,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"k6 instalado.","start":56.712,"end":57.991,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un endpoint compatible con OpenAI y los objetivos del día 25.","start":58.541,"end":62.062,"terms":[{"start":27,"end":33,"say":"open ei ai"}]}],[{"text":"Presupuesto aprobado si la API es paga: limitá max_tokens, duración y usuarios.","start":62.612,"end":67.119,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"k6 no mide streaming de forma nativa: para TTFT bajo carga hace falta una extensión de SSE o una herramienta específica para LLM.","start":67.669,"end":74.765,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":20,"say":"estrímin"},{"start":87,"end":90,"say":"ese ese e"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":75.565,"end":80.887,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":76.065,"end":77.241,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":77.541,"end":80.887,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":81.687,"end":83.815,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"El mismo archivo sigue acá.","start":82.187,"end":83.815,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":84.615,"end":87.254,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":85.115,"end":87.254,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":88.054,"end":123.543,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":88.554,"end":89.45,"terms":[]},{"text":"Umbrales desde los objetivos.","start":89.75,"end":91.471,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Partí de la línea base del día 25 y de objetivos escritos.","start":92.021,"end":95.611,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los thresholds del script salen de ahí.","start":95.911,"end":97.992,"terms":[{"start":4,"end":14,"say":"trésholds"}]}],[{"text":"Los umbrales del script son los objetivos que escribiste ayer, traducidos a código: el percentil noventa y cinco del tiempo al primer token, el de la latencia total y la tasa máxima de errores.","start":98.542,"end":108.135,"terms":[]},{"text":"No los inventes al escribir el script ni aceptes los que traiga un ejemplo.","start":108.435,"end":112.083,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un umbral sin origen es decoración: nadie sabe qué significa que falle.","start":112.383,"end":116.298,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con los objetivos del producto como umbrales, un resultado en rojo quiere decir que los usuarios van a tener una experiencia peor que la acordada.","start":116.598,"end":123.543,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":124.343,"end":159.996,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":124.843,"end":125.74,"terms":[]},{"text":"Una mezcla de prompts realista.","start":126.04,"end":127.935,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Usá una mezcla de prompts parecida a la real.","start":128.485,"end":131.065,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con un solo prompt repetido, el caché te da números que producción no va a ver.","start":131.365,"end":135.129,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cargá los prompts desde un archivo con la mezcla del perfil de carga: tipos y proporciones.","start":135.679,"end":140.198,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si repetís un único prompt, pasan dos cosas.","start":140.498,"end":143.195,"terms":[]},{"text":"El caché del proveedor o de tu propia aplicación responde mucho más rápido que en uso real, y los números salen mejores de lo que producción va a ver.","start":143.495,"end":150.521,"terms":[]},{"text":"Además, todas las respuestas tienen el mismo largo, y perdés la variabilidad que estresa al sistema.","start":150.821,"end":155.572,"terms":[]},{"text":"Usá suficientes prompts distintos como para que no se repitan durante la prueba.","start":155.872,"end":159.996,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":160.796,"end":195.045,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":161.296,"end":162.193,"terms":[]},{"text":"Carga por etapas.","start":162.493,"end":163.657,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cargá por etapas: subís, sostenés, subís de nuevo.","start":164.207,"end":167.39,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las mesetas son donde se lee el comportamiento.","start":167.69,"end":170.143,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Subí la carga en escalones: rampa hasta un nivel, mantenelo unos minutos, rampa hasta el siguiente.","start":170.693,"end":175.723,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las mesetas son donde se lee el comportamiento, porque el sistema tuvo tiempo de estabilizarse.","start":176.023,"end":180.507,"terms":[]},{"text":"Durante las rampas, los números mezclan dos estados.","start":180.807,"end":183.793,"terms":[]},{"text":"Mantené cada meseta el tiempo suficiente como para completar varias veces la request más larga del perfil.","start":184.093,"end":189.297,"terms":[{"start":77,"end":84,"say":"ricuést"}]},{"text":"Si una respuesta larga tarda cuarenta segundos, una meseta de un minuto apenas alcanza a mostrar una ronda.","start":189.597,"end":195.045,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":195.845,"end":231.8,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":196.345,"end":197.346,"terms":[]},{"text":"Encontrar la rodilla.","start":197.646,"end":198.949,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Buscá la rodilla: el punto donde los tokens por segundo totales dejan de crecer y la latencia empieza a subir.","start":199.499,"end":204.83,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esa es tu capacidad.","start":205.13,"end":206.503,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Graficá para cada meseta los tokens totales por segundo y la latencia.","start":207.053,"end":210.782,"terms":[]},{"text":"Al principio, más usuarios producen más tokens por segundo con una latencia estable.","start":211.082,"end":215.288,"terms":[]},{"text":"En algún punto, los tokens por segundo dejan de crecer y la latencia empieza a subir.","start":215.588,"end":219.956,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esa es la rodilla, y marca la capacidad real del sistema.","start":220.256,"end":223.312,"terms":[]},{"text":"Más allá de ese punto, cada usuario adicional solo hace esperar más a los demás.","start":223.612,"end":227.724,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tu capacidad utilizable queda un poco por debajo, con margen para picos.","start":228.024,"end":231.8,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":232.6,"end":271.469,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":233.1,"end":234.101,"terms":[]},{"text":"Separar los errores.","start":234.401,"end":235.727,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Separá los errores: 429 es límite de cuota del proveedor; 5xx y timeouts son saturación.","start":236.277,"end":242.596,"terms":[{"start":64,"end":72,"say":"táimauts"}]},{"text":"Se arreglan distinto.","start":242.896,"end":244.187,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"No todos los errores significan lo mismo.","start":244.737,"end":246.981,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un 429 es el límite de cuota del proveedor: llegaste al máximo de requests o de tokens por minuto que tu cuenta permite, y se resuelve pidiendo más cuota o agregando una cola con reintentos.","start":247.281,"end":257.094,"terms":[{"start":66,"end":74,"say":"ricuésts"}]},{"text":"Un 5xx o un timeout es saturación, de tu infraestructura o de la del proveedor, y se resuelve con capacidad o reduciendo el trabajo por request.","start":257.394,"end":265.256,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":19,"say":"táimaut"},{"start":136,"end":143,"say":"ricuést"}]},{"text":"Contalos por separado en el script.","start":265.556,"end":267.475,"terms":[]},{"text":"Mezclados en una sola tasa de error, llevan a la solución equivocada.","start":267.775,"end":271.469,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":272.269,"end":306.504,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":272.769,"end":273.735,"terms":[]},{"text":"Umbrales como criterio y ejecución programada.","start":274.035,"end":276.674,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Dejá los thresholds como criterio de pasa o falla y corré la prueba de forma programada, no en cada PR.","start":277.224,"end":282.59,"terms":[{"start":9,"end":19,"say":"trésholds"},{"start":100,"end":102,"say":"pe erre"}]}],[{"text":"Dejá los umbrales como criterio automático de pasa o falla, así la prueba puede correr sin que alguien interprete gráficos cada vez.","start":283.14,"end":289.598,"terms":[]},{"text":"Programala de forma periódica o antes de cada release, no en cada pull request: es lenta, cuesta dinero si usás una API paga y necesita un entorno estable para ser comparable.","start":289.898,"end":298.782,"terms":[{"start":46,"end":53,"say":"rilís"},{"start":66,"end":78,"say":"pul ricuést"}]},{"text":"Guardá el resumen de cada corrida.","start":299.082,"end":300.896,"terms":[]},{"text":"La tendencia de la rodilla a lo largo del tiempo te avisa de una degradación antes de que se convierta en un incidente.","start":301.196,"end":306.504,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":307.304,"end":347.444,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":307.804,"end":308.724,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Con cifras ilustrativas: un equipo carga su asistente en mesetas de cinco, diez, veinte y cuarenta usuarios, tres minutos cada una, con cincuenta prompts distintos.","start":309.274,"end":317.845,"terms":[]},{"text":"Hasta veinte usuarios, los tokens por segundo crecen de forma casi proporcional y el percentil noventa y cinco de latencia se mantiene en nueve segundos.","start":318.145,"end":325.694,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con cuarenta, los tokens por segundo quedan iguales que con veinte y la latencia sube a veintidós segundos.","start":325.994,"end":331.221,"terms":[]},{"text":"La rodilla está alrededor de veinte.","start":331.521,"end":333.474,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la última meseta aparecen errores.","start":333.774,"end":336.029,"terms":[]},{"text":"Al separarlos, casi todos son 429.","start":336.329,"end":339.443,"terms":[]},{"text":"No es saturación del modelo sino el límite de cuota de la cuenta.","start":339.743,"end":342.811,"terms":[]},{"text":"La acción correcta es gestionar la cuota y sumar una cola, no agregar servidores.","start":343.111,"end":347.444,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":348.244,"end":365.33,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":348.744,"end":350.268,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Repetir un solo prompt y medir el caché en lugar del sistema.","start":350.818,"end":354.025,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Subir la carga en una sola rampa sin mesetas.","start":354.575,"end":357.155,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Sumar los 429 y los 5xx en una única tasa de error.","start":357.705,"end":361.666,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Dejar el timeout por defecto y cortar las respuestas largas.","start":362.216,"end":365.33,"terms":[{"start":9,"end":16,"say":"táimaut"}]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":366.13,"end":384.053,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":366.63,"end":368.897,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Los umbrales del script coinciden con los objetivos escritos.","start":369.447,"end":372.584,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Los prompts salen de un archivo con la mezcla del perfil.","start":373.134,"end":376.179,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"La prueba tiene mesetas y ubicaste la rodilla.","start":376.729,"end":379.297,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Los errores 429 se cuentan aparte de 5xx y timeouts.","start":379.847,"end":384.053,"terms":[{"start":43,"end":51,"say":"táimauts"}]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":384.853,"end":395.406,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":385.353,"end":386.923,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Los scripts generados suelen venir sin timeout, sin mezcla de prompts y con thresholds inventados.","start":387.473,"end":392.444,"terms":[{"start":39,"end":46,"say":"táimaut"},{"start":76,"end":86,"say":"trésholds"}]},{"text":"Se revisan contra tus objetivos antes de correr.","start":392.744,"end":395.406,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":396.206,"end":409.313,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 26.","start":396.706,"end":398.415,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":398.715,"end":403.954,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 27: Performance (3): IA generando y analizando pruebas de carga.","start":404.504,"end":409.313,"terms":[{"start":21,"end":32,"say":"perfórmans"},{"start":38,"end":40,"say":"i a"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d26-en","day":26,"lang":"en","title":"Performance (2): load testing an LLM endpoint with k6","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d26-en.mp4","duration":438.189,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":16.689,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 26 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.357,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Performance (2): load testing an LLM endpoint with k6.","start":4.907,"end":8.869,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A staged load test against an LLM endpoint, with token and goodput metrics, to find the point where the system stops scaling.","start":9.419,"end":16.689,"terms":[{"start":59,"end":66,"say":"good put"}]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":17.489,"end":33.538,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"With yesterday's baseline and targets you can now ask the question that matters to whoever operates the system: how many users it supports before the experience degrades.","start":17.989,"end":27.175,"terms":[]},{"text":"The answer is a curve with a breaking point, and finding it requires loading gradually and watching the right metrics.","start":27.475,"end":33.538,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":34.338,"end":57.889,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"k6 works well for this.","start":34.838,"end":36.757,"terms":[]},{"text":"Scripts are code, they run locally and in CI, and thresholds turn the test into a pass or fail criterion.","start":37.057,"end":43.909,"terms":[]},{"text":"But a load test against a model has traps of its own: caching that flatters the results, provider quota limits that look like saturation and default timeouts that cut long answers off.","start":44.209,"end":54.289,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide builds the test while avoiding those three.","start":54.589,"end":57.889,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":58.689,"end":80.872,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":59.189,"end":60.887,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"k6 installed.","start":61.437,"end":63.03,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"An OpenAI-compatible endpoint and the day 25 targets.","start":63.58,"end":67.321,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Approved budget if the API is paid: cap max_tokens, duration and users.","start":67.871,"end":73.365,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"k6 doesn't natively measure streaming: TTFT under load needs an SSE extension or an LLM-specific tool.","start":73.915,"end":80.872,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":81.672,"end":87.969,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":82.172,"end":83.452,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":83.752,"end":87.969,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":88.769,"end":91.28,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The same file continues here.","start":89.269,"end":91.28,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":92.08,"end":94.847,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":92.58,"end":94.847,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":95.647,"end":133.679,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":96.147,"end":97.183,"terms":[]},{"text":"Thresholds from the targets.","start":97.483,"end":99.273,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Start from the day 25 baseline and written targets.","start":99.823,"end":103.332,"terms":[]},{"text":"The script's thresholds come from there.","start":103.632,"end":105.98,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The script's thresholds are the targets you wrote yesterday, translated into code: the ninety-fifth percentile of time to first token, that of total latency and the maximum error rate.","start":106.53,"end":116.355,"terms":[]},{"text":"Don't invent them while writing the script or accept whatever an example brings.","start":116.655,"end":121.348,"terms":[]},{"text":"A threshold with no origin is decoration: nobody knows what it means when it fails.","start":121.648,"end":127.2,"terms":[]},{"text":"With product targets as thresholds, a red result means users will have a worse experience than agreed.","start":127.5,"end":133.679,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":134.479,"end":173.836,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":134.979,"end":136.177,"terms":[]},{"text":"A realistic prompt mix.","start":136.477,"end":138.373,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Use a prompt mix similar to the real one.","start":138.923,"end":141.514,"terms":[]},{"text":"With a single repeated prompt, caching gives you numbers production won't see.","start":141.814,"end":146.171,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Load prompts from a file with the workload profile's mix: types and proportions.","start":146.721,"end":151.89,"terms":[]},{"text":"If you repeat a single prompt, two things happen.","start":152.19,"end":155.327,"terms":[]},{"text":"The provider's cache or your own application's answers far faster than in real use, and numbers come out better than production will see.","start":155.627,"end":163.49,"terms":[]},{"text":"Also, all answers have the same length, and you lose the variability that stresses the system.","start":163.79,"end":169.783,"terms":[]},{"text":"Use enough different prompts that they don't repeat during the test.","start":170.083,"end":173.836,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":174.636,"end":209.139,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":175.136,"end":176.218,"terms":[]},{"text":"Load in stages.","start":176.518,"end":178.03,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Load in stages: ramp, hold, ramp again.","start":178.58,"end":181.845,"terms":[]},{"text":"Plateaus are where you read behavior.","start":182.145,"end":184.609,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Raise load in steps: ramp to a level, hold it for a few minutes, ramp to the next.","start":185.159,"end":190.479,"terms":[]},{"text":"Plateaus are where you read behavior, because the system had time to stabilize.","start":190.779,"end":195.367,"terms":[]},{"text":"During ramps the numbers blend two states.","start":195.667,"end":198.422,"terms":[]},{"text":"Hold each plateau long enough to complete the profile's longest request several times.","start":198.722,"end":203.496,"terms":[]},{"text":"If a long answer takes forty seconds, a one-minute plateau barely manages to show one round.","start":203.796,"end":209.139,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":209.939,"end":247.3,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":210.439,"end":211.452,"terms":[]},{"text":"Finding the knee.","start":211.752,"end":213.032,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Look for the knee: the point where total tokens per second stop growing and latency starts climbing.","start":213.582,"end":219.203,"terms":[]},{"text":"That's your capacity.","start":219.503,"end":221.201,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Plot total tokens per second and latency for each plateau.","start":221.751,"end":225.469,"terms":[]},{"text":"At first, more users produce more tokens per second with stable latency.","start":225.769,"end":230.323,"terms":[]},{"text":"At some point tokens per second stop growing and latency starts climbing.","start":230.623,"end":234.758,"terms":[]},{"text":"That's the knee, and it marks the system's real capacity.","start":235.058,"end":238.451,"terms":[]},{"text":"Beyond that point each additional user only makes the others wait longer.","start":238.751,"end":242.945,"terms":[]},{"text":"Your usable capacity sits a little below it, with headroom for peaks.","start":243.245,"end":247.3,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":248.1,"end":289.407,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":248.6,"end":249.728,"terms":[]},{"text":"Separating errors.","start":250.028,"end":251.331,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Separate errors: 429 is the provider's rate limit; 5xx and timeouts are saturation.","start":251.881,"end":258.188,"terms":[]},{"text":"They get fixed differently.","start":258.488,"end":260.197,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Not all errors mean the same.","start":260.747,"end":262.759,"terms":[]},{"text":"A 429 is the provider's quota limit: you reached the maximum requests or tokens per minute your account allows, and it's solved by requesting more quota or adding a queue with retries.","start":263.059,"end":273.928,"terms":[]},{"text":"A 5xx or a timeout is saturation, of your infrastructure or the provider's, and it's solved with capacity or by reducing work per request.","start":274.228,"end":283.275,"terms":[]},{"text":"Count them separately in the script.","start":283.575,"end":285.424,"terms":[]},{"text":"Blended into a single error rate, they lead to the wrong fix.","start":285.724,"end":289.407,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":290.207,"end":327.205,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":290.707,"end":291.893,"terms":[]},{"text":"Thresholds as the criterion and scheduled runs.","start":292.193,"end":295.18,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Keep thresholds as the pass or fail criterion and run the test on a schedule, not on every PR.","start":295.73,"end":301.979,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Keep thresholds as the automatic pass or fail criterion, so the test can run without someone interpreting charts each time.","start":302.529,"end":309.811,"terms":[]},{"text":"Schedule it periodically or before each release, not on every pull request: it's slow, costs money if you use a paid API and needs a stable environment to be comparable.","start":310.111,"end":320.551,"terms":[]},{"text":"Save each run's summary.","start":320.851,"end":322.351,"terms":[]},{"text":"The trend of the knee over time warns you of a degradation before it becomes an incident.","start":322.651,"end":327.205,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":328.005,"end":369.631,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":328.505,"end":329.61,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"With illustrative figures: a team loads its assistant in plateaus of five, ten, twenty and forty users, three minutes each, with fifty different prompts.","start":330.16,"end":339.358,"terms":[]},{"text":"Up to twenty users, tokens per second grow almost proportionally and the ninety-fifth percentile of latency holds at nine seconds.","start":339.658,"end":347.521,"terms":[]},{"text":"With forty, tokens per second stay the same as with twenty and latency rises to twenty-two seconds.","start":347.821,"end":353.524,"terms":[]},{"text":"The knee is around twenty.","start":353.824,"end":355.312,"terms":[]},{"text":"In the last plateau errors appear.","start":355.612,"end":357.786,"terms":[]},{"text":"Separating them, almost all are 429s.","start":358.086,"end":361.525,"terms":[]},{"text":"It isn't model saturation but the account's quota limit.","start":361.825,"end":364.928,"terms":[]},{"text":"The right action is managing quota and adding a queue, not adding servers.","start":365.228,"end":369.631,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":370.431,"end":389.526,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":370.931,"end":372.594,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Repeating a single prompt and measuring the cache instead of the system.","start":373.144,"end":377.337,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Raising load in a single ramp with no plateaus.","start":377.887,"end":380.955,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Adding 429s and 5xxs into a single error rate.","start":381.505,"end":385.85,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Leaving the default timeout and cutting long answers off.","start":386.4,"end":389.526,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":390.326,"end":408.689,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":390.826,"end":393.069,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The script's thresholds match the written targets.","start":393.619,"end":396.443,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Prompts come from a file with the profile's mix.","start":396.993,"end":400.223,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The test has plateaus and you located the knee.","start":400.773,"end":403.446,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"429 errors are counted apart from 5xxs and timeouts.","start":403.996,"end":408.689,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":409.489,"end":421.319,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":409.989,"end":411.884,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Generated scripts often come with no timeout, no prompt mix and made-up thresholds.","start":412.434,"end":418.242,"terms":[]},{"text":"Review them against your targets before running.","start":418.542,"end":421.319,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":422.119,"end":436.689,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 26.","start":422.619,"end":424.317,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":424.617,"end":430.598,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 27: Performance (3): AI generating and analyzing load tests.","start":431.148,"end":436.689,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d27-es","day":27,"lang":"es","title":"Performance (3): IA generando y analizando pruebas de carga","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d27-es.mp4","duration":415.938,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":16.411,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 27 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.765,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Performance (3): IA generando y analizando pruebas de carga.","start":4.315,"end":7.824,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":11,"say":"perfórmans"},{"start":17,"end":19,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Cómo usar un LLM para escribir un script de carga desde una especificación y para proponer hipótesis sobre los resultados, con un método para verificar cada una.","start":8.374,"end":16.411,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":17.211,"end":34.538,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Escribir un script de carga desde cero y leer después un resumen con cientos de métricas son las dos partes más lentas de las pruebas de performance, y las dos son texto.","start":17.711,"end":25.829,"terms":[{"start":137,"end":148,"say":"perfórmans"}]},{"text":"Un modelo puede acelerar ambas.","start":26.129,"end":27.931,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lo que no puede hacer es saber cómo es tu tráfico, cuáles son tus objetivos ni qué estaba pasando en tus servidores durante la prueba.","start":28.231,"end":34.538,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":35.338,"end":57.716,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Si no le das esa información, la completa con valores razonables y la presenta con seguridad.","start":35.838,"end":40.949,"terms":[]},{"text":"El script resultante corre, y prueba una carga que no es la tuya.","start":41.249,"end":44.758,"terms":[]},{"text":"El análisis resultante suena experto, y describe cuellos de botella genéricos.","start":45.058,"end":49.275,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta guía muestra qué entradas hacen útil a la IA en performance, cómo revisar lo que genera antes de gastar una corrida y cómo convertir su análisis en hipótesis que se verifican.","start":49.575,"end":57.716,"terms":[{"start":47,"end":49,"say":"i a"},{"start":53,"end":64,"say":"perfórmans"}]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":58.516,"end":72.832,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":59.016,"end":60.946,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"k6 y una API de práctica con especificación OpenAPI.","start":61.496,"end":64.726,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un perfil de tráfico agregado (porcentaje por endpoint), nunca logs crudos con datos personales.","start":65.276,"end":70.375,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Objetivos de servicio escritos.","start":70.925,"end":72.832,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":73.632,"end":78.953,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":74.132,"end":75.307,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":75.607,"end":78.953,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":79.753,"end":82.392,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":80.253,"end":82.392,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":83.192,"end":85.831,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":83.692,"end":85.831,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":86.631,"end":127.01,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":87.131,"end":88.028,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tres entradas obligatorias.","start":88.328,"end":90.037,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Dale al modelo tres insumos: la especificación de la API, la mezcla de tráfico agregada y los objetivos.","start":90.587,"end":96.313,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sin eso inventa los tres.","start":96.613,"end":98.207,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El modelo necesita tres cosas para escribir un script que sirva.","start":98.757,"end":102.033,"terms":[]},{"text":"La especificación de la API, para conocer los endpoints, los parámetros y los formatos reales.","start":102.333,"end":107.433,"terms":[]},{"text":"La mezcla de tráfico agregada, es decir qué porcentaje de las llamadas va a cada operación, sacada de tus registros sin datos de usuarios.","start":107.733,"end":114.922,"terms":[]},{"text":"Y los objetivos de latencia y error.","start":115.222,"end":117.326,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si falta cualquiera de las tres, la va a inventar.","start":117.626,"end":120.392,"terms":[]},{"text":"Y lo va a hacer de forma verosímil, que es peor que hacerlo mal: una mezcla de tráfico inventada no se ve distinta de una real.","start":120.692,"end":127.01,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":127.81,"end":164.041,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":128.31,"end":129.207,"terms":[]},{"text":"Requisitos explícitos en el pedido.","start":129.507,"end":131.53,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pedí el script con requisitos explícitos: escenarios, think time, datos desde archivo, variables de entorno, thresholds y timeouts.","start":132.08,"end":139.129,"terms":[{"start":109,"end":119,"say":"trésholds"},{"start":122,"end":130,"say":"táimauts"}]}],[{"text":"Pedí el script con una lista de requisitos, no con una descripción general: escenarios separados por tipo de operación, tiempo de espera entre acciones de un mismo usuario, datos leídos desde un archivo, URLs y claves por variables de entorno, umbrales con tus objetivos y timeouts adecuados al largo de las respuestas.","start":139.679,"end":155.483,"terms":[{"start":204,"end":208,"say":"u erre eles"},{"start":273,"end":281,"say":"táimauts"}]},{"text":"Cada requisito corresponde a un defecto frecuente en los scripts generados.","start":155.783,"end":159.478,"terms":[]},{"text":"Guardá el pedido como plantilla en el repo, igual que los prompts de los días anteriores.","start":159.778,"end":164.041,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":164.841,"end":206.114,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":165.341,"end":166.238,"terms":[]},{"text":"Revisión con checklist.","start":166.538,"end":167.818,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Revisalo con checklist antes de correr: ¿la mezcla coincide?, ¿hay think time?, ¿los datos no se repiten?, ¿cero secretos en el código?, ¿los thresholds son los tuyos?","start":168.368,"end":177.262,"terms":[{"start":142,"end":152,"say":"trésholds"}]}],[{"text":"Antes de correr nada, revisá el script con cinco preguntas.","start":177.812,"end":181.101,"terms":[]},{"text":"¿La mezcla de escenarios coincide con la que pasaste?","start":181.401,"end":184.05,"terms":[]},{"text":"¿Hay tiempo de espera entre acciones, o cada usuario virtual dispara sin pausa?","start":184.35,"end":188.3,"terms":[]},{"text":"¿Los datos varían, o se repite el mismo registro?","start":188.6,"end":191.482,"terms":[]},{"text":"¿Hay algún secreto escrito en el código?","start":191.782,"end":193.852,"terms":[]},{"text":"¿Los umbrales son los tuyos, o aparecieron otros?","start":194.152,"end":196.917,"terms":[]},{"text":"Son cinco minutos de lectura.","start":197.217,"end":199.112,"terms":[]},{"text":"Correr un script equivocado cuesta una hora de prueba, presupuesto de API y, sobre todo, conclusiones sacadas de datos que no representan nada.","start":199.412,"end":206.114,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":206.914,"end":240.175,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":207.414,"end":208.415,"terms":[]},{"text":"Corrida en seco y corrida real.","start":208.715,"end":210.575,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Dry run con un usuario y una iteración para ver que funciona, y después la corrida real exportando el resumen.","start":211.125,"end":216.817,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":7,"say":"drai ran"}]}],[{"text":"Ejecutá primero con un usuario y una iteración.","start":217.367,"end":220.214,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sirve para confirmar que el script funciona: que autentica, que las respuestas son las esperadas y que los checks pasan.","start":220.514,"end":226.02,"terms":[]},{"text":"Encontrar un error de autenticación con mil usuarios virtuales es una forma cara de enterarse.","start":226.32,"end":231.164,"terms":[]},{"text":"Recién después lanzá la corrida real y exportá el resumen a un archivo.","start":231.464,"end":235.007,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ese archivo, junto con la ventana exacta de tiempo de la prueba, es la entrada del paso siguiente.","start":235.307,"end":240.175,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":240.975,"end":278.786,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":241.475,"end":242.476,"terms":[]},{"text":"Análisis con métricas del servidor.","start":242.776,"end":244.764,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Para el análisis, pasá el resumen junto con métricas del servidor de la misma ventana.","start":245.314,"end":249.565,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pedí hipótesis ordenadas, con la métrica que las respalda y cómo confirmarlas.","start":249.865,"end":253.897,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Para el análisis, pasale al modelo el resumen de la prueba junto con las métricas del servidor de la misma ventana: CPU, memoria, conexiones a la base, longitud de colas, uso de GPU si corresponde.","start":254.447,"end":264.701,"terms":[{"start":116,"end":119,"say":"ce pe u"},{"start":178,"end":181,"say":"ge pe u"}]},{"text":"Pedí hipótesis ordenadas por probabilidad, cada una con la métrica que la respalda y la forma de confirmarla.","start":265.001,"end":270.251,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ese formato lo obliga a citar datos en lugar de enumerar causas típicas.","start":270.551,"end":274.397,"terms":[]},{"text":"Una hipótesis que no nombra una métrica de tus datos se descarta sin más análisis.","start":274.697,"end":278.786,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":279.586,"end":314.276,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":280.086,"end":281.052,"terms":[]},{"text":"Verificar la primera hipótesis.","start":281.352,"end":283.224,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Verificá la hipótesis principal con una medición dirigida.","start":283.774,"end":286.958,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un cuello de botella no confirmado es una sospecha.","start":287.258,"end":290.024,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tomá la hipótesis mejor ubicada y diseñá una medición dirigida para confirmarla o descartarla.","start":290.574,"end":295.29,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si dice que el cuello de botella es el pool de conexiones, repetí la prueba con el pool ampliado y mirá si la rodilla se mueve.","start":295.59,"end":301.537,"terms":[{"start":39,"end":43,"say":"pul"},{"start":83,"end":87,"say":"pul"}]},{"text":"Si no se mueve, la hipótesis era incorrecta y pasás a la siguiente.","start":301.837,"end":305.462,"terms":[]},{"text":"Hasta que una medición lo confirma, un cuello de botella es una sospecha.","start":305.762,"end":309.433,"terms":[]},{"text":"Reportarlo como hallazgo sin verificar hace que el equipo optimice algo que no era el problema.","start":309.733,"end":314.276,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":315.076,"end":355.715,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":315.576,"end":316.495,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un equipo le pide a un modelo un script para su API con la especificación, la mezcla de tráfico y los objetivos.","start":317.045,"end":322.853,"terms":[]},{"text":"La revisión con checklist encuentra dos problemas: no hay tiempo de espera entre acciones y el umbral de latencia es de quinientos milisegundos, cuando su objetivo era dos segundos.","start":323.153,"end":331.55,"terms":[]},{"text":"Corrigen ambos.","start":331.85,"end":332.944,"terms":[]},{"text":"La corrida en seco pasa.","start":333.244,"end":334.848,"terms":[]},{"text":"La corrida real muestra una rodilla en ciento veinte usuarios.","start":335.148,"end":338.355,"terms":[]},{"text":"Para el análisis entregan el resumen y las métricas del servidor.","start":338.655,"end":341.874,"terms":[]},{"text":"La primera hipótesis: el pool de conexiones a la base se agota, porque las conexiones activas llegan al máximo justo cuando sube la latencia.","start":342.174,"end":348.841,"terms":[{"start":25,"end":29,"say":"pul"}]},{"text":"Amplían el pool, repiten, y la rodilla pasa a ciento ochenta.","start":349.141,"end":352.592,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":15,"say":"pul"}]},{"text":"Hipótesis confirmada, con cifras ilustrativas.","start":352.892,"end":355.715,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":356.515,"end":373.671,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":357.015,"end":358.539,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pedir el script sin especificación, mezcla de tráfico ni objetivos.","start":359.089,"end":362.818,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Correr el script generado sin revisarlo contra el checklist.","start":363.368,"end":366.239,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pedir el análisis solo con el resumen de la herramienta de carga.","start":366.789,"end":370.007,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Reportar como cuello de botella una hipótesis sin verificar.","start":370.557,"end":373.671,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":374.471,"end":391.163,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":374.971,"end":377.238,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El pedido incluye especificación, mezcla de tráfico y objetivos.","start":377.788,"end":381.483,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El script pasó el checklist de cinco preguntas.","start":382.033,"end":384.485,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Hubo una corrida en seco antes de la corrida real.","start":385.035,"end":387.452,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"La primera hipótesis se confirmó o descartó con una medición.","start":388.002,"end":391.163,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":391.963,"end":401.041,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":392.463,"end":394.033,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Sin métricas del servidor, diagnostica con seguridad causas genéricas.","start":394.583,"end":398.278,"terms":[]},{"text":"El análisis vale lo que valen los datos que le das.","start":398.578,"end":401.041,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":401.841,"end":414.438,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 27.","start":402.341,"end":403.946,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":404.246,"end":409.485,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 28: Evals en CI: quality gates para features con IA.","start":410.035,"end":414.438,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"ci ai"},{"start":42,"end":47,"say":"gueits"},{"start":53,"end":61,"say":"fíchers"},{"start":66,"end":68,"say":"i a"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d27-en","day":27,"lang":"en","title":"Performance (3): AI generating and analyzing load tests","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d27-en.mp4","duration":453.287,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":17.328,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 27 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.508,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Performance (3): AI generating and analyzing load tests.","start":5.058,"end":9.055,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"How to use an LLM to write a load script from a specification and to propose hypotheses about the results, with a method to verify each.","start":9.605,"end":17.328,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":18.128,"end":36.453,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Writing a load script from scratch and later reading a summary with hundreds of metrics are the two slowest parts of performance testing, and both are text.","start":18.628,"end":27.048,"terms":[]},{"text":"A model can speed both up.","start":27.348,"end":29.312,"terms":[]},{"text":"What it can't do is know what your traffic looks like, what your targets are or what was happening on your servers during the test.","start":29.612,"end":36.453,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":37.253,"end":62.627,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"If you don't give it that information, it fills it in with reasonable values and presents them confidently.","start":37.753,"end":43.723,"terms":[]},{"text":"The resulting script runs, and tests a load that isn't yours.","start":44.023,"end":47.823,"terms":[]},{"text":"The resulting analysis sounds expert, and describes generic bottlenecks.","start":48.123,"end":52.537,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide shows which inputs make AI useful in performance, how to review what it generates before spending a run and how to turn its analysis into hypotheses that get verified.","start":52.837,"end":62.627,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":63.427,"end":77.975,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":63.927,"end":65.624,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"k6 and a practice API with an OpenAPI spec.","start":66.174,"end":69.846,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"An aggregated traffic profile (percentage per endpoint), never raw logs with personal data.","start":70.396,"end":75.89,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Written service targets.","start":76.44,"end":77.975,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":78.775,"end":85.072,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":79.275,"end":80.555,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":80.855,"end":85.072,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":85.872,"end":88.639,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":86.372,"end":88.639,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":89.439,"end":92.205,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":89.939,"end":92.205,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":93.005,"end":134.673,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":93.505,"end":94.541,"terms":[]},{"text":"Three required inputs.","start":94.841,"end":96.597,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Give the model three inputs: the API spec, the aggregated traffic mix and the targets.","start":97.147,"end":102.78,"terms":[]},{"text":"Without them it invents all three.","start":103.08,"end":105.045,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The model needs three things to write a useful script.","start":105.595,"end":108.581,"terms":[]},{"text":"The API spec, to know the real endpoints, parameters and formats.","start":108.881,"end":113.354,"terms":[]},{"text":"The aggregated traffic mix, meaning what percentage of calls goes to each operation, taken from your logs with no user data.","start":113.654,"end":121.191,"terms":[]},{"text":"And the latency and error targets.","start":121.491,"end":123.828,"terms":[]},{"text":"If any of the three is missing, it will invent it.","start":124.128,"end":126.928,"terms":[]},{"text":"And it will do so plausibly, which is worse than doing it badly: an invented traffic mix doesn't look different from a real one.","start":127.228,"end":134.673,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":135.473,"end":176.963,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":135.973,"end":137.171,"terms":[]},{"text":"Explicit requirements in the request.","start":137.471,"end":139.935,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Request the script with explicit requirements: scenarios, think time, data from a file, environment variables, thresholds and timeouts.","start":140.485,"end":149.219,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Ask for the script with a list of requirements, not a general description: scenarios split by operation type, think time between actions of the same user, data read from a file, URLs and keys through environment variables, thresholds with your targets and timeouts suited to answer length.","start":149.769,"end":167.302,"terms":[]},{"text":"Each requirement corresponds to a frequent defect in generated scripts.","start":167.602,"end":171.692,"terms":[]},{"text":"Save the request as a template in the repo, just like the prompts from earlier days.","start":171.992,"end":176.963,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":177.763,"end":224.458,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":178.263,"end":179.346,"terms":[]},{"text":"Review with a checklist.","start":179.646,"end":181.274,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Review it with a checklist before running: does the mix match? is there think time? is data non-repeating? zero secrets in code? are the thresholds yours?","start":181.824,"end":192.483,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Before running anything, review the script with five questions.","start":193.033,"end":196.705,"terms":[]},{"text":"Does the scenario mix match the one you passed?","start":197.005,"end":199.677,"terms":[]},{"text":"Is there think time between actions, or does each virtual user fire without pause?","start":199.977,"end":204.914,"terms":[]},{"text":"Does data vary, or is the same record repeated?","start":205.214,"end":208.445,"terms":[]},{"text":"Is any secret written in the code?","start":208.745,"end":211,"terms":[]},{"text":"Are the thresholds yours, or did others appear?","start":211.3,"end":214.123,"terms":[]},{"text":"That's five minutes of reading.","start":214.423,"end":216.318,"terms":[]},{"text":"Running a wrong script costs an hour of testing, API budget and, above all, conclusions drawn from data that represents nothing.","start":216.618,"end":224.458,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":225.258,"end":262.025,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":225.758,"end":226.77,"terms":[]},{"text":"Dry run and real run.","start":227.07,"end":228.873,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Dry run with one user and one iteration to confirm it works, then the real run exporting the summary.","start":229.423,"end":235.242,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Run first with one user and one iteration.","start":235.792,"end":239.08,"terms":[]},{"text":"It confirms the script works: that it authenticates, that responses are the expected ones and that checks pass.","start":239.38,"end":246.337,"terms":[]},{"text":"Finding an authentication error with a thousand virtual users is an expensive way to find out.","start":246.637,"end":251.737,"terms":[]},{"text":"Only then launch the real run and export the summary to a file.","start":252.037,"end":255.998,"terms":[]},{"text":"That file, together with the exact time window of the test, is the input for the next step.","start":256.298,"end":262.025,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":262.825,"end":303.98,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":263.325,"end":264.453,"terms":[]},{"text":"Analysis with server metrics.","start":264.753,"end":266.904,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"For analysis, pass the summary along with server metrics from the same window.","start":267.454,"end":271.926,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ask for ranked hypotheses, with the supporting metric and how to confirm each.","start":272.226,"end":276.792,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"For analysis, give the model the test summary together with server metrics from the same window: CPU, memory, database connections, queue length, GPU usage if applicable.","start":277.342,"end":288.153,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ask for hypotheses ranked by likelihood, each with the supporting metric and the way to confirm it.","start":288.453,"end":293.912,"terms":[]},{"text":"That format forces it to cite data instead of listing typical causes.","start":294.212,"end":298.383,"terms":[]},{"text":"A hypothesis that doesn't name a metric from your data gets discarded without further analysis.","start":298.683,"end":303.98,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":304.78,"end":342.116,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":305.28,"end":306.467,"terms":[]},{"text":"Verify the top hypothesis.","start":306.767,"end":308.801,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Verify the top hypothesis with a targeted measurement.","start":309.351,"end":312.57,"terms":[]},{"text":"An unconfirmed bottleneck is a suspicion.","start":312.87,"end":315.427,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Take the top-ranked hypothesis and design a targeted measurement to confirm or discard it.","start":315.977,"end":321.041,"terms":[]},{"text":"If it says the bottleneck is the connection pool, repeat the test with a larger pool and see whether the knee moves.","start":321.341,"end":327.567,"terms":[]},{"text":"If it doesn't move, the hypothesis was wrong and you move to the next.","start":327.867,"end":332.061,"terms":[]},{"text":"Until a measurement confirms it, a bottleneck is a suspicion.","start":332.361,"end":336.16,"terms":[]},{"text":"Reporting it as a finding without verifying gets the team optimizing something that wasn't the problem.","start":336.46,"end":342.116,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":342.916,"end":387.84,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":343.416,"end":344.522,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A team asks a model for a script for its API with the spec, the traffic mix and the targets.","start":345.072,"end":350.705,"terms":[]},{"text":"The checklist review finds two problems: there's no think time between actions and the latency threshold is five hundred milliseconds, when their target was two seconds.","start":351.005,"end":360.296,"terms":[]},{"text":"They fix both.","start":360.596,"end":361.806,"terms":[]},{"text":"The dry run passes.","start":362.106,"end":363.711,"terms":[]},{"text":"The real run shows a knee at one hundred and twenty users.","start":364.011,"end":367.404,"terms":[]},{"text":"For analysis they hand over the summary and the server metrics.","start":367.704,"end":370.887,"terms":[]},{"text":"The top hypothesis: the database connection pool is exhausted, because active connections hit the maximum exactly when latency rises.","start":371.187,"end":379.63,"terms":[]},{"text":"They enlarge the pool, repeat, and the knee moves to one hundred and eighty.","start":379.93,"end":384.194,"terms":[]},{"text":"Hypothesis confirmed, with illustrative figures.","start":384.494,"end":387.84,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":388.64,"end":406.969,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":389.14,"end":390.803,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Asking for the script without spec, traffic mix or targets.","start":391.353,"end":395.187,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Running the generated script without reviewing it against the checklist.","start":395.737,"end":399.513,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Asking for analysis with only the load tool's summary.","start":400.063,"end":403.119,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Reporting an unverified hypothesis as the bottleneck.","start":403.669,"end":406.969,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":407.769,"end":425.203,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":408.269,"end":410.512,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The request includes spec, traffic mix and targets.","start":411.062,"end":414.478,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The script passed the five-question checklist.","start":415.028,"end":418.119,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"There was a dry run before the real run.","start":418.669,"end":421.017,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The top hypothesis was confirmed or discarded with a measurement.","start":421.567,"end":425.203,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":426.003,"end":436.765,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":426.503,"end":428.398,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Without server metrics, it confidently diagnoses generic causes.","start":428.948,"end":433.386,"terms":[]},{"text":"The analysis is worth what the data you feed it is worth.","start":433.686,"end":436.765,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":437.565,"end":451.787,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 27.","start":438.065,"end":439.81,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":440.11,"end":446.091,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 28: Evals in CI: quality gates for AI features.","start":446.641,"end":451.787,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d28-es","day":28,"lang":"es","title":"Evals en CI: quality gates para features con IA","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d28-es.mp4","duration":400.114,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":13.624,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 28 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.707,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Evals en CI: quality gates para features con IA.","start":4.257,"end":7.15,"terms":[{"start":9,"end":11,"say":"ci ai"},{"start":21,"end":26,"say":"gueits"},{"start":32,"end":40,"say":"fíchers"},{"start":45,"end":47,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Cómo llevar los evals al pipeline en tres niveles (PR, nocturno y release) con un gate que bloquea el merge y un costo controlado.","start":7.7,"end":13.624,"terms":[{"start":25,"end":33,"say":"páiplain"},{"start":51,"end":53,"say":"pe erre"},{"start":66,"end":73,"say":"rilís"},{"start":82,"end":86,"say":"gueit"},{"start":102,"end":107,"say":"merch"}]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":14.424,"end":34.504,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un eval que corre solo cuando alguien se acuerda termina sin correr.","start":14.924,"end":18.247,"terms":[]},{"text":"La forma de que la calidad de una funcionalidad con IA se sostenga en el tiempo es la misma que para el resto del software: un gate en el pipeline que bloquea el cambio cuando algo empeora.","start":18.547,"end":27.199,"terms":[{"start":52,"end":54,"say":"i a"},{"start":114,"end":122,"say":"sóftuer"},{"start":127,"end":131,"say":"gueit"},{"start":138,"end":146,"say":"páiplain"}]},{"text":"Lo que cambia son las restricciones.","start":27.499,"end":29.476,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los evals cuestan dinero por corrida, tardan más que un test unitario y su resultado varía.","start":29.776,"end":34.504,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":35.304,"end":54.082,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Meter todos los evals en cada pull request vuelve el pipeline lento y caro, y el equipo termina salteándolo.","start":35.804,"end":41.1,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":42,"say":"pul ricuést"},{"start":53,"end":61,"say":"páiplain"}]},{"text":"No meter ninguno deja pasar regresiones.","start":41.4,"end":43.876,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta guía organiza los evals en tres niveles, por PR, nocturno y por release, cada uno con un alcance, un costo y una consecuencia distintos, y muestra cómo mantener el gasto bajo control.","start":44.176,"end":54.082,"terms":[{"start":50,"end":52,"say":"pe erre"},{"start":69,"end":76,"say":"rilís"}]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":54.882,"end":67.933,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":55.382,"end":57.312,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un repo con evals (día 8) y golden dataset (día 17).","start":57.862,"end":61.208,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"CI: GitHub Actions o Jenkins.","start":61.758,"end":63.839,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":2,"say":"ci ai"},{"start":11,"end":18,"say":"ákshons"}]}],[{"text":"Una API key guardada como secreto del CI, con límite de gasto.","start":64.389,"end":67.933,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":11,"say":"ki"},{"start":38,"end":40,"say":"ci ai"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":68.733,"end":74.054,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":69.233,"end":70.408,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":70.708,"end":74.054,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":74.854,"end":77.493,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y este es el siguiente archivo del setup.","start":75.354,"end":77.493,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"sétap"}]}]]},{"section":"Los tres niveles","start":78.293,"end":102.761,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los tres niveles.","start":78.793,"end":79.98,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"PR: corre cuando cambian prompts, evals o código de IA.","start":80.53,"end":83.632,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":2,"say":"pe erre"},{"start":52,"end":54,"say":"i a"}]},{"text":"Set dev reducido, 3 corridas, checks de formato y contenido.","start":83.932,"end":87.441,"terms":[]},{"text":"Bloquea el merge.","start":87.741,"end":88.87,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"merch"}]}],[{"text":"Nocturno: todas las noches.","start":89.42,"end":91.118,"terms":[]},{"text":"Set de test completo x5, juez y red teaming.","start":91.418,"end":94.114,"terms":[{"start":32,"end":43,"say":"red tíming"}]},{"text":"Alerta al equipo y muestra tendencia.","start":94.414,"end":96.506,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Release: antes de publicar.","start":97.056,"end":98.684,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":7,"say":"rilís"}]},{"text":"Regresión completa más performance.","start":98.984,"end":101.077,"terms":[{"start":23,"end":34,"say":"perfórmans"}]},{"text":"Aprueba una persona.","start":101.377,"end":102.761,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":103.561,"end":137.472,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":104.061,"end":104.957,"terms":[]},{"text":"El nivel de PR.","start":105.257,"end":106.502,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"pe erre"}]}],[{"text":"Empezá por el nivel PR: un subset chico y rápido que corre solo cuando cambian prompts, evals o código de IA.","start":107.052,"end":112.639,"terms":[{"start":20,"end":22,"say":"pe erre"},{"start":106,"end":108,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Es un subconjunto chico y rápido, pensado para dar una respuesta en pocos minutos.","start":113.189,"end":117.569,"terms":[]},{"text":"Corre solo cuando el cambio toca prompts, configuración de evals o código que llama al modelo.","start":117.869,"end":122.446,"terms":[]},{"text":"Para un cambio de estilos en el frontend no tiene sentido gastar tokens.","start":122.746,"end":126.313,"terms":[]},{"text":"Incluí los chequeos deterministas de formato y contenido, y un conjunto reducido de casos con pocas repeticiones.","start":126.613,"end":132.235,"terms":[]},{"text":"Su función no es medir la calidad con precisión, sino atrapar las roturas evidentes antes del merge.","start":132.535,"end":137.472,"terms":[{"start":94,"end":99,"say":"merch"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":138.272,"end":171.092,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":138.772,"end":139.668,"terms":[]},{"text":"El código de salida como gate.","start":139.968,"end":141.643,"terms":[{"start":25,"end":29,"say":"gueit"}]}],[{"text":"El comando de eval devuelve un código de salida distinto de cero si hay casos que fallan.","start":142.193,"end":146.328,"terms":[]},{"text":"Eso alcanza para bloquear el merge.","start":146.628,"end":148.663,"terms":[{"start":29,"end":34,"say":"merch"}]}],[{"text":"El mecanismo es el mismo de cualquier test.","start":149.213,"end":151.607,"terms":[]},{"text":"El comando de evaluación termina con un código distinto de cero cuando hay casos fallidos, y el pipeline marca el paso en rojo.","start":151.907,"end":157.924,"terms":[{"start":96,"end":104,"say":"páiplain"}]},{"text":"Con eso alcanza para bloquear el merge mediante las reglas de protección de la rama.","start":158.224,"end":162.15,"terms":[{"start":33,"end":38,"say":"merch"}]},{"text":"No hace falta construir nada especial.","start":162.45,"end":164.752,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sí hay que verificar que funciona: forzá un fallo a propósito en una rama de prueba y confirmá que el pull request queda bloqueado.","start":165.052,"end":171.092,"terms":[{"start":102,"end":114,"say":"pul ricuést"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":171.892,"end":203.492,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":172.392,"end":173.288,"terms":[]},{"text":"Artefactos siempre, también en fallo.","start":173.588,"end":175.611,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Guardá el resultado como artefacto siempre, también cuando falla.","start":176.161,"end":179.496,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sin el detalle nadie puede diagnosticar.","start":179.796,"end":181.935,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Configurá el paso que guarda los resultados para que se ejecute aunque el eval falle.","start":182.485,"end":186.504,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es un detalle de configuración que se olvida seguido, y entonces el pipeline queda en rojo sin ninguna información sobre qué caso falló ni qué respondió el modelo.","start":186.804,"end":194.202,"terms":[{"start":68,"end":76,"say":"páiplain"}]},{"text":"El artefacto tiene que incluir entradas, salidas y veredictos por caso.","start":194.502,"end":198.255,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con ese archivo, quien abrió el PR puede diagnosticar en minutos sin volver a correr todo en su máquina.","start":198.555,"end":203.492,"terms":[{"start":32,"end":34,"say":"pe erre"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":204.292,"end":237.623,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":204.792,"end":205.793,"terms":[]},{"text":"Controlar el costo.","start":206.093,"end":207.361,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Controlá el costo: filtro por paths, caché de respuestas, subset en PR y una API key de CI con tope de gasto.","start":207.911,"end":213.777,"terms":[{"start":68,"end":70,"say":"pe erre"},{"start":81,"end":84,"say":"ki"},{"start":88,"end":90,"say":"ci ai"}]}],[{"text":"Hay cuatro palancas.","start":214.327,"end":215.711,"terms":[]},{"text":"Filtros por ruta, para no correr evals en cambios que no los afectan.","start":216.011,"end":219.566,"terms":[]},{"text":"Caché de respuestas, para no pagar dos veces por la misma entrada con el mismo prompt.","start":219.866,"end":224.049,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un subconjunto en el nivel de PR en lugar del conjunto completo.","start":224.349,"end":227.66,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"pe erre"}]},{"text":"Y una clave de API exclusiva de CI con tope de gasto, de modo que un error de configuración o un bucle no consuman el presupuesto del mes.","start":227.96,"end":234.604,"terms":[{"start":32,"end":34,"say":"ci ai"}]},{"text":"Publicá el gasto de cada corrida junto al resultado.","start":234.904,"end":237.623,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":238.423,"end":272.66,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":238.923,"end":239.924,"terms":[]},{"text":"El nivel nocturno.","start":240.224,"end":241.411,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Sumá el nivel nocturno con el set completo, cinco corridas, el juez y el red teaming.","start":241.961,"end":246.015,"terms":[{"start":73,"end":84,"say":"red tíming"}]},{"text":"Ahí mirás tendencia, no un PR puntual.","start":246.315,"end":248.663,"terms":[{"start":27,"end":29,"say":"pe erre"}]}],[{"text":"Una vez por día, con el conjunto de test completo, cinco repeticiones, el juez calibrado y el red teaming.","start":249.213,"end":254.533,"terms":[{"start":94,"end":105,"say":"red tíming"}]},{"text":"Acá sí se mide con precisión.","start":254.833,"end":256.717,"terms":[]},{"text":"El resultado no bloquea un PR en particular: alerta al equipo y alimenta un gráfico de tendencia.","start":257.017,"end":262.093,"terms":[{"start":27,"end":29,"say":"pe erre"}]},{"text":"Es donde vas a detectar los problemas que ningún PR introdujo, como una actualización silenciosa del proveedor o una degradación gradual.","start":262.393,"end":269.222,"terms":[{"start":49,"end":51,"say":"pe erre"}]},{"text":"Como corre de noche, el tiempo y el costo son más tolerables.","start":269.522,"end":272.66,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":273.46,"end":304.967,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":273.96,"end":274.926,"terms":[]},{"text":"El nivel de release.","start":275.226,"end":276.471,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":19,"say":"rilís"}]}],[{"text":"En release corré regresión y performance, y dejá la decisión final a una persona con el reporte adelante.","start":277.021,"end":282.236,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":10,"say":"rilís"},{"start":29,"end":40,"say":"perfórmans"}]}],[{"text":"Antes de publicar corren la regresión completa y las pruebas de performance.","start":282.786,"end":286.458,"terms":[{"start":64,"end":75,"say":"perfórmans"}]},{"text":"La diferencia con los otros niveles es quién decide.","start":286.758,"end":289.372,"terms":[]},{"text":"Acá el resultado es un reporte que una persona lee y firma.","start":289.672,"end":293.03,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los gates automáticos cubren lo que se puede expresar como umbral.","start":293.33,"end":296.77,"terms":[{"start":4,"end":9,"say":"gueits"}]},{"text":"La decisión de salir con una métrica en el límite o con un riesgo conocido es un juicio de negocio, y alguien tiene que hacerse responsable de ella con la información delante.","start":297.07,"end":304.967,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":305.767,"end":340.356,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":306.267,"end":307.187,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un equipo arma los tres niveles.","start":307.737,"end":309.667,"terms":[]},{"text":"El de PR corre quince casos con tres repeticiones, solo ante cambios en las carpetas de prompts y evals, y tarda cuatro minutos.","start":309.967,"end":316.494,"terms":[{"start":6,"end":8,"say":"pe erre"}]},{"text":"La primera semana bloquea un pull request donde un cambio de redacción rompía el formato JSON en dos casos.","start":316.794,"end":322.091,"terms":[{"start":29,"end":41,"say":"pul ricuést"},{"start":89,"end":93,"say":"yeison"}]},{"text":"El autor abre el artefacto, ve las dos salidas y lo corrige en diez minutos.","start":322.391,"end":326.062,"terms":[]},{"text":"El nivel nocturno corre ciento veinte casos con cinco repeticiones.","start":326.362,"end":329.767,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un martes, la tasa de aprobación cae cuatro puntos sin que hubiera ningún PR mergeado.","start":330.067,"end":334.504,"terms":[{"start":74,"end":76,"say":"pe erre"}]},{"text":"La ficha de corrida muestra que el modelo que respondió no era el esperado, por un alias sin fijar que nadie había notado.","start":334.804,"end":340.356,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":341.156,"end":356.257,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":341.656,"end":343.18,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Correr el conjunto completo en cada pull request.","start":343.73,"end":346.089,"terms":[{"start":36,"end":48,"say":"pul ricuést"}]}],[{"text":"No guardar artefactos cuando el eval falla.","start":346.639,"end":349.022,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Usar en CI una clave de API sin tope de gasto.","start":349.572,"end":352.466,"terms":[{"start":8,"end":10,"say":"ci ai"}]}],[{"text":"Tratar un verde aislado como prueba de que la calidad se mantiene.","start":353.016,"end":356.257,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":357.057,"end":374.004,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":357.557,"end":359.824,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El nivel de PR corre solo ante cambios relevantes y en pocos minutos.","start":360.374,"end":363.953,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"pe erre"}]}],[{"text":"Un fallo forzado bloquea el merge.","start":364.503,"end":366.467,"terms":[{"start":28,"end":33,"say":"merch"}]}],[{"text":"Los resultados se guardan como artefacto también cuando falla.","start":367.017,"end":370.34,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Existe un nivel nocturno con tendencia y una clave con tope.","start":370.89,"end":374.004,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":374.804,"end":383.883,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":375.304,"end":376.874,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"El mismo código puede pasar un gate y fallar horas después.","start":377.424,"end":380.631,"terms":[{"start":31,"end":35,"say":"gueit"}]},{"text":"Con modelos, un verde puntual no reemplaza a la tendencia.","start":380.931,"end":383.883,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":384.683,"end":398.614,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 28.","start":385.183,"end":386.799,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":387.099,"end":392.338,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 29: Infra de QA con IA: runners efímeros, modelos locales, secretos y costos.","start":392.888,"end":398.614,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":37,"end":39,"say":"i a"},{"start":41,"end":48,"say":"ráners"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d28-en","day":28,"lang":"en","title":"Evals in CI: quality gates for AI features","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d28-en.mp4","duration":436.314,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":16.12,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 28 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.299,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Evals in CI: quality gates for AI features.","start":4.849,"end":8.404,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"How to bring evals into the pipeline at three tiers (PR, nightly and release) with a gate that blocks merges and controlled cost.","start":8.954,"end":16.12,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":16.92,"end":38.614,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An eval that runs only when someone remembers ends up not running.","start":17.42,"end":21.498,"terms":[]},{"text":"The way to sustain the quality of an AI feature over time is the same as for the rest of software: a gate in the pipeline that blocks the change when something gets worse.","start":21.798,"end":30.984,"terms":[]},{"text":"What changes are the constraints.","start":31.284,"end":33.272,"terms":[]},{"text":"Evals cost money per run, take longer than a unit test and their result varies.","start":33.572,"end":38.614,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":39.414,"end":59.713,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Putting every eval on every pull request makes the pipeline slow and expensive, and the team ends up skipping it.","start":39.914,"end":46.046,"terms":[]},{"text":"Putting in none lets regressions through.","start":46.346,"end":48.659,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide organizes evals into three tiers, per PR, nightly and per release, each with a different scope, cost and consequence, and shows how to keep spending under control.","start":48.959,"end":59.713,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":60.513,"end":74.817,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":61.013,"end":62.71,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A repo with evals (day 8) and a golden dataset (day 17).","start":63.26,"end":66.932,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"CI: GitHub Actions or Jenkins.","start":67.482,"end":70.515,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"An API key stored as a CI secret, with a spending cap.","start":71.065,"end":74.817,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":75.617,"end":81.914,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":76.117,"end":77.397,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":77.697,"end":81.914,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":82.714,"end":85.481,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And this is the next setup file.","start":83.214,"end":85.481,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The three tiers","start":86.281,"end":114.115,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The three tiers.","start":86.781,"end":88.06,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"PR: runs when prompts, evals or AI code change.","start":88.61,"end":92.909,"terms":[]},{"text":"Reduced dev set, 3 runs, format and content checks.","start":93.209,"end":97.263,"terms":[]},{"text":"Blocks the merge.","start":97.563,"end":98.889,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Nightly: every night.","start":99.439,"end":101.334,"terms":[]},{"text":"Full test set x5, judge and red teaming.","start":101.634,"end":104.934,"terms":[]},{"text":"Alerts the team and shows the trend.","start":105.234,"end":107.454,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Release: before shipping.","start":108.004,"end":109.83,"terms":[]},{"text":"Full regression plus performance.","start":110.13,"end":112.141,"terms":[]},{"text":"A person signs off.","start":112.441,"end":114.115,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":114.915,"end":152.704,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":115.415,"end":116.451,"terms":[]},{"text":"The PR tier.","start":116.751,"end":118.17,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Start with the PR tier: a small, fast subset that runs only when prompts, evals or AI code change.","start":118.72,"end":125.944,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"It's a small, fast subset designed to answer within a few minutes.","start":126.494,"end":130.375,"terms":[]},{"text":"It runs only when the change touches prompts, eval config or code that calls the model.","start":130.675,"end":136.064,"terms":[]},{"text":"For a frontend style change, spending tokens makes no sense.","start":136.364,"end":140.373,"terms":[]},{"text":"Include the deterministic format and content checks, and a reduced set of cases with few repetitions.","start":140.673,"end":147.037,"terms":[]},{"text":"Its job isn't measuring quality precisely, but catching obvious breakage before the merge.","start":147.337,"end":152.704,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":153.504,"end":187.357,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":154.004,"end":155.202,"terms":[]},{"text":"The exit code as the gate.","start":155.502,"end":157.479,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The eval command returns a non-zero exit code when cases fail.","start":158.029,"end":162.571,"terms":[]},{"text":"That's enough to block the merge.","start":162.871,"end":164.812,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The mechanism is the same as for any test.","start":165.362,"end":168.024,"terms":[]},{"text":"The eval command ends with a non-zero code when cases fail, and the pipeline marks the step red.","start":168.324,"end":174.271,"terms":[]},{"text":"That's enough to block the merge through branch protection rules.","start":174.571,"end":178.195,"terms":[]},{"text":"Nothing special needs building.","start":178.495,"end":180.344,"terms":[]},{"text":"You do need to verify it works: force a failure on purpose on a test branch and confirm the pull request ends up blocked.","start":180.644,"end":187.357,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":188.157,"end":223.496,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":188.657,"end":189.74,"terms":[]},{"text":"Artifacts always, on failure too.","start":190.04,"end":192.875,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Always save the result as an artifact, including on failure.","start":193.425,"end":197.247,"terms":[]},{"text":"Without the detail nobody can diagnose.","start":197.547,"end":200.197,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Configure the step that saves results to run even when the eval fails.","start":200.747,"end":204.836,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's a config detail often forgotten, and then the pipeline goes red with no information about which case failed or what the model answered.","start":205.136,"end":212.674,"terms":[]},{"text":"The artifact must include inputs, outputs and verdicts per case.","start":212.974,"end":217.284,"terms":[]},{"text":"With that file, whoever opened the PR can diagnose in minutes without rerunning everything on their machine.","start":217.584,"end":223.496,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":224.296,"end":260.053,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":224.796,"end":225.809,"terms":[]},{"text":"Controlling cost.","start":226.109,"end":227.423,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Control cost: path filters, response caching, a subset on PRs and a CI API key with a spending cap.","start":227.973,"end":235.534,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"There are four levers.","start":236.084,"end":237.457,"terms":[]},{"text":"Path filters, so evals don't run on changes that don't affect them.","start":237.757,"end":242.055,"terms":[]},{"text":"Response caching, so you don't pay twice for the same input with the same prompt.","start":242.355,"end":247.071,"terms":[]},{"text":"A subset at the PR tier instead of the full set.","start":247.371,"end":250.416,"terms":[]},{"text":"And a CI-only API key with a spending cap, so a config error or a loop doesn't consume the month's budget.","start":250.716,"end":257.266,"terms":[]},{"text":"Publish each run's spend next to the result.","start":257.566,"end":260.053,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":260.853,"end":297.04,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":261.353,"end":262.482,"terms":[]},{"text":"The nightly tier.","start":262.782,"end":263.958,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Add the nightly tier with the full set, five runs, the judge and red teaming.","start":264.508,"end":269.154,"terms":[]},{"text":"That's where you watch the trend, not a single PR.","start":269.454,"end":272.626,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Once a day, with the full test set, five repetitions, the calibrated judge and red teaming.","start":273.176,"end":278.856,"terms":[]},{"text":"This is where you measure precisely.","start":279.156,"end":281.376,"terms":[]},{"text":"The result doesn't block a particular PR: it alerts the team and feeds a trend chart.","start":281.676,"end":286.451,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's where you'll detect problems no PR introduced, such as a silent provider update or gradual degradation.","start":286.751,"end":293.208,"terms":[]},{"text":"Since it runs at night, time and cost are more tolerable.","start":293.508,"end":297.04,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":297.84,"end":330.904,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":298.34,"end":299.527,"terms":[]},{"text":"The release tier.","start":299.827,"end":300.956,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"At release run regression and performance, and leave the final call to a person with the report in front of them.","start":301.506,"end":307.325,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Before shipping, full regression and performance tests run.","start":307.875,"end":311.674,"terms":[]},{"text":"The difference from the other tiers is who decides.","start":311.974,"end":314.717,"terms":[]},{"text":"Here the result is a report a person reads and signs.","start":315.017,"end":318.294,"terms":[]},{"text":"Automatic gates cover what can be expressed as a threshold.","start":318.594,"end":321.928,"terms":[]},{"text":"The decision to ship with a metric at the limit or with a known risk is a business judgment, and someone has to take responsibility for it with the information in front of them.","start":322.228,"end":330.904,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":331.704,"end":371.39,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":332.204,"end":333.309,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A team sets up the three tiers.","start":333.859,"end":335.94,"terms":[]},{"text":"The PR one runs fifteen cases with three repetitions, only on changes to the prompts and evals folders, and takes four minutes.","start":336.24,"end":343.94,"terms":[]},{"text":"In the first week it blocks a pull request where a wording change broke the JSON format in two cases.","start":344.24,"end":350.326,"terms":[]},{"text":"The author opens the artifact, sees the two outputs and fixes it in ten minutes.","start":350.626,"end":355.366,"terms":[]},{"text":"The nightly tier runs one hundred and twenty cases with five repetitions.","start":355.666,"end":359.732,"terms":[]},{"text":"One Tuesday the pass rate drops four points with no PR merged.","start":360.032,"end":364.295,"terms":[]},{"text":"The run card shows the model that answered wasn't the expected one, because of an unpinned alias nobody had noticed.","start":364.595,"end":371.39,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":372.19,"end":388.638,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":372.69,"end":374.353,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Running the full set on every pull request.","start":374.903,"end":377.518,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Not saving artifacts when the eval fails.","start":378.068,"end":380.996,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Using an API key with no spending cap in CI.","start":381.546,"end":384.881,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Treating a single green as proof that quality holds.","start":385.431,"end":388.638,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":389.438,"end":407.093,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":389.938,"end":392.181,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The PR tier runs only on relevant changes and within minutes.","start":392.731,"end":396.669,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A forced failure blocks the merge.","start":397.219,"end":399.602,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Results are saved as an artifact on failure too.","start":400.152,"end":403.278,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A nightly tier with a trend and a capped key exists.","start":403.828,"end":407.093,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":407.893,"end":417.773,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":408.393,"end":410.288,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The same code can pass a gate and fail hours later.","start":410.838,"end":413.999,"terms":[]},{"text":"With models, a single green doesn't replace the trend.","start":414.299,"end":417.773,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":418.573,"end":434.814,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 28.","start":419.073,"end":420.642,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":420.942,"end":426.924,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 29: QA infra for AI: ephemeral runners, local models, secrets and cost.","start":427.474,"end":434.814,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d29-es","day":29,"lang":"es","title":"Infra de QA con IA: runners efímeros, modelos locales, secretos y costos","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d29-es.mp4","duration":432.044,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":14.878,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 29 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.672,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Infra de QA con IA: runners efímeros, modelos locales, secretos y costos.","start":4.222,"end":8.683,"terms":[{"start":9,"end":11,"say":"cu a"},{"start":16,"end":18,"say":"i a"},{"start":20,"end":27,"say":"ráners"}]}],[{"text":"Las cinco decisiones de infraestructura que hacen que las pruebas con IA sean reproducibles, seguras y pagables.","start":9.233,"end":14.878,"terms":[{"start":70,"end":72,"say":"i a"}]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":15.678,"end":32.715,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los evals, los agentes y las pruebas de seguridad de los últimos días corren sobre algo: runners, contenedores, modelos, claves y datos.","start":16.178,"end":23.391,"terms":[{"start":89,"end":96,"say":"ráners"}]},{"text":"Esa infraestructura rara vez se diseña.","start":23.691,"end":25.945,"terms":[]},{"text":"Se va armando con lo que hay, y un día el pipeline cuesta el triple, un resultado no se puede reproducir o una clave aparece en un log.","start":26.245,"end":32.715,"terms":[{"start":42,"end":50,"say":"páiplain"}]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":33.515,"end":57.101,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Las decisiones de infraestructura para QA con IA son pocas y tienen consecuencias largas.","start":34.015,"end":38.545,"terms":[{"start":39,"end":41,"say":"cu a"},{"start":46,"end":48,"say":"i a"}]},{"text":"Dónde corre el modelo define el costo y la privacidad.","start":38.845,"end":41.658,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cómo son los runners define la reproducibilidad.","start":41.958,"end":44.48,"terms":[{"start":13,"end":20,"say":"ráners"}]},{"text":"Dónde trabaja un agente define cuánto daño puede causar un error.","start":44.78,"end":48.056,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esta guía recorre esas decisiones con una regla práctica para cada una, y con dos métricas que casi nadie publica y que conviene tener desde el primer día: tokens y gasto por corrida.","start":48.356,"end":57.101,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":57.901,"end":64.478,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":58.401,"end":60.331,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El pipeline del día 28.","start":60.881,"end":62.695,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":11,"say":"páiplain"}]}],[{"text":"Docker en el runner.","start":63.245,"end":64.478,"terms":[{"start":13,"end":19,"say":"ráner"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":65.278,"end":70.6,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":65.778,"end":66.953,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":67.253,"end":70.6,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Las cinco decisiones","start":71.4,"end":113.087,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Las cinco decisiones.","start":71.9,"end":73.342,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Dónde corre el modelo: API en la nube (simple, paga por token, los datos salen) o modelo local en el runner (gratis, privado, más lento y menos capaz).","start":73.892,"end":81.499,"terms":[{"start":101,"end":107,"say":"ráner"}]},{"text":"Mi criterio: local para smoke y formato, nube para evals con juez.","start":81.799,"end":85.656,"terms":[{"start":24,"end":29,"say":"smouk"}]}],[{"text":"Runners: persistentes o efímeros (un contenedor limpio por corrida).","start":86.206,"end":89.738,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":7,"say":"ráners"}]},{"text":"Mi criterio: efímeros siempre.","start":90.038,"end":91.887,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Dónde trabaja un agente: en el runner con credenciales o en un sandbox sin credenciales de producción y con salida de red limitada.","start":92.437,"end":98.628,"terms":[{"start":31,"end":37,"say":"ráner"}]},{"text":"Mi criterio: sandbox.","start":98.928,"end":100.382,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Secretos: una key compartida o una por ambiente, con permisos mínimos y tope de gasto.","start":100.932,"end":105.242,"terms":[{"start":14,"end":17,"say":"ki"}]},{"text":"Mi criterio: por ambiente.","start":105.542,"end":107.17,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Datos: copia de producción o sintéticos y anonimizados.","start":107.72,"end":110.764,"terms":[]},{"text":"Mi criterio: nunca producción.","start":111.064,"end":113.087,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":113.887,"end":153.592,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":114.387,"end":115.283,"terms":[]},{"text":"Dónde corre el modelo.","start":115.583,"end":116.747,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Decidí dónde corre el modelo en CI.","start":117.297,"end":119.32,"terms":[{"start":32,"end":34,"say":"ci ai"}]},{"text":"Un modelo local como servicio sirve para smoke y checks de formato sin costo; los evals con juez necesitan un modelo más capaz.","start":119.62,"end":125.985,"terms":[{"start":41,"end":46,"say":"smouk"}]}],[{"text":"Hay dos opciones, y lo habitual es combinarlas.","start":126.535,"end":129.359,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un modelo local, levantado como servicio dentro del runner, no cuesta por token y no saca datos de tu infraestructura, pero es más lento y menos capaz.","start":129.659,"end":137.393,"terms":[{"start":52,"end":58,"say":"ráner"}]},{"text":"Una API en la nube ofrece modelos más capaces, cobra por uso y recibe tus datos.","start":137.693,"end":142.131,"terms":[]},{"text":"La regla que mejor funciona es asignar cada trabajo al modelo más barato que lo resuelve: chequeos de humo y de formato con el modelo local, evals con juez con un modelo más capaz.","start":142.431,"end":150.979,"terms":[]},{"text":"Medí antes de asumir que el chico alcanza.","start":151.279,"end":153.592,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":154.392,"end":192.634,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":154.892,"end":155.788,"terms":[]},{"text":"Runners efímeros.","start":156.088,"end":157.356,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":7,"say":"ráners"}]}],[{"text":"Usá runners efímeros: cada corrida arranca limpia.","start":157.906,"end":160.765,"terms":[{"start":4,"end":11,"say":"ráners"}]},{"text":"El estado que queda de una corrida anterior es una causa frecuente de resultados que no se pueden reproducir.","start":161.065,"end":166.211,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un runner efímero arranca limpio en cada corrida: un contenedor nuevo, sin archivos, cachés ni procesos de la corrida anterior.","start":166.761,"end":172.882,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":9,"say":"ráner"}]},{"text":"Los runners persistentes acumulan estado, como una dependencia instalada a mano, un archivo temporal o un modelo que alguien actualizó.","start":173.182,"end":179.837,"terms":[{"start":4,"end":11,"say":"ráners"}]},{"text":"Ese estado hace que el mismo commit dé resultados distintos según en qué máquina cayó.","start":180.137,"end":184.261,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con modelos, que ya aportan su propia variación, sumar la del entorno hace imposible saber qué cambió.","start":184.561,"end":189.521,"terms":[]},{"text":"Efímero siempre, con las versiones fijadas en la imagen.","start":189.821,"end":192.634,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":193.434,"end":229.096,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":193.934,"end":194.83,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sandbox para agentes.","start":195.13,"end":196.456,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Si un agente ejecuta comandos en CI, que lo haga en un sandbox: contenedor sin credenciales de producción y con salida de red restringida.","start":197.006,"end":203.615,"terms":[{"start":33,"end":35,"say":"ci ai"}]}],[{"text":"Si un agente ejecuta comandos dentro del pipeline, por ejemplo para reparar un test o investigar un fallo, tiene que hacerlo en un entorno aislado: un contenedor sin credenciales de producción, con el sistema de archivos limitado al repositorio y con la salida de red restringida a los destinos necesarios.","start":204.165,"end":218.378,"terms":[{"start":41,"end":49,"say":"páiplain"}]},{"text":"El agente lee contenido que puede traer instrucciones maliciosas, como vimos en el día veinticuatro.","start":218.678,"end":223.476,"terms":[]},{"text":"El sandbox garantiza que, si una de esas instrucciones tiene efecto, no haya nada valioso a su alcance.","start":223.776,"end":229.096,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":229.896,"end":266.001,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":230.396,"end":231.397,"terms":[]},{"text":"Secretos.","start":231.697,"end":232.617,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Secretos: una API key por ambiente, con permisos mínimos y tope de gasto, y verificá que no aparezca en logs ni en artefactos.","start":233.167,"end":239.613,"terms":[{"start":18,"end":21,"say":"ki"}]}],[{"text":"Usá una clave de API por entorno: una para desarrollo, otra para CI y otra para producción.","start":240.163,"end":245.529,"terms":[{"start":65,"end":67,"say":"ci ai"}]},{"text":"Cada una con los permisos mínimos y con un tope de gasto.","start":245.829,"end":248.746,"terms":[]},{"text":"Así una clave filtrada o un bucle descontrolado en CI no afectan a producción ni agotan el presupuesto general.","start":249.046,"end":254.494,"terms":[{"start":51,"end":53,"say":"ci ai"}]},{"text":"Guardalas en el gestor de secretos del sistema de CI, nunca en el repositorio.","start":254.794,"end":258.848,"terms":[{"start":50,"end":52,"say":"ci ai"}]},{"text":"Y verificá de forma activa que no aparezcan en los logs ni en los artefactos, porque algunas herramientas imprimen la configuración completa al fallar.","start":259.148,"end":266.001,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":266.801,"end":300.109,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":267.301,"end":268.302,"terms":[]},{"text":"El costo como métrica.","start":268.602,"end":270.091,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Costo como métrica: reportá tokens y gasto por corrida junto al pass rate.","start":270.641,"end":274.37,"terms":[{"start":69,"end":73,"say":"reit"}]},{"text":"Lo que no se mide, crece.","start":274.67,"end":276.263,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Publicá en cada corrida los tokens consumidos y el gasto, al lado de la tasa de aprobación.","start":276.813,"end":281.321,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lo que no se mide crece.","start":281.621,"end":283.156,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un dataset que se duplica, un juez que se agrega o una repetición más son todos cambios razonables que nadie suma.","start":283.456,"end":288.892,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con el número a la vista, cada cambio muestra su costo y el equipo puede decidir si vale la pena.","start":289.192,"end":293.838,"terms":[]},{"text":"También permite detectar anomalías: una corrida que cuesta diez veces lo habitual suele indicar un error, no más trabajo.","start":294.138,"end":300.109,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":300.909,"end":334.981,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":301.409,"end":302.375,"terms":[]},{"text":"Reproducibilidad.","start":302.675,"end":303.885,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Reproducibilidad: versiones fijas de herramientas (lockfile) y de modelos, y los resultados archivados con su ficha de corrida.","start":304.435,"end":310.602,"terms":[{"start":51,"end":59,"say":"lókfail"}]}],[{"text":"Dos cosas hacen reproducible una corrida.","start":311.152,"end":313.454,"terms":[]},{"text":"La primera es fijar versiones: las herramientas mediante el lockfile y la imagen del contenedor, y los modelos mediante su identificador exacto.","start":313.754,"end":320.966,"terms":[{"start":60,"end":68,"say":"lókfail"}]},{"text":"La segunda es archivar los resultados con su ficha de corrida, la del día quince, durante un tiempo definido.","start":321.266,"end":326.842,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cuando alguien pregunte por qué un resultado cambió entre dos fechas, vas a poder comparar qué versión de cada cosa se usó en cada una, en lugar de intentar recordarlo.","start":327.142,"end":334.981,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":335.781,"end":369.72,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":336.281,"end":337.201,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un equipo revisa su pipeline de evals después de un mes con gastos más altos de lo previsto.","start":337.751,"end":342.189,"terms":[{"start":20,"end":28,"say":"páiplain"}]},{"text":"Al publicar los tokens por corrida, descubren que el chequeo de formato, que corre en cada pull request, usa el modelo más caro.","start":342.489,"end":348.296,"terms":[{"start":91,"end":103,"say":"pul ricuést"}]},{"text":"Lo pasan a un modelo local levantado como servicio en el runner.","start":348.596,"end":351.873,"terms":[{"start":57,"end":63,"say":"ráner"}]},{"text":"El formato se valida igual, y ese paso deja de tener costo.","start":352.173,"end":355.566,"terms":[]},{"text":"Los evals con juez siguen en la nube.","start":355.866,"end":357.854,"terms":[]},{"text":"Encuentran además que dos de sus tres runners eran persistentes y tenían versiones distintas de una herramienta, lo que explicaba resultados diferentes para el mismo commit.","start":358.154,"end":366.121,"terms":[{"start":38,"end":45,"say":"ráners"}]},{"text":"Los reemplazan por contenedores efímeros con la imagen fijada.","start":366.421,"end":369.72,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":370.52,"end":386.91,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":371.02,"end":372.544,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Usar el modelo más caro para chequeos que resuelve uno local.","start":373.094,"end":376.243,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Mantener runners persistentes con estado acumulado.","start":376.793,"end":379.489,"terms":[{"start":9,"end":16,"say":"ráners"}]}],[{"text":"Dejar que un agente corra en CI con credenciales de producción.","start":380.039,"end":383.304,"terms":[{"start":29,"end":31,"say":"ci ai"}]}],[{"text":"Compartir una sola clave de API entre todos los entornos.","start":383.854,"end":386.91,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":387.71,"end":405.458,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":388.21,"end":390.477,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cada tipo de eval tiene asignado un modelo, con su justificación.","start":391.027,"end":394.489,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Los runners son efímeros y la imagen fija las versiones.","start":395.039,"end":398.153,"terms":[{"start":4,"end":11,"say":"ráners"}]}],[{"text":"Los agentes corren en un sandbox sin credenciales de producción.","start":398.703,"end":401.991,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cada corrida publica tokens y gasto junto al resultado.","start":402.541,"end":405.458,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":406.258,"end":416.428,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":406.758,"end":408.328,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Un modelo local chico alcanza para smoke y formato, no para evaluar criterio.","start":408.878,"end":413.072,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":40,"say":"smouk"}]},{"text":"Elegir el modelo por costo sin medir calidad sale caro.","start":413.372,"end":416.428,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":417.228,"end":430.544,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 29.","start":417.728,"end":419.31,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":419.61,"end":424.848,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 30: Observabilidad de LLM en producción: traces, evals online y alertas.","start":425.398,"end":430.544,"terms":[{"start":58,"end":64,"say":"tréises"},{"start":72,"end":78,"say":"onláin"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d29-en","day":29,"lang":"en","title":"QA infra for AI: ephemeral runners, local models, secrets and cost","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d29-en.mp4","duration":481.503,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":16.109,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 29 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.264,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"QA infra for AI: ephemeral runners, local models, secrets and cost.","start":4.814,"end":9.949,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The five infrastructure decisions that keep AI testing reproducible, safe and affordable.","start":10.499,"end":16.109,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":16.909,"end":37.44,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The evals, agents and security tests from the last few days run on something: runners, containers, models, keys and data.","start":17.409,"end":25.295,"terms":[]},{"text":"That infrastructure is rarely designed.","start":25.595,"end":28.059,"terms":[]},{"text":"It gets assembled from whatever is at hand, and one day the pipeline costs three times as much, a result can't be reproduced or a key shows up in a log.","start":28.359,"end":37.44,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":38.24,"end":64.775,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Infrastructure decisions for QA with AI are few and have long consequences.","start":38.74,"end":43.909,"terms":[]},{"text":"Where the model runs defines cost and privacy.","start":44.209,"end":47.172,"terms":[]},{"text":"What runners are like defines reproducibility.","start":47.472,"end":50.389,"terms":[]},{"text":"Where an agent works defines how much damage a mistake can cause.","start":50.689,"end":54.511,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide walks through those decisions with a practical rule for each, and with two metrics almost nobody publishes that are worth having from day one: tokens and spend per run.","start":54.811,"end":64.775,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":65.575,"end":72.071,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":66.075,"end":67.773,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The pipeline from day 28.","start":68.323,"end":70.288,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Docker on the runner.","start":70.838,"end":72.071,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":72.871,"end":79.168,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":73.371,"end":74.651,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":74.951,"end":79.168,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The five decisions","start":79.968,"end":128.493,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The five decisions.","start":80.468,"end":81.991,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Where the model runs: cloud API (simple, pay per token, data leaves) or a local model on the runner (free, private, slower and less capable).","start":82.541,"end":91.635,"terms":[]},{"text":"My rule: local for smoke and format, cloud for judged evals.","start":91.935,"end":96.245,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Runners: persistent or ephemeral (a clean container per run).","start":96.795,"end":100.605,"terms":[]},{"text":"My rule: always ephemeral.","start":100.905,"end":103.242,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Where an agent works: on the runner with credentials or in a sandbox with no production credentials and limited network egress.","start":103.792,"end":110.865,"terms":[]},{"text":"My rule: sandbox.","start":111.165,"end":113.095,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Secrets: one shared key or one per environment, with least privilege and a spending cap.","start":113.645,"end":119.092,"terms":[]},{"text":"My rule: per environment.","start":119.392,"end":121.438,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Data: a production copy or synthetic and anonymized.","start":121.988,"end":125.927,"terms":[]},{"text":"My rule: never production.","start":126.227,"end":128.493,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":129.293,"end":173.689,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":129.793,"end":130.829,"terms":[]},{"text":"Where the model runs.","start":131.129,"end":132.583,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Decide where the model runs in CI.","start":133.133,"end":135.701,"terms":[]},{"text":"A local model as a service covers smoke and format checks at no cost; judged evals need a more capable model.","start":136.001,"end":142.239,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"There are two options, and the usual thing is to combine them.","start":142.789,"end":146.704,"terms":[]},{"text":"A local model, started as a service inside the runner, has no per-token cost and takes no data out of your infrastructure, but it's slower and less capable.","start":147.004,"end":155.586,"terms":[]},{"text":"A cloud API offers more capable models, charges per use and receives your data.","start":155.886,"end":161.195,"terms":[]},{"text":"The rule that works best is assigning each job to the cheapest model that solves it: smoke and format checks with the local model, judged evals with a more capable one.","start":161.495,"end":170.727,"terms":[]},{"text":"Measure before assuming the small one is enough.","start":171.027,"end":173.689,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":174.489,"end":218.21,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":174.989,"end":176.187,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ephemeral runners.","start":176.487,"end":177.906,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Use ephemeral runners: every run starts clean.","start":178.456,"end":181.512,"terms":[]},{"text":"Leftover state from a previous run is a frequent cause of results you can't reproduce.","start":181.812,"end":186.633,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"An ephemeral runner starts clean on every run: a new container, with no files, caches or processes from the previous run.","start":187.183,"end":194.72,"terms":[]},{"text":"Persistent runners accumulate state, such as a dependency installed by hand, a temporary file or a model someone updated.","start":195.02,"end":202.5,"terms":[]},{"text":"That state makes the same commit give different results depending on which machine it landed on.","start":202.8,"end":207.795,"terms":[]},{"text":"With models, which already bring their own variation, adding the environment's makes it impossible to know what changed.","start":208.095,"end":214.703,"terms":[]},{"text":"Always ephemeral, with versions pinned in the image.","start":215.003,"end":218.21,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":219.01,"end":261.546,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":219.51,"end":220.593,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sandbox for agents.","start":220.893,"end":222.567,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"If an agent executes commands in CI, have it do so in a sandbox: a container with no production credentials and restricted network egress.","start":223.117,"end":231.491,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"If an agent executes commands inside the pipeline, for example to repair a test or investigate a failure, it must do so in an isolated environment: a container with no production credentials, with the file system limited to the repository and with network egress restricted to the necessary destinations.","start":232.041,"end":249.168,"terms":[]},{"text":"The agent reads content that may carry malicious instructions, as we saw on day twenty-four.","start":249.468,"end":254.881,"terms":[]},{"text":"The sandbox guarantees that, if one of those instructions takes effect, nothing valuable is within its reach.","start":255.181,"end":261.546,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":262.346,"end":300.842,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":262.846,"end":263.858,"terms":[]},{"text":"Secrets.","start":264.158,"end":265.032,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Secrets: one API key per environment, with least privilege and a spending cap, and verify it doesn't show up in logs or artifacts.","start":265.582,"end":273.479,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Use one API key per environment: one for development, one for CI and one for production.","start":274.029,"end":279.756,"terms":[]},{"text":"Each with least privilege and a spending cap.","start":280.056,"end":282.752,"terms":[]},{"text":"That way a leaked key or a runaway loop in CI affects neither production nor the overall budget.","start":283.052,"end":288.546,"terms":[]},{"text":"Store them in the CI system's secrets manager, never in the repository.","start":288.846,"end":293.063,"terms":[]},{"text":"And actively verify they don't show up in logs or artifacts, because some tools print the full configuration when they fail.","start":293.363,"end":300.842,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":301.642,"end":339.072,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":302.142,"end":303.271,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cost as a metric.","start":303.571,"end":304.921,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cost as a metric: report tokens and spend per run next to the pass rate.","start":305.471,"end":309.955,"terms":[]},{"text":"What isn't measured grows.","start":310.255,"end":312.173,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Publish tokens consumed and spend on every run, next to the pass rate.","start":312.723,"end":317.114,"terms":[]},{"text":"What isn't measured grows.","start":317.414,"end":319.332,"terms":[]},{"text":"A dataset that doubles, a judge that gets added or one more repetition are all reasonable changes nobody adds up.","start":319.632,"end":326.508,"terms":[]},{"text":"With the number in view, each change shows its cost and the team can decide whether it's worth it.","start":326.808,"end":331.954,"terms":[]},{"text":"It also lets you detect anomalies: a run costing ten times the usual tends to indicate an error, not more work.","start":332.254,"end":339.072,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":339.872,"end":375.953,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":340.372,"end":341.559,"terms":[]},{"text":"Reproducibility.","start":341.859,"end":343.429,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Reproducibility: pinned tool versions (lockfile) and model versions, and results archived with their run card.","start":343.979,"end":350.936,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Two things make a run reproducible.","start":351.486,"end":353.787,"terms":[]},{"text":"The first is pinning versions: tools through the lockfile and the container image, and models through their exact identifier.","start":354.087,"end":360.73,"terms":[]},{"text":"The second is archiving results with their run card, the one from day fifteen, for a defined period.","start":361.03,"end":367.035,"terms":[]},{"text":"When someone asks why a result changed between two dates, you'll be able to compare which version of each thing was used on each, instead of trying to remember.","start":367.335,"end":375.953,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":376.753,"end":413.78,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":377.253,"end":378.358,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A team reviews its eval pipeline after a month with higher spending than planned.","start":378.908,"end":383.125,"terms":[]},{"text":"On publishing tokens per run, they discover the format check, which runs on every pull request, uses the most expensive model.","start":383.425,"end":391.416,"terms":[]},{"text":"They move it to a local model started as a service on the runner.","start":391.716,"end":394.992,"terms":[]},{"text":"Format is validated just the same, and that step stops costing anything.","start":395.292,"end":399.695,"terms":[]},{"text":"Judged evals stay in the cloud.","start":399.995,"end":402.076,"terms":[]},{"text":"They also find that two of their three runners were persistent and had different versions of a tool, which explained different results for the same commit.","start":402.376,"end":410.018,"terms":[]},{"text":"They replace them with ephemeral containers with a pinned image.","start":410.318,"end":413.78,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":414.58,"end":432.096,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":415.08,"end":416.743,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Using the most expensive model for checks a local one handles.","start":417.293,"end":421.046,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Keeping persistent runners with accumulated state.","start":421.596,"end":424.455,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Letting an agent run in CI with production credentials.","start":425.005,"end":428.362,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Sharing a single API key across all environments.","start":428.912,"end":432.096,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":432.896,"end":451.736,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":433.396,"end":435.64,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each type of eval has an assigned model, with its justification.","start":436.19,"end":440.058,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Runners are ephemeral and the image pins versions.","start":440.608,"end":443.676,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Agents run in a sandbox with no production credentials.","start":444.226,"end":447.839,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each run publishes tokens and spend next to the result.","start":448.389,"end":451.736,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":452.536,"end":463.634,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":453.036,"end":454.931,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A small local model is enough for smoke and format, not for judgment.","start":455.481,"end":459.512,"terms":[]},{"text":"Choosing a model on cost without measuring quality gets expensive.","start":459.812,"end":463.634,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":464.434,"end":480.003,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 29.","start":464.934,"end":466.609,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":466.909,"end":472.89,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 30: LLM observability in production: traces, online evals and alerts.","start":473.44,"end":480.003,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d30-es","day":30,"lang":"es","title":"Observabilidad de LLM en producción: traces, evals online y alertas","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d30-es.mp4","duration":447.786,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":15.273,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 30 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.463,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Observabilidad de LLM en producción: traces, evals online y alertas.","start":4.013,"end":8.103,"terms":[{"start":37,"end":43,"say":"tréises"},{"start":51,"end":57,"say":"onláin"}]}],[{"text":"Qué registrar de cada llamada a un modelo en producción, cómo evaluar una muestra de forma continua y qué alertas justifican despertar a alguien.","start":8.653,"end":15.273,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":16.073,"end":33.504,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Todo lo que probaste antes del release describe cómo se comportaba el sistema con tus casos, en tu entorno, ese día.","start":16.573,"end":22.079,"terms":[{"start":31,"end":38,"say":"rilís"}]},{"text":"En producción llegan entradas que nadie imaginó, el proveedor actualiza el modelo, los documentos cambian y los usuarios encuentran usos nuevos.","start":22.379,"end":29.626,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un sistema con IA puede degradarse sin que nadie haya hecho un deploy.","start":29.926,"end":33.504,"terms":[{"start":15,"end":17,"say":"i a"}]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":34.304,"end":53.884,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Por eso la calidad en estos sistemas no termina en el gate.","start":34.804,"end":37.779,"terms":[{"start":54,"end":58,"say":"gueit"}]},{"text":"Sigue en producción, con las mismas preguntas y otros instrumentos: trazas en lugar de reportes, evals sobre tráfico real en lugar de datasets, alertas en lugar de umbrales de CI.","start":38.079,"end":47.172,"terms":[{"start":176,"end":178,"say":"ci ai"}]},{"text":"Esta guía arma ese circuito y lo cierra, de modo que cada fallo encontrado en producción vuelva como un caso de prueba y no se repita.","start":47.472,"end":53.884,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":54.684,"end":69.766,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":55.184,"end":57.114,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Una feature con LLM en un ambiente con tráfico (staging sirve).","start":57.664,"end":61.056,"terms":[{"start":4,"end":11,"say":"fícher"},{"start":48,"end":55,"say":"stéiyin"}]}],[{"text":"Una herramienta de trazas para LLM; Langfuse es open source y se puede levantar en local.","start":61.606,"end":66.299,"terms":[{"start":48,"end":59,"say":"open sors"}]}],[{"text":"Los scorers del eval harness (días 8 y 16).","start":66.849,"end":69.766,"terms":[{"start":21,"end":28,"say":"járnes"}]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":70.566,"end":75.888,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este es el setup.","start":71.066,"end":72.241,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":16,"say":"sétap"}]},{"text":"Pausá el video si querés copiarlo: está completo en la guía escrita.","start":72.541,"end":75.888,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Qué registrar y qué medir","start":76.688,"end":125.447,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Qué registrar y qué medir.","start":77.188,"end":78.676,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Modelo pedido y modelo que respondió: gen_ai.request.model y gen_ai.response.model (convenciones GenAI de OpenTelemetry).","start":79.226,"end":85.347,"terms":[{"start":45,"end":52,"say":"ricuést"},{"start":97,"end":102,"say":"yen ei ai"}]}],[{"text":"Tokens de entrada y salida: gen_ai.usage.input_tokens y gen_ai.usage.output_tokens.","start":85.897,"end":91.148,"terms":[{"start":69,"end":75,"say":"áutput"}]}],[{"text":"Operación: gen_ai.operation.name.","start":91.698,"end":94.382,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Versión del prompt, latencia total y al primer token: atributos propios y la duración del span.","start":94.932,"end":99.776,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Llamadas a herramientas y resultado: spans hijos.","start":100.326,"end":103.22,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Indicador de calidad: pass rate de los evals online.","start":103.77,"end":106.675,"terms":[{"start":27,"end":31,"say":"reit"},{"start":45,"end":51,"say":"onláin"}]},{"text":"Alerta ante una caída sostenida, no un punto aislado.","start":106.975,"end":110.054,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Indicador de latencia: TTFT p95, según el objetivo del día 25.","start":110.604,"end":115.321,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Indicador de errores: 429, 5xx y timeouts por debajo del máximo acordado.","start":115.871,"end":121.481,"terms":[{"start":33,"end":41,"say":"táimauts"}]}],[{"text":"Indicador de costo: gasto por hora contra el presupuesto diario.","start":122.031,"end":125.447,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":126.247,"end":166.566,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":126.747,"end":127.643,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un span por llamada al modelo.","start":127.943,"end":129.618,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Instrumentá cada llamada al modelo como un span con modelo, versión de prompt, tokens, latencia y resultado.","start":130.168,"end":135.825,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las convenciones GenAI de OpenTelemetry te dan los nombres.","start":136.125,"end":139.459,"terms":[{"start":17,"end":22,"say":"yen ei ai"}]}],[{"text":"Instrumentá cada llamada al modelo como un span que registre qué modelo se pidió y cuál respondió, la versión del prompt, los tokens de entrada y de salida, la latencia y el resultado.","start":140.009,"end":149.068,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las convenciones de OpenTelemetry para IA generativa definen nombres estándar para esos atributos.","start":149.368,"end":154.699,"terms":[{"start":39,"end":41,"say":"i a"}]},{"text":"Usarlas evita inventar un esquema propio y permite cambiar de herramienta de observabilidad sin reinstrumentar.","start":154.999,"end":160.284,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las llamadas a herramientas y las búsquedas de un RAG van como spans hijos, para poder ver la cadena completa de una respuesta.","start":160.584,"end":166.566,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":167.366,"end":203.6,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":167.866,"end":168.763,"terms":[]},{"text":"Qué se guarda y por cuánto tiempo.","start":169.063,"end":170.888,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cuidá lo que guardás: los prompts y respuestas pueden traer datos personales.","start":171.438,"end":175.272,"terms":[]},{"text":"Enmascará o muestreá, y definí retención.","start":175.572,"end":178.152,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Los prompts y las respuestas pueden contener datos personales, y una traza completa los guarda en texto plano.","start":178.702,"end":184.336,"terms":[]},{"text":"Decidí de forma explícita qué se almacena.","start":184.636,"end":186.972,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las opciones son enmascarar los campos sensibles antes de guardar, guardar el contenido completo solo de una muestra, o conservar solo los metadatos por defecto.","start":187.272,"end":195.192,"terms":[]},{"text":"Definí además un plazo de retención y quién tiene acceso.","start":195.492,"end":198.479,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es una decisión para tomar junto con seguridad y legales, y conviene tomarla antes de instrumentar.","start":198.779,"end":203.6,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":204.4,"end":240.285,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":204.9,"end":205.796,"terms":[]},{"text":"Evals en línea.","start":206.096,"end":207.155,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Evals online: corré los checks deterministas sobre todo el tráfico y el juez sobre una muestra.","start":207.705,"end":212.364,"terms":[{"start":6,"end":12,"say":"onláin"}]},{"text":"Son los mismos scorers que usás en CI.","start":212.664,"end":214.942,"terms":[{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"ci ai"}]}],[{"text":"Los mismos scorers que usás en CI pueden correr sobre el tráfico real.","start":215.492,"end":219.221,"terms":[{"start":31,"end":33,"say":"ci ai"}]},{"text":"Los chequeos deterministas, de formato, campos obligatorios y largos, son baratos y se aplican a todas las respuestas.","start":219.521,"end":225.956,"terms":[]},{"text":"El juez cuesta tokens, así que se aplica a una muestra.","start":226.256,"end":229.196,"terms":[]},{"text":"El resultado es una tasa de aprobación continua sobre lo que los usuarios reciben de verdad.","start":229.496,"end":234.154,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es la señal que te avisa de una degradación que ningún dataset podía anticipar, porque sale de entradas que no tenías.","start":234.454,"end":240.285,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":241.085,"end":281.453,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":241.585,"end":242.586,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cuatro indicadores con alerta.","start":242.886,"end":244.711,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Definí cuatro indicadores (calidad, latencia, errores y costo) con objetivo y alerta.","start":245.261,"end":250.21,"terms":[]},{"text":"Alertá por tendencia sostenida, no por un caso.","start":250.51,"end":253.31,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Definí cuatro indicadores, cada uno con su objetivo y su alerta.","start":253.86,"end":257.718,"terms":[]},{"text":"Calidad: la tasa de aprobación de los evals en línea.","start":258.018,"end":260.841,"terms":[]},{"text":"Latencia: el tiempo al primer token en el percentil noventa y cinco.","start":261.141,"end":264.65,"terms":[]},{"text":"Errores: límites de cuota, errores del servidor y timeouts.","start":264.95,"end":268.134,"terms":[{"start":50,"end":58,"say":"táimauts"}]},{"text":"Costo: el gasto por hora contra el presupuesto diario.","start":268.434,"end":271.304,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las alertas se configuran sobre tendencia sostenida, no sobre un caso aislado.","start":271.604,"end":275.845,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con un sistema que varía por naturaleza, alertar por cada respuesta mala genera ruido hasta que nadie mira.","start":276.145,"end":281.453,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":282.253,"end":314.457,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":282.753,"end":283.754,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cerrar el circuito.","start":284.054,"end":285.241,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cerrá el circuito: cada fallo de producción, anonimizado, entra al set de regresión.","start":285.791,"end":290.403,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cada fallo encontrado en producción, por una alerta, por un eval en línea o por el reclamo de un usuario, se anonimiza y se suma al conjunto de regresión.","start":290.953,"end":298.792,"terms":[]},{"text":"Este paso convierte la observabilidad en mejora.","start":299.092,"end":301.626,"terms":[]},{"text":"Sin él, el mismo tipo de fallo vuelve con el próximo cambio de prompt.","start":301.926,"end":305.446,"terms":[]},{"text":"Con él, tu dataset se va pareciendo cada vez más al uso real.","start":305.746,"end":309.069,"terms":[]},{"text":"Asigná un responsable y una frecuencia: revisar los fallos de la semana y decidir cuáles entran.","start":309.369,"end":314.457,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":315.257,"end":350.305,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":315.757,"end":316.723,"terms":[]},{"text":"El plan de rollback.","start":317.023,"end":318.14,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":19,"say":"rólbak"}]}],[{"text":"Tené el plan de vuelta atrás escrito: prompt y modelo con versión fija y un flag para volver a la versión anterior sin deploy.","start":318.69,"end":325.079,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tené escrito cómo se vuelve atrás antes de necesitarlo.","start":325.629,"end":328.487,"terms":[]},{"text":"Requiere tres cosas: la versión del prompt y la del modelo fijadas y registradas, la versión anterior todavía disponible, y un flag que permita volver sin hacer un deploy.","start":328.787,"end":337.509,"terms":[]},{"text":"Probá el rollback una vez, en un entorno de prueba.","start":337.809,"end":340.459,"terms":[{"start":9,"end":17,"say":"rólbak"}]},{"text":"Un plan que nunca se ejecutó suele fallar en el peor momento.","start":340.759,"end":343.838,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cuando la alerta de calidad se dispare, la primera acción tiene que ser volver a un estado conocido, y el análisis viene después.","start":344.138,"end":350.305,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":351.105,"end":384.464,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":351.605,"end":352.525,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Un asistente de soporte lleva dos meses en producción con trazas y evals en línea.","start":353.075,"end":357.153,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un jueves, la tasa de aprobación del juez sobre la muestra baja de forma sostenida durante tres horas, sin ningún deploy.","start":357.453,"end":363.435,"terms":[]},{"text":"Salta la alerta de calidad.","start":363.735,"end":365.351,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las trazas muestran que el modelo que responde tiene un identificador distinto del de la semana anterior: el alias que usaban apuntó a una versión nueva.","start":365.651,"end":372.806,"terms":[]},{"text":"Activan el flag para volver a la versión fijada anterior y la tasa se recupera.","start":373.106,"end":377.056,"terms":[]},{"text":"Después anonimizan doce conversaciones fallidas, las suman a regresión y corren el eval completo contra la versión nueva antes de decidir si la adoptan.","start":377.356,"end":384.464,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":385.264,"end":401.978,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":385.764,"end":387.287,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Guardar prompts y respuestas completos sin enmascarar ni definir retención.","start":387.837,"end":391.59,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Alertar por cada respuesta fallida en lugar de por tendencia.","start":392.14,"end":395.068,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Registrar solo el modelo pedido y no el que respondió.","start":395.618,"end":398.419,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"No sumar los fallos de producción al conjunto de regresión.","start":398.969,"end":401.978,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":402.778,"end":421.444,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":403.278,"end":405.545,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cada llamada al modelo genera un span con modelo, versión de prompt y tokens.","start":406.095,"end":410.207,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Hay una política escrita de enmascarado, muestreo y retención.","start":410.757,"end":414.173,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Los evals en línea corren con chequeos deterministas y juez sobre muestra.","start":414.723,"end":418.348,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El rollback por flag se probó al menos una vez.","start":418.898,"end":421.444,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":11,"say":"rólbak"}]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":422.244,"end":431.821,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":422.744,"end":424.314,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"El modelo que responde puede no ser el que pediste si usás un alias.","start":424.864,"end":428.221,"terms":[]},{"text":"Registrá ambos o no vas a poder explicar un cambio de comportamiento.","start":428.521,"end":431.821,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":432.621,"end":446.286,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 30.","start":433.121,"end":434.552,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":434.852,"end":440.091,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Seguí con el día 31: Capstone: el pipeline completo de calidad con IA y tu hoja de ruta 2027.","start":440.641,"end":446.286,"terms":[{"start":21,"end":29,"say":"cápstoun"},{"start":34,"end":42,"say":"páiplain"},{"start":67,"end":69,"say":"i a"}]}]]}]},{"id":"d30-en","day":30,"lang":"en","title":"LLM observability in production: traces, online evals and alerts","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d30-en.mp4","duration":491.521,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":17.56,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 30 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.102,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"LLM observability in production: traces, online evals and alerts.","start":4.652,"end":9.461,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"What to record for every production model call, how to continuously evaluate a sample and which alerts justify waking someone up.","start":10.011,"end":17.56,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":18.36,"end":38.334,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Everything you tested before release describes how the system behaved with your cases, in your environment, on that day.","start":18.86,"end":25.829,"terms":[]},{"text":"In production, inputs nobody imagined arrive, the provider updates the model, documents change and users find new uses.","start":26.129,"end":34.049,"terms":[]},{"text":"An AI system can degrade without anyone having deployed anything.","start":34.349,"end":38.334,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":39.134,"end":61.57,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That's why quality in these systems doesn't end at the gate.","start":39.634,"end":42.899,"terms":[]},{"text":"It continues in production, with the same questions and different instruments: traces instead of reports, evals on real traffic instead of datasets, alerts instead of CI thresholds.","start":43.199,"end":53.883,"terms":[]},{"text":"This guide builds that circuit and closes it, so that every failure found in production comes back as a test case and doesn't repeat.","start":54.183,"end":61.57,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":62.37,"end":77.963,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":62.87,"end":64.567,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"An LLM feature in an environment with traffic (staging works).","start":65.117,"end":68.847,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"An LLM tracing tool; Langfuse is open source and can run locally.","start":69.397,"end":73.974,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The scorers from the eval harness (days 8 and 16).","start":74.524,"end":77.963,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Setup","start":78.763,"end":85.06,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This is the setup.","start":79.263,"end":80.543,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.","start":80.843,"end":85.06,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"What to record and what to measure","start":85.86,"end":144.661,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"What to record and what to measure.","start":86.36,"end":88.568,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Requested model and responding model: gen_ai.request.model and gen_ai.response.model (OpenTelemetry GenAI conventions).","start":89.118,"end":98.49,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Input and output tokens: gen_ai.usage.input_tokens and gen_ai.usage.output_tokens.","start":99.04,"end":107.019,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Operation: gen_ai.operation.name.","start":107.569,"end":110.88,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Prompt version, total latency and time to first token: custom attributes and the span duration.","start":111.43,"end":117.296,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tool calls and outcome: child spans.","start":117.846,"end":120.867,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Quality indicator: pass rate of online evals.","start":121.417,"end":124.74,"terms":[]},{"text":"Alert on a sustained drop, not an isolated point.","start":125.04,"end":128.375,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Latency indicator: TTFT p95, per the day 25 target.","start":128.925,"end":133.989,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Error indicator: 429, 5xx and timeouts below the agreed maximum.","start":134.539,"end":140.44,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Cost indicator: hourly spend against the daily budget.","start":140.99,"end":144.661,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":145.461,"end":189.194,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":145.961,"end":146.997,"terms":[]},{"text":"One span per model call.","start":147.297,"end":149.169,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Instrument every model call as a span with model, prompt version, tokens, latency and outcome.","start":149.719,"end":155.887,"terms":[]},{"text":"The OpenTelemetry GenAI conventions give you the names.","start":156.187,"end":159.73,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Instrument every model call as a span recording which model was requested and which one answered, the prompt version, input and output tokens, latency and outcome.","start":160.28,"end":170.43,"terms":[]},{"text":"The OpenTelemetry conventions for generative AI define standard names for those attributes.","start":170.73,"end":176.224,"terms":[]},{"text":"Using them avoids inventing your own schema and lets you change observability tools without re-instrumenting.","start":176.524,"end":182.866,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tool calls and a RAG's searches go as child spans, so you can see the full chain behind an answer.","start":183.166,"end":189.194,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":189.994,"end":227.772,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":190.494,"end":191.692,"terms":[]},{"text":"What gets stored and for how long.","start":191.992,"end":194.015,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Mind what you store: prompts and responses can carry personal data.","start":194.565,"end":198.933,"terms":[]},{"text":"Mask or sample, and set retention.","start":199.233,"end":201.79,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Prompts and responses can contain personal data, and a full trace stores them in plain text.","start":202.34,"end":207.974,"terms":[]},{"text":"Decide explicitly what gets stored.","start":208.274,"end":210.505,"terms":[]},{"text":"The options are masking sensitive fields before saving, storing full content only for a sample, or keeping only metadata by default.","start":210.805,"end":218.587,"terms":[]},{"text":"Also define a retention period and who has access.","start":218.887,"end":221.943,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's a decision to make together with security and legal, and it's best made before instrumenting.","start":222.243,"end":227.772,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":228.572,"end":265.711,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":229.072,"end":230.154,"terms":[]},{"text":"Online evals.","start":230.454,"end":231.827,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Online evals: run deterministic checks on all traffic and the judge on a sample.","start":232.377,"end":237.348,"terms":[]},{"text":"They're the same scorers you use in CI.","start":237.648,"end":240.286,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The same scorers you use in CI can run on real traffic.","start":240.836,"end":244.496,"terms":[]},{"text":"Deterministic checks, for format, required fields and lengths, are cheap and apply to every response.","start":244.796,"end":251.312,"terms":[]},{"text":"The judge costs tokens, so it applies to a sample.","start":251.612,"end":254.819,"terms":[]},{"text":"The result is a continuous pass rate on what users really receive.","start":255.119,"end":258.93,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's the signal that warns you of a degradation no dataset could anticipate, because it comes from inputs you didn't have.","start":259.23,"end":265.711,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":266.511,"end":310.466,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":267.011,"end":268.023,"terms":[]},{"text":"Four indicators with alerts.","start":268.323,"end":270.567,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Define four indicators (quality, latency, errors and cost) with a target and an alert.","start":271.117,"end":276.599,"terms":[]},{"text":"Alert on sustained trend, not on one case.","start":276.899,"end":280.257,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Define four indicators, each with its target and its alert.","start":280.807,"end":284.595,"terms":[]},{"text":"Quality: the pass rate of online evals.","start":284.895,"end":287.811,"terms":[]},{"text":"Latency: time to first token at the ninety-fifth percentile.","start":288.111,"end":292.189,"terms":[]},{"text":"Errors: quota limits, server errors and timeouts.","start":292.489,"end":296.033,"terms":[]},{"text":"Cost: hourly spend against the daily budget.","start":296.333,"end":299.308,"terms":[]},{"text":"Alerts are configured on sustained trend, not on an isolated case.","start":299.608,"end":303.918,"terms":[]},{"text":"With a system that varies by nature, alerting on every bad response generates noise until nobody looks.","start":304.218,"end":310.466,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":311.266,"end":345.92,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":311.766,"end":312.895,"terms":[]},{"text":"Closing the loop.","start":313.195,"end":314.289,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Close the loop: every production failure, anonymized, joins the regression set.","start":314.839,"end":319.997,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Every failure found in production, through an alert, an online eval or a user complaint, gets anonymized and added to the regression set.","start":320.547,"end":328.409,"terms":[]},{"text":"This step turns observability into improvement.","start":328.709,"end":331.858,"terms":[]},{"text":"Without it, the same kind of failure returns with the next prompt change.","start":332.158,"end":336.514,"terms":[]},{"text":"With it, your dataset looks more and more like real usage.","start":336.814,"end":340.555,"terms":[]},{"text":"Assign an owner and a cadence: review the week's failures and decide which ones go in.","start":340.855,"end":345.92,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":346.72,"end":385.658,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":347.22,"end":348.407,"terms":[]},{"text":"The rollback plan.","start":348.707,"end":350.161,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Have the rollback plan written: pinned prompt and model versions and a flag to return to the previous version without a deploy.","start":350.711,"end":357.795,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Have it written down how to go back before you need it.","start":358.345,"end":361.251,"terms":[]},{"text":"It requires three things: the prompt and model versions pinned and recorded, the previous version still available, and a flag that lets you go back without a deploy.","start":361.551,"end":371.491,"terms":[]},{"text":"Try the rollback once, in a test environment.","start":371.791,"end":374.557,"terms":[]},{"text":"A plan that was never executed tends to fail at the worst moment.","start":374.857,"end":378.714,"terms":[]},{"text":"When the quality alert fires, the first action must be returning to a known state, and analysis comes afterwards.","start":379.014,"end":385.658,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":386.458,"end":422.057,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":386.958,"end":388.063,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"A support assistant has been in production for two months with traces and online evals.","start":388.613,"end":393.411,"terms":[]},{"text":"One Thursday, the judge's pass rate on the sample drops steadily for three hours, with no deploy.","start":393.711,"end":399.716,"terms":[]},{"text":"The quality alert fires.","start":400.016,"end":401.795,"terms":[]},{"text":"Traces show the model answering has a different identifier from last week's: the alias they used pointed to a new version.","start":402.095,"end":408.994,"terms":[]},{"text":"They flip the flag back to the previous pinned version and the rate recovers.","start":409.294,"end":413.43,"terms":[]},{"text":"Then they anonymize twelve failed conversations, add them to regression and run the full eval against the new version before deciding whether to adopt it.","start":413.73,"end":422.057,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":422.857,"end":441.116,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":423.357,"end":425.02,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Storing full prompts and responses with no masking or retention policy.","start":425.57,"end":429.775,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Alerting on every failed response instead of on trend.","start":430.325,"end":433.439,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Recording only the requested model and not the one that answered.","start":433.989,"end":437.556,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Not adding production failures to the regression set.","start":438.106,"end":441.116,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":441.916,"end":461.811,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":442.416,"end":444.659,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Each model call generates a span with model, prompt version and tokens.","start":445.209,"end":449.67,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"There is a written policy for masking, sampling and retention.","start":450.22,"end":453.996,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Online evals run with deterministic checks and a judge on a sample.","start":454.546,"end":458.322,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The flag-based rollback was tried at least once.","start":458.872,"end":461.811,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":462.611,"end":473.861,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":463.111,"end":465.007,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The model that answers may not be the one you requested if you use an alias.","start":465.557,"end":469.808,"terms":[]},{"text":"Record both or you won't be able to explain a behavior change.","start":470.108,"end":473.861,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":474.661,"end":490.021,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 30.","start":475.161,"end":476.661,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":476.961,"end":482.943,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Continue with day 31: Capstone: the full AI quality pipeline and your 2027 roadmap.","start":483.493,"end":490.021,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d31-es","day":31,"lang":"es","title":"Capstone: el pipeline completo de calidad con IA y tu hoja de ruta 2027","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d31-es.mp4","duration":426.915,"slides":[{"section":"Video guía","start":0,"end":16.422,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Día 31 de la serie 31 días de QA + IA.","start":0.5,"end":3.765,"terms":[{"start":30,"end":32,"say":"cu a"},{"start":35,"end":37,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Capstone: el pipeline completo de calidad con IA y tu hoja de ruta 2027.","start":4.315,"end":8.683,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":8,"say":"cápstoun"},{"start":13,"end":21,"say":"páiplain"},{"start":46,"end":48,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Los 30 días anteriores unidos en un solo pipeline, un checklist de 12 puntos para autoevaluarte y un plan de 90 días con evidencia pública.","start":9.233,"end":16.422,"terms":[{"start":41,"end":49,"say":"páiplain"}]}]]},{"section":"El problema","start":17.222,"end":35.733,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Durante treinta días viste piezas sueltas: una regla para delegar, un eval, un agente configurado, un modelo de amenazas, una prueba de carga, un gate.","start":17.722,"end":26.026,"terms":[{"start":146,"end":150,"say":"gueit"}]},{"text":"Cada una resuelve un problema concreto.","start":26.326,"end":28.476,"terms":[]},{"text":"El valor mayor aparece cuando se conectan, porque ahí dejan de ser experimentos y pasan a ser un sistema de calidad para software que usa IA.","start":28.776,"end":35.733,"terms":[{"start":121,"end":129,"say":"sóftuer"},{"start":138,"end":140,"say":"i a"}]}]]},{"section":"Cómo encaja","start":36.533,"end":55.754,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Este último día no agrega herramientas.","start":37.033,"end":39.254,"terms":[]},{"text":"Arma el mapa completo, te pide una evaluación honesta de qué tenés funcionando de verdad y te ayuda a elegir qué construir primero.","start":39.554,"end":45.802,"terms":[]},{"text":"La serie no pretende que implementes las doce piezas.","start":46.102,"end":48.868,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pretende que sepas cuáles le importan a tu producto, en qué orden, y que dentro de tres meses puedas mostrar evidencia de haber avanzado.","start":49.168,"end":55.754,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requisitos","start":56.554,"end":64.919,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para seguir esta guía necesitás:","start":57.054,"end":58.984,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El repo qa-ai-lab con lo que hayas construido en la serie.","start":59.534,"end":62.415,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"La matriz de habilidades del día 2.","start":62.965,"end":64.919,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist de 12 piezas","start":65.719,"end":118.631,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Checklist de 12 piezas.","start":66.219,"end":67.835,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Regla de delegación: verificable, barato de revisar, reversible (día 1).","start":68.385,"end":72.649,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Repo con reglas para agentes, herramientas y permisos (día 9).","start":73.199,"end":76.58,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tests escritos por personas, implementación por agentes, con guardrails (días 13 y 14).","start":77.13,"end":82.067,"terms":[{"start":61,"end":71,"say":"gárdreils"}]}],[{"text":"Agentes de Playwright con revisión del plan y del diff (días 10 y 11).","start":82.617,"end":86.16,"terms":[{"start":11,"end":21,"say":"pléirait"}]}],[{"text":"Eval harness con prompt versionado (día 8).","start":86.71,"end":89.174,"terms":[{"start":5,"end":12,"say":"járnes"}]}],[{"text":"Modelos con versión fija y juez calibrado (días 15 y 16).","start":89.724,"end":93.187,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Golden dataset con dev, test y regresión (día 17).","start":93.737,"end":96.723,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Corridas repetidas, rangos y costo por llamada (días 18 y 19).","start":97.273,"end":101.351,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Regresión de prompts antes de cada cambio (día 20).","start":101.901,"end":104.678,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Threat model, red teaming y tests de inyección (días 22 a 24).","start":105.228,"end":109.097,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":6,"say":"tret"},{"start":14,"end":25,"say":"red tíming"}]}],[{"text":"Objetivos y pruebas de performance con métricas de tokens (días 25 a 27).","start":109.647,"end":114.003,"terms":[{"start":23,"end":34,"say":"perfórmans"}]}],[{"text":"Gates en CI, infra reproducible y observabilidad (días 28 a 30).","start":114.553,"end":118.631,"terms":[{"start":0,"end":5,"say":"gueits"},{"start":9,"end":11,"say":"ci ai"}]}]]},{"section":"Paso 1 de 6","start":119.431,"end":161.888,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 1.","start":119.931,"end":120.828,"terms":[]},{"text":"El recorrido completo.","start":121.128,"end":122.547,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Recorré el diagrama de punta a punta: un cambio entra por el repo, lo produce un agente o una persona, y pasa por tests, evals, seguridad y performance antes de llegar a producción, donde se sigue midiendo.","start":123.097,"end":133.525,"terms":[{"start":140,"end":151,"say":"perfórmans"}]}],[{"text":"Seguí un cambio de punta a punta sobre el diagrama.","start":134.075,"end":136.585,"terms":[]},{"text":"Entra por el repositorio, escrito por una persona o por un agente que trabaja con reglas y permisos.","start":136.885,"end":142.031,"terms":[]},{"text":"Pasa por los tests deterministas y los guardrails.","start":142.331,"end":144.888,"terms":[{"start":39,"end":49,"say":"gárdreils"}]},{"text":"Si toca algo relacionado con un modelo, pasa por los evals con su dataset y su juez calibrado.","start":145.188,"end":149.916,"terms":[]},{"text":"Antes del release, por seguridad y por performance.","start":150.216,"end":153.028,"terms":[{"start":10,"end":17,"say":"rilís"},{"start":39,"end":50,"say":"perfórmans"}]},{"text":"En producción sigue medido con trazas y evals en línea, y cada fallo vuelve al conjunto de regresión.","start":153.328,"end":158.346,"terms":[]},{"text":"El circuito cerrado es lo que hace que el sistema mejore con el uso.","start":158.646,"end":161.888,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 2 de 6","start":162.688,"end":195.881,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 2.","start":163.188,"end":164.085,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un checklist honesto.","start":164.385,"end":165.618,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Completá el checklist con honestidad.","start":166.168,"end":168.133,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"Lo vi en un video\" no cuenta; cuenta lo que corre en un repo.","start":168.433,"end":171.222,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Completá el checklist de doce piezas con una regla estricta: una pieza cuenta solo si corre en un repositorio y alguien la usa.","start":171.772,"end":177.765,"terms":[]},{"text":"Haberla leído, haberla visto en un video o haberla probado una tarde no cuenta.","start":178.065,"end":181.887,"terms":[]},{"text":"La regla incomoda, y por eso sirve.","start":182.187,"end":184.326,"terms":[]},{"text":"La mayoría de los equipos descubre que tiene dos o tres piezas reales y varias a medio hacer.","start":184.626,"end":189.11,"terms":[]},{"text":"No es un mal resultado.","start":189.41,"end":190.934,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es un punto de partida preciso, y es lo que necesitás para planificar sin engañarte.","start":191.234,"end":195.881,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 3 de 6","start":196.681,"end":228.153,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 3.","start":197.181,"end":198.077,"terms":[]},{"text":"Tres piezas, por riesgo.","start":198.377,"end":200.075,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Elegí tres piezas faltantes y ordenalas por riesgo de tu producto, no por lo que más te divierte.","start":200.625,"end":205.585,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"De las que faltan, elegí tres y ordenalas según el riesgo de tu producto, no según lo que te resulte más interesante.","start":206.135,"end":212.117,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si tu sistema tiene un agente con herramientas y no hay tests de inyección, eso va antes que optimizar costos.","start":212.417,"end":217.748,"terms":[]},{"text":"Si una funcionalidad con un modelo está en producción sin ningún eval, eso va primero.","start":218.048,"end":222.358,"terms":[]},{"text":"Preguntate qué fallo te haría más daño mañana y qué pieza lo habría detectado.","start":222.658,"end":226.666,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esa es la primera.","start":226.966,"end":228.153,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 4 de 6","start":228.953,"end":261.832,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 4.","start":229.453,"end":230.454,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un plan de noventa días.","start":230.754,"end":232.313,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Armá el plan de 90 días: una pieza por mes, con un entregable verificable para cada una.","start":232.863,"end":237.602,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Asigná una pieza por mes, cada una con un entregable verificable.","start":238.152,"end":241.696,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"Aprender sobre evals\" no es un entregable.","start":241.996,"end":244.332,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"Un eval de treinta casos para el clasificador, corriendo en el pipeline de PR\" sí.","start":244.632,"end":248.721,"terms":[{"start":64,"end":72,"say":"páiplain"},{"start":76,"end":78,"say":"pe erre"}]},{"text":"Un mes por pieza parece lento.","start":249.021,"end":250.754,"terms":[]},{"text":"Es realista si lo hacés junto con tu trabajo habitual.","start":251.054,"end":253.82,"terms":[]},{"text":"Poné las fechas en el calendario y contale el plan a alguien de tu equipo.","start":254.12,"end":257.478,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un compromiso que conoce otra persona se cumple bastante más que uno privado.","start":257.778,"end":261.832,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 5 de 6","start":262.632,"end":297.53,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 5.","start":263.132,"end":264.133,"terms":[]},{"text":"Publicar evidencia.","start":264.433,"end":265.725,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Publicá evidencia: el repo con un README que explique qué mide cada parte, y un caso escrito con números propios.","start":266.275,"end":272.036,"terms":[{"start":34,"end":40,"say":"ridmi"}]}],[{"text":"A medida que termines cada pieza, dejala visible.","start":272.586,"end":275.328,"terms":[]},{"text":"Un repositorio con un README que explique qué mide cada parte y cómo correrla.","start":275.628,"end":279.439,"terms":[{"start":22,"end":28,"say":"ridmi"}]},{"text":"Un caso escrito con tus propios números: qué problema había, qué mediste, qué decidiste y qué pasó después.","start":279.739,"end":285.036,"terms":[]},{"text":"Usá datos propios o sintéticos, nunca información de un cliente.","start":285.336,"end":289.17,"terms":[]},{"text":"Esa evidencia sirve dentro de tu equipo, para que otros adopten lo que armaste, y hacia afuera, porque muestra criterio aplicado y no solo conocimiento de herramientas.","start":289.47,"end":297.53,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Paso 6 de 6","start":298.33,"end":329.895,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Paso 6.","start":298.83,"end":299.796,"terms":[]},{"text":"Volver a la matriz.","start":300.096,"end":301.399,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Actualizá la matriz del día 2 y fijá una fecha para repetir la autoevaluación.","start":301.949,"end":306.119,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Abrí la matriz de habilidades del día dos y actualizala con lo que hiciste en este mes.","start":306.669,"end":310.724,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las filas que estaban en cero o en uno deberían tener ahora evidencia enlazada.","start":311.024,"end":314.951,"terms":[]},{"text":"Poné una fecha para repetir la autoevaluación dentro de tres meses, al terminar el plan.","start":315.251,"end":319.712,"terms":[]},{"text":"Las herramientas concretas de esta serie van a cambiar, algunas en pocos meses.","start":320.012,"end":324.043,"terms":[]},{"text":"Lo que se mantiene es el método: definir qué es correcto, medirlo de forma repetible y decidir con datos.","start":324.343,"end":329.895,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Ejemplo","start":330.695,"end":368.015,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Un ejemplo.","start":331.195,"end":332.115,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Una SDET completa el checklist al terminar la serie.","start":332.665,"end":335.663,"terms":[{"start":4,"end":8,"say":"ese de e te"}]},{"text":"Piezas reales: la regla de delegación, un repo con reglas para agentes y el ciclo de TDD con guardrails.","start":335.963,"end":341.213,"terms":[{"start":93,"end":103,"say":"gárdreils"}]},{"text":"A medio hacer: un eval que corre a mano.","start":341.513,"end":343.699,"terms":[]},{"text":"Faltantes: todo lo demás.","start":343.999,"end":345.789,"terms":[]},{"text":"Su producto tiene un asistente con acceso a datos de clientes y a una herramienta de mail.","start":346.089,"end":350.225,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ordena por riesgo.","start":350.525,"end":351.874,"terms":[]},{"text":"Primer mes, tests de inyección sobre las acciones del agente.","start":352.174,"end":355.161,"terms":[]},{"text":"Segundo mes, el eval con dataset versionado dentro del pipeline.","start":355.461,"end":358.97,"terms":[{"start":55,"end":63,"say":"páiplain"}]},{"text":"Tercer mes, trazas y una alerta de calidad en producción.","start":359.27,"end":362.395,"terms":[]},{"text":"Deja costos y performance para el trimestre siguiente, y lo anota como una decisión, no como un olvido.","start":362.695,"end":368.015,"terms":[{"start":14,"end":25,"say":"perfórmans"}]}]]},{"section":"Errores comunes","start":368.815,"end":384.961,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Los errores más comunes.","start":369.315,"end":370.839,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Marcar como hecha una pieza que solo viste o probaste una vez.","start":371.389,"end":374.689,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Elegir qué construir por interés y no por riesgo del producto.","start":375.239,"end":378.446,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Planear las doce piezas para el mismo trimestre.","start":378.996,"end":381.564,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Publicar evidencia con datos o detalles de un cliente.","start":382.114,"end":384.961,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":385.761,"end":403.196,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Para saber que quedó bien, revisá esto.","start":386.261,"end":388.528,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El checklist está completo con la regla de que solo cuenta lo que corre.","start":389.078,"end":392.238,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Hay tres piezas elegidas y ordenadas por riesgo.","start":392.788,"end":395.473,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"El plan de noventa días tiene un entregable verificable por mes.","start":396.023,"end":399.241,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"La matriz del día dos está actualizada y tiene fecha de revisión.","start":399.791,"end":403.196,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Lo que la IA no hizo bien","start":403.996,"end":412.645,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Y lo que la IA no hizo bien.","start":404.496,"end":406.066,"terms":[{"start":12,"end":14,"say":"i a"}]}],[{"text":"Resume bien y no prioriza.","start":406.616,"end":408.244,"terms":[]},{"text":"Decidir qué riesgo va primero en tu producto sigue siendo trabajo de una persona.","start":408.544,"end":412.645,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"La guía escrita","start":413.445,"end":425.415,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Eso fue el día 31.","start":413.945,"end":415.596,"terms":[]},{"text":"En la guía escrita, en gastongenaud.com, tenés el código para copiar y las preguntas frecuentes.","start":415.896,"end":421.135,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Con esta guía se cierra la serie.","start":421.685,"end":423.545,"terms":[]},{"text":"Gracias por llegar hasta acá.","start":423.845,"end":425.415,"terms":[]}]]}]},{"id":"d31-en","day":31,"lang":"en","title":"Capstone: the full AI quality pipeline and your 2027 roadmap","video":"https://gastongenaud.com/publications/qa-ai/videos/d31-en.mp4","duration":472.089,"slides":[{"section":"Video guide","start":0,"end":17.757,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Day 31 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.","start":0.5,"end":4.148,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Capstone: the full AI quality pipeline and your 2027 roadmap.","start":4.698,"end":9.658,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The previous 30 days tied into a single pipeline, a 12-point checklist to self-assess and a 90-day plan with public evidence.","start":10.208,"end":17.757,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The problem","start":18.557,"end":38.485,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"For thirty days you saw separate pieces: a rule for delegating, an eval, a configured agent, a threat model, a load test, a gate.","start":19.057,"end":27.605,"terms":[]},{"text":"Each solves a concrete problem.","start":27.905,"end":30.148,"terms":[]},{"text":"The greater value appears when they connect, because that's where they stop being experiments and become a quality system for software that uses AI.","start":30.448,"end":38.485,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"How it fits together","start":39.285,"end":61.477,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"This last day adds no tools.","start":39.785,"end":42.086,"terms":[]},{"text":"It assembles the full map, asks you for an honest assessment of what you really have working and helps you choose what to build first.","start":42.386,"end":49.239,"terms":[]},{"text":"The series doesn't expect you to implement all twelve pieces.","start":49.539,"end":53.152,"terms":[]},{"text":"It expects you to know which ones matter to your product, in what order, and to be able to show evidence of progress three months from now.","start":53.452,"end":61.477,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Requirements","start":62.277,"end":71.351,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To follow this guide you need:","start":62.777,"end":64.475,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The qa-ai-lab repo with whatever you built during the series.","start":65.025,"end":68.696,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The skills matrix from day 2.","start":69.246,"end":71.351,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"12-piece checklist","start":72.151,"end":132.563,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"12-piece checklist.","start":72.651,"end":74.139,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Delegation rule: verifiable, cheap to review, reversible (day 1).","start":74.689,"end":79.359,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Repo with rules for agents, tools and permissions (day 9).","start":79.909,"end":83.824,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Tests written by people, implementation by agents, with guardrails (days 13 and 14).","start":84.374,"end":90.31,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Playwright agents with plan and diff review (days 10 and 11).","start":90.86,"end":94.972,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Eval harness with a versioned prompt (day 8).","start":95.522,"end":98.392,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pinned model versions and a calibrated judge (days 15 and 16).","start":98.942,"end":103.252,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Golden dataset with dev, test and regression (day 17).","start":103.802,"end":108.205,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Repeated runs, ranges and cost per call (days 18 and 19).","start":108.755,"end":113.39,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Prompt regression before every change (day 20).","start":113.94,"end":117.066,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Threat model, red teaming and injection tests (days 22 to 24).","start":117.616,"end":122.042,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Performance targets and tests with token metrics (days 25 to 27).","start":122.592,"end":126.983,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"CI gates, reproducible infra and observability (days 28 to 30).","start":127.533,"end":132.563,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 1 of 6","start":133.363,"end":182.67,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 1.","start":133.863,"end":134.899,"terms":[]},{"text":"The full journey.","start":135.199,"end":136.362,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Walk the diagram end to end: a change enters through the repo, produced by an agent or a person, and goes through tests, evals, security and performance before reaching production, where measuring continues.","start":136.912,"end":149.326,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Follow a change end to end on the diagram.","start":149.876,"end":152.433,"terms":[]},{"text":"It enters through the repository, written by a person or by an agent working with rules and permissions.","start":152.733,"end":158.506,"terms":[]},{"text":"It goes through deterministic tests and guardrails.","start":158.806,"end":162.164,"terms":[]},{"text":"If it touches anything related to a model, it goes through evals with their dataset and calibrated judge.","start":162.464,"end":168.411,"terms":[]},{"text":"Before release, through security and performance.","start":168.711,"end":171.767,"terms":[]},{"text":"In production it stays measured with traces and online evals, and every failure returns to the regression set.","start":172.067,"end":178.548,"terms":[]},{"text":"The closed loop is what makes the system improve with use.","start":178.848,"end":182.67,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 2 of 6","start":183.47,"end":221.631,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 2.","start":183.97,"end":185.168,"terms":[]},{"text":"An honest checklist.","start":185.468,"end":187.108,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Fill in the checklist honestly.","start":187.658,"end":189.739,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"I saw it in a video\" doesn't count; what runs in a repo does.","start":190.039,"end":194.07,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Fill in the twelve-piece checklist with a strict rule: a piece counts only if it runs in a repository and someone uses it.","start":194.62,"end":201.705,"terms":[]},{"text":"Having read about it, seen it in a video or tried it one afternoon doesn't count.","start":202.005,"end":206.57,"terms":[]},{"text":"The rule is uncomfortable, and that's why it works.","start":206.87,"end":210.008,"terms":[]},{"text":"Most teams discover they have two or three real pieces and several half done.","start":210.308,"end":214.734,"terms":[]},{"text":"That isn't a bad result.","start":215.034,"end":216.685,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's a precise starting point, and it's what you need to plan without fooling yourself.","start":216.985,"end":221.631,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 3 of 6","start":222.431,"end":257.12,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 3.","start":222.931,"end":224.014,"terms":[]},{"text":"Three pieces, by risk.","start":224.314,"end":226.093,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Pick three missing pieces and order them by your product's risk, not by what's most fun.","start":226.643,"end":231.974,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"From the missing ones, pick three and order them by your product's risk, not by what you find most interesting.","start":232.524,"end":239.098,"terms":[]},{"text":"If your system has a tool-using agent and there are no injection tests, that goes before optimizing costs.","start":239.398,"end":245.577,"terms":[]},{"text":"If a feature with a model is in production with no eval at all, that goes first.","start":245.877,"end":250.64,"terms":[]},{"text":"Ask yourself which failure would hurt you most tomorrow and which piece would have caught it.","start":250.94,"end":255.413,"terms":[]},{"text":"That's the first one.","start":255.713,"end":257.12,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 4 of 6","start":257.92,"end":293.133,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 4.","start":258.42,"end":259.433,"terms":[]},{"text":"A ninety-day plan.","start":259.733,"end":261.071,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Build the 90-day plan: one piece per month, each with a verifiable deliverable.","start":261.621,"end":266.639,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Assign one piece per month, each with a verifiable deliverable.","start":267.189,"end":270.93,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"Learn about evals\" isn't a deliverable.","start":271.23,"end":273.647,"terms":[]},{"text":"\"A thirty-case eval for the classifier, running in the PR pipeline\" is.","start":273.947,"end":278.187,"terms":[]},{"text":"One month per piece looks slow.","start":278.487,"end":280.615,"terms":[]},{"text":"It's realistic if you do it alongside your usual work.","start":280.915,"end":284.319,"terms":[]},{"text":"Put the dates on the calendar and tell someone on your team about the plan.","start":284.619,"end":288.441,"terms":[]},{"text":"A commitment another person knows about gets kept far more often than a private one.","start":288.741,"end":293.133,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 5 of 6","start":293.933,"end":332.778,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 5.","start":294.433,"end":295.561,"terms":[]},{"text":"Publish evidence.","start":295.861,"end":297.106,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Publish evidence: the repo with a README explaining what each part measures, and a written case with your own numbers.","start":297.656,"end":304.393,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"As you finish each piece, leave it visible.","start":304.943,"end":307.778,"terms":[]},{"text":"A repository with a README explaining what each part measures and how to run it.","start":308.078,"end":312.574,"terms":[]},{"text":"A written case with your own numbers: what the problem was, what you measured, what you decided and what happened next.","start":312.874,"end":319.703,"terms":[]},{"text":"Use your own or synthetic data, never a client's information.","start":320.003,"end":323.884,"terms":[]},{"text":"That evidence works inside your team, so others adopt what you built, and outside it, because it shows applied judgment and not just knowledge of tools.","start":324.184,"end":332.778,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Step 6 of 6","start":333.578,"end":366.35,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"Step 6.","start":334.078,"end":335.264,"terms":[]},{"text":"Back to the matrix.","start":335.564,"end":336.937,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Update the day 2 matrix and set a date to repeat the self-assessment.","start":337.487,"end":341.483,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Open the skills matrix from day two and update it with what you did this month.","start":342.033,"end":346.552,"terms":[]},{"text":"Rows that were at zero or one should now have linked evidence.","start":346.852,"end":350.617,"terms":[]},{"text":"Set a date to repeat the self-assessment three months from now, when the plan ends.","start":350.917,"end":355.494,"terms":[]},{"text":"The specific tools in this series will change, some within a few months.","start":355.794,"end":359.999,"terms":[]},{"text":"What holds is the method: define what correct means, measure it repeatably and decide with data.","start":360.299,"end":366.35,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Example","start":367.15,"end":408.267,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"An example.","start":367.65,"end":368.756,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"An SDET fills in the checklist at the end of the series.","start":369.306,"end":372.478,"terms":[{"start":3,"end":7,"say":"ess det"}]},{"text":"Real pieces: the delegation rule, a repo with rules for agents and the TDD cycle with guardrails.","start":372.778,"end":379.039,"terms":[]},{"text":"Half done: an eval run by hand.","start":379.339,"end":381.791,"terms":[]},{"text":"Missing: everything else.","start":382.091,"end":384.102,"terms":[]},{"text":"Her product has an assistant with access to customer data and an email tool.","start":384.402,"end":388.758,"terms":[]},{"text":"She orders by risk.","start":389.058,"end":390.478,"terms":[]},{"text":"First month, injection tests on the agent's actions.","start":390.778,"end":394.147,"terms":[]},{"text":"Second month, the eval with a versioned dataset inside the pipeline.","start":394.447,"end":398.49,"terms":[]},{"text":"Third month, traces and a quality alert in production.","start":398.79,"end":402.252,"terms":[]},{"text":"She leaves cost and performance for the following quarter, and writes that down as a decision, not an oversight.","start":402.552,"end":408.267,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Common mistakes","start":409.067,"end":426.107,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"The most common mistakes.","start":409.567,"end":411.23,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Marking as done a piece you only saw or tried once.","start":411.78,"end":415.138,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Choosing what to build by interest and not by product risk.","start":415.688,"end":419.173,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Planning all twelve pieces for the same quarter.","start":419.723,"end":422.42,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Publishing evidence with a client's data or details.","start":422.97,"end":426.107,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Checklist","start":426.907,"end":444.876,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"To know it is done, check this.","start":427.407,"end":429.65,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The checklist is filled in under the rule that only what runs counts.","start":430.2,"end":433.79,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"Three pieces are chosen and ordered by risk.","start":434.34,"end":436.897,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The ninety-day plan has one verifiable deliverable per month.","start":437.447,"end":441.049,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"The day two matrix is updated and has a review date.","start":441.599,"end":444.876,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"Where AI fell short","start":445.676,"end":456.321,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"And where AI fell short.","start":446.176,"end":448.071,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"It summarizes well and doesn't prioritize.","start":448.621,"end":451.712,"terms":[]},{"text":"Deciding which risk comes first in your product is still a person's job.","start":452.012,"end":456.321,"terms":[]}]]},{"section":"The written guide","start":457.121,"end":470.589,"paragraphs":[[{"text":"That was day 31.","start":457.621,"end":459.377,"terms":[]},{"text":"The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently asked questions.","start":459.677,"end":465.659,"terms":[]}],[{"text":"This guide closes the series.","start":466.209,"end":468.452,"terms":[]},{"text":"Thanks for making it this far.","start":468.752,"end":470.589,"terms":[]}]]}]}]};
